s&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2026&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering Workforce&quot;,&quot;STEM Education&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Directorate Profiles&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Profiles of the National Science Foundation Directorates&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Profiles of the National Science Foundation Directorates&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:6958,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NCSES 26-204&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;&lt;p&gt;The Directorate Profiles from the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the U.S. National Science Foundation (NSF) provide visualizations on research doctorate recipients from U.S. institutions, by NSF directorate. This profile presents data for doctorates in STEM education, using the 2025 Survey of Earned Doctorates and the 2023 Survey of Doctorate Recipients.&lt;/p&gt;&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/30/2026&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;ncses26204&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;ncses26204&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2026.09.30&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Directorate for STEM Education (EDU)&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2026&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering Workforce&quot;,&quot;STEM Education&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reference&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Profiles of the National Science Foundation Directorates&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Profiles of the National Science Foundation Directorates&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:6918,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NCSES 26-209&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;&lt;p&gt;The Directorate Profiles from the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the U.S. National Science Foundation (NSF) provide visualizations on research doctorate recipients from U.S. institutions, by NSF directorate. This reference document provides additional methodology information for the graphics in the 7 profiles, as well as tabular versions of the data used.&lt;/p&gt;&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/30/2026&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;ncses26209&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;ncses26209&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2026.09.30&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Directorate Profiles: Technical Notes&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2026&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering Workforce&quot;,&quot;STEM Education&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:6778,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 26-326&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;&lt;p&gt;These tables release data from the 2025 Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED), an annual census of research doctorate recipients from U.S. universities. The tables include long-term trend data on doctorate recipients by field; educational pathways to the doctorate and sources of financial support; postgraduation employment plans; and representation of different demographic groups and temporary visa holders among doctorate recipients. Data are also available in an interactive data tool (https://ncsesdata.nsf.gov/builder/sed) and the Restricted Data Analysis System (https://ncsesdata.nsf.gov/rdas/). The SED is sponsored by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the U.S. National Science Foundation and by three other federal agencies: the National Institutes of Health, Department of Education, and National Endowment for the Humanities.&lt;/p&gt;&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/25/2026&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf26326&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf26326&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2026.09.25&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities: 2025 Data Tables&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2026&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:6878,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 26-328&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;&lt;p id=\&quot;isPasted\&quot;&gt;Federal Facilities Research and Development Survey: Fiscal Year 2025&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;These data tables are from the FY 2025 Federal Facilities Research and Development (FFRD) Survey. These tables present data on research and experimental development (R&amp;amp;D) expenditures for all federally owned and operated facilities in the United States. This survey is the unique source of federal performer-reported data on R&amp;amp;D expenditures and is conducted by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the U.S. National Science Foundation.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/24/2026&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf26328&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf26328&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2026.09.24&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Federal Facilities Research and Development (FFRD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Facilities Research and Development (FFRD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Facilities Research and Development: Fiscal Year 2025&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2026&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:6798,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 26-327&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;&lt;p&gt;Federal expenditures for research and experimental development saw an increase of 7% from FY 2024 to FY 2025. However, the Department of Defense (DOD) and all other non-defense civilian agencies show very different year-to-year trends. While DOD increased 16%, civilian agencies declined 14%. DOD’s relative share of federal R&amp;amp;D performance outweighs the downward trend of the civilian agencies in FY 2025. These data are from the latest edition of the Federal Facilities Research and Development Survey, sponsored by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the U.S. National Science Foundation.&lt;/p&gt;&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/24/2026&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf26327&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf26327&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2026.09.24&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Federal Facilities Research and Development (FFRD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Facilities Research and Development (FFRD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;R&amp;D Performance within Department of Defense Facilities Increased 16% while R&amp;D within Civilian Federal Facilities Decreased 14% in FY 2025&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2026&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:6678,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 26-323&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;&lt;p&gt;These data tables provide data on research and development (R&amp;amp;D) expenditures for federally funded research and development centers (FFRDCs). Expenditures data are displayed for individually named FFRDCs, by source of funding and type of R&amp;amp;D. Data are from the FY 2025 FFRDC Research and Development Survey, conducted by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the U.S. National Science Foundation.&lt;/p&gt;&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;08/20/2026&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf26323&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf26323&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2026.08.20&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Expenditures: FY 2025&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2026&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:6658,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 26-322&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;&lt;p&gt;Federally funded research and development centers (FFRDCs) in the United States enable federal agencies to meet their research and development (R&amp;amp;D) needs through government-owned, contractor-operated research centers. These centers provide capabilities that cannot be met as effectively through other resources. The nation’s 41 FFRDCs spent $31.8 billion on R&amp;amp;D in FY 2025, an increase of 0.34% over FY 2024 in current dollars. These and the other statistics in this report come from the FY 2025 FFRDC Research and Development Survey, conducted by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the U.S. National Science Foundation&lt;/p&gt;&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;08/20/2026&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf26322&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf26322&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2026.08.20&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;R&amp;D Spending at Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers Holds Steady in FY 2025&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2026&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Innovation and Global Competitiveness&quot;,&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:6598,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 26-321&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;&lt;p&gt;The detailed tables in this report present data from the 2024 Annual Business Survey (ABS) (data year 2023). The ABS is the primary source of data on research and development of for-profit, nonfarm businesses with one to nine employees operating in the 50 U.S. states and the District of Columbia. The ABS also collects data on innovation, technology, intellectual property, research activities at nonprofit organizations, financing, management practice, company information, and business owner characteristics of business of all sizes. The 2024 ABS is the seventh year of the ABS. The ABS is cosponsored by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the U.S. National Science Foundation and the Census Bureau&lt;/p&gt;&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;08/17/2026&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf26321&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf26321&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2026.08.17&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Annual Business Survey (ABS)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Annual Business Survey (ABS)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Annual Business Survey: 2024 (Data Year 2023)&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2026&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Innovation and Global Competitiveness&quot;,&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:6638,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 26-324&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;&lt;p&gt;This InfoBrief presents research and development (R&amp;amp;D) data on microbusinesses, defined here as businesses with one to nine domestic employees. In 2023, microbusinesses reported a total of $8.3 billion in R&amp;amp;D costs, of which 86%, or $7.1 billion, was domestic and 72%, or $5.9 billion, was performed by the microbusinesses themselves. Data for this InfoBrief are from the Annual Business Survey (ABS), developed and cosponsored by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the U.S. National Science Foundation and by the Census Bureau.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;08/17/2026&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf26324&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf26324&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2026.08.17&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Annual Business Survey (ABS)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Annual Business Survey (ABS)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Microbusinesses Performed $5.9 Billion of R&amp;D in the United States in 2023&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2026&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:6538,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 26-320&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;These tables present the results of the FY 2024 Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions (now the Federal Science and Engineering Support module within the Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development). Data include science and engineering support by type of activity, as well as rankings by state, agency, and individual institution. This survey is the only source of comprehensive data on federal science and engineering funding to individual academic and nonprofit institutions.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;06/24/2026&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf26320&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf26320&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2026.06.24&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions: Fiscal Year 2024&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Federal Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Financial Support&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Institutions&quot;,&quot;Minorities in Higher Education&quot;,&quot;Nonprofit R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Race and Ethnicity&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Capital Expenditures&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Agreement (contract, grant, reimbursement, etc.)&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;,&quot;Type of R&amp;D (basic, applied, experimental development)&quot;,&quot;Work Activity&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2026&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:6518,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 26-319&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;In FY 2024, federal agency obligations to universities and colleges for science and engineering (S&amp;E) activities decreased 4.3% from FY 2023 to nearly $47 billion. S&amp;E activities include research and experimental development (R&amp;D); R&amp;D plant; facilities and equipment for instruction in S&amp;E; S&amp;E fellowships, traineeships, and training grants; and other general support for S&amp;E. Estimates are from the FY 2024 Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support module within the Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development, conducted by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the U.S. National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;06/24/2026&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf26319&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf26319&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2026.06.24&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;In FY 2024, Federal Science and Engineering Support for Higher Education Declined 4.3% from FY 2023; Federal Obligations to Nonprofits Totaled $11 Billion&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Clinical Trials and Medical Projects&quot;,&quot;Federal Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Financial Support&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Institutions&quot;,&quot;Nonprofit R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Agreement (contract, grant, reimbursement, etc.)&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;,&quot;Type of R&amp;D (basic, applied, experimental development)&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2026&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:6438,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 26-316&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This set of data tables presents data on the research and development (R&amp;D) outlays and obligations of federal agencies. Outlays are provided by agency for total R&amp;D and total R&amp;D plant. Obligations are provided for R&amp;D by agency for type of work (basic research, applied research, and experimental development), field of R&amp;D (for basic research, applied research, and experimental development), type of performer, and geographic area. Obligations are provided for R&amp;D plant by agency for type of performer and geographic area. These data, collected by the Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development from the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the U.S. National Science Foundation, are the primary source of information about federal funding for R&amp;D in the United States.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;05/04/2026&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf26316&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf26316&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2026.05.04&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Funds for Research and Development: Fiscal Years 2024–25&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;By Country&quot;,&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Federal Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Federally Funded Research and Development Centers (FFRDC) R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Institutions&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Capital Expenditures&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Cost Type&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Agreement (contract, grant, reimbursement, etc.)&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;,&quot;Type of R&amp;D (basic, applied, experimental development)&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2026&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:6498,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 26-317&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Federal obligations for research and experimental development saw an increase of 4.4% from FY 2023 to FY 2024. However, although preliminary federal agency obligations for FY 2025 indicate most federal agencies will see declining R&amp;D obligations in FY 2025 the total is estimated to decrease by 0.1%. These data are from the latest edition of the Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development, sponsored by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the U.S. National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;05/04/2026&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf26317&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf26317&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2026.05.04&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal R&amp;D Obligations Increased 4.4% in FY 2024; Most Agencies Estimate Declines in FY 2025&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;COVID-19 Pandemic Impacts&quot;,&quot;Federal Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Cost Type&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;,&quot;Type of R&amp;D (basic, applied, experimental development)&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2026&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reports&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering Indicators&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering Indicators&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:6398,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSB-SEP-2026-1&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;&lt;p&gt;The past quarter century has reshaped the U.S. science and engineering enterprise through the business sector’s dominant role in funding and performing research and development (R&amp;amp;D), the concentration of innovation activity in information technologies and critical and emerging technology areas, and the rise of China as a competitor and R&amp;amp;D performer. The United States maintains comparative advantages in highly cited research and patents, venture capital–backed innovation, and high-technology services, where U.S. firms dominate global production. U.S. universities remain top destinations for international postsecondary students.&lt;/p&gt;&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;05/04/2026&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsbsep20261&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsbsep20261&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2026.05.04&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;The State of U.S. Science and Engineering 2026&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2026&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Innovation and Global Competitiveness&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reports&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering Indicators&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering Indicators&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:5978,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSB-2026-2&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Science, technology, and innovation activities expand the frontiers of fundamental and technical knowledge, often leading to new discoveries. These may be translated into outputs such as new products, licensed technologies, and companies, ultimately impacting economic output, productivity, and global trade. This thematic report presents indicators of these activities and the innovation process, which contribute to the economic competitiveness and national security of the United States and countries globally.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;05/01/2026&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsb20262&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsb20262&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2026.05.01&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Translation to Impact: U.S. and Global Science, Technology, and Innovation Output&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2026&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:6458,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 26-318&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;In FY 2023, federal agency obligations to universities and colleges for science and engineering (S&amp;E) support totaled $49.0 billion, with higher education institutions in California and New York receiving the most ($6.1 billion and $3.9 billion, respectively). However, when the data were normalized by the number of recipient institutions within each state, Maryland and Arizona received the most federal support for S&amp;E on a per-institution basis. Estimates are from the FY 2023 Survey of Federal S&amp;E Support module within the Survey of Federal Funds for R&amp;D, conducted by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the U.S. National Science Foundation. &quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;03/17/2026&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf26318&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf26318&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2026.03.17&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Distribution of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Higher Education Institutions Across States&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Federal Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Institutions&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2026&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;STEM Education&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reports&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:6378,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 26-315&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The number of doctorate recipients from U.S. universities reached 58,131 in 2024, an increase of 325 students from 2023, similar to the increase between 2022 and 2023. Since the inception of the Survey of Earned Doctorates in academic year 1958, there has been an upward trend in the number of doctorates awarded by U.S. institutions—with an average annual growth of 3.0%, punctuated by periods of slower growth and some declines.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;03/13/2026&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf26315&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf26315&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2026.03.13&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities: 2024&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Citizenship&quot;,&quot;COVID-19 Pandemic Impacts&quot;,&quot;Employment Sector&quot;,&quot;Employment Status&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Degrees&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Enrollment&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Institutions&quot;,&quot;Minorities in Higher Education&quot;,&quot;Postdoctorates&quot;,&quot;Postgraduation Plans&quot;,&quot;Race and Ethnicity&quot;,&quot;Sex&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2026&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reference&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:6000,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Annual update to the Master Government List of Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers for 2026.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;03/11/2026&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://ncses.nsf.gov/resource/master-gov-lists-ffrdc&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf00000&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2026.03.11&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Master Government List of Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers: 2026&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2026&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding, by Budget Function&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding, by Budget Function&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:6198,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 26-309&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report contains data on the budget authority provided to U.S. federal agencies to fund the research and development (R&amp;D) and R&amp;D plant components of their programs in FYs 2024–26. FY 2024 numbers are final budget authority, FY 2025 numbers are preliminary and based on the annualized continuing resolution, FY 2026 numbers are from the president’s proposed budget. The tables in this report provide this budget authority detail, by agency and major programs, for each of the government budget functions in which R&amp;D is present. Several concluding tables also show budget authority data for R&amp;D in earlier years. Data were compiled by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the U.S. National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;03/10/2026&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf26309&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf26309&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2026.03.10&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding, by Budget Function: Fiscal Years 2024–26&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Federal Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Capital Expenditures&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Cost Type&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Intensity&quot;,&quot;Type of R&amp;D (basic, applied, experimental development)&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2026&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:6338,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 26-313&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report provides current data on the levels of and key trends in the performance and funding of research and development (R&amp;D) in the United States, with comparisons to the historical record (back to 1953). Detailed statistical tables present data on U.S. R&amp;D expenditures by performing sector, source of funds, type of R&amp;D, and state. Data are drawn from multiple surveys from the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the U.S. National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;02/27/2026&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf26313&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf26313&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2026.02.27&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources: 2023–24 Data Update&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Federally Funded Research and Development Centers (FFRDC) R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Nonprofit R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Production and Value Added&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Cost Type&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Intensity&quot;,&quot;Type of R&amp;D (basic, applied, experimental development)&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2026&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:6358,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 26-314&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Research and experimental development (R&amp;D) performed in the United States totaled $937 billion in 2023: $138 billion was basic research, $174 billion was applied research, and $625 billion was experimental development. U.S. total R&amp;D expenditures are estimated to be $993 billion for 2024, or $792 billion when adjusted for inflation (in constant 2017 dollars). Data from the National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources annual series by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics (NCSES) within the U.S. National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;02/27/2026&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf26314&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf26314&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2026.02.27&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;U.S. R&amp;D Totaled $937 Billion in 2023; Estimate for 2024 Indicates Further Increase to $993 Billion&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Federal Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Federally Funded Research and Development Centers (FFRDC) R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Cost Type&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Intensity&quot;,&quot;Type of R&amp;D (basic, applied, experimental development)&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2026&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;STEM Education&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:6178,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 26-308&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Between 2023 and 2024, the number of postdoctoral appointees (postdocs) rose by 6.1%, from 65,850 to 69,877—the highest number ever reported to the Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS). Before 2024, the 66,247 postdocs reported in 2019 was the high for the number of postdocs at GSS institutions. Graduate student counts were more complex, with generally stable enrollment in the master’s science, engineering, and health (SEH) program enrollment between 2023 and 2024 and a slight increase in doctoral SEH programs during that period. Findings are from the 2024 GSS, funded by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the U.S. National Science Foundation and by the National Institutes of Health. &quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;02/18/2026&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf26308&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf26308&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2026.02.18&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Postdoctoral Appointments Rise While Graduate Enrollment Slows&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Citizenship&quot;,&quot;COVID-19 Pandemic Impacts&quot;,&quot;Employment Status&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Degrees&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Enrollment&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Non-faculty Research Personnel&quot;,&quot;Postdoctorates&quot;,&quot;Race and Ethnicity&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Employment&quot;,&quot;Sex&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2026&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;STEM Education&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:6158,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 26-307&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report presents data from the 2024 Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering, sponsored by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the U.S. National Science Foundation and by the National Institutes of Health. The tables show trends within science, engineering, and selected health fields on graduate student enrollment, postdoc appointments, and doctorate-holding nonfaculty researchers, as well as counts by selected characteristics, such as sex, ethnicity, race, citizenship, field of study, and source of financial support.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;02/18/2026&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf26307&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf26307&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2026.02.18&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering: Fall 2024&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Citizenship&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Financial Support&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Degrees&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Enrollment&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Institutions&quot;,&quot;Minorities in Higher Education&quot;,&quot;Non-faculty Research Personnel&quot;,&quot;Postdoctorates&quot;,&quot;Postgraduation Plans&quot;,&quot;Race and Ethnicity&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Employment&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;Sex&quot;,&quot;Work-related Training&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2026&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering Workforce&quot;,&quot;STEM Education&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoChart&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:6258,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 26-312&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Doctorate recipients’ expected median salaries vary by broad field of study and position type. However, in 2024, doctorate recipients in every broad field of study with definite post-graduation commitments in the United States expected to earn more in industry than in academia or postdoc positions. Industry also had the largest spread of expected salaries, where the highest median salary by field of study (computer and information sciences) was nearly double that of the lowest median salary (non-S&amp;E fields). &quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;02/17/2026&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf26312&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf26312&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2026.02.17&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Expected Median Salaries for Doctorate Recipients Are Highest in the Industry Sector&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Employment Sector&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Income and Salary&quot;,&quot;Postdoctorates&quot;,&quot;Postgraduation Plans&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2026&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;STEM Education&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoChart&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:6238,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 26-311&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Although most doctoral recipients in each broad field of study are funded through research assistantships or traineeships; teaching assistantships; or fellowships, scholarships, and dissertation grants, the prevalence of the financial support type varies by field. In 2024, research assistantships and traineeships were the most common source of financial support in most broad fields. About one-third of doctorate recipients in psychology, health sciences, and non-S&amp;E fields relied on their own resources as their primary funding source.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;02/17/2026&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf26311&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf26311&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2026.02.17&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Research Assistantships Are Doctorate Recipients’ Most Common Source of Primary Support in Most Fields&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Financial Support&quot;,&quot;Postdoctorates&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2026&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;STEM Education&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reports&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering Indicators&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering Indicators&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:5358,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSB-2026-1&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Talent is critical to the U.S. science and engineering enterprise and competitiveness. The U.S. science, technology, engineering, and mathematics (STEM) workforce represented about a quarter of the total domestic workforce in 2023, with distinct education, employment, and wage patterns. Recent assessments reveal incomplete recovery for U.S. K–12 students from pandemic-related STEM learning losses. STEM degree production grew at U.S. higher education institutions at all levels between 2013 and 2023. In 2023, the United States was the leading destination for international students, despite a small decline in overall share since 2013.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;02/12/2026&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsb20261&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsb20261&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2026.02.12&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;STEM Talent: Education, Training, and Workforce&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2026&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Innovation and Global Competitiveness&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:6218,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 26-306&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Nearly one-quarter (23%) of the estimated 4.9 million for-profit companies with at least one employee introduced an innovation during 2020–22. Ten percent of companies reported a product innovation and 20% of companies reported a business process innovation during this period. Data are from the 2023 Annual Business Survey (ABS) (data year 2022), which provides a comprehensive view of business innovation in the United States. The ABS was developed and is cosponsored by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the U.S. National Science Foundation and by the Census Bureau.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;02/11/2026&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf26306&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf26306&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2026.02.11&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Annual Business Survey (ABS)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Annual Business Survey (ABS)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Innovation Data from the 2023 Annual Business Survey: Data Year 2022&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;COVID-19 Pandemic Impacts&quot;,&quot;Knowledge and Technology Intensive (KTI) Industries&quot;,&quot;Product &amp; Business Process Innovation&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Intensity&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2026&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:6298,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 26-305&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Total higher education research and development (R&amp;D) expenditures reached $117.7 billion in FY 2024, an increase of $8.9 billion or 8.1% from FY 2023. Federally funded R&amp;D at universities exceeded $64 billion, accounting for 55% of total higher education R&amp;D in FY 2024. The data in this report are from the Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey, sponsored by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the U.S. National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;02/10/2026&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf26305&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf26305&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2026.02.10&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Universities Report 8.1% Growth in R&amp;D Expenditures in FY 2024, Reaching Over $117 Billion&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Clinical Trials and Medical Projects&quot;,&quot;Federal Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Institutions&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Cost Type&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2026&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBytes&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:6278,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 26-310&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;In FY 2023, federal agency obligations to the top 10 higher education recipient institutions totaled $9.9 billion, or 20.1% of all federal S&amp;E support obligations to higher education institutions. In FY 2014, the top 10 recipient institutions totaled $7.2 billion, or 22.7% of all federal S&amp;E support obligations to higher education institutions. This InfoBytes presents statistics on changes in the population of the top 10 recipient institutions during the past decade. Estimates are from the FY 2023 Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support module within the Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development, conducted by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the U.S. National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;02/05/2026&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf26310&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf26310&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2026.02.05&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Funding for Science and Engineering: Trends in Rankings Among Top Universities, FYs 2014–23&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;COVID-19 Pandemic Impacts&quot;,&quot;Federal Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Institutions&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2026&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;STEM Education&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Working Paper&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:5898,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NCSES 25-222&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Imputation of missing data from surveys aims to mitigate the risk of nonresponse bias associated with item nonresponse and provide data users with a complete data set with no missing values to facilitate data analyses. In addition, the imputation procedures seek to support multivariate inferences by reflecting the relationships among survey variables. Particularly, in imputation for longitudinal surveys, it is crucial to maintain longitudinal relationships in the data by preserving temporal patterns and accurately predicting missing data at a given time. The research described in this working paper investigates the Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR) to determine the effects of the systematic usage of information from past cycles in the cross-sectional imputation and in the longitudinal imputation. The key goal is to improve donor matching in the cross-sectional imputation and, in turn, enhance the data quality of (1) the cross-sectional data sets by strengthening the imputation accuracy and (2) the longitudinal data sets by reducing the chance of creating unobserved and unverifiable longitudinal patterns. The SDR is sponsored by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the U.S. National Science Foundation and by the National Institutes of Health.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;01/21/2026&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;ncses25222&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;ncses25222&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2026.01.21&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;A Unified Approach to Cross-Sectional and Longitudinal Imputation with Applications to the Survey of Doctorate Recipients&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Employment Sector&quot;,&quot;Employment Status&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Degrees&quot;,&quot;Income and Salary&quot;,&quot;Occupation&quot;,&quot;Postdoctorates&quot;,&quot;Race and Ethnicity&quot;,&quot;Sex&quot;,&quot;Type of R&amp;D (basic, applied, experimental development)&quot;,&quot;Work Activity&quot;,&quot;Work-related Training&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2026&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;STEM Education&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Working Paper&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:5878,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NCSES 25-221&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This working paper outlines the research conducted to develop public use synthetic microdata for the longitudinal sample of the Survey of Doctorate Recipients (LSDR) and summarizes the findings that led to the construction of the synthetic data file. Synthetic microdata were generated by using a select synthetic data approach. This paper includes the rationale behind choosing the selected aspects of synthetic data generation, a comparison of each option in consideration, and variance estimation methods, which allows users to determine the uncertainty of the estimates. The public use synthetic microdata for LSDR and corresponding estimates discussed and included in this working paper are designated as experimental statistical products.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;01/12/2026&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;ncses25221&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;ncses25221&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2026.01.12&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Developing Public Use Longitudinal Synthetic Microdata with Applications to the Survey of Doctorate Recipients&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Attitudes About Science&quot;,&quot;Citizenship&quot;,&quot;Clinical Trials and Medical Projects&quot;,&quot;Disability Status&quot;,&quot;Employment Sector&quot;,&quot;Employment Status&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Degrees&quot;,&quot;Income and Salary&quot;,&quot;Occupation&quot;,&quot;Race and Ethnicity&quot;,&quot;Sex&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2026&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:6078,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 26-304&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This publication releases data tables for the FY 2024 Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey. The tables present data on research and development expenditures at higher education institutions across all academic disciplines and include R&amp;D expenditures by institution, R&amp;D field, geographic area, source of funds, type of R&amp;D (basic research, applied research, and experimental development), cost categories (salaries, software, equipment, and indirect costs), headcounts and full-time equivalents, and trends over time. The HERD Survey is conducted by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the U.S. National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;12/23/2025&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf26304&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf26304&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2025.12.23&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Research and Development: Fiscal Year 2024&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Clinical Trials and Medical Projects&quot;,&quot;Federal Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Federally Funded Research and Development Centers (FFRDC) R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Institutions&quot;,&quot;Non-faculty Research Personnel&quot;,&quot;Occupation&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Capital Expenditures&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Cost Type&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Employment&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Agreement (contract, grant, reimbursement, etc.)&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Intensity&quot;,&quot;Sex&quot;,&quot;Type of R&amp;D (basic, applied, experimental development)&quot;,&quot;Work Activity&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2025&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:6038,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 26-303&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;State government agencies’ expenditures for research and development (R&amp;D) rose nearly 11% in FY 2024 from the prior year to $3.3 billion. Although state government expenditures for R&amp;D are relatively small within the national portfolio of R&amp;D performance, state agencies in FY 2024 performed $812 million in R&amp;D while also financing $2.5 billion in extramural R&amp;D to businesses, higher education institutions, nonprofit organizations, and others. Data are from the Survey of State Government Research and Development, sponsored by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the U.S. National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;12/11/2025&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf26303&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf26303&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2025.12.11&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of State Government Research and Development&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of State Government Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;State Government Agencies’ Expenditures for R&amp;D Increase 11% from FY 2023 to FY 2024&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Capital Expenditures&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Cost Type&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Agreement (contract, grant, reimbursement, etc.)&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;,&quot;State Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;State Government R&amp;D-related Facilities&quot;,&quot;Type of R&amp;D (basic, applied, experimental development)&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2025&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:5918,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 26-302&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The tables in this report present FY 2024 and time series data for the 50 states, the District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico. Data are available on total R&amp;D expenditures; intramural and extramural performers; source of funds; and R&amp;D by governmental function by state and department or agency, employee count, and full-time equivalent counts. These data were collected by the Survey of State Government Research and Development, conducted by the Census Bureau for the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the U.S. National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;12/11/2025&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf26302&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf26302&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2025.12.11&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of State Government Research and Development&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of State Government Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of State Government Research and Development: FY 2024&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Federal Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Capital Expenditures&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Cost Type&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Employment&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Intensity&quot;,&quot;State Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;State Government R&amp;D-related Facilities&quot;,&quot;Type of R&amp;D (basic, applied, experimental development)&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2025&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:5998,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 26-301&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;These data tables are from the FY 2024 Federal Facilities Research and Development (FFRD) Survey. These tables present data on research and experimental development (R&amp;D) expenditures for all federally owned and operated facilities in the United States. This new survey is the unique source of federal performer-reported data on R&amp;D expenditures and is conducted by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the U.S. National Science Foundation. &quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;12/09/2025&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf26301&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf26301&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2025.12.09&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Federal Facilities Research and Development (FFRD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Facilities Research and Development (FFRD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Facilities Research and Development: Fiscal Year 2024&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Federal Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Federally Funded Research and Development Centers (FFRDC) R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Institutions&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Cost Type&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Employment&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;,&quot;State Government R&amp;D-related Facilities&quot;,&quot;Type of R&amp;D (basic, applied, experimental development)&quot;,&quot;Work Activity&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2025&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:6058,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 26-300&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;In FY 2024, $49.1 billion of research and experimental development (R&amp;D) was performed within federally owned and operated facilities. Data presented in this InfoBrief are from the Federal Facilities Research and Development Survey sponsored by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the U.S. National Science Foundation. These data include annual expenditures for R&amp;D performed within federal facilities, which includes work performed by federal employees, military personnel, and onsite contractors. This data collection represents the second cycle of this new survey designed to collect R&amp;D expenditure and personnel data at the federal facility level. &quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;12/09/2025&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf26300&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf26300&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2025.12.09&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Federal Facilities Research and Development (FFRD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Facilities Research and Development (FFRD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;R&amp;D Performance within Federal Facilities Totaled $49.1 Billion in FY 2024&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Federal Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Federally Funded Research and Development Centers (FFRDC) R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Cost Type&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Employment&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;Type of R&amp;D (basic, applied, experimental development)&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2025&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:5818,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 25-354&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The tables in this report present data from the Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey. The BERD Survey is the primary source of information on domestic and global research and development (R&amp;D) expenditures and the R&amp;D workforce for companies with 10 or more employees operating in the 50 U.S. states and the District of Columbia. The BERD Survey is cosponsored by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics and the Census Bureau. These data are used by government agencies, corporations, research organizations, and academics to assess trends in the performance and funding of R&amp;D.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/29/2025&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf25354&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf25354&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2025.09.29&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development: 2023&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;By Country&quot;,&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Clinical Trials and Medical Projects&quot;,&quot;Employment Status&quot;,&quot;Federal Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Degrees&quot;,&quot;Knowledge and Technology Intensive (KTI) Industries&quot;,&quot;Occupation&quot;,&quot;Product &amp; Business Process Innovation&quot;,&quot;Production and Value Added&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Capital Expenditures&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Cost Type&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Employment&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Intensity&quot;,&quot;Sex&quot;,&quot;Type of R&amp;D (basic, applied, experimental development)&quot;,&quot;Work Activity&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2025&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:5778,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 25-353&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Businesses continued to increase their research and development (R&amp;D) performance in 2023, spending $722 billion on R&amp;D in the United States, a 4.4% increase from 2022. Funding from the companies’ own sources accounted for $635 billion of this spending in 2023, a 4.5% increase from 2022. Funding from other sources accounted for $86 billion, a 3.4% increase from 2022. Data for this InfoBrief are from the 2023 Business Enterprise Research and Development Survey, developed and cosponsored by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the U.S. National Science Foundation and by the Census Bureau, which collected and tabulated data for the survey.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/29/2025&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf25353&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf25353&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2025.09.29&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business R&amp;D Performance in the United States Increases to $722 Billion in 2023&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Knowledge and Technology Intensive (KTI) Industries&quot;,&quot;Occupation&quot;,&quot;Production and Value Added&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Capital Expenditures&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Cost Type&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Employment&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Intensity&quot;,&quot;Sex&quot;,&quot;Technology Transfer Activities&quot;,&quot;Type of R&amp;D (basic, applied, experimental development)&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2025&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:5738,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 25-350&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;These tables present the results of the Nonprofit Research Activities FY 2023 module of the 2024 Annual Business Survey, funded by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the U.S. National Science Foundation. It is the fourth collection of nonprofit R&amp;D data by the Annual Business Survey and the continuation of an annual data series for this population. These data are the primary source of information about tax-exempt nonprofit organizations’ R&amp;D performance and funding in the United States.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/24/2025&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf25350&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf25350&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2025.09.24&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Nonprofit Research Activities (NPRA) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Nonprofit Research Activities (NPRA) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Nonprofit Research Activities: FY 2023&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Employment Status&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Knowledge and Technology Intensive (KTI) Industries&quot;,&quot;Nonprofit R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Occupation&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Capital Expenditures&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Cost Type&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Employment&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Agreement (contract, grant, reimbursement, etc.)&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;,&quot;Type of R&amp;D (basic, applied, experimental development)&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2025&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:5798,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 25-355&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;U.S. nonprofit organizations spent an estimated $30.8 billion on research and experimental development (R&amp;D) performed within their organizations in FY 2023, an increase of 10% in current dollars from FY 2022. The largest funding source for nonprofit R&amp;D in FY 2023 was the federal government, contributing $12.4 billion, or 40%, of the total. Data are from the Nonprofit Research Activities (NPRA) FY 2023 module of the 2024 Annual Business Survey. The NPRA Survey was funded by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics (NCSES) within the U.S. National Science Foundation, and data collection was conducted by the Census Bureau.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/24/2025&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf25355&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf25355&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2025.09.24&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Nonprofit Research Activities (NPRA) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Nonprofit Research Activities (NPRA) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;U.S. Nonprofits Spent $31 Billion on R&amp;D Activities in FY 2023&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Nonprofit R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Occupation&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Capital Expenditures&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Cost Type&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Employment&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;,&quot;Type of R&amp;D (basic, applied, experimental development)&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2025&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering Workforce&quot;,&quot;STEM Education&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:5177,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 25-331&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The number of college graduates living in the United States reached 71.7 million in 2023, of which approximately 70.9 million had ever held a job. The number of employed college graduates increased by 4.3 million (8.3%) between 2021 and 2023, and the number of college graduates not working decreased by 1.3 million (7.9%). This growth in employment and decline in nonemployment coincided with the end of the COVID-19 pandemic and underlies a series of developments in the college-graduate labor market. This InfoBrief presents a comparison of selected labor market outcomes between the 2021 and 2023 cycles of the National Survey of College Graduates, sponsored by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the U.S. National Science Foundation. &quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/23/2025&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf25331&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf25331&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2025.09.23&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;National Survey of College Graduates (NSCG)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Survey of College Graduates (NSCG)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;The College-Graduate Workforce in the Transition to a Post-Pandemic Labor Market: Trends in Employment, Professional Engagement, and Work Arrangements&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;COVID-19 Pandemic Impacts&quot;,&quot;Employment Sector&quot;,&quot;Employment Status&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Degrees&quot;,&quot;Income and Salary&quot;,&quot;Job Satisfaction&quot;,&quot;Minorities in Higher Education&quot;,&quot;Occupation&quot;,&quot;Race and Ethnicity&quot;,&quot;Work Activity&quot;,&quot;Work-related Training&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2025&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBytes&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:5278,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 25-337&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Latest visualizations from the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics using data from the National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources (annual series) on federally funded research and development.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/16/2025&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf25337&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf25337&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2025.09.16&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Funding for Research Increased from 2010 to 2022, yet the Federal Government’s Share of Total Research Funding Declined&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;R&amp;D Cost Type&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Intensity&quot;,&quot;Type of R&amp;D (basic, applied, experimental development)&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2025&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering Workforce&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:5758,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 25-352&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;In 2022, there were 36 million individuals working in a STEM occupation; 51% had a sub-baccalaureate credential as their highest level of education attainment and 49% had a bachelor’s degree or higher. Additionally, 22 million STEM workers (15% of all workers) held a non-degree work credential. These work-related and sub-baccalaureate credentials can serve as an important catalyst in career entry or advancement. Data are from the 2022 National Training, Education, and Workforce Survey (NTEWS) Pilot, which has a special focus on the skilled technical workforce (workers highly skilled in STEM fields who do not possess a bachelor’s degree or higher), conducted by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the U.S. National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/10/2025&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf25352&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf25352&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2025.09.10&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;National Training, Education, and Workforce Survey (NTEWS)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Training, Education, and Workforce Survey (NTEWS)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;New Pilot Data on the Prevalence of Work-Related Credentials among STEM Workers from the National Training, Education, and Workforce Survey&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Certifications and Licenses&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Degrees&quot;,&quot;Occupation&quot;,&quot;Sex&quot;,&quot;Skilled Technical Workforce&quot;,&quot;Work-related Training&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2025&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;STEM Education&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:5658,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 25-349&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;These tables release data from the 2024 Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED), an annual census of research doctorate recipients from U.S. universities. The tables include long-term trend data on doctorate recipients by field; educational pathways to the doctorate and sources of financial support; postgraduation employment plans; and representation of different demographic groups, and temporary visa holders among doctorate recipients. Data are also available in an interactive data tool (https://ncsesdata.nsf.gov/builder/sed) and the Restricted Data Analysis System (https://ncsesdata.nsf.gov/rdas/). The SED is sponsored by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the U.S. National Science Foundation and by three other federal agencies: the National Institutes of Health, Department of Education, and National Endowment for the Humanities.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;08/28/2025&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf25349&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf25349&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2025.08.28&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities: 2024 Data Tables&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;By Country&quot;,&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Citizenship&quot;,&quot;Disability Status&quot;,&quot;Employment Sector&quot;,&quot;Employment Status&quot;,&quot;Family Characteristics&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Financial Support&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Degrees&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Enrollment&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Income and Salary&quot;,&quot;Institutions&quot;,&quot;Minorities in Higher Education&quot;,&quot;Occupation&quot;,&quot;Postdoctorates&quot;,&quot;Postgraduation Plans&quot;,&quot;Race and Ethnicity&quot;,&quot;Sex&quot;,&quot;Skilled Technical Workforce&quot;,&quot;Student Loan Debt&quot;,&quot;Work Activity&quot;,&quot;Work-related Training&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2025&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:5618,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 25-348&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;These data tables provide data on research and development (R&amp;D) expenditures for federally funded research and development centers (FFRDCs). Expenditures data are displayed for individually named FFRDCs, by source of funding and type of R&amp;D. Data are from the FY 2024 FFRDC Research and Development Survey, conducted by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the U.S. National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;08/26/2025&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf25348&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf25348&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2025.08.26&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Expenditures: FY 2024&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Federal Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Federally Funded Research and Development Centers (FFRDC) R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Institutions&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Cost Type&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;,&quot;Type of R&amp;D (basic, applied, experimental development)&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2025&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:5698,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 25-351&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Federally funded research and development centers (FFRDCs) in the United States enable federal agencies to meet their research and development (R&amp;D) needs through government-owned, contractor-operated research centers. These centers provide capabilities that cannot be met as effectively through other resources. The nation’s 42 FFRDCs spent $31.7 billion on R&amp;D in FY 2024, an increase of $2.4 billion from FY 2023. These and the other statistics in this report come from the FY 2024 FFRDC Research and Development Survey, conducted by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the U.S. National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;08/26/2025&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf25351&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf25351&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2025.08.26&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;R&amp;D Spending at Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers Surpassed $31 Billion in FY 2024&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Federal Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Federally Funded Research and Development Centers (FFRDC) R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Institutions&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Cost Type&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2025&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering Indicators&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering Indicators&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:5578,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 25-347&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The number and share of Open-access (OA) articles has grown since 2003 from an infrequent type of peer-reviewed publication to almost half of global publications in 2022. OA publications are peer-reviewed research articles that are accessible to any reader without a journal subscription or payment. This InfoBrief by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the U.S. National Science Foundation explores global patterns of OA publications from 2003 through 2022 using Scopus, a bibliometric database of publication metadata. OA publication patterns vary among countries and can reflect the prominence of OA as a mode of publication by authors at institutions within those countries and the international impact of OA publications.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;08/06/2025&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf25347&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf25347&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2025.08.06&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Open-Access Publishing in a Global Context&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;By Country&quot;,&quot;Income and Salary&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Publications &amp; Articles&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2025&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reports&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering Indicators&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering Indicators&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:5538,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSB-2025-7&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The funding and performance of research and experimental development (R&amp;D) activities and the publication of peer-reviewed research, an output of R&amp;D activity, serve as important indicators of the competitiveness of U.S. science and engineering in a global context. According to internationally comparable data, the United States had the largest national R&amp;D investment in 2022—above China, Japan, and Germany—and this investment was driven primarily by the business sector. The United States also remains a leader in peer-reviewed research publications in terms of output, scientific impact, and international collaborations.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;07/23/2025&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsb20257&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsb20257&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2025.07.23&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Discovery: R&amp;D Activity and Research Publications&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2025&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;STEM Education&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:5558,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 25-346&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;In preparation for the 2024 Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS), a comprehensive eligibility review was conducted of institutions in the 2023 GSS with six or fewer organizational units. The eligibility review was focused on identifying whether institutions offered at least one research-based master’s or doctoral program in science, engineering, or health. This review resulted in 53 of 687 GSS institutions in the 2023 survey being declared ineligible for inclusion in the 2024 GSS data collection, or newly ineligible. When these newly ineligible institutions were excluded from the 2023 data, analysis showed that 0.8% fewer graduate students and 1.3% fewer master’s students would have been reported in the 2023 GSS. In 2023, these newly ineligible institutions reported 6,909 of the 818,095 graduate students in the GSS. However, the impact would not have been distributed evenly by institution type or field of study. The GSS is sponsored by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the U.S. National Science Foundation and by the National Institutes of Health. &quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;07/16/2025&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf25346&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf25346&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2025.07.16&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Impact of the 2024 GSS Institutional Eligibility Review on Counts of GSS Master’s Students&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Attitudes About Science&quot;,&quot;Certifications and Licenses&quot;,&quot;Citizenship&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Degrees&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Enrollment&quot;,&quot;Institutions&quot;,&quot;Minorities in Higher Education&quot;,&quot;Non-faculty Research Personnel&quot;,&quot;Postdoctorates&quot;,&quot;Race and Ethnicity&quot;,&quot;Sex&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2025&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Innovation and Global Competitiveness&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:5418,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 25-343&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Of the 4.9 million for-profit U.S. businesses with at least one employee, 9.0% introduced a product innovation during 2018–20. Rates vary by demographic characteristics of business owners, such as sex, minority status, and place of birth. These differences may be explained in part by varying rates of business ownership by industry based on owner demographics. This InfoBrief explores relationships between the incidence of product innovation, business owner demographics, and industry. Data are from the Annual Business Survey, developed and cosponsored by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation and by the Census Bureau.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;07/15/2025&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf25343&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf25343&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2025.07.15&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Annual Business Survey (ABS)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Annual Business Survey (ABS)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Relationship Between Product Innovation and Business Owner Demographics Varies by Industry&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Citizenship&quot;,&quot;Knowledge and Technology Intensive (KTI) Industries&quot;,&quot;Minorities in Higher Education&quot;,&quot;Occupation&quot;,&quot;Product &amp; Business Process Innovation&quot;,&quot;Race and Ethnicity&quot;,&quot;Sex&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2025&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Innovation and Global Competitiveness&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:5398,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 25-342&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;U.S. businesses with younger owners reported product innovation at higher rates than did businesses with older owners in 2020. The estimated share of businesses that introduced a product innovation during 2018–20 was 15.0% for businesses majority-owned by persons under 25 years of age, compared with 6.6% for businesses majority-owned by persons 65 years or older. Data are from the Annual Business Survey, which is cosponsored by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation and by the Census Bureau.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;07/15/2025&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf25342&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf25342&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2025.07.15&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Annual Business Survey (ABS)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Annual Business Survey (ABS)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Relationships Between Business Owner Age, Education, and Experience and Product Innovation&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Employment Sector&quot;,&quot;Family Characteristics&quot;,&quot;Federally Funded Research and Development Centers (FFRDC) R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Degrees&quot;,&quot;Knowledge and Technology Intensive (KTI) Industries&quot;,&quot;Occupation&quot;,&quot;Product &amp; Business Process Innovation&quot;,&quot;Skilled Technical Workforce&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2025&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoChart&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:5518,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 25-345&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Since the 1950s, over half of R&amp;D expenditures at U.S. colleges and universities have been funded by the federal government, with the share of institution funding increasing to a fourth of the total in recent years. Data are from the Higher Education Research and Development Survey, conducted by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the U.S. National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;07/10/2025&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf25345&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf25345&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2025.07.10&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Since the 1950s, Over Half of R&amp;D Expenditures at U.S. Colleges and Universities Have Been Funded by the Federal Government&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Nonprofit R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Cost Type&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2025&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoChart&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:5180,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 25-335&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The ratio of U.S. research and development (R&amp;D) to gross domestic product (GDP) reached 3.43% in 2022. An increase in the R&amp;D-to-GDP ratio from 2010 to 2022 can primarily be attributed to experimental development, which comprised 67% of total U.S. R&amp;D in 2022. Basic research comprised 15% of total U.S. R&amp;D in 2022, while applied research comprised 18%. Data are from the National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources series from the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the U.S. National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;07/08/2025&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf25335&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf25335&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2025.07.08&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;U.S. R&amp;D-to-GDP Ratio Reached 3.43% in 2022, Driven by Experimental Development&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;R&amp;D Intensity&quot;,&quot;Type of R&amp;D (basic, applied, experimental development)&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2025&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:5478,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 25-340&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;From 2001 to 2021, expenditures for research and development (R&amp;D) performed by for-profit businesses in the United States doubled. Over half of the growth in U.S. business R&amp;D over this period can be attributed to 2 states: California and Washington. In 2001, the 10 largest states in terms of business R&amp;D accounted for 70% of the national estimate that could be attributed to a state, and the 3 largest states accounted for 37%. In 2021, the 10 largest states accounted for 75% of the national estimate, and the 3 largest states accounted for 50%. Data are from the Business Enterprise Research and Development Survey, cosponsored by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the U.S. National Science Foundation and the Census Bureau.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;07/01/2025&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf25340&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf25340&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2025.07.01&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Geographic Distribution of Business R&amp;D Performance in 2021&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Intensity&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2025&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoChart&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:5458,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 25-344&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Between FY 1956 and FY 2024, the share of federal obligations for research, including both basic and applied research, increased from 26% to 49% of the federal government’s total of research and development (R&amp;D) and R&amp;D plant, whereas the share for development decreased from 65% to 48%. During the same period, the share for R&amp;D plant decreased from 9% to 3%. Data are from the Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development, conducted by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the U.S. National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;06/18/2025&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf25344&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf25344&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2025.06.18&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Since the 1950s, Federal R&amp;D Funding Has Shifted from Development to Research&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Federal Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Capital Expenditures&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Cost Type&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;Type of R&amp;D (basic, applied, experimental development)&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2025&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:5319,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 25-339&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;These tables present the results of the FY 2023 Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions (now the Federal Science and Engineering Support module within the Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development). Data include science and engineering support by type of activity, as well as rankings by state, agency, and individual institution. This survey is the only source of comprehensive data on federal science and engineering funding to individual academic and nonprofit institutions&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;06/02/2025&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf25339&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf25339&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2025.06.02&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions: Fiscal Year 2023&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Federal Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Federally Funded Research and Development Centers (FFRDC) R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Financial Support&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Institutions&quot;,&quot;Minorities in Higher Education&quot;,&quot;Nonprofit R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Race and Ethnicity&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Capital Expenditures&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Agreement (contract, grant, reimbursement, etc.)&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;,&quot;Type of R&amp;D (basic, applied, experimental development)&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2025&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:5378,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 25-341&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;In FY 2023, federal agency obligations to universities and colleges for science and engineering (S&amp;E) activities increased 9.7% from FY 2022 to a historical high of nearly $49 billion. S&amp;E activities include research and experimental development (R&amp;D); R&amp;D plant; facilities and equipment for instruction in S&amp;E; S&amp;E fellowships, traineeships, and training grants; and other general support for S&amp;E. Estimates are from the FY 2023 Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support module within the Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development, conducted by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the U.S. National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;06/02/2025&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf25341&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf25341&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2025.06.02&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;In FY 2023, Federal Science and Engineering Support for Higher Education Totaled $49 Billion; Federal R&amp;D to Nonprofits Totaled $12 Billion&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Federal Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Financial Support&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Institutions&quot;,&quot;Nonprofit R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Capital Expenditures&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Cost Type&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Agreement (contract, grant, reimbursement, etc.)&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;,&quot;Type of R&amp;D (basic, applied, experimental development)&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2025&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoChart&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:5178,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 25-333&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;In 2022, U.S. research expenditures reached $247 billion (using constant 2017 dollars). The business sector continued to be the main driver of U.S. research expenditure funding, increasing from $65 billion (38% of total) in 2010 to $123 billion (50% of total) in 2022. Federal government research funding was $85 billion in constant 2017 dollars, though its share or total funding decreased from 45% in 2010 to 34% in 2022. Other nonfederal sources contributed $39 billion in 2022. Data are from the National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources series from the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the U.S. National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;05/29/2025&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf25333&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf25333&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2025.05.29&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business Sector Funded Half of U.S. Research Performance in 2022&quot;,&quot