 Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;01/16/2024&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf24315&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf24315&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2024.01.16&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Funding to University Affiliated Research Centers Totaled $1.5 Billion in FY 2021&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Federal Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Federally Funded Research and Development Centers (FFRDC) R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Institutions&quot;,&quot;Nonprofit R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2024&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoChart&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:4156,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 24-313&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;FY 2021 total federal obligations and outlays for federal research and development (R&amp;D) plant totaled $4.3 and $3.8 billion, respectively, with most funding coming from the Department of Energy. Data are from the Survey of Federal Funds for R&amp;D, conducted by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;01/16/2024&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf24313&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf24313&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2024.01.16&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;The Department of Energy Accounted for over 60% of All Federal Funding for R&amp;D Plant in FY 2021&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Federal Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Institutions&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Capital Expenditures&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2024&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:4132,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 24-312&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics (NCSES), within the National Science Foundation (NSF) has reclassified federally funded research and development centers (FFRDCs) in its Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development as federal intramural performers of R&amp;D with the release of Volume 71 (FYs 2021-22). FFRDCs had been previously classified as extramural performers. This report provides background and context for this reclassification and discusses some of the effects of the reclassification that may be of note to data users. It also discusses changes to the survey as part of a redesign.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;01/04/2024&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf24312&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf24312&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2024.01.04&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Reclassification of Federally Funded Research and Development Centers as Federal Intramural Performers of R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;COVID-19 Pandemic Impacts&quot;,&quot;Federal Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Federally Funded Research and Development Centers (FFRDC) R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Institutions&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Agreement (contract, grant, reimbursement, etc.)&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;,&quot;Type of R&amp;D (basic, applied, experimental development)&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2024&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:4112,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 24-309&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This set of detailed tables present data on the research and development (R&amp;D) outlays and obligations of federal agencies. Outlays are provided by agency for total R&amp;D and total R&amp;D plant. Obligations are provided for R&amp;D by agency for type of work (basic research, applied research, experimental development), field of R&amp;D (for basic research, applied research, and experimental development), type of performer, and geographic area. Obligations are provided for R&amp;D plant by agency for type of performer and geographic area. These data, collected by the Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development from the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics, are the primary source of information about federal funding for R&amp;D in the United States.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;12/21/2023&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf24309&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf24309&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2023.12.21&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Funds for Research and Development: Fiscal Years 2021–22&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;By Country&quot;,&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Disability Status&quot;,&quot;Federal Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Federally Funded Research and Development Centers (FFRDC) R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Institutions&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Capital Expenditures&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Cost Type&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Agreement (contract, grant, reimbursement, etc.)&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;,&quot;Type of R&amp;D (basic, applied, experimental development)&quot;,&quot;Work Activity&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2023&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Interactive&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering State Profiles&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering State Profiles&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:5751,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Updated data for Science and Engineering State Profiles are now available. This product provides state-level rankings and totals of science and engineering data for the 50 states, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico. The data tool allows users to compare state profiles for up to seven states or download the entire data set. Science and Engineering State Profiles is produced by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics of the National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;12/18/2023&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/states/download/state-profiles-2023-12.xlsx&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf00000&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2023.12.18&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering State Profiles: Fall 2023 Update&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2023&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Innovation and Global Competitiveness&quot;,&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reports&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering Indicators&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering Indicators&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3347,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSB-2023-33&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Academic publications in the form of peer-reviewed journal articles and conference proceedings are an important medium where scientific discoveries are made and shared. This report presents data that reflect the expanding volume of research activity, variations in scientific impact, and a growing research ecosystem of international and domestic collaborations. Publication output grew 5% over the past 4 years, led by China and other middle-income countries. Also, over the last 4 years, publications classified as open access increased by over 8%, while articles in closed-access journals rose by almost 4%.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;12/11/2023&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsb202333&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsb202333&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2023.12.11&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Publications Output: U.S. Trends and International Comparisons&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2023&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;STEM Education&quot;,&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:3847,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 23-349&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;New research data suggest that 51% of science and engineering (S&amp;E) doctorate recipients who graduated from U.S. research-intensive institutions between academic years 2014 and 2017 received research funding during their graduate studies. Among those S&amp;E doctorate recipients that linked to the administrative records of research funding, 42% received research funding solely from federal funders. Data are from a new resource, SED-UMETRICS linked data. The Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED) is an annual census of research doctorate recipients conducted by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation. The SED was linked to UMETRICS (Measuring the Impacts of Research on Innovation, Competitiveness, and Science) data developed from administrative records from member universities of the Institute for Research on Innovation and Science. &quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;12/04/2023&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf23349&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf23349&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2023.12.04&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Research Funding for U.S. Doctorate Recipients at Research-Intensive Institutions&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Federal Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Financial Support&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Degrees&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Enrollment&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Institutions&quot;,&quot;Minorities in Higher Education&quot;,&quot;Postgraduation Plans&quot;,&quot;Race and Ethnicity&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Agreement (contract, grant, reimbursement, etc.)&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Intensity&quot;,&quot;Sex&quot;,&quot;Student Loan Debt&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2023&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;STEM Education&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Working Paper&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:3889,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NCSES 23-215&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report documents the methodology and technical approach and summarizes results of linking Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED) data collected by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics (NCSES) within the National Science Foundation to a new source of research data (UMETRICS [Measuring the Impacts of Research on Innovation, Competitiveness, and Science]) processed by the Institute for Research on Innovation and Science (IRIS).\n\nThis data linkage research shows it is feasible to combine the two data sources, even though differences exist in coverage of populations and time periods and they have different identifiers. The implementation approach can inform other similar efforts and can inform the implementation of the Foundations for Evidence-Based Policymaking Act of 2018. The data linkage approach, while initially time consuming, is replicable and can scale to additional years and data sets. Measuring linkage quality when combining administrative and survey data requires defining both the relevant population to be matched and the match rate. Those definitions will differ depending on the goal of the linkage. The value of the linkage effort is considerable in terms of (1) expanding understanding of survey responses to key questions of interest, notably using grant funding to enrich understanding of reported sources of graduate school financial support and (2) adding new measures reflecting the dynamics of research experiences of doctorate recipients. There is substantial potential to engage researchers and the academic community to inform the linkage quality results as well as to expand and enhance the value of the data linkage project documented in this report.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;12/04/2023&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;ncses23215&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;ncses23215&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2023.12.04&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;SED-UMETRICS Linkage Technical Report&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Certifications and Licenses&quot;,&quot;Citizenship&quot;,&quot;Family Characteristics&quot;,&quot;Federal Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Financial Support&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Degrees&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Enrollment&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Institutions&quot;,&quot;Minorities in Higher Education&quot;,&quot;Occupation&quot;,&quot;Postdoctorates&quot;,&quot;Postgraduation Plans&quot;,&quot;Race and Ethnicity&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Cost Type&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Agreement (contract, grant, reimbursement, etc.)&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Intensity&quot;,&quot;Sex&quot;,&quot;Work-related Training&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2023&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:4072,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 24-308&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This publication releases data tables for the FY 2022 Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey. The tables present data on research and development expenditures at higher education institutions across all academic disciplines and include R&amp;D expenditures by institution, R&amp;D field, geographic area, source of funds, type of R&amp;D (basic research, applied research, and experimental development), cost categories (salaries, software, equipment, and indirect costs), and trends over time. The HERD Survey is conducted by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;11/30/2023&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf24308&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf24308&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2023.11.30&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Research and Development: Fiscal Year 2022&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Clinical Trials and Medical Projects&quot;,&quot;Federal Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Degrees&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Enrollment&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Institutions&quot;,&quot;Minorities in Higher Education&quot;,&quot;Non-faculty Research Personnel&quot;,&quot;Nonprofit R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Race and Ethnicity&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Capital Expenditures&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Cost Type&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Employment&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Agreement (contract, grant, reimbursement, etc.)&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;,&quot;Skilled Technical Workforce&quot;,&quot;State Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Type of R&amp;D (basic, applied, experimental development)&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2023&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:4073,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 24-307&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Academic institutions spent $97.8 billion on R&amp;D in FY 2022, an increase of $8.0 billion in current dollars from FY 2021. Federal sources funded almost $54 billion (55%) of the total and accounted for $4.9 billion of the year-over-year increase. Academic R&amp;D expenditures in all science fields combined increased by $5.9 billion (8.4%) in FY 2022. The data in this report are from the Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey, sponsored by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics (NCSES) within the National Science Foundation. &quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;11/30/2023&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf24307&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf24307&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2023.11.30&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;R&amp;D Expenditures at U.S. Universities Increased by $8 Billion in FY 2022&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;By Country&quot;,&quot;Clinical Trials and Medical Projects&quot;,&quot;Federal Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Institutions&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Capital Expenditures&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Cost Type&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2023&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:4011,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 24-305&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;State government agencies’ expenditures for research and development increased by 5.2% from FY 2021 to $2.6 billion in FY 2022. Although state government expenditures for R&amp;D are relatively small within the national portfolio of R&amp;D performance, state agencies themselves performed $697 million in R&amp;D while also financing $1.953 billion in extramural R&amp;D in FY 2022 to businesses, higher education, nonprofit institutions, and others. Data are from the Survey of State Government Research and Development, sponsored by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;11/27/2023&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf24305&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf24305&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2023.11.27&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of State Government Research and Development&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of State Government Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;State Government Agencies’ Expenditures for R&amp;D Totaled $2.6 Billion in FY 2022, an Increase of 5% from FY 2021&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Capital Expenditures&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Cost Type&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;,&quot;State Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2023&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:4051,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 24-306&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The 23 data tables in this report present FY 2022 and time series data for the 50 states, the District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico. Data are available on total R&amp;D expenditures; intramural and extramural performers; source of funds; and R&amp;D by governmental function by state and department or agency, employee count, and full-time equivalent counts. These data were collected by the Survey of State Government Research and Development, conducted by the Census Bureau for the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;11/27/2023&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf24306&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf24306&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2023.11.27&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of State Government Research and Development&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of State Government Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of State Government Research and Development: FY 2022&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Clinical Trials and Medical Projects&quot;,&quot;Federal Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Capital Expenditures&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Cost Type&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Employment&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;,&quot;State Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;State Government R&amp;D-related Facilities&quot;,&quot;Type of R&amp;D (basic, applied, experimental development)&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2023&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;STEM Education&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reports&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering Indicators&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering Indicators&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3111,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSB-2023-32&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Higher education in science and engineering (S&amp;E) develops the talent that sustains the U.S. S&amp;E enterprise. This report describes U.S. undergraduate and graduate education in S&amp;E and S&amp;E-related fields with international comparisons. The number of S&amp;E certificates and degrees awarded is increasing, as is the share of all degrees awarded in S&amp;E fields. Representation of women varies by S&amp;E field, and some racial and ethnic groups remain underrepresented in S&amp;E degree attainment. The United States is among the top countries in awarding S&amp;E degrees and S&amp;E graduate enrollment of international students has increased rapidly since 2020.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;11/16/2023&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsb202332&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsb202332&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2023.11.16&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education in Science and Engineering&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2023&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Innovation and Global Competitiveness&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3971,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 24-303&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The detailed tables in this report present data from the 2022 Annual Business Survey (ABS) (data year 2021). The ABS is the primary source of data on research and development of for-profit, nonfarm businesses with one to nine employees operating in the 50 U.S. states and the District of Columbia. The ABS also collects data on innovation, technology, intellectual property, research activities at nonprofit organizations, financing, management practice, company information, and business owner characteristics of business of all sizes. The 2022 ABS is the fifth year of the ABS. The survey is conducted annually by the Census Bureau for the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;10/30/2023&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf24303&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf2430