1 billion (75%) was performed by the microbusinesses themselves. Data are from the Annual Business Survey, developed and cosponsored by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation and by the Census Bureau.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;10/30/2023&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf24302&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf24302&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2023.10.30&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Annual Business Survey (ABS)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Annual Business Survey (ABS)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Microbusinesses Performed $6.1 Billion of R&amp;D in the United States in 2021&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Employment Sector&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Knowledge and Technology Intensive (KTI) Industries&quot;,&quot;Occupation&quot;,&quot;Patenting and Trademark Activities&quot;,&quot;Product &amp; Business Process Innovation&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Cost Type&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Employment&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;,&quot;Sex&quot;,&quot;Type of R&amp;D (basic, applied, experimental development)&quot;,&quot;Work Activity&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2023&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;STEM Education&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reports&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering Indicators&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering Indicators&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3128,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSB-2023-31&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Elementary and secondary STEM education is the foundation for student entry into postsecondary STEM majors and occupations. This report presents national trends in K–12 student achievement in STEM and compares U.S. STEM achievement with that of other nations. The report shows that student achievement in mathematics, which had already plateaued for the past decade, has now regressed approximately 20 years during the COVID-19 pandemic. A pre-pandemic international assessment of mathematics and science in 2019 shows that eighth graders in the U.S ranked about in the middle of education systems in countries with advanced economies. &quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;10/26/2023&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsb202331&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsb202331&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2023.10.26&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Elementary and Secondary STEM Education&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2023&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:3933,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 24-304&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;These tables present the results of the Nonprofit Research Activities FY 2021 module of the 2022 Annual Business Survey, conducted by the Census Bureau for the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation. It is the second collection of nonprofit R&amp;D data by the Annual Business Survey and the continuation of an annual data series for this population. These data are the primary source of information about 501(c) nonprofit organizations’ R&amp;D performance and funding in the United States.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;10/23/2023&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf24304&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf24304&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2023.10.23&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Nonprofit Research Activities (NPRA) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Nonprofit Research Activities (NPRA) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Nonprofit Research Activities: FY 2021&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;COVID-19 Pandemic Impacts&quot;,&quot;Employment Status&quot;,&quot;Federally Funded Research and Development Centers (FFRDC) R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Institutions&quot;,&quot;Knowledge and Technology Intensive (KTI) Industries&quot;,&quot;Nonprofit R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Occupation&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Cost Type&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Employment&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Agreement (contract, grant, reimbursement, etc.)&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;,&quot;Type of R&amp;D (basic, applied, experimental development)&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2023&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:3911,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 24-301&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;U.S. nonprofits spent an estimated $27.2 billion on research and experimental development (R&amp;D) performed within their organizations in FY 2021. The largest funding source was the federal government, contributing $11.6 billion, or 43%, of the total. Data in this InfoBrief are from the Nonprofit Research Activities (NPRA) FY 2021 module of the 2022 Annual Business Survey, conducted by the Census Bureau for the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics (NCSES) within the National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;10/23/2023&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf24301&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf24301&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2023.10.23&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Nonprofit Research Activities (NPRA) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Nonprofit Research Activities (NPRA) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;U.S. Nonprofits&#39; R&amp;D Performance Totaled $27 Billion in FY 2021&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Nonprofit R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Occupation&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Cost Type&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Employment&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;,&quot;Type of R&amp;D (basic, applied, experimental development)&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2023&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reports&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering Indicators&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering Indicators&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3109,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSB-2023-26&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Academic institutions in the United States contribute considerably to U.S. basic research. The federal government funds over half of academic research and development (R&amp;D), but its share of support has declined while the share funded by institutions has grown. Academic R&amp;D spending remains concentrated in very high research activity institutions, which train most U.S. science and engineering (S&amp;E) doctoral students and employ most S&amp;E postdoctoral researchers (postdocs). This report presents data on funding by source and S&amp;E field for R&amp;D activities and equipment and provides information on research space. The report also benchmarks the United States against other nations and discusses trends on the demographics of the academic workforce and on investments in the training of students and postdocs.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;10/05/2023&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsb202326&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsb202326&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2023.10.05&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Academic Research and Development&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2023&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3909,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 23-351&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The tables in this report present data from the Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey. The BERD Survey is the primary source of information on domestic and global research and development (R&amp;D) expenditures and the R&amp;D workforce for companies with 10 or more employees operating in the 50 U.S. states and the District of Columbia. The survey is conducted annually by the Census Bureau in accordance with an interagency agreement with the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation. These data are used by government agencies, corporations, research organizations, and academics to assess trends in the performance and funding of R&amp;D.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/29/2023&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf23351&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf23351&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2023.09.29&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development: 2021&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;By Country&quot;,&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Citizenship&quot;,&quot;Employment Status&quot;,&quot;Federal Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Degrees&quot;,&quot;Institutions&quot;,&quot;Knowledge and Technology Intensive (KTI) Industries&quot;,&quot;Occupation&quot;,&quot;Product &amp; Business Process Innovation&quot;,&quot;Production and Value Added&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Capital Expenditures&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Cost Type&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Employment&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Agreement (contract, grant, reimbursement, etc.)&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Intensity&quot;,&quot;Sex&quot;,&quot;Skilled Technical Workforce&quot;,&quot;Type of R&amp;D (basic, applied, experimental development)&quot;,&quot;Work Activity&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2023&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3888,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 23-350&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Businesses continued to increase their research and development performance in 2021, spending $602 billion on R&amp;D in the United States, a 12.1% increase from 2020. Funding from the companies’ own sources accounted for $528 billion of this spending in 2021, a 13.2% increase from 2020. Funding from other sources accounted for $75 billion, a 4.5% increase from 2020. Data for this InfoBrief are from the 2021 Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey, developed and cosponsored by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation and by the Census Bureau, which collected and tabulated data for the survey.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/28/2023&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf23350&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf23350&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2023.09.28&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business R&amp;D Performance in the United States Tops $600 Billion in 2021&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Institutions&quot;,&quot;Occupation&quot;,&quot;Production and Value Added&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Capital Expenditures&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Cost Type&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Employment&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Intensity&quot;,&quot;Sex&quot;,&quot;Type of R&amp;D (basic, applied, experimental development)&quot;,&quot;Work Activity&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2023&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;STEM Education&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reports&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3910,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 24-300&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report summarizes trends in U.S. doctoral education by using data from the 2022 Survey of Earned Doctorates, an annual census of research doctorate recipients from U.S. universities. Important trends in this population are highlighted in this report, including the representation of women, minorities, and temporary visa holders; fields that are attracting graduate students; time to complete doctoral degree; employment opportunities after graduation; and educational pathways to the doctorate. A special section focuses on the impact of the COVID-19 pandemic on doctorate recipients’ graduate experiences and postgraduation plans. Detailed data tables and additional resources are also available.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/28/2023&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf24300&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf24300&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2023.09.28&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities: 2022&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;By Country&quot;,&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Citizenship&quot;,&quot;COVID-19 Pandemic Impacts&quot;,&quot;Disability Status&quot;,&quot;Employment Sector&quot;,&quot;Employment Status&quot;,&quot;Family Characteristics&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Financial Support&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Degrees&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Income and Salary&quot;,&quot;Institutions&quot;,&quot;Minorities in Higher Education&quot;,&quot;Occupation&quot;,&quot;Postdoctorates&quot;,&quot;Postgraduation Plans&quot;,&quot;Race and Ethnicity&quot;,&quot;Sex&quot;,&quot;Skilled Technical Workforce&quot;,&quot;Student Loan Debt&quot;,&quot;Type of R&amp;D (basic, applied, experimental development)&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2023&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;STEM Education&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3908,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 23-353&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;New data from the Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED) show an increase in doctoral\ndegree awards for the 2022 academic year and the highest number of research doctorates\never conferred in a single academic year. This was preceded by declines in doctorate\nconferrals from spring 2020 through spring 2021 after the shutdown of most colleges and\nuniversities at the start of the COVID-19 pandemic in March 2020. A higher proportion\nof recent doctorate recipients reported definite postgraduation employment commitments\nthan in prior years. The data also show a long-term decline of postgraduation\nemployment commitments within the academic sector and an increase in employment\ncommitments in the business sector. These data are from the 2022 SED, conducted by the\nNational Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science\nFoundation and cosponsored by the National Institutes of Health, the Department of\nEducation, and the National Endowment for the Humanities.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/28/2023&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf23353&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf23353&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2023.09.28&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Research Doctorate Conferrals Rebound, Leading to Record Number of U.S. Doctorate Recipients in 2022&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;COVID-19 Pandemic Impacts&quot;,&quot;Employment Sector&quot;,&quot;Employment Status&quot;,&quot;Family Characteristics&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Degrees&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Postdoctorates&quot;,&quot;Postgraduation Plans&quot;,&quot;Sex&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2023&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:3892,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 23-352&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Federal obligations for research and experimental development rose nearly 14% from FY 2020 to FY 2021, reaching $190 billion. Obligations for research and experimental development from COVID-19 pandemic-related stimulus funding totaled $35.6 billion. These data are from the latest edition of the Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development, sponsored by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/25/2023&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf23352&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf23352&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2023.09.25&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Obligations for R&amp;D Increased Nearly 14% in FY 2021, Supported by COVID-19 Pandemic-Related Funding&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;COVID-19 Pandemic Impacts&quot;,&quot;Federal Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Federally Funded Research and Development Centers (FFRDC) R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Cost Type&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Agreement (contract, grant, reimbursement, etc.)&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;,&quot;Type of R&amp;D (basic, applied, experimental development)&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2023&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:3828,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 23-348&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This InfoBrief provides current data on the United States’ 42 federally funded research and development centers (FFRDCs). FFRDCs spent $26.5 billion on R&amp;D in FY 2022, an annual increase of 6.4% in current dollars. The federal government funded $26.0 billion of the R&amp;D total, which represented a 5.9% increase in federal R&amp;D support to FFRDCs. These and the other statistics in this report come from the FY 2022 FFRDC Research and Development Survey, conducted by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/06/2023&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf23348&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf23348&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2023.09.06&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers Spent $26.5 Billion on R&amp;D in FY 2022&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Federal Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Federally Funded Research and Development Centers (FFRDC) R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Institutions&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Cost Type&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2023&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:3767,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 23-347&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;These tables provide data on R&amp;D expenditures for federally funded research and development centers (FFRDCs). Expenditures data are displayed for individually named FFRDCs, by source of funding, and type of R&amp;D. Data are from the FY 2022 FFRDC Research and Development Survey, by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/06/2023&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf23347&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf23347&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2023.09.06&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Expenditures: Fiscal Year 2022&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Federal Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Federally Funded Research and Development Centers (FFRDC) R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Institutions&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;,&quot;Type of R&amp;D (basic, applied, experimental development)&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2023&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Interactive&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering State Profiles&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering State Profiles&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:5713,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Updated data for Science and Engineering State Profiles are now available. This product provides state-level rankings and totals of science and engineering data for the 50 states, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico. The data tool allows users to compare state profiles for up to seven states or download the entire data set. Science and Engineering State Profiles is produced by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics of the National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;07/18/2023&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/states/download/state-profiles-2023-07.xlsx&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf00000&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2023.07.18&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering State Profiles: Spring 2023 Update&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2023&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering Workforce&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Working Paper&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:3628,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NCSES 23-213&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Previous applications of person-centered approaches to investigate job satisfaction and job importance using the Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR) have focused on describing cross-sectional relations between these variables within a collection cycle. The working paper presented here expands this previous research to explore whether (and if so, how) doctoral degree recipients transition between job satisfaction classes over time using latent transition analysis. The results show that more than half of the doctoral degree recipients in the sample transitioned to a different job satisfaction class at some point during the 2015, 2017, and 2019 SDR cycles. Notably, among respondents who transitioned to a new job satisfaction class between the 2015 and 2017 surveys, those who reported in 2015 being very satisfied with their current job’s level of independence, challenge, and responsibility but only moderately satisfied with their job’s salary and benefits in 2015 were most likely to report being only somewhat satisfied with all factors of their current job at the 2017 cycle. In addition, individuals who reported being only somewhat satisfied with all factors of their current job in 2017 and transitioned to a new job satisfaction class in 2019 were more than twice as likely to report being dissatisfied with their opportunities for advancement at the 2019 cycle than being very satisfied with all factors of their job in 2019. This finding indicates a potential pathway by which individuals who were initially very satisfied with at least some factors of their current job become dissatisfied. The results also showed that the probability of changing job satisfaction classes differed depending on an individual’s job importance class membership, employment sector, and self-reported race and ethnicity. Conclusions based on these results, as well as limitations and future directions for this research, are discussed.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;07/11/2023&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;ncses23213&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;ncses23213&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2023.07.11&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Investigating the Stability of Job Importance and Job Satisfaction over Time: A Latent Transition Analysis of the Survey of Doctorate Recipients&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Attitudes About Science&quot;,&quot;Citizenship&quot;,&quot;Disability Status&quot;,&quot;Employment Sector&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Degrees&quot;,&quot;Job Satisfaction&quot;,&quot;Minorities in Higher Education&quot;,&quot;Occupation&quot;,&quot;Postdoctorates&quot;,&quot;Race and Ethnicity&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Employment&quot;,&quot;Sex&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2023&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBytes&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:3667,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 23-345&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Latest visualizations from the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics using data from the Survey of State Government Research and Development: FY 2021 on state agency expenditures for R&amp;D by governmental functions (agriculture, energy, environment and natural resources, health, transportation).&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;07/05/2023&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf23345&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf23345&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2023.07.05&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of State Government Research and Development&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of State Government Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;State Government Agency Expenditures for R&amp;D, by Geographic Area and Function: FY 2021&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;R&amp;D Cost Type&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;State Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2023&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoChart&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering Indicators&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering Indicators&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3627,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 23-346&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;In 2020, the United States totaled $730 billion in domestic R&amp;D expenditures in 2020, up 8% from 2019 ($678 billion). Other top performers include Japan ($172 billion), Germany ($147 billion), South Korea ($111 billion), the United Kingdom ($90 billion), and France ($75 billion). China, the second-highest performer of domestic R&amp;D in recent years, totaled $465 billion in 2018, the most recent year with estimates reported by the Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development. In the same year, the United States totaled $618 billion. &quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;06/22/2023&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf23346&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf23346&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2023.06.22&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;United States Exceeds $700 Billion in Gross Domestic Expenditures on R&amp;D in 2020&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;By Country&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Cost Type&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Intensity&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2023&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBytes&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4575,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 23-344&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Latest visualizations from the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics using data from National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources: 2020–21 on trends in total R&amp;D expenditures by state consisting of three map charts: state R&amp;D expenditures 2020, R&amp;D share of state GDP, and change in state R&amp;D expenditures 2010–20.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;06/14/2023&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf23344&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf23344&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2023.06.14&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;California Leads in Innovative Capacity of U.S. States as Measured by U.S. R&amp;D Expenditures&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Production and Value Added&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Cost Type&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2023&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoChart&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3548,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 23-338&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Expenditures on the performance of basic research funded by the business sector have increased dramatically over the past 2 decades. Funding from the business sector for basic research has increased from $10.4 billion in 2000 to an estimated $36.0 billion in 2021 in constant 2012 dollars. Longer-term trends in funding shares show a decreased relative importance of the federal government as a funding source of basic research. Data are from the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources. &quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;06/13/2023&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf23338&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf23338&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2023.06.13&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business Sector Increases Funding for Basic Research&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;,&quot;Type of R&amp;D (basic, applied, experimental development)&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2023&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoChart&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3549,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 23-339&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The ratio of U.S. research and development (R&amp;D) to gross domestic product (GDP), at 3.40% in 2021, exceeded 3% for the first time in 2019. However, the decades between the previous peak, 1964, and recent years have witnessed notable changes in the funding sources for domestic R&amp;D. Data are from the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources. &quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;06/13/2023&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf23339&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf23339&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2023.06.13&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federally Funded R&amp;D Declines as a Share of GDP and Total R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Intensity&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2023&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoChart&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3547,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 23-340&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The ratio of U.S. research and development (R&amp;D) to gross domestic product (GDP), at 3.40% in 2021, exceeded 3% for the first time in 2019. The previous peak of the R&amp;D-to-GDP ratio was in 1964. Focusing on the long-term trends masks changes over the past decade that have driven R&amp;D to account for over 3% of the value of domestically produced goods and services. Data are from the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources. &quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;06/13/2023&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf23340&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf23340&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2023.06.13&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Recent Increase in R&amp;D to GDP Ratio Driven by Increases in Experimental Development&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;R&amp;D Cost Type&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Intensity&quot;,&quot;Type of R&amp;D (basic, applied, experimental development)&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2023&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Innovation and Global Competitiveness&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoChart&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering Indicators&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering Indicators&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3387,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 23-342&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Based on the location of inventors, the counties with the highest number of semiconductor utility patents granted are primarily in the western United States and in the northeast. Santa Clara County residents in California’s Silicon Valley received one-fifth (19,108) of all utility patents granted between 2000 and 2020. The second highest number of semiconductor utility patents was granted to residents of Ada County, Idaho (8,719), followed by Dutchess County, New York (4,460). Residents of Maricopa County in southwest Arizona were granted 4,270 utility patents. Data are from Science and Engineering Indicators 2022.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;06/05/2023&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf23342&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf23342&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2023.06.05&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;One-Fifth of USPTO Semiconductor Utility Patents Granted to Residents of Santa Clara County, California between 2000 and 2020&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Patenting and Trademark Activities&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2023&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Innovation and Global Competitiveness&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoChart&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering Indicators&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering Indicators&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3388,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 23-341&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Residents of San Diego County, California received the most biotechnology utility patents (3,824) for a single U.S. county between 2000 and 2020. The second largest was to residents of Middlesex County, Massachusetts (3,197), where the city of Boston, with an active life sciences industry, is located. The third and fourth highest numbers were granted to residents of two California counties: San Mateo (2,462) and Santa Clara (2,302). In total, these four counties alone accounted for over 20% of the more than 50,000 biotechnology utility patents issued nationally. Data are from Science and Engineering Indicators 2022.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;06/01/2023&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf23341&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf23341&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2023.06.01&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Coastal Counties in the West and Northeast Lead the United States in Biotechnology Utility Patents Granted to Residents between 2000 and 2020&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Patenting and Trademark Activities&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2023&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Innovation and Global Competitiveness&quot;,&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoChart&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering Indicators&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering Indicators&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3389,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 23-343&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Two Michigan counties (Oakland and Wayne) in the greater Detroit area had the first and third greatest numbers of utility patents in environmental technologies granted to U.S. residents between 2000 and 2020 (655 and 436 utility patents, respectively). The greater Detroit region has historically been associated with the automobile industry. During this time, residents of Harris County, Texas received the second greatest number (505). Between 2000 and 2020, U.S. residents were granted almost 22,300 environmental technology utility patents. Regionally, the Great Lakes, the southwest, and the coastal counties account for higher numbers of environmental technology utility patents. Data are from Science and Engineering Indicators 2022.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;06/01/2023&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf23343&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf23343&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2023.06.01&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Great Lakes, Coastal, and Southwest Counties Have Highest Numbers of Environmental Technologies Utility Patents Granted between 2000 and 2020&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Patenting and Trademark Activities&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2023&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoChart&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3469,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 23-335&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;During the 2018–20 period, microbusiness research and development (R&amp;D) performance increased for both manufacturing and nonmanufacturing industries. In 2020, microbusinesses performed $5.6 billion of R&amp;D. Data are from the 2021 Annual Business Survey: Data Year 2020, cosponsored by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation and by the Census Bureau.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;05/24/2023&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf23335&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf23335&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2023.05.24&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Annual Business Survey (ABS)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Annual Business Survey (ABS)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Among Microbusinesses, Domestic R&amp;D Performance Increased by 24% from 2018 to 2020&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Capital Expenditures&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Employment&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2023&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Innovation and Global Competitiveness&quot;,&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoChart&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:3468,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 23-334&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;In 2020, male majority-owned microbusinesses (businesses with one to nine employees) performed 80% of the total $5.6 billion of domestic research and development, while female majority-owned microbusinesses accounted for 9% of the total. Data are from the 2021 Annual Business Survey: Data Year 2020, cosponsored by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation and by the Census Bureau.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;05/24/2023&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf23334&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf23334&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2023.05.24&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Annual Business Survey (ABS)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Annual Business Survey (ABS)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Male Majority-Owned Microbusinesses Performed 80% of Domestic R&amp;D Costs&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Occupation&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Cost Type&quot;,&quot;Sex&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2023&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Innovation and Global Competitiveness&quot;,&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoChart&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3467,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 23-333&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Minority-owned microbusinesses accounted for 20% of the $5.6 billion of research and development performed by microbusinesses in 2020, while nonminority-owned microbusinesses accounted for 72% of the total. Data are from the 2021 Annual Business Survey: Data Year 2020, cosponsored by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation and by the Census Bureau.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;05/24/2023&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf23333&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf23333&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2023.05.24&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Annual Business Survey (ABS)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Annual Business Survey (ABS)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Minority-Owned U.S. Businesses Account for 20% of Domestic Microbusiness R&amp;D Performance Costs&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Occupation&quot;,&quot;Race and Ethnicity&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Cost Type&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2023&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Innovation and Global Competitiveness&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBytes&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:4573,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 23-331&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Latest visualization from the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics using data from the Annual Business Survey on product innovation trends.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;05/24/2023&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf23331&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf23331&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2023.05.24&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Annual Business Survey (ABS)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Annual Business Survey (ABS)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Product Innovation Trends 2016–18 to 2018–20: Findings from the Annual Business Survey&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Product &amp; Business Process Innovation&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2023&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Innovation and Global Competitiveness&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoChart&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3508,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 23-336&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Innovation is central to improvements in living standards and can have important impacts on individuals, businesses, and entire economic sectors. The Annual Business Survey is cosponsored by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation and by the Census Bureau, and measures product innovation as “the introduction of new or improved products (goods or services) that differed significantly from the business’s previous products or processes.” Minority-owned companies demonstrated higher rates of innovation (10.8%) than did nonminority-owned companies (8.5%). Hispanic or Latino–owned companies demonstrated higher rates of innovation (10.8%) than did companies owned by persons who were not Hispanic (8.8%).&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;05/23/2023&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf23336&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf23336&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2023.05.23&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Annual Business Survey (ABS)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Annual Business Survey (ABS)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Product Innovation and Race or Ethnicity of Primary Business Owner&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Citizenship&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Knowledge and Technology Intensive (KTI) Industries&quot;,&quot;Minorities in Higher Education&quot;,&quot;Occupation&quot;,&quot;Product &amp; Business Process Innovation&quot;,&quot;Race and Ethnicity&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2023&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Innovation and Global Competitiveness&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoChart&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3527,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 23-337&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Innovation is central to improvements in living standards and can have important impacts on individuals, businesses, and entire economic sectors. The Annual Business Survey is cosponsored by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation and by the Census Bureau and measures product innovation as “the introduction of new or improved products (goods or services) that differed significantly from the business’s previous products or processes.” About 10% of female-majority–owned companies were product innovators compared with 9% of male-majority–owned companies, a small but statistically significant difference. &quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;05/23/2023&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf23337&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf23337&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2023.05.23&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Annual Business Survey (ABS)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Annual Business Survey (ABS)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Product Innovation and Sex of the Primary Business Owner&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Occupation&quot;,&quot;Product &amp; Business Process Innovation&quot;,&quot;Sex&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2023&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:3327,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 23-329&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;About 80% of U.S. nonprofit organizations’ research and development (R&amp;D) performance was accounted for by nine states and the District of Columbia in FY 2020. By this measure, R&amp;D was more concentrated geographically among nonprofits than among for-profit businesses and institutions of higher education in 2020. Data are from the Nonprofit Research Activities FY 2020 module of the 2021 Annual Business Survey, conducted by the Census Bureau for the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation. &quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;05/09/2023&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf23329&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf23329&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2023.05.09&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Nonprofit Research Activities (NPRA) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Nonprofit Research Activities (NPRA) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Geographic Distribution of Nonprofit R&amp;D Performance in FY 2020&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Employment Sector&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Institutions&quot;,&quot;Nonprofit R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Employment&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Intensity&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2023&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Innovation and Global Competitiveness&quot;,&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoChart&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering Indicators&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering Indicators&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3427,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 23-332&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;California led the United States in 2020 value-added production from research and development (R&amp;D)-intensive industries, which include such industries as information technology services, software, pharmaceuticals, and air and spacecraft. California and the four states with the next-largest shares (Texas, Washington, New York, and Massachusetts) amounted to nearly half of U.S. value-added production from R&amp;D-intensive industries in 2020.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;05/08/2023&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf23332&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf23332&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2023.05.08&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;California Accounted for a Quarter of U.S. Value-Added Production by R&amp;D-Intensive Industries in 2020&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Knowledge and Technology Intensive (KTI) Industries&quot;,&quot;Production and Value Added&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2023&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3307,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 23-314&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The tables in this report present data from the Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey. BERD is the primary source of information on domestic and global research and development (R&amp;D) expenditures and the R&amp;D workforce for companies operating in the 50 U.S. states and the District of Columbia. The survey is conducted annually by the Census Bureau in accordance with an interagency agreement with the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics (NCSES) within the National Science Foundation. These data are used by government agencies, corporations, research organizations, and academics to assess trends in the performance and funding of R&amp;D.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;05/02/2023&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf23314&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf23314&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2023.05.02&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development: 2020&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;By Country&quot;,&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Disability Status&quot;,&quot;Employment Status&quot;,&quot;Faculty Rank, Tenure Status&quot;,&quot;Family Characteristics&quot;,&quot;Federal Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Federally Funded Research and Development Centers (FFRDC) R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Degrees&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Institutions&quot;,&quot;Knowledge and Technology Intensive (KTI) Industries&quot;,&quot;Occupation&quot;,&quot;Patenting and Trademark Activities&quot;,&quot;Product &amp; Business Process Innovation&quot;,&quot;Production and Value Added&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Capital Expenditures&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Cost Type&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Employment&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Agreement (contract, grant, reimbursement, etc.)&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Intensity&quot;,&quot;Sex&quot;,&quot;Skilled Technical Workforce&quot;,&quot;Technology Transfer Activities&quot;,&quot;Type of R&amp;D (basic, applied, experimental development)&quot;,&quot;Work Activity&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2023&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBytes&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:4572,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 23-330&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Latest visualization from the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics using data from the Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions on federal obligations for R&amp;D and R&amp;D plant to nonprofit organizations by selected agencies for FY 2020.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;04/17/2023&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf23330&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf23330&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2023.04.17&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Obligations for R&amp;D and R&amp;D Plant to Nonprofit Organizations, by Selected Agencies: FY 2020&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Federal Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Nonprofit R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2023&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering Workforce&quot;,&quot;STEM Education&quot;,&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoChart&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3292,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 23-328&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;According to the 2020 Business Enterprise Research and Development Survey (BERD), 27% of U.S. business R&amp;D employees were women. For all manufacturing subsectors and nonmanufacturing sectors with at least 100,000 R&amp;D workers, the female share of R&amp;D employment ranged from 14% to 47%. Women represented about half of all R&amp;D employees in just two detailed industry groups that meet this employment threshold: pharmaceuticals and medicines (2017 North American Industry Classification System [NAICS] 3254, 51%) and scientific research and development services (NAICS 5417, 50%). Machinery (NAICS 333) and transportation equipment manufacturing (NAICS 336) had the two lowest shares of female R&amp;D employees in this group of industries. Women made up about a quarter of the R&amp;D employees in the information sector (NAICS 51) and in the professional, scientific, and technical services other than R&amp;D sector (NAICS 54 less 5417). Recent data show similar variation by field for degrees awarded to women in 2020.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;03/20/2023&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf23328&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf23328&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2023.03.20&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Women Made Up Over a Quarter of All Business R&amp;D Employees in 2020&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Occupation&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Employment&quot;,&quot;Sex&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2023&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3250,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 23-327&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;In 2020, businesses invested $32.5 billion in assets such as buildings, equipment, and software to support their U.S. research and development activities, according to the Business Enterprise Research and Development Survey developed and cosponsored by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation. Of the $32.5 billion of R&amp;D capital expenditures, equipment accounted for almost half ($15.7 billion), followed by capitalized software ($8.4 billion). &quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;03/17/2023&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf23327&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf23327&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2023.03.17&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Businesses Invested $32.5 Billion in Assets to Support Their R&amp;D Activities in the United States in 2020&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Knowledge and Technology Intensive (KTI) Industries&quot;,&quot;Production and Value Added&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Capital Expenditures&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Cost Type&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Employment&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Intensity&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2023&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering Workforce&quot;,&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3187,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 23-322&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Labor costs accounted for $372 billion of U.S. business research and development expenditures in 2020—or over two-thirds of the $538 billion in business R&amp;D performance. In 2020, these labor costs included $300.0 billion in salaries, wages, and fringe benefits; $48.3 billion in stock-based compensation; and $23.5 billion in temporary staffing. This InfoBrief explores the labor costs of business R&amp;D, highlighting selected characteristics over time and across industries. Data are from the 2020 Business Enterprise Research and Development Survey (BERD), developed and cosponsored by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation and by the Census Bureau, which collected and tabulated data for the survey.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;02/21/2023&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf23322&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf23322&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2023.02.21&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Labor Costs Account for Over Two-Thirds of U.S. Business R&amp;D Performance in 2020&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Income and Salary&quot;,&quot;Occupation&quot;,&quot;Production and Value Added&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Cost Type&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Employment&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2023&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering Workforce&quot;,&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoChart&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:3227,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 23-326&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;State governments’ intramural research and development personnel totaled 9,132 employees across the 50 state governments and the District of Columbia in FY 2021. Intramural R&amp;D personnel are a state department’s or agency’s own employees (i.e., not public university employees) who perform or support R&amp;D. Of the 9,132 employees, 4,854 were researchers, 2,981 were technicians, and 1,297 were support staff. In New York state, agency R&amp;D personnel totaled 2,942, of which 1,347 were researchers, 1,184 were technicians, and 411 were support staff, nearly one-third of all state government R&amp;D personnel. Researchers include R&amp;D scientists, engineers, and their managers. Technicians include wildlife technicians, lab technicians, and field staff. Support staff include accountants, facilities management, grant specialists, and clerical staffs. Data are from the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics’ Survey of State Government Research and Development.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;02/21/2023&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf23326&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf23326&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2023.02.21&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of State Government Research and Development&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of State Government Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;New York State Agencies Account for Nearly One-Third of All State Governments’ Intramural R&amp;D Personnel in FY 2021&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Employment&quot;,&quot;State Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2023&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;STEM Education&quot;,&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoChart&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:3167,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 23-325&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;In FY 2020, across higher education institutions, the ratio of federal obligations for research &amp; development (R&amp;D) and fellowship, training, and traineeship grants (FTTGs) per full-time equivalent enrolled student was $5,5000 per student for R&amp;D and $200 per student for FTTGs. For universities with very high research activity (Carnegie Classification R1), the ratios nearly doubled. Compared to R1 schools, all other institution types had lower R&amp;D obligations per student. But for FTTG obligations, historically Black colleges and universities (HBCUs) equaled the ratio of R1 schools, and tribal colleges and universities exceeded it.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;02/08/2023&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf23325&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf23325&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2023.02.08&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Per-Student Federal Obligations for R&amp;D and Fellowship, Training, and Traineeship Grants at HBCUs and Other Minority-Serving Institutions&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Federal Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Institutions&quot;,&quot;Minorities in Higher Education&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2023&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reports&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Women, Minorities, and Persons with Disabilities in STEM&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Women, Minorities, and Persons with Disabilities in STEM&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:5583,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 23-315&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Women, minorities, and persons with disabilities have historically been underrepresented in the science and engineering (S&amp;E) enterprise. The report Diversity and STEM: Women, Minorities, and Persons with Disabilities 2023 provides statistical information about these three groups in the context of STEM (science, technology, engineering, and mathematics). This biennial report assesses the current standing of women, persons from racial or ethnic minority groups, and persons with disabilities in STEM employment and S&amp;E education in relation to other groups. Data are from the Census Bureau, the National Center for Education Statistics, and the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;01/30/2023&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://ncses.nsf.gov/pubs/nsf23315/assets/nsf23315-report.pdf&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf23315&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2023.01.30&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Diversity and STEM: Women, Minorities, and Persons with Disabilities 2023&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2023&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering Workforce&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:2925,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 23-307&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The number of college graduates in the United States reached nearly 68.6 million in 2021, of which approximately 68 million had ever held a job, with nearly 52 million currently employed and 16 million not employed. Although the number of employed college graduates increased by 1.2 million (2.5%) between 2019 and 2021, the number of nonemployed college graduates increased by 2.5 million (17.9%). This large increase in nonemployment coincided with the COVID-19 pandemic and signals important changes in the labor market for college graduates. This InfoBrief provides an overview of the effects of the COVID-19 pandemic on the college-educated workforce, drawing on new data from the National Survey of College Graduates, conducted by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;01/27/2023&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf23307&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf23307&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2023.01.27&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;National Survey of College Graduates (NSCG)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Survey of College Graduates (NSCG)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Effects of the COVID-19 Pandemic on Employment, Earnings, and Professional Engagement: New Insights from the 2021 National Survey of College Graduates&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;COVID-19 Pandemic Impacts&quot;,&quot;Employment Status&quot;,&quot;Family Characteristics&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Degrees&quot;,&quot;Income and Salary&quot;,&quot;Job Satisfaction&quot;,&quot;Minorities in Higher Education&quot;,&quot;Occupation&quot;,&quot;Race and Ethnicity&quot;,&quot;Sex&quot;,&quot;Work Activity&quot;,&quot;Work-related Training&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2023&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering Workforce&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:2905,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 23-306&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;These tables were compiled using data from the 2021 National Survey of College Graduates (NSCG). The NSCG, conducted by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation, is a repeated cross-sectional biennial survey that collects information on the nation’s college-educated workforce. This survey is a unique source for examining the relationship between degree field and occupation, as well as examining other characteristics of college-educated individuals, including work activities, salary, and demographic information.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;01/27/2023&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf23306&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf23306&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2023.01.27&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;National Survey of College Graduates (NSCG)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Survey of College Graduates (NSCG)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Survey of College Graduates: 2021&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;By Country&quot;,&quot;Certifications and Licenses&quot;,&quot;Citizenship&quot;,&quot;Disability Status&quot;,&quot;Employment Sector&quot;,&quot;Employment Status&quot;,&quot;Family Characteristics&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Degrees&quot;,&quot;Income and Salary&quot;,&quot;Job Satisfaction&quot;,&quot;Occupation&quot;,&quot;Race and Ethnicity&quot;,&quot;Sex&quot;,&quot;Student Loan Debt&quot;,&quot;Work Activity&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2023&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering Workforce&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:3110,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 23-319&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Tables from the 2021 Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR) present data on the demographic characteristics, labor force status, occupations, employment sector, and median annual salaries of individuals with a U.S. research doctoral degree in a science, engineering, or health (SEH) field. The 2021 cycle of the SDR also had content to measure the effects of the coronavirus pandemic on the U.S.-trained SEH doctorate holders. The SDR is sponsored by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation and by the National Institutes of Health.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;01/27/2023&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf23319&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf23319&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2023.01.27&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients, 2021&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;By Country&quot;,&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Citizenship&quot;,&quot;COVID-19 Pandemic Impacts&quot;,&quot;Disability Status&quot;,&quot;Employment Sector&quot;,&quot;Employment Status&quot;,&quot;Faculty Rank, Tenure Status&quot;,&quot;Family Characteristics&quot;,&quot;Federal Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Federally Funded Research and Development Centers (FFRDC) R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Degrees&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Income and Salary&quot;,&quot;Institutions&quot;,&quot;Minorities in Higher Education&quot;,&quot;Occupation&quot;,&quot;Postdoctorates&quot;,&quot;Race and Ethnicity&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Employment&quot;,&quot;Sex&quot;,&quot;Skilled Technical Workforce&quot;,&quot;State Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Work Activity&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2023&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering Workforce&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:3112,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 23-318&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The coronavirus pandemic has had a profound effect on the education, employment, and professional activities of many individuals worldwide since its emergence in early 2020. The magnitude of COVID-related employment disruptions for U.S.-trained doctorate recipients can be characterized as modest. This InfoBrief presents findings from the Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR), which is conducted by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation. The latest wave of the SDR was conducted in 2021 and included new questions related to the coronavirus pandemic, providing an opportunity to assess the pandemic’s impact on U.S.-trained SEH doctorate holders, such as employment status, salary, and work conditions.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;01/27/2023&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf23318&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf23318&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2023.01.27&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;U.S. Residing Doctoral Scientists and Engineers Report Modest Professional Impacts from the Coronavirus Pandemic: Findings from the 2021 Survey of Doctorate Recipients&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;By Country&quot;,&quot;COVID-19 Pandemic Impacts&quot;,&quot;Employment Sector&quot;,&quot;Employment Status&quot;,&quot;Federal Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Degrees&quot;,&quot;Income and Salary&quot;,&quot;Job Satisfaction&quot;,&quot;Postdoctorates&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Employment&quot;,&quot;Work-related Training&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2023&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoChart&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding, by Budget Function&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding, by Budget Function&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3147,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 23-323&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The FY 2023 proposed federal budget authority for research and development and R&amp;D plant totaled $191 billion, up 12.7% from the proposed FY 2022 amount (preliminary amount before the FY 2022 omnibus appropriations bill was passed on 10 March 2022). Under the FY 2023 proposal, R&amp;D and R&amp;D plant for the National defense functional category totaled nearly $90 billion, or 47% of total R&amp;D and R&amp;D plant, civilian functions made up the remainder of the total. Although the National defense budget function rose 14% between FY 2022 (preliminary) and FY 2023 (proposed), the Health functional category increased 10%. Data are from the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;01/26/2023&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf23323&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf23323&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2023.01.26&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Budget Authority for R&amp;D and R&amp;D Plant for National Defense and Civilian Functions Totaled $191 billion in FY 2023 Proposed Budget&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Federal Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Capital Expenditures&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2023&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding, by Budget Function&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding, by Budget Function&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3188,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 23-324&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report contains data on the budget authority provided to U.S. federal agencies to fund the research and development (R&amp;D) and R&amp;D plant components of their programs in FYs 2021–23. The tables in this report provide this budget authority detail by agency and major programs for each of the governmental functions in which R&amp;D is present. Several concluding tables also show budget authority data for R&amp;D in earlier years. Data were compiled by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;01/26/2023&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf23324&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf23324&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2023.01.26&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding, by Budget Function: Fiscal Years 2021–23&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;By Country&quot;,&quot;Clinical Trials and Medical Projects&quot;,&quot;Federal Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Capital Expenditures&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Cost Type&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Intensity&quot;,&quot;Type of R&amp;D (basic, applied, experimental development)&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2023&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;STEM Education&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:3067,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 23-311&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The COVID-19 pandemic has disrupted graduate enrollment patterns in science, engineering, and health fields. Enrollment declines seen in 2020 among temporary visa holders and first-time, full-time master’s and doctoral students were reversed in 2021, driven by a 44.3% increase in first-time, full-time master’s students and by a 13.4% increase in first-time, full-time doctoral students between 2020 and 2021. Full-time master’s enrollment and doctoral enrollment in 2021 were the highest enrollments reported since separate data for master’s and doctoral students became available in 2017. Conversely, overall postdoctoral (postdoc) appointments continued to decline. These data are from the 2021 Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering, sponsored by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation and by the National Institutes of Health.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;01/17/2023&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf23311&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf23311&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2023.01.17&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;2021 Graduate Enrollment in Science, Engineering, and Health Fields at All-Time High as Postdocs Continue to Decline&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Citizenship&quot;,&quot;COVID-19 Pandemic Impacts&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Degrees&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Enrollment&quot;,&quot;Minorities in Higher Education&quot;,&quot;Non-faculty Research Personnel&quot;,&quot;Occupation&quot;,&quot;Postdoctorates&quot;,&quot;Race and Ethnicity&quot;,&quot;Sex&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2023&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reference&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Master Government List of Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Master Government List of Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:5659,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The Master Government list of Federally Funded Research and Development Centers (FFRDCs), maintained by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics of the National Science Foundation, has been updated for 2023. FFRDCs are listed by sponsoring agency, administrator type, location, and type of activity.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;01/17/2023&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://ncses.nsf.gov/resource/master-gov-lists-ffrdc/prior-lists&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf00000&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2023.01.17&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Master Government List of Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers: FY 2023&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2023&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering Workforce&quot;,&quot;STEM Education&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:3127,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 23-312&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report presents data from the 2021 Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering, sponsored by National Center for Science and Engineering Statis