ta Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:2845,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 23-302&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The 23 data tables in this report present FY 2021 and time series data for the 50 states and the District of Columbia. Data are available on total R&amp;D expenditures; intramural and extramural performers; source of funds; and R&amp;D by governmental function by state and department or agency, employee count, and full-time equivalent counts. These data were collected by the Survey of State Government Research and Development, conducted by the Census Bureau for the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;11/29/2022&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf23302&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf23302&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2022.11.29&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of State Government Research and Development&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of State Government Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of State Government Research and Development: FY 2021&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Federal Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Capital Expenditures&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Cost Type&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Employment&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;,&quot;State Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;State Government R&amp;D-related Facilities&quot;,&quot;Type of R&amp;D (basic, applied, experimental development)&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2022&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Interactive&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering State Profiles&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering State Profiles&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:5647,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Updated data for Science and Engineering State Profiles are now available. This product provides state-level rankings and totals of science and engineering data for the 50 states, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico. The data tool allows users to compare state profiles for up to seven states or download the entire data set. Science and Engineering State Profiles is produced by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics of the National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;10/31/2022&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/states/download/state-profiles-2022-10.xlsx&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf00000&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2022.10.31&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering State Profiles: Fall 2022 Update&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2022&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering Workforce&quot;,&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Postdoctorates at Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Postdoctorates at Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:2647,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 22-336&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The detailed tables in this report contain aggregate counts of postdocs at federally funded research and development centers (FFRDCs), by citizenship, ethnicity, race, sex, source of financial support, and field of research. Data are from the Survey of Postdocs at Federally Funded Research and Development Centers, which is the primary source of information on postdocs employed in FFRDCs in the United States. The survey is sponsored by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation and by the National Institutes of Health.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;10/25/2022&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf22336&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf22336&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2022.10.25&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Postdocs at Federally Funded Research and Development Centers&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Postdocs at Federally Funded Research and Development Centers&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Postdocs at Federally Funded Research and Development Centers: Fall 2021&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Citizenship&quot;,&quot;Federal Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Federally Funded Research and Development Centers (FFRDC) R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Institutions&quot;,&quot;Postdoctorates&quot;,&quot;Race and Ethnicity&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;,&quot;Sex&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2022&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering Workforce&quot;,&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Postdoctorates at Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Postdoctorates at Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:2786,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 22-345&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;In the United States, federally funded research and development centers (FFRDCs) advance strategic research in science, engineering, and health, and the 25 FFRDCs with postdoctoral programs contribute to the training of the next generation of scientists and engineers. The number of postdoctoral researchers (postdocs) receiving training at these FFRDCs has grown by more than 1,000 postdocs (3.2%) since 2013, with 3,637 postdocs reported in 2021. Between 2019 and 2021, the number of postdocs who are U.S. citizens and permanent residents increased by 350 (24.5%), whereas the number of postdocs with a temporary visa declined by 48 (2.5%). Data in this report are from the 2021 Survey of Postdocs at Federally Funded Research and Development Centers. The survey is conducted in conjunction with the Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering, which is sponsored by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation and by the National Institutes of Health.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;10/25/2022&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf22345&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf22345&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2022.10.25&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Postdocs at Federally Funded Research and Development Centers&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Postdocs at Federally Funded Research and Development Centers&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;The Shifting Demographic Composition of Postdoctoral Researchers at Federally Funded Research and Development Centers in 2021&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Citizenship&quot;,&quot;COVID-19 Pandemic Impacts&quot;,&quot;Federal Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Federally Funded Research and Development Centers (FFRDC) R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Institutions&quot;,&quot;Postdoctorates&quot;,&quot;Race and Ethnicity&quot;,&quot;Sex&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2022&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;STEM Education&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reports&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:2785,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 23-300&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report summarizes trends in U.S. doctoral education by using data from the 2021 Survey of Earned Doctorates, an annual census of research doctorate recipients from U.S. universities. Important trends in this population are highlighted in this report, including the representation of women, minorities, and temporary visa holders; fields that are attracting graduate students; time to complete doctoral degree; employment opportunities after graduation; and educational pathways to the doctorate. A special section focuses on the impact of the COVID-19 pandemic on doctorate recipients’ graduate experiences and postgraduation plans. Detailed data tables and additional resources are also available.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;10/18/2022&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf23300&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf23300&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2022.10.18&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities: 2021&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Attitudes About Science&quot;,&quot;By Country&quot;,&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Citizenship&quot;,&quot;COVID-19 Pandemic Impacts&quot;,&quot;Disability Status&quot;,&quot;Employment Sector&quot;,&quot;Employment Status&quot;,&quot;Faculty Rank, Tenure Status&quot;,&quot;Family Characteristics&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Financial Support&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Degrees&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Income and Salary&quot;,&quot;Institutions&quot;,&quot;Minorities in Higher Education&quot;,&quot;Occupation&quot;,&quot;Postdoctorates&quot;,&quot;Postgraduation Plans&quot;,&quot;Race and Ethnicity&quot;,&quot;Sex&quot;,&quot;Skilled Technical Workforce&quot;,&quot;Student Loan Debt&quot;,&quot;Work Activity&quot;,&quot;Work-related Training&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2022&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;STEM Education&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Working Paper&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:2805,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NCSES 23-200&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This working paper describes the motivation for changing the Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED) field of study (FOS) taxonomy used for collection and reporting of education data items in the 2021 SED, and the improvements to education data items. The paper also examines the impact of these changes on the distribution of historical FOS data and provides guidance on conducting trend data comparisons.  &quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;10/18/2022&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;ncses23200&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;ncses23200&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2022.10.18&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates Field of Study Taxonomy Changes in 2021 and Impact on Trend Data&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Degrees&quot;,&quot;Institutions&quot;,&quot;Postdoctorates&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2022&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Innovation and Global Competitiveness&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:2766,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 22-344&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The detailed tables in this report present data from the 2020 Annual Business Survey (ABS) (data year 2019). The ABS is the primary source of data on research and development of for profit, nonfarm businesses with one to nine employees operating in the 50 U.S. states and the District of Columbia. The ABS also collects data on innovation, technology, intellectual property, and business owner characteristics of business of all sizes. The survey is conducted annually by the Census Bureau for the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;10/07/2022&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf22344&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf22344&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2022.10.07&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Annual Business Survey: 2020 (Data Year 2019)&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;By Country&quot;,&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Citizenship&quot;,&quot;Federal Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Knowledge and Technology Intensive (KTI) Industries&quot;,&quot;Minorities in Higher Education&quot;,&quot;Occupation&quot;,&quot;Patenting and Trademark Activities&quot;,&quot;Product &amp; Business Process Innovation&quot;,&quot;Production and Value Added&quot;,&quot;Race and Ethnicity&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Cost Type&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Employment&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Agreement (contract, grant, reimbursement, etc.)&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Intensity&quot;,&quot;Sex&quot;,&quot;Skilled Technical Workforce&quot;,&quot;Technology Transfer Activities&quot;,&quot;Type of R&amp;D (basic, applied, experimental development)&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2022&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:2745,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 22-343&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Businesses spent $538 billion on R&amp;D performance in 2020, with $466 billion of funding from companies’ own sources and $71 billion from other sources. These amounts follow a 2-year period of double-digit annual growth in the level of national domestic R&amp;D performance, and this robust performance seems likely to continue, based on the large increase in funding that companies planned to spend from their own funds for domestic R&amp;D performance in 2021. Data were based on the Business Enterprise Research and Development Survey (BERD), cosponsored by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation and by the Census Bureau. &quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;10/04/2022&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf22343&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf22343&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2022.10.04&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Businesses Spent Over a Half Trillion Dollars for R&amp;D Performance in the United States During 2020, a 9.1% Increase Over 2019&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;COVID-19 Pandemic Impacts&quot;,&quot;Federal Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Federally Funded Research and Development Centers (FFRDC) R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Knowledge and Technology Intensive (KTI) Industries&quot;,&quot;Occupation&quot;,&quot;Patenting and Trademark Activities&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Capital Expenditures&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Cost Type&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Employment&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Intensity&quot;,&quot;Sex&quot;,&quot;Technology Transfer Activities&quot;,&quot;Type of R&amp;D (basic, applied, experimental development)&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2022&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering Workforce&quot;,&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoChart&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:2665,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 22-339&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Among the 50.5 million college graduates employed in all occupations across the United States in 2019, approximately 3.7 million (7%) were R&amp;D workers—graduates who reported that their primary work activity was in basic research, applied research, or experimental development. Of approximately 7.5 million graduates in science and engineering (S&amp;E) occupations, roughly 1 in 5 (23%) were R&amp;D workers. Biological, agricultural, and other life scientists had the highest concentration of R&amp;D workers among them (59%), followed by physical and related scientists (47%). Concentrations within these two groups of scientists were even higher for those with a doctorate degree. Data are from the National Survey of College Graduates, by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/29/2022&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf22339&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf22339&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2022.09.29&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;National Survey of College Graduates (NSCG)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Survey of College Graduates (NSCG)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;A Quarter of College Graduates and About Half of Doctorate Holders in Science and Engineering Occupations Were R&amp;D Workers in 2019&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Higher Education Degrees&quot;,&quot;Occupation&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Employment&quot;,&quot;Work Activity&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2022&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Working Paper&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:2725,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NCSES 22-212&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The Survey of Nonprofit Research Activities (NPRA Survey) collects information on activities related to research and development that are performed or funded by nonprofits in the United States. The NPRA Survey is part of the data collection portfolio directed by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics (NCSES) within the National Science Foundation (NSF). The FY 2016 NPRA Survey was conducted in 2018 with a sample of nonprofit organizations in the United States. The overall response rate was 48% unweighted and 61% weighted. Due to a low response rate, particularly for certain subgroups such as hospitals (35% unweighted and 45% weighted response rate), not all of the NPRA Survey data met NCSES’ criteria for official statistics. NCSES decided to undertake additional assessment in order to determine the subset of NPRA data that would meet the current NCSES statistical standards required for official release. This working paper identifies which data from the FY 2016 NPRA Survey met NCSES’ statistical standards. This document summarizes the steps taken to conduct this additional assessment, and it also includes detailed information on the data quality comparisons.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/22/2022&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;ncses22212&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;ncses22212&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2022.09.22&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Nonprofit Research Activities (NPRA) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Nonprofit Research Activities (NPRA) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Assessment of the FY 2016 Survey of Nonprofit Research Activities to Determine Whether Data Meet Current Statistical Standards for Publication&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;By Country&quot;,&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Certifications and Licenses&quot;,&quot;Citizenship&quot;,&quot;Clinical Trials and Medical Projects&quot;,&quot;Faculty Rank, Tenure Status&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Financial Support&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Degrees&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Enrollment&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Institutions&quot;,&quot;New Construction&quot;,&quot;Nonprofit R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Occupation&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Capital Expenditures&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Cost Type&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Employment&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Agreement (contract, grant, reimbursement, etc.)&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;,&quot;Repairs and Renovations&quot;,&quot;Skilled Technical Workforce&quot;,&quot;State Government R&amp;D-related Facilities&quot;,&quot;Student Loan Debt&quot;,&quot;Type of R&amp;D (basic, applied, experimental development)&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2022&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:2626,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 22-338&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;These tables present the results of the FY 2016 Nonprofit Research Activities (NPRA) Survey. This survey is the primary source of information on research and experimental development (R&amp;D) performed or funded by 501(c) nonprofit organizations in the United States. The NPRA Survey is conducted by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics (NCSES) within the National Science Foundation (NSF).&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/22/2022&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf22338&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf22338&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2022.09.22&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Nonprofit Research Activities (NPRA) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Nonprofit Research Activities (NPRA) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Nonprofit Research Activities: Fiscal Year 2016&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;By Country&quot;,&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Citizenship&quot;,&quot;Faculty Rank, Tenure Status&quot;,&quot;Financial Support&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Degrees&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Enrollment&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Institutions&quot;,&quot;New Construction&quot;,&quot;Nonprofit R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Cost Type&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Employment&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Agreement (contract, grant, reimbursement, etc.)&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;,&quot;Repairs and Renovations&quot;,&quot;Skilled Technical Workforce&quot;,&quot;State Government R&amp;D-related Facilities&quot;,&quot;Student Loan Debt&quot;,&quot;Type of R&amp;D (basic, applied, experimental development)&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2022&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:2645,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 22-337&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The nonprofit sector is one of four major sectors of the economy (along with business, government, and higher education) that performs or funds research and experimental development (R&amp;D). Nationally, nonprofits spent an estimated $22.6 billion on R&amp;D performed by their organizations in FY 2016, which is equivalent to 4% of the estimated total spent on R&amp;D in the United States in 2016. Data in this InfoBrief are from the FY 2016 Survey of Nonprofit Research Activities (NPRA Survey), sponsored by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics (NCSES) within the National Science Foundation. The FY 2016 NPRA Survey marks the first nationally representative collection of R&amp;D data from U.S. 501(c) organizations other than universities and colleges in more than 20 years. This InfoBrief presents the subset of top-level official estimates.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/22/2022&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf22337&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf22337&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2022.09.22&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Nonprofit Research Activities (NPRA) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Nonprofit Research Activities (NPRA) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;U.S. Nonprofits Spent $22.6 Billion on R&amp;D Activities in FY 2016&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;By Country&quot;,&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Citizenship&quot;,&quot;Faculty Rank, Tenure Status&quot;,&quot;Financial Support&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Degrees&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Enrollment&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;New Construction&quot;,&quot;Nonprofit R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Occupation&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Employment&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Agreement (contract, grant, reimbursement, etc.)&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;,&quot;Repairs and Renovations&quot;,&quot;State Government R&amp;D-related Facilities&quot;,&quot;Student Loan Debt&quot;,&quot;Type of R&amp;D (basic, applied, experimental development)&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2022&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:2705,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 22-341&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;In FY 2020, federal agency obligations to universities and colleges for science and engineering (S&amp;E) activities increased to a historical high of $39.1 billion in current dollars, up 3% from FY 2019. Federal obligations to higher education institutions from pandemic-related stimulus funds totaled $1.4 billion during the same period. Estimates are from the FY 2020 Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions, conducted by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation. \n&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/20/2022&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf22341&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf22341&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2022.09.20&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Science and Engineering Support to Higher Education Increased 3% in FY 2020&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;By Country&quot;,&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Citizenship&quot;,&quot;COVID-19 Pandemic Impacts&quot;,&quot;Faculty Rank, Tenure Status&quot;,&quot;Federal Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Financial Support&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Degrees&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Enrollment&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Institutions&quot;,&quot;Minorities in Higher Education&quot;,&quot;New Construction&quot;,&quot;Race and Ethnicity&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Employment&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Agreement (contract, grant, reimbursement, etc.)&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;,&quot;Repairs and Renovations&quot;,&quot;State Government R&amp;D-related Facilities&quot;,&quot;Student Loan Debt&quot;,&quot;Type of R&amp;D (basic, applied, experimental development)&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2022&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:2726,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 22-342&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;These tables present FY 2020 data on federal obligations, including pandemic-related stimulus, to academic and nonprofit institutions for science and engineering research and development. Data include science and engineering support by type of activity, as well as ranking by state, agency, and individual institution. Data are from the Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/20/2022&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf22342&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf22342&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2022.09.20&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions: Fiscal Year 2020&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;By Country&quot;,&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Citizenship&quot;,&quot;Faculty Rank, Tenure Status&quot;,&quot;Federal Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Federally Funded Research and Development Centers (FFRDC) R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Financial Support&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Degrees&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Enrollment&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Institutions&quot;,&quot;Minorities in Higher Education&quot;,&quot;New Construction&quot;,&quot;Nonprofit R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Race and Ethnicity&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Capital Expenditures&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Cost Type&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Employment&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Agreement (contract, grant, reimbursement, etc.)&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;,&quot;Repairs and Renovations&quot;,&quot;Sex&quot;,&quot;State Government R&amp;D-related Facilities&quot;,&quot;Student Loan Debt&quot;,&quot;Type of R&amp;D (basic, applied, experimental development)&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2022&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:2605,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 22-333&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This InfoBrief presents research and development and other data on microbusinesses (those with 1–9 domestic employees), by industry, type of R&amp;D, funding source, state, and business owner demographics. In 2019, microbusinesses spent $6.3 billion on R&amp;D costs in the United States, of which $5.2 billion was performed by the microbusinesses themselves. This InfoBrief distinguishes between all R&amp;D costs and R&amp;D performance costs that includes only the costs for the R&amp;D performed by the business. Data for this InfoBrief are from the Annual Business Survey (ABS), developed and cosponsored by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation and by the Census Bureau.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/20/2022&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf22333&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf22333&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2022.09.20&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Annual Business Survey (ABS)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Annual Business Survey (ABS)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Microbusinesses Performed $5.2 Billion of R&amp;D in the United States in 2019&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;By Country&quot;,&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Citizenship&quot;,&quot;Employment Sector&quot;,&quot;Faculty Rank, Tenure Status&quot;,&quot;Federal Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Financial Support&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Degrees&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Enrollment&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Income and Salary&quot;,&quot;Knowledge and Technology Intensive (KTI) Industries&quot;,&quot;Minorities in Higher Education&quot;,&quot;New Construction&quot;,&quot;Occupation&quot;,&quot;Patenting and Trademark Activities&quot;,&quot;Product &amp; Business Process Innovation&quot;,&quot;Race and Ethnicity&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Capital Expenditures&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Cost Type&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Employment&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Intensity&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Publications &amp; Articles&quot;,&quot;Repairs and Renovations&quot;,&quot;Sex&quot;,&quot;State Government R&amp;D-related Facilities&quot;,&quot;Student Loan Debt&quot;,&quot;Type of R&amp;D (basic, applied, experimental development)&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2022&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Innovation and Global Competitiveness&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBytes&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:3711,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NCSES 22-214&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Visualizations from the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics present information on international utility patenting in selected critical and emerging technologies. Data are from the Science and Engineering Indicators 2022 Invention, Knowledge Transfer, and Innovation report.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/16/2022&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;ncses22214&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;ncses22214&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2022.09.16&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Utility Patenting in Selected Critical and Emerging Technologies Based on International Patents Granted by Country or Economy&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Patenting and Trademark Activities&quot;,&quot;Technology Transfer Activities&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2022&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoChart&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:2687,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 22-340&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Federally funded research and development centers (FFRDCs) in California and New Mexico account for more than half ($13.2 billion) of the $24.9 billion in total R&amp;D expenditures at FFRDCs in FY 2021. The remaining $11.8 billion (47.2%) of FY 2021 FFRDC R&amp;D occurred across 17 states. Virginia had 11 FFRDCs, the most of any state.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/07/2022&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf22340&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf22340&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2022.09.07&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Over 50% of FFRDC FY 2021 R&amp;D Spending Was in Two States&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Federal Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Federally Funded Research and Development Centers (FFRDC) R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Institutions&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2022&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Innovation and Global Competitiveness&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Working Paper&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:2686,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NCSES 22-213&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This working paper examines differences in the rate of digitalization across industry, firm size class, and location along with how digitalization and cloud computing is associated with different types of innovation. Digitalization—the conversion of text, pictures, video, or sound into a digital form that can be processed by a computer and often referred to as the Fourth Industrial Revolution—has been occurring since the widespread adoption of computers by businesses. The introduction of cloud computing can help to accelerate the digital transition by replacing large capital expenditures on information technology (IT) with IT infrastructure, support, and software as a pay-as-you-go service. The introduction of the cloud may thus reduce the cost of experimenting with new ways of doing business reliant on digitalization, and this phenomenon may be observed in the data on innovation. &quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;08/25/2022&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;ncses22213&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;ncses22213&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2022.08.25&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Annual Business Survey (ABS)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Annual Business Survey (ABS)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Digitalization, Cloud Computing, and Innovation in U.S. Businesses&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;By Country&quot;,&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;COVID-19 Pandemic Impacts&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Knowledge and Technology Intensive (KTI) Industries&quot;,&quot;Occupation&quot;,&quot;Product &amp; Business Process Innovation&quot;,&quot;Production and Value Added&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Capital Expenditures&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Cost Type&quot;,&quot;Technology Transfer Activities&quot;,&quot;Type of R&amp;D (basic, applied, experimental development)&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2022&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Working Paper&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:2525,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NCSES 22-211&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Developing linkages between administrative data and National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics (NCSES) survey data provides new opportunities to examine the impacts of federal funding on the U.S. science and engineering enterprise. This working paper describes a novel effort to match respondents of the NCSES Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR) to investigators in the National Science Foundation (NSF) awards database. Specific inputs to the matching process included data from the 2015 SDR—which consisted of 78,320 respondents, representing 920,050 U.S.-residing and 127,800 non-U.S.-residing doctoral scientists and engineers—and roughly 700,000 NSF award-investigator records spanning the last 60 years. Matching was performed sequentially using deterministic methods, primarily relying on survey respondent and award investigator names and e-mail addresses, as well as institution information. The matching process resulted in 7,363 SDR respondents matched to NSF awards, with an average of 4.4 awards matched to each individual who was matched to any award. Data limitations of this matched set, as well as illustrative use examples, are discussed. This proof-of-concept exploration indicates the potential of data linkages to improve the utility of NCSES survey data and to open a wide range of applications for research and program evaluation. &quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;08/12/2022&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;ncses22211&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;ncses22211&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2022.08.12&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Matching SDR Respondents to Investigators of NSF Awards&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;By Country&quot;,&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Citizenship&quot;,&quot;Employment Sector&quot;,&quot;Faculty Rank, Tenure Status&quot;,&quot;Federal Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Federally Funded Research and Development Centers (FFRDC) R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Financial Support&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Degrees&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Enrollment&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Institutions&quot;,&quot;Minorities in Higher Education&quot;,&quot;New Construction&quot;,&quot;Nonprofit R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Occupation&quot;,&quot;Postgraduation Plans&quot;,&quot;Race and Ethnicity&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Employment&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Agreement (contract, grant, reimbursement, etc.)&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;,&quot;Repairs and Renovations&quot;,&quot;Sex&quot;,&quot;State Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;State Government R&amp;D-related Facilities&quot;,&quot;Student Loan Debt&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2022&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;STEM Education&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:2625,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 22-335&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This InfoBrief examines the baccalaureate origins of doctorate recipients from 2010–20 by citizenship status, race, and ethnicity. Black, Hispanic, and AIAN doctorate recipients from 2010–20 disproportionately earned a bachelor’s degree from minority serving institutions, underscoring the importance of these colleges and universities for enhancing the diversity of the U.S. research enterprise. Although most Black and Hispanic doctorate recipients earned a bachelor’s degree from public colleges and universities, a higher proportion of Black and Hispanic students with undergraduate degrees from private colleges and universities went on to earn a research doctorate. Data from the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics’ Survey of Earned Doctorates were used to report the numbers of research doctorate recipients between 2010 and 2020, as well as their baccalaureate-origin institutions. Data from the Department of Education&#39;s Integrated Postsecondary Education Data System (IPEDS) Completions Survey provide the numbers of bachelor&#39;s degrees awarded between 2001 and 2011.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;08/09/2022&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf22335&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf22335&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2022.08.09&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Baccalaureate Origins of Underrepresented Minority Research Doctorate Recipients&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;By Country&quot;,&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Citizenship&quot;,&quot;Faculty Rank, Tenure Status&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Financial Support&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Degrees&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Enrollment&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Institutions&quot;,&quot;Minorities in Higher Education&quot;,&quot;New Construction&quot;,&quot;Race and Ethnicity&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Employment&quot;,&quot;Repairs and Renovations&quot;,&quot;Sex&quot;,&quot;State Government R&amp;D-related Facilities&quot;,&quot;Student Loan Debt&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2022&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:2585,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 22-334&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This InfoBrief provides current data on the United States’ 43 federally funded research and development centers (FFRDCs), privately operated research and development organizations that are exclusively or substantially financed by the federal government. In FY 2021, these FFRDCs spent $24.9 billion on R&amp;D, an annual increase of 6%, supported by a rise in federal R&amp;D support. Total FFRDC R&amp;D expenditures have seen an average annual constant dollar increase of almost 2% from 2012 to 2021. These data are drawn from the FY 2021 FFRDC Research and Development Survey, conducted by National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation. &quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;07/28/2022&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf22334&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf22334&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2022.07.28&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers Report 6% Increase in R&amp;D Spending in FY 2021&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;By Country&quot;,&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Citizenship&quot;,&quot;Faculty Rank, Tenure Status&quot;,&quot;Federal Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Federally Funded Research and Development Centers (FFRDC) R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Financial Support&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Degrees&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Enrollment&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;New Construction&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Cost Type&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Employment&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;,&quot;Repairs and Renovations&quot;,&quot;State Government R&amp;D-related Facilities&quot;,&quot;Student Loan Debt&quot;,&quot;Type of R&amp;D (basic, applied, experimental development)&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2022&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:2465,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 22-332&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;These tables provide data on R&amp;D expenditures for federally funded research and development centers (FFRDCs). Expenditures data are displayed for individual FFRDCs by source of funding and type of R&amp;D. Data are from the FY 2021 FFRDC Research and Development Survey, by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;07/28/2022&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf22332&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf22332&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2022.07.28&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Expenditures: Fiscal Year 2021&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Federal Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Federally Funded Research and Development Centers (FFRDC) R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Institutions&quot;,&quot;Nonprofit R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Cost Type&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;,&quot;State Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Type of R&amp;D (basic, applied, experimental development)&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2022&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering Workforce&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:2445,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 22-331&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The National Survey of College Graduates, conducted by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation, provides data on the characteristics of the nation&#39;s college graduates, with a focus on those in the science and engineering workforce. This survey is a unique source for examining the relationship between degree field and occupation, as well as examining other characteristics of college-educated individuals, including work activities, salary, and demographic information.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;07/14/2022&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf22331&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf22331&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2022.07.14&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;National Survey of College Graduates (NSCG)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Survey of College Graduates (NSCG)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Survey of College Graduates: 2017&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;By Country&quot;,&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Certifications and Licenses&quot;,&quot;Citizenship&quot;,&quot;Disability Status&quot;,&quot;Employment Sector&quot;,&quot;Employment Status&quot;,&quot;Faculty Rank, Tenure Status&quot;,&quot;Family Characteristics&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Financial Support&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Degrees&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Enrollment&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Income and Salary&quot;,&quot;Institutions&quot;,&quot;Job Satisfaction&quot;,&quot;K-12 STEM Education&quot;,&quot;Minorities in Higher Education&quot;,&quot;New Construction&quot;,&quot;Non-faculty Research Personnel&quot;,&quot;Occupation&quot;,&quot;Race and Ethnicity&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Employment&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Publications &amp; Articles&quot;,&quot;Repairs and Renovations&quot;,&quot;Sex&quot;,&quot;Skilled Technical Workforce&quot;,&quot;State Government R&amp;D-related Facilities&quot;,&quot;Student Loan Debt&quot;,&quot;Work Activity&quot;,&quot;Work-related Training&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2022&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering Workforce&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Working Paper&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:2425,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NCSES 22-207&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Person-centered approaches to data analysis can provide valuable information about the science and engineering workforce that cannot be obtained solely by using variable-centered approaches. The purpose of this working paper is to illustrate the use of a specific person-centered method (latent class analysis [LCA]) in a large, nationally representative data set (the 2017 Survey of Doctorate Recipients) to investigate the importance of and satisfaction with nine different job factors, including salary, benefits, and job security. The results of the analyses found that doctorate recipients belonged to one of five job-factor importance classes: (1) all nine job factors are very important; (2) all nine job factors are somewhat important; (3) a job’s benefits, salary, and security are more important than the job’s level of responsibility; (4) a job’s challenge and level of independence are more important than the job’s salary and benefits; and (5) a job’s opportunities for advancement are unimportant. A similar five classes were also found for self-reported satisfaction with each of the same nine job factors. Further analysis found several small, but potentially important, differences in the demographic composition of the importance and satisfaction classes. A congruence analysis showed that while the job factors rated as very important were often the same factors for which doctoral recipients reported the highest satisfaction, this relationship was far from perfect. In addition to discussing modeling assumptions, model selection, and the presentation and interpretation of model results, the paper highlights how the LCA results differ from variable-centered results and relate to existing job satisfaction theories. &quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;06/28/2022&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;ncses22207&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;ncses22207&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2022.06.28&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Job Importance versus Job Satisfaction: A Latent Class Analysis of the 2017 Survey of Doctorate Recipients&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Attitudes About Science&quot;,&quot;By Country&quot;,&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Citizenship&quot;,&quot;Disability Status&quot;,&quot;Employment Sector&quot;,&quot;Employment Status&quot;,&quot;Faculty Rank, Tenure Status&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Financial Support&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Degrees&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Enrollment&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Income and Salary&quot;,&quot;Job Satisfaction&quot;,&quot;New Construction&quot;,&quot;Occupation&quot;,&quot;Postdoctorates&quot;,&quot;Postgraduation Plans&quot;,&quot;Race and Ethnicity&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Employment&quot;,&quot;Repairs and Renovations&quot;,&quot;Sex&quot;,&quot;State Government R&amp;D-related Facilities&quot;,&quot;Student Loan Debt&quot;,&quot;Work Activity&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2022&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering Workforce&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Working Paper&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:2345,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NCSES 22-208&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Replication or reproducibility of results is a cornerstone of scientific research, as replication studies can identify artifacts that affect internal validity, investigate sampling error, increase generalizability, provide further testing of the original hypothesis, and evaluate claims of fraud. The purpose of the current working paper is to determine whether the five-class, three-response latent class solutions for job importance and job satisfaction found by Fritz (2022) using the 2017 cycle of the Survey of Doctorate Recipients data replicate using the 2015 and 2019 cycles data. A series of latent class analyses were conducted using the Mplus statistical software, which determined that the five-class, three-response solutions for job importance and job satisfaction were also the best models for the 2015 and 2019 data. In addition, the class prevalences and response probabilities were highly consistent across time, indicating that the interpretation of the latent classes is the same across time as well. All of this gives strong evidence that the 2017 results were successfully replicated in the 2015 and 2019 data, increasing confidence in the original results and also setting the stage for a future longitudinal investigation of the latent classes using latent transition analysis.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;06/28/2022&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;ncses22208&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;ncses22208&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2022.06.28&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Replicating the Job Importance and Job Satisfaction Latent Class Analysis from the 2017 Survey of Doctorate Recipients with the 2015 and 2019 Cycles&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Attitudes About Science&quot;,&quot;By Country&quot;,&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Citizenship&quot;,&quot;Employment Sector&quot;,&quot;Employment Status&quot;,&quot;Faculty Rank, Tenure Status&quot;,&quot;Financial Support&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Degrees&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Enrollment&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Income and Salary&quot;,&quot;Job Satisfaction&quot;,&quot;New Construction&quot;,&quot;Occupation&quot;,&quot;Race and Ethnicity&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Employment&quot;,&quot;Repairs and Renovations&quot;,&quot;Sex&quot;,&quot;State Government R&amp;D-related Facilities&quot;,&quot;Student Loan Debt&quot;,&quot;Work Activity&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2022&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Interactive&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering State Profiles&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering State Profiles&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:5568,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Updated data for Science and Engineering State Profiles are now available. This product provides state-level rankings and totals of science and engineering data for the 50 states, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico. The data tool allows users to compare state profiles for up to seven states or download the entire data set. Science and Engineering State Profiles is produced by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics of the National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;06/13/2022&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/states/download/state-profiles-2022-06.xlsx&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf00000&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2022.06.13&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering State Profiles: Spring 2022 Update&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2022&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering Workforce&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:2245,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 22-327&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Using the 2015–19 period from the ongoing Survey of Doctorate Recipients longitudinal panel (LSDR), conducted by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics (NCSES) within the National Science Foundation (NSF), this InfoBrief offers insights into the change dynamics among doctoral scientists and engineers, who are interviewed biennially. The LSDR is a new resource for analysis of labor force dynamics and other employment topics. In 2015, U.S.-trained doctoral scientists and engineers under age 66 participated in the labor force worldwide at a rate of 96%, which declined to 92% by 2019. &quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;06/01/2022&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf22327&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf22327&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2022.06.01&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Labor Force Transitions of U.S.-Trained Doctoral Scientists and Engineers: Findings from a New Longitudinal Panel&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Citizenship&quot;,&quot;Employment Sector&quot;,&quot;Employment Status&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Degrees&quot;,&quot;Institutions&quot;,&quot;Occupation&quot;,&quot;Postdoctorates&quot;,&quot;Race and Ethnicity&quot;,&quot;Sex&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2022&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering Workforce&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:2285,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 22-326&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR), conducted by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation, provides demographic, education, and career history information from individuals with a U.S. research doctoral degree in a science, engineering, or health (SEH) field. This report contains technical documentation for the longitudinal subsample of the 2015 SDR (the LSDR 2015–25 panel), which is designed to provide information about employment changes among the population of U.S.-trained science, engineering, and health (SEH) doctorate holders less than 65 years of age in 2015 over a 10-year period (2015–25). The first release of data from the LSDR 2015–25 panel includes survey data from three cycles of the SDR: 2015, 2017, and 2019. &quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;06/01/2022&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf22326&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf22326&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2022.06.01&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients, Longitudinal Data: 2015–19&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;By Country&quot;,&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Citizenship&quot;,&quot;Disability Status&quot;,&quot;Employment Sector&quot;,&quot;Employment Status&quot;,&quot;Faculty Rank, Tenure Status&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Financial Support&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Degrees&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Enrollment&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Institutions&quot;,&quot;Minorities in Higher Education&quot;,&quot;New Construction&quot;,&quot;Occupation&quot;,&quot;Race and Ethnicity&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Employment&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Publications &amp; Articles&quot;,&quot;Repairs and Renovations&quot;,&quot;Sex&quot;,&quot;State Government R&amp;D-related Facilities&quot;,&quot;Student Loan Debt&quot;,&quot;Work Activity&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2022&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:2385,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 22-330&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The U.S. research and development system consists of activities from a range of R&amp;D performers and sources of funding. These include the federal government, nonfederal governments, business, higher education institutions, nonprofit organizations, and private businesses. Organizations that perform R&amp;D often receive significant levels of outside funding, and organizations that fund R&amp;D may also themselves be performers. New data from surveys performed by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation indicate that R&amp;D performed in the United States totaled $67 billion in 2019. The estimate for 2020 is $708 billion.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;06/01/2022&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf22330&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf22330&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2022.06.01&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;U.S. R&amp;D increased by $62 billion in 2019, to $667 billion; estimate for 2020 indicates a further rise to $708 billion&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;By Country&quot;,&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Citizenship&quot;,&quot;Employment Sector&quot;,&quot;Faculty Rank, Tenure Status&quot;,&quot;Federal Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Federally Funded Research and Development Centers (FFRDC) R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Financial Support&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Degrees&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Enrollment&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Institutions&quot;,&quot;New Construction&quot;,&quot;Nonprofit R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Cost Type&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Employment&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Intensity&quot;,&quot;Repairs and Renovations&quot;,&quot;State Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;State Government R&amp;D-related Facilities&quot;,&quot;Student Loan Debt&quot;,&quot;Type of R&amp;D (basic, applied, experimental development)&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2022&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;STEM Education&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBytes&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:3710,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NCSES 22-210&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:null,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;05/31/2022&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;ncses22210&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;ncses22210&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2022.05.31&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Student Achievement in Science: Selected Results from the National Assessment of Educational Progress&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;K-12 STEM Education&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2022&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reference&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:2365,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NCSES 22-209&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This document provides definitions of research and development from U.S. and international sources. The first section (I) presents statistical definitions of R&amp;D from the Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development (OECD) Frascati Manual 2015: Guidelines for Collecting and Reporting Data on Research and Experimental Development. The next three sections are organized by sectors of the U.S. economy that perform or fund R&amp;D—businesses (II), federal and state governments (III), and academic and nonprofit organizations (IV). Sources for definitions of R&amp;D include the Office of Management and Budget (OMB), federal procurement, tax and accounting guidance, and surveys from the National Center for Science and  Engineering Statistics (NCSES) within the National Science Foundation (NSF). The last section (V) presents R&amp;D definitions from international statistical manuals on National Accounts and globalization.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;05/19/2022&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;ncses22209&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;ncses22209&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2022.05.19&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Definitions of Research and Development: An Annotated Compilation of  Official Sources&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;By Country&quot;,&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Federal Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Federally Funded Research and Development Centers (FFRDC) R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;New Construction&quot;,&quot;Nonprofit R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Patenting and Trademark Activities&quot;,&quot;Product &amp; Business Process Innovation&quot;,&quot;Production and Value Added&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Capital Expenditures&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Cost Type&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Agreement (contract, grant, reimbursement, etc.)&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Intensity&quot;,&quot;Repairs and Renovations&quot;,&quot;State Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;State Government R&amp;D-related Facilities&quot;,&quot;Type of R&amp;D (basic, applied, experimental development)&quot;,&quot;Work Activity&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2022&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering Workforce&quot;,&quot;Innovation and Global Competitiveness&quot;,&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Working Paper&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:2105,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NCSES 22-202&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The purpose of this working paper is to refine and evaluate the occupation-driven approach for analyzing regional invention suggested by Wojan, Dotzel, and Low (2015), who argue that patenting rates should be computed on the subset of workers that might plausibly contribute to patenting. We introduce several modifications to their original analysis, including use of a special tabulation of more detailed occupation data and added controls for regional characteristics that could confound identification of the inventive class—the group of occupations most associated with patent production. We then critically evaluate the findings by implementing a simultaneous equations model of the interrelationship between the composition and productivity of the inventive class. Substitutions of alternative subpopulations of knowledge workers such as the college-educated population for the inventive class allow us to assess the value added of the updated inventive class construct. Simultaneous equations results confirm the existence of an interrelationship between patent production and membership in the inventive class in rural regions and highlight an apparent disarticulation of inventive employment and patent-intensive manufacturing employment in urban areas.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;05/06/2022&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;ncses22202&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;ncses22202&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2022.05.06&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;An Occupational Approach to Analyzing Regional Invention&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Employment Status&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Degrees&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Income and Salary&quot;,&quot;Institutions&quot;,&quot;Knowledge and Technology Intensive (KTI) Industries&quot;,&quot;Occupation&quot;,&quot;Patenting and Trademark Activities&quot;,&quot;Product &amp; Business Process Innovation&quot;,&quot;Production and Value Added&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Employment&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Publications &amp; Articles&quot;,&quot;Sex&quot;,&quot;Work Activity&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2022&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;STEM Education&quot;,&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reports&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering Indicators&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering Indicators&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:2305,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSB-2022-7&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Public perceptions of science and technology in the United States affect many aspects of civic life. This report provides insights into public confidence in science, understanding of scientific research principles, and Americans’ engagement with science through media, scientific activities, or children’s science activities. Confidence in science remains high among Americans: 89% of Americans believe scientists work to make life better for the average person. During the COVID-19 pandemic, U.S. adults tended to think more often about the impact of science in their everyday life than they had before the pandemic.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;05/04/2022&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsb20227&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsb20227&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2022.05.04&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Technology: Public Perceptions, Awareness, and Information Sources&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Attitudes About Science&quot;,&quot;By Country&quot;,&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Citizenship&quot;,&quot;Clinical Trials and Medical Projects&quot;,&quot;COVID-19 Pandemic Impacts&quot;,&quot;Faculty Rank, Tenure Status&quot;,&quot;Family Characteristics&quot;,&quot;Federal Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Financial Support&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Degrees&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Enrollment&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Income and Salary&quot;,&quot;K-12 STEM Education&quot;,&quot;New Construction&quot;,&quot;Race and Ethnicity&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Employment&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;,&quot;Repairs and Renovations&quot;,&quot;Sex&quot;,&quot;State Government R&amp;D-related Facilities&quot;,&quot;Student Loan Debt&quot;,&quot;Type of R&amp;D (basic, applied, experimental development)&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2022&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:2287,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 22-329&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The detailed statistical tables in this report present data on domestic and global research and development expenditures and the R&amp;D workforce of for-profit, nonfarm businesses with 10 or more paid employees operating in the 50 U.S. states and the District of Columbia. These data were collected by the Business Enterprise Research and Development Survey (BERD), formally called the Business Research and Development Survey, conducted annually by the Census Bureau for the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation. These data are used by government agencies, corporations, research organizations, and academics to assess trends in the performance and funding of R&amp;D.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;04/28/2022&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf22329&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf22329&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2022.04.28&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development: 2019&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;By Country&quot;,&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Citizenship&quot;,&quot;Clinical Trials and Medical Projects&quot;,&quot;Employment Sector&quot;,&quot;Employment Status&quot;,&quot;Faculty Rank, Tenure Status&quot;,&quot;Federal Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Federally Funded Research and Development Centers (FFRDC) R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Financial Support&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Degrees&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Enrollment&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Income and Salary&quot;,&quot;Institutions&quot;,&quot;Knowledge and Technology Intensive (KTI) Industries&quot;,&quot;New Construction&quot;,&quot;Occupation&quot;,&quot;Production and Value Added&quot;,&quot;Race and Ethnicity&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Capital Expenditures&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Cost Type&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Employment&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Agreement (contract, grant, reimbursement, etc.)&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Intensity&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Publications &amp; Articles&quot;,&quot;Repairs and Renovations&quot;,&quot;Sex&quot;,&quot;Skilled Technical Workforce&quot;,&quot;State Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;State Government R&amp;D-related Facilities&quot;,&quot;Student Loan Debt&quot;,&quot;Type of R&amp;D (basic, applied, experimental development)&quot;,&quot;Work Activity&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2022&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:2286,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 22-323&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This set of detailed tables present data on the research and development outlays and obligations of federal agencies. Outlays are provided by agency for total R&amp;D and total R&amp;D plant. Obligations are provided for R&amp;D by agency for type of work (basic research, applied research, experimental development), field of science or engineering (for research only), type of performer, and geographic area. Obligations are provided for R&amp;D plant by agency for type of performer and geographic area. These data, collected by the Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development from the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics, are the primary source of information about federal funding for R&amp;D in the United States.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;04/28/2022&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf22323&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf22323&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2022.04.28&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Funds for Research and Development: Fiscal Years 2020–21&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;By Country&quot;,&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Citizenship&quot;,&quot;COVID-19 Pandemic Impacts&quot;,&quot;Disability Status&quot;,&quot;Faculty Rank, Tenure Status&quot;,&quot;Federal Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Federally Funded Research and Development Centers (FFRDC) R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Financial Support&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Degrees&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Enrollment&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Income and Salary&quot;,&quot;Institutions&quot;,&quot;New Construction&quot;,&quot;Nonprofit R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Race and Ethnicity&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Capital Expenditures&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Cost Type&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Employment&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Agreement (contract, grant, reimbursement, etc.)&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;,&quot;Repairs and Renovations&quot;,&quot;State Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;State Government R&amp;D-related Facilities&quot;,&quot;Student Loan Debt&quot;,&quot;Type of R&amp;D (basic, applied, experimental development)&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2022&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:2307,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 22-328&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;In 2019, U.S.-located businesses reported almost $105 billion in foreign research and development performance. This InfoBrief discusses both the geographic and industry distribution of foreign R&amp;D and employment reported by U.S.-located businesses, including R&amp;D performed by foreign affiliates of U.S. multinational enterprises, focusing particularly on IT-related industries in both manufacturing and nonmanufacturing. Data were from the according to estimates from the Business Enterprise Research and Development survey, conducted by the Census Bureau in partnership with the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;04/28/2022&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf22328&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf22328&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2022.04.28&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Foreign R&amp;D Reported by IT-Related Industries Account for About Half or More of U.S.-Owned R&amp;D Performed in India, China, Canada, and Israel&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;By Country&quot;,&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Citizenship&quot;,&quot;Faculty Rank, Tenure Status&quot;,&quot;Federal Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Financial Support&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Degrees&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Enrollment&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Knowledge and Technology Intensive (KTI) Industries&quot;,&quot;New Construction&quot;,&quot;Production and Value Added&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Employment&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Intensity&quot;,&quot;Repairs and Renovations&quot;,&quot;State Government R&amp;D-related Facilities&quot;,&quot;Student Loan Debt&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2022&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reports&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering Indicators&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering Indicators&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:2265,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSB 2022-5&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Investment in R&amp;D is essential for a country’s success in the global economy and for its ability to address challenges and opportunities in diverse societal areas such as health, environment, and national security. This report analyzes trends in U.S. R&amp;D performance and funding, both domestically and within a global context. The growth of U.S. total R&amp;D has accelerated in the most recent years, due mainly to sizable increases in business R&amp;D. Globally, the United States remains the largest R&amp;D performer; however, the global concentration of R&amp;D continues to shift from the United States and Europe to East-Southeast and South Asia.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;04/28/2022&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsb20225&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsb20225&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2022.04.28&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Research and Development: U.S. Trends and International Comparisons&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;By Country&quot;,&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Citizenship&quot;,&quot;Employment Sector&quot;,&quot;Faculty Rank, Tenure Status&quot;,&quot;Federal Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Federally Funded Research and Development Centers (FFRDC) R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Financial Support&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Degrees&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Enrollment&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Knowledge and Technology Intensive (KTI) Industries&quot;,&quot;New Construction&quot;,&quot;Nonprofit R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Product &amp; Business Process Innovation&quot;,&quot;Production and Value Added&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Capital Expenditures&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Cost Type&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Employment&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Intensity&quot;,&quot;Repairs and Renovations&quot;,&quot;State Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;State Government R&amp;D-related Facilities&quot;,&quot;Student Loan Debt&quot;,&quot;Type of R&amp;D (basic, applied, experimental development)&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2022&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Innovation and Global Competitiveness&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Working Paper&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:2226,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NCSES 22-206&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;On May 5, 2020, the National Science Board (NSB) released its Vision 2030 report (NSB 2020), which lays out four central priorities for the National Science Foundation this decade. One such priority, “expand the geography of innovation,” envisions innovation hubs operating in every state to help retain the nation’s leadership in the science and engineering enterprise. This working paper uses the very large sample size of the 2017 Annual Business Survey (N ≈ 850,000) to produce substate-level estimates of self-reported innovation rates. The analysis provides a baseline for assessing efforts to actively seed and nurture innovation clusters throughout the country, identifying areas that are exceeding expectations and others that may be falling behind. Because the concentration of innovation-intensive industry varies across the country, a relative innovation rate that controls for the innovation rate expected from a region’s industrial structure is provided. The analysis is descriptive and does not examine possible explanations of differences in regional innovation rates. &quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;04/20/2022&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;ncses22206&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;ncses22206&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2022.04.20&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Annual Business Survey (ABS)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Annual Business Survey (ABS)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;The Geography of Self-Reported Innovation: Results from the 2017 Annual Business Survey&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;By Country&quot;,&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Institutions&quot;,&quot;Knowledge and Technology Intensive (KTI) Industries&quot;,&quot;Patenting and Trademark Activities&quot;,&quot;Product &amp; Business Process Innovation&quot;,&quot;Production and Value Added&quot;,&quot;Race and Ethnicity&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Employment&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2022&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Innovation and Global Competitiveness&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reports&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering Indicators&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering Indicators&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:2225,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSB-2022-6&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Knowledge- and technology-intensive (KTI) industries produce innovative products and technologies that are essential for economic growth and for addressing diverse social challenges from health to national security. This report analyzes data on U.S. KTI production, trade, and two enabling technologies: biotechnology and artificial intelligence (AI). The United States is the largest global producer of KTI services. U.S. KTI exports rely less on foreign production than do those from other countries. The United States also leads in many aspects of biotechnology and AI, although other countries are rapidly building these capabilities.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;04/19/2022&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsb20226&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsb20226&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2022.04.19&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Production and Trade of Knowledge- and Technology-Intensive Industries&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Amount of Research Space&quot;,&quot;Business R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;By Country&quot;,&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Citizenship&quot;,&quot;Employment Sector&quot;,&quot;Employment Status&quot;,&quot;Faculty Rank, Tenure Status&quot;,&quot;Federal Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Financial Support&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Degrees&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Enrollment&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Institutions&quot;,&quot;Knowledge and Technology Intensive (KTI) Industries&quot;,&quot;New Construction&quot;,&quot;Occupation&quot;,&quot;Patenting and Trademark Activities&quot;,&quot;Product &amp; Business Process Innovation&quot;,&quot;Production and Value Added&quot;,&quot;Race and Ethnicity&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Employment&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Intensity&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Publications &amp; Articles&quot;,&quot;Repairs and Renovations&quot;,&quot;Sex&quot;,&quot;Skilled Technical Workforce&quot;,&quot;State Government R&amp;D-related Facilities&quot;,&quot;Student Loan Debt&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2022&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;STEM Education&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:1985,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 22-317&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;According to the Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS), there have been shifts in enrollment of both U.S. citizen and permanent resident and temporary visa–holding graduate students within science, engineering, and health fields. The GSS, sponsored by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation, found that from 2019 to 2020, master’s enrollment increased, while doctoral enrollment remained relatively stable. Meanwhile, the number of postdoctoral researchers and nonfaculty researchers declined between 2019 and 2020. Despite these changes, a record 697,813 graduate students were reported to GSS for 2020.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;04/05/2022&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf22317&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf22317&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2022.04.05&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Assessing the Impact of COVID-19 on Science, Engineering, and Health Graduate Enrollment: U.S. Part-Time Enrollment Increases as Full-Time Temporary Visa Holder Enrollment Declines&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;By Country&quot;,&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Citizenship&quot;,&quot;COVID-19 Pandemic Impacts&quot;,&quot;Faculty Rank, Tenure Status&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Financial Support&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Degrees&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Enrollment&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Institutions&quot;,&quot;New Construction&quot;,&quot;Non-faculty Research Personnel&quot;,&quot;Occupation&quot;,&quot;Postdoctorates&quot;,&quot;Race and Ethnicity&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Employment&quot;,&quot;Repairs and Renovations&quot;,&quot;Sex&quot;,&quot;State Government R&amp;D-related Facilities&quot;,&quot;Student Loan Debt&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2022&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;STEM Education&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:2145,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 22-319&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report presents data from the 2020 Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering, sponsored by National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation and by the National Institutes of Health. The tables show trends within science, engineering, and selected health fields on graduate student enrollment, postdoc appointments, and other doctorate-holding nonfaculty researcher (NFR) appointments, as well as counts by selected characteristics, such as sex, race and ethnicity, citizenship, field of study, and primary source and mechanism of support.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;04/05/2022&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf22319&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf22319&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2022.04.05&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering: Fall 2020&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Amount of Research Space&quot;,&quot;By Country&quot;,&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Citizenship&quot;,&quot;Clinical Trials and Medical Projects&quot;,&quot;COVID-19 Pandemic Impacts&quot;,&quot;Disability Status&quot;,&quot;Faculty Rank, Tenure Status&quot;,&quot;Federal Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Financial Support&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Degrees&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Enrollment&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Institutions&quot;,&quot;Minorities in Higher Education&quot;,&quot;New Construction&quot;,&quot;Non-faculty Research Personnel&quot;,&quot;Occupation&quot;,&quot;Postdoctorates&quot;,&quot;Postgraduation Plans&quot;,&quot;Race and Ethnicity&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Employment&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Publications &amp; Articles&quot;,&quot;Repairs and Renovations&quot;,&quot;Sex&quot;,&quot;State Government R&amp;D-related Facilities&quot;,&quot;Student Loan Debt&quot;,&quot;Work-related Training&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2022&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Innovation and Global Competitiveness&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:2185,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 22-325&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;During 2016–18, nearly a third of the estimated 4.8 million for-profit companies with at least one employee introduced an innovation. Per Oslo Manual 2018, innovation is a new or improved product, which can be a good or a service, as well as a new or improved business process—this definition has changed since the publication of the 2005 Oslo Manual. This InfoBrief provides how the changing definition of innovation has impacted the Annual Business Survey, which provides a comprehensive view of the incidences of innovation in the United States. &quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;03/31/2022&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf22325&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf22325&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2022.03.31&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Annual Business Survey (ABS)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Annual Business Survey (ABS)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Innovation Data from the 2019 Annual Business Survey&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;By Country&quot;,&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Citizenship&quot;,&quot;Faculty Rank, Tenure Status&quot;,&quot;Financial Support&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Degrees&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Enrollment&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;New Construction&quot;,&quot;Product &amp; Business Process Innovation&quot;,&quot;Race and Ethnicity&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Employment&quot;,&quot;Repairs and Renovations&quot;,&quot;Sex&quot;,&quot;State Government R&amp;D-related Facilities&quot;,&quot;Student Loan Debt&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2022&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Innovation and Global Competitiveness&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Working Paper&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:1965,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NCSES 22-201&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Innovation is typically studied within businesses, but other sectors of the economy, such as governments and the household sector, can also undertake innovation activity. In order to fully understand innovation at the economy and society level, innovation must be understood beyond the business sector. However, there are numerous challenges to understanding non-business innovation. Individual innovation in particular is assumed to be relatively rare in the general population and thus expensive to measure using probability-based samples. At the same time, relatively few studies have examined individual innovation using non-probability samples. The lack of research on individual innovation leads to uncertainty about the best ways of asking questions related to individual innovation or which topics to prioritize if a probability-based sample is ever used to estimate individual innovation rates. In 2019, NCSES conducted a study of innovation at the individual level using Amazon Mechanical Turk (MTurk) for recruiting respondents and administrating the survey. &quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;03/31/2022&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;ncses22201&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;ncses22201&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2022.03.31&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Using Mechanical Turk in Study of Individual Innovation&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Attitudes About Science&quot;,&quot;By Country&quot;,&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Citizenship&quot;,&quot;Disability Status&quot;,&quot;Federal Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Degrees&quot;,&quot;Institutions&quot;,&quot;Minorities in Higher Education&quot;,&quot;Occupation&quot;,&quot;Patenting and Trademark Activities&quot;,&quot;Product &amp; Business Process Innovation&quot;,&quot;Race and Ethnicity&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Employment&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;,&quot;Sex&quot;,&quot;Technology Transfer Activities&quot;,&quot;Work Activity&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2022&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:2165,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 22-324&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Federal obligations for research and experimental development rose nearly 18% from FY 2019 to FY 2020, reaching $167.4 billion. This is the largest annual increase in current dollars since 1963. Obligations for research and experimental development from stimulus funding totaled $19.5 billion. These data are from the latest edition of the Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development, sponsored by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;03/24/2022&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf22324&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf22324&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2022.03.24&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Driven by Stimulus Funding, Federal R&amp;D Obligations Increased 18% in 2020; Largest Year-to-Year Change since 1963&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;By Country&quot;,&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Citizenship&quot;,&quot;COVID-19 Pandemic Impacts&quot;,&quot;Employment Sector&quot;,&quot;Faculty Rank, Tenure Status&quot;,&quot;Federal Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Federally Funded Research and Development Centers (FFRDC) R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Financial Support&quot;,&quot;Higher Educati