20%) as a type of IP protection that is viewed as very important (9%) or somewhat important (11%) to their business. The ABS is conducted by the Census Bureau in partnership with the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation. &quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;10/15/2021&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf22305&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf22305&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2021.10.15&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Annual Business Survey (ABS)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Annual Business Survey (ABS)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Nondisclosure Agreements, Trade Secrets, and Trademarks Considered Very Important to More U.S. Businesses than Were Patents or Copyrights in 2017&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;By Country&quot;,&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Citizenship&quot;,&quot;Faculty Rank, Tenure Status&quot;,&quot;Financial Support&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Degrees&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Enrollment&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Knowledge and Technology Intensive (KTI) Industries&quot;,&quot;New Construction&quot;,&quot;Patenting and Trademark Activities&quot;,&quot;Product &amp; Business Process Innovation&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Employment&quot;,&quot;Repairs and Renovations&quot;,&quot;State Government R&amp;D-related Facilities&quot;,&quot;Student Loan Debt&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2021&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:1625,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 22-302&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This InfoBrief provides current data on the United States’ 42 federally funded research and development centers (FFRDCs), privately operated R&amp;D organizations that are exclusively or substantially financed by the federal government. In FY 2020, these FFRDCs spent $23.5 billion on R&amp;D, an annual increase of more than 3% supported by a rise in federal R&amp;D support. Total FFRDC R&amp;D expenditures have seen an average annual constant dollar increase of almost 1% from 2011 to 2020. These data are drawn from the FY 2020 FFRDC Research and Development Survey, conducted by National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation. &quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;10/12/2021&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf22302&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf22302&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2021.10.12&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers Report 3% Increase in R&amp;D Spending in FY 2020&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;By Country&quot;,&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Citizenship&quot;,&quot;Faculty Rank, Tenure Status&quot;,&quot;Federal Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Federally Funded Research and Development Centers (FFRDC) R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Financial Support&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Degrees&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Enrollment&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Institutions&quot;,&quot;New Construction&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Employment&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;,&quot;Repairs and Renovations&quot;,&quot;State Government R&amp;D-related Facilities&quot;,&quot;Student Loan Debt&quot;,&quot;Type of R&amp;D (basic, applied, experimental development)&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2021&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:1525,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 22-304&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;These tables provide data on R&amp;D expenditures for federally funded research and development centers (FFRDCs). Expenditures data are displayed for individually named FFRDCs, by source of funding, and type of R&amp;D. Data are from the FY 2020 FFRDC Research and Development Survey, by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;10/12/2021&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf22304&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf22304&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2021.10.12&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Expenditures: Fiscal Year 2020&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;By Country&quot;,&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Citizenship&quot;,&quot;Faculty Rank, Tenure Status&quot;,&quot;Federal Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Federally Funded Research and Development Centers (FFRDC) R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Financial Support&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Degrees&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Enrollment&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Institutions&quot;,&quot;New Construction&quot;,&quot;Nonprofit R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Capital Expenditures&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Cost Type&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Employment&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;,&quot;Repairs and Renovations&quot;,&quot;State Government R&amp;D-related Facilities&quot;,&quot;Student Loan Debt&quot;,&quot;Type of R&amp;D (basic, applied, experimental development)&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2021&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering Workforce&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoChart&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:1584,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 22-306&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Most employed early career stayers (86%) were working in a science and engineering (S&amp;E) occupation in 2017. An early career stayer is defined as an S&amp;E doctorate recipient who held a temporary visa at graduation from a U.S. academic institution (graduated during 2006–15), were at the early stage of their career, and were living in the United States in 2017. Another 7% of these employed early career stayers with S&amp;E doctorates worked in S&amp;E-related occupations (e.g., healthcare practitioners, S&amp;E managers, and S&amp;E technicians and technologists). Data are from surveys by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;10/12/2021&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf22306&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf22306&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2021.10.12&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Most Foreign-Born S&amp;E Doctorate Recipients Who Stay in the United States after Graduation Work in S&amp;E Occupations&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Citizenship&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Degrees&quot;,&quot;Occupation&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2021&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Working Paper&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:5381,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NCSES 21-202&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The nonprofit sector is one of four major sectors of the economy, along with business, government, and higher education, that performs or funds research and development. The FY 2016 Nonprofit Research Activities Survey marks the first nationally representative collection of R&amp;D data from the nonprofit sector in more than 20 years. This working paper presents the results of that effort, followed by a discussion of the methodology, alternative estimates to help assess the accuracy of results, and lessons learned that can inform future data collections. The survey was conducted by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/21/2021&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2021/ncses21202/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;ncses21202&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2021.09.21&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Nonprofit Research Activities (NPRA) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Nonprofit Research Activities (NPRA) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;FY 2016 Nonprofit Research Activities Survey: Summary of Methodology, Assessment of Quality, and Synopsis of Results&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2021&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reports&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering Indicators&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering Indicators&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:1464,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSB-2021-3&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report examines R&amp;D performed by U.S. higher education institutions. It discusses funding by source and S&amp;E field for R&amp;D activities and equipment and provides information on research space. It benchmarks the United States against other nations, and it looks at how academic R&amp;D investments relate to investments in education and training of students and postdoctoral researchers. Academic R&amp;D remains focused on basic research. Most of it is performed by a small percentage of U.S. higher education institutions. The federal government funds over half of academic R&amp;D, although institutional support represents an increasing share.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/14/2021&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsb20213&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsb20213&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2021.09.14&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Academic Research and Development&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Amount of Research Space&quot;,&quot;Business R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;By Country&quot;,&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Citizenship&quot;,&quot;Clinical Trials and Medical Projects&quot;,&quot;COVID-19 Pandemic Impacts&quot;,&quot;Faculty Rank, Tenure Status&quot;,&quot;Federal Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Financial Support&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Degrees&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Enrollment&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Income and Salary&quot;,&quot;Institutions&quot;,&quot;Minorities in Higher Education&quot;,&quot;New Construction&quot;,&quot;Nonprofit R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Postdoctorates&quot;,&quot;Product &amp; Business Process Innovation&quot;,&quot;Race and Ethnicity&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Capital Expenditures&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Cost Type&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Employment&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Agreement (contract, grant, reimbursement, etc.)&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Intensity&quot;,&quot;Repairs and Renovations&quot;,&quot;Sex&quot;,&quot;State Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;State Government R&amp;D-related Facilities&quot;,&quot;Student Loan Debt&quot;,&quot;Type of R&amp;D (basic, applied, experimental development)&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2021&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Innovation and Global Competitiveness&quot;,&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:1544,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 21-339&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The Business Research and Development Survey (BRDS), sponsored by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics (NCSES) within the National Science Foundation, provides data on R&amp;D expenditures and R&amp;D employees for for-profit, publicly or privately held, nonfarm business in the United States that performed or funded R&amp;D either domestically or abroad. This InfoBrief examines how BRDS asked respondents to report the importance to their company of several types of intellectual property (IP) protection—including utility patents, design patents, trademarks, copyrights, mask works, and trade secrets. More companies viewed trade secrets as important than any other type of IP protection, with more than half of U.S. businesses that performed or funded R&amp;D reporting trade secrets as very important to their company in 2018 and more than three-fourths reporting them as very or somewhat important.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/02/2021&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf21339&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf21339&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2021.09.02&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Business Research and Development Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business Research and Development Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Three-Quarters of U.S. Businesses that Performed or Funded R&amp;D Viewed Trade Secrets as Important in 2018&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;By Country&quot;,&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Citizenship&quot;,&quot;Faculty Rank, Tenure Status&quot;,&quot;Federal Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Financial Support&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Degrees&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Enrollment&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;New Construction&quot;,&quot;Patenting and Trademark Activities&quot;,&quot;Product &amp; Business Process Innovation&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Employment&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;,&quot;Repairs and Renovations&quot;,&quot;State Government R&amp;D-related Facilities&quot;,&quot;Student Loan Debt&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2021&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Working Paper&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:5374,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NCSES 21-201&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Most studies of innovation that examine determinants of success or failure look exclusively at the factors that make innovation successful. To better understand causes and patterns of unsuccessful or failed innovation within firms, we conducted 19 case studies of failure from the past 20 years. These cases draw primarily from coverage by the popular and trade presses and highlight clear models of failure. This paper examines all aspects of the product or business process life cycle and found that innovation failure typically revolves around firms’ inadequate pre-launch development activities, which lead to products that do not function properly or in which the marketplace has no interest. There is a gap between innovation, market-specific product launch, and successful innovation as typically presented in the literature. This paper exploits this gap and identifies those products and processes that are introduced but fail to achieve widespread adoption as unsuccessful innovation or innovation failures.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/02/2021&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2021/ncses21201/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;ncses21201&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2021.09.02&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Understanding Unsuccessful Innovation&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2021&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering Workforce&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reports&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering Indicators&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering Indicators&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:1428,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSB-2021-2&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Individuals in the STEM workforce make important contributions to improve a nation’s living standards, economic growth, and global competitiveness. This report details several aspects of the U.S. STEM workforce, including growth, demographic makeup, earnings, and unemployment. The STEM workforce is made of occupations that require STEM skills and expertise, including occupations not typically considered STEM fields (e.g., maintenance and repair, construction trades, and production occupations). In 2019, the STEM workforce represented 23% of the total U.S. workforce, and over half of this workforce does not have a bachelor’s degree.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;08/31/2021&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsb20212&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsb20212&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2021.08.31&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;The STEM Labor Force of Today: Scientists, Engineers, and Skilled Technical Workers&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;By Country&quot;,&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Certifications and Licenses&quot;,&quot;Citizenship&quot;,&quot;COVID-19 Pandemic Impacts&quot;,&quot;Employment Sector&quot;,&quot;Employment Status&quot;,&quot;Faculty Rank, Tenure Status&quot;,&quot;Federal Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Financial Support&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Degrees&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Enrollment&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Income and Salary&quot;,&quot;Minorities in Higher Education&quot;,&quot;New Construction&quot;,&quot;Occupation&quot;,&quot;Postdoctorates&quot;,&quot;Postgraduation Plans&quot;,&quot;Product &amp; Business Process Innovation&quot;,&quot;Race and Ethnicity&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Employment&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;,&quot;Repairs and Renovations&quot;,&quot;Sex&quot;,&quot;Skilled Technical Workforce&quot;,&quot;State Government R&amp;D-related Facilities&quot;,&quot;Student Loan Debt&quot;,&quot;Work Activity&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2021&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering Workforce&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:1484,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 21-336&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Foreign-born workers are a growing part of the U.S. science and engineering (S&amp;E) workforce, and they earned about one-third of S&amp;E doctorate degrees awarded by U.S. institutions in 2017. Most of these S&amp;E doctorate recipients remained in the United States for employment after graduation (72%), and many become U.S. citizens or permanent residents (67%). China and India were the two largest source countries for U.S.-trained S&amp;E doctorate holders with temporary visas at graduation. At least half of the early career S&amp;E doctorate holders with temporary visas at graduation who were in the United States in 2017 (early career stayers) worked in the same field as their doctorate. Data were from the Survey of Earned Doctorates and the Survey of Doctorate Recipients.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;08/19/2021&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf21336&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf21336&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2021.08.19&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Where Are They Now? Most Early Career U.S.-Trained S&amp;E Doctorate Recipients with Temporary Visas at Graduation Stay and Work in the United States after Graduation&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;By Country&quot;,&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Citizenship&quot;,&quot;Employment Sector&quot;,&quot;Employment Status&quot;,&quot;Faculty Rank, Tenure Status&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Financial Support&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Degrees&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Enrollment&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Institutions&quot;,&quot;New Construction&quot;,&quot;Occupation&quot;,&quot;Postdoctorates&quot;,&quot;Postgraduation Plans&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Employment&quot;,&quot;Repairs and Renovations&quot;,&quot;State Government R&amp;D-related Facilities&quot;,&quot;Student Loan Debt&quot;,&quot;Work Activity&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2021&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering Workforce&quot;,&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:1466,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 21-335&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The National Survey of College Graduates (NSCG), sponsored by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics (NCSES) within the National Science Foundation, provides data on the characteristics of U.S. college graduates, with a focus on those in the science and engineering workforce. Workers engaged in basic research, applied research, and experimental development (R&amp;D) as a primary work activity (RD1) is the new NCSES headline measure for &lt;i&gt;R&amp;D workers&lt;/i&gt;. This InfoBrief examines how data from the NSCG enables this recommended measure—which excludes design and R&amp;D as a secondary activity, aligning more closely with international guidance—as well as broader approaches to measuring R&amp;D workers.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;08/09/2021&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf21335&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf21335&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2021.08.09&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;National Survey of College Graduates (NSCG)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Survey of College Graduates (NSCG)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Measuring R&amp;D Workers Using NCSES Statistics&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;By Country&quot;,&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Citizenship&quot;,&quot;Employment Sector&quot;,&quot;Faculty Rank, Tenure Status&quot;,&quot;Financial Support&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Degrees&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Enrollment&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;New Construction&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Employment&quot;,&quot;Repairs and Renovations&quot;,&quot;State Government R&amp;D-related Facilities&quot;,&quot;Student Loan Debt&quot;,&quot;Type of R&amp;D (basic, applied, experimental development)&quot;,&quot;Work Activity&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2021&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Innovation and Global Competitiveness&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:1445,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 21-334&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;More than two-fifths (43%) of the estimated 4.6 million for-profit companies with at least one employee introduced an innovation during 2015–17. Innovation is defined as the implementation of one or more new or significantly improved products or processes, a new marketing method, or a new organizational method. Eighteen percent of these companies introduced one or more product innovations, 16% introduced one or more process innovations, 23% introduced a marketing innovation, and 26% introduced an organizational innovation. Data are from the 2017 Annual Business Survey, which provides the first comprehensive view of the incidence of innovation by businesses located in the United States.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;07/28/2021&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf21334&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf21334&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2021.07.28&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Annual Business Survey (ABS)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Annual Business Survey (ABS)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;First Comprehensive Innovation Survey for the United States: Data from the 2017 Annual Business Survey&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;By Country&quot;,&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Citizenship&quot;,&quot;Faculty Rank, Tenure Status&quot;,&quot;Financial Support&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Degrees&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Enrollment&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;New Construction&quot;,&quot;Product &amp; Business Process Innovation&quot;,&quot;Race and Ethnicity&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Employment&quot;,&quot;Repairs and Renovations&quot;,&quot;Sex&quot;,&quot;State Government R&amp;D-related Facilities&quot;,&quot;Student Loan Debt&quot;,&quot;Technology Transfer Activities&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2021&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;Research and Development Funding and Performance&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:1427,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 21-332&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;In FY 2019, federal agency obligations to universities and colleges for science and engineering (S&amp;E) activities increased to a historical high of $38.1 billion in current dollars, up 8% from FY 2018. During the same period, obligations for S&amp;E support to historically Black colleges and universities (HBCUs) increased at a slower rate, up 7% between FY 2018 and FY 2019, to $341 million. Although the past 2 fiscal years show increased federal obligations for S&amp;E to HBCUs, there is still an overall long-term decline since the early 2000s. Estimates are from the FY 2019 Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions, conducted by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;07/15/2021&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsf21332&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf21332&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2021.07.15&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Growth in Federal S&amp;E Support to HBCUs Continue to Lag Behind Increases to All Institutions in FY 2019&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;By Country&quot;,&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Citizenship&quot;,&quot;Faculty Rank, Tenure Status&quot;,&quot;Federal Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Federally Funded Research and Development Centers (FFRDC) R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Financial Support&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Degrees&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Enrollment&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&q