:&quot;2019&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:5284,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 19-325&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Small businesses contribute to the U.S. economy by bringing growth and innovation and by being more adaptable to changes in the economy. Although extensive research has been done on small businesses—sometimes defined as fewer than 250 or 500 employees—very little is known about microbusinesses, businesses with one to four employees. This InfoBrief provides new information on microbusinesses and their research and development activities, strategies, and drivers of competitiveness. Data are from the Microbusiness R&amp;D and Innovation Survey, developed and cosponsored by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation and by the U.S. Census Bureau.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/05/2019&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2019/nsf19325/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf19325&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2019.09.05&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey - Microbusiness (BRDI-M)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey - Microbusiness (BRDI-M)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Microbusinesses Spent $4.8 Billion on R&amp;D Performance in the United States in 2016, According to New Survey&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2019&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;STEM Education&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reports&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering Indicators&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering Indicators&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:403,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSB-2019-6&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report presents national trends in K–12 student achievement and compares U.S. student performance with that of other nations. It explores mathematics and science knowledge from kindergarten to fifth grade, followed by STEM achievement for eighth graders, finding differences across socioeconomic status. International comparisons show that U.S. students score in the middle of advanced economies. The report also finds certain STEM coursetaking in high school relates to the choice of a STEM major in college or to entering the skilled technical workforce among students who transition directly to work.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/04/2019&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsb20196&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsb20196&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2019.09.04&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Elementary and Secondary Mathematics and Science Education&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;Attitudes About Science&quot;,&quot;By Country&quot;,&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Certifications and Licenses&quot;,&quot;Citizenship&quot;,&quot;Employment Status&quot;,&quot;Faculty Rank, Tenure Status&quot;,&quot;Family Characteristics&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Financial Support&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Degrees&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Enrollment&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Income and Salary&quot;,&quot;Institutions&quot;,&quot;K-12 STEM Education&quot;,&quot;Minorities in Higher Education&quot;,&quot;New Construction&quot;,&quot;Occupation&quot;,&quot;Product &amp; Business Process Innovation&quot;,&quot;Race and Ethnicity&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Employment&quot;,&quot;Repairs and Renovations&quot;,&quot;Sex&quot;,&quot;Skilled Technical Workforce&quot;,&quot;State Government R&amp;D-related Facilities&quot;,&quot;Student Loan Debt&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2019&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[&quot;STEM Education&quot;],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reports&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering Indicators&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering Indicators&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:404,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSB-2019-7&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report examines trends in U.S. S&amp;E higher education within a global context. It contains information on U.S. higher education institutions, including S&amp;E degree awards, student demographics, cost, and debt. S&amp;E fields have grown at all degree levels. Yet many groups of Americans remain underrepresented among degree recipients. The United States continues to attract the most international students worldwide, but the number enrolled in U.S. institutions has declined over the past 2 years. China has rapidly increased the number of S&amp;E degrees awarded, compared with more moderate rises in the United States and the European Union.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/04/2019&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;nsb20197&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsb20197&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2019.09.04&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education in Science and Engineering&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[&quot;By Country&quot;,&quot;By State or Region&quot;,&quot;Citizenship&quot;,&quot;Faculty Rank, Tenure Status&quot;,&quot;Federal Government/Agency R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Fields of Study&quot;,&quot;Financial Support&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Degrees&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Enrollment&quot;,&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;Income and Salary&quot;,&quot;Institutions&quot;,&quot;Minorities in Higher Education&quot;,&quot;New Construction&quot;,&quot;Product &amp; Business Process Innovation&quot;,&quot;Race and Ethnicity&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Employment&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding by Government&quot;,&quot;R&amp;D Funding Source&quot;,&quot;Repairs and Renovations&quot;,&quot;Sex&quot;,&quot;State Government R&amp;D-related Facilities&quot;,&quot;Student Loan Debt&quot;],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2019&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:5287,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 19-324&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS) estimates that companies performed $356 billion of research and development in the United States in 2015. This InfoBrief examines the differences in industry patterns of R&amp;D that arise from using various methods for classifying companies to industries. This InfoBrief also highlights the ways BRDIS data can be used to describe industry patterns of business R&amp;D in the United States.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/03/2019&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2019/nsf19324/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf19324&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2019.09.03&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;,&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Examining Different Industry Classification Methods for Business R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2019&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;ar