51.8 billion.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;07/30/2018&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2018/nsf18311/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf18311&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2018.07.30&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal R&amp;D Obligations Increase 3% in FY 2017: Research Obligations Decrease Slightly While Those for Development Increase 7%&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2018&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal S&amp;E Support to Universities, Colleges, and NPOs&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal S&amp;E Support to Universities, Colleges, and NPOs&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:5210,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 18-310&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;In FY 2016, federal agencies obligated $31.6 billion to institutions of higher education in support of science and engineering (S&amp;E), up from the $30.5 billion obligated in FY 2015. Funding for research and development to universities and colleges accounted for 90% of the total federal S&amp;E support to higher education institutions. These data are from the FY 2016 Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions, conducted by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;07/30/2018&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2018/nsf18310/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf18310&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2018.07.30&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Science and Engineering Obligations to Academic Institutions Reach $31.6 Billion in FY 2016; Support to HBCUs Declines for the Second Year in a Row&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2018&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Interactive&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering State Profiles&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering State Profiles&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:5219,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Updated data for Science and Engineering State Profiles are now available. This product provides state-level rankings and totals of science and engineering data for the 50 states, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico. The data tool allows users to compare state profiles for up to seven states or download the entire data set. Science and Engineering State Profiles is produced by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics of the National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;07/26/2018&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/states/download/state-profiles-2018-04.xlsx&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf00000&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2018.07.26&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering State Profiles: Spring 2018 Update&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2018&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal Funds for R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Funds for R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:5194,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This set of detailed tables present data on the research and development obligations and outlays of federal agencies. Obligations are provided by type of work (basic research, applied research, development, and R&amp;D plant), agency, field of science or engineering (for research only), geographic area, and performer. These data, collected by the Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development, are the primary source of information about federal funding for R&amp;D in the United States.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;07/12/2018&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://ncsesdata.nsf.gov/fedfunds/2016/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf00000&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2018.07.12&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Funds for Research and Development: Fiscal Years 2016–17&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2018&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reports&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:5169,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 17-310&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Women, Minorities, and Persons with Disabilities in Science and Engineering (S&amp;E) provides the latest information about the participation of these groups in S&amp;E higher education and employment. These updated data cover occupations, salaries, employment status, and disability status.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;06/19/2018&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2017/nsf17310/data.cfm&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf17310&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2018.06.19&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Women, Minorities, and Persons with Disabilities in Science and Engineering: 2017 (Data update)&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2018&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal S&amp;E Support to Universities, Colleges, and NPOs&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal S&amp;E Support to Universities, Colleges, and NPOs&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:5193,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Tables present FY 2016 data on federal obligations to academic and nonprofit institutions for science and engineering research and development. Data include type of activity and trends, as well as ranking by state, agency, and individual institution. Data are from the Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;05/22/2018&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://ncsesdata.nsf.gov/fedsupport/2016/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf00000&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2018.05.22&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions: Fiscal Year 2016&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2018&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:5204,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 18-309&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report provides current data on the levels and key trends of the performance and funding of research and development in the United States, with comparisons to the historical record (back to 1953). Detailed statistical tables present data on U.S. R&amp;D expenditures by performing sector, source of funds, type of R&amp;D, and state. Data are drawn from five surveys from the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;05/21/2018&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2018/nsf18309/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf18309&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2018.05.21&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources: 2015-16 Data Update&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2018&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding, by Budget Function&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding, by Budget Function&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:5178,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 18-308&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report contains data on the budget authority provided to U.S. federal agencies to fund the research and development and R&amp;D plant components of their programs in FYs 2016–18. The 24 detailed statistical tables in this report provide this budget authority detail by agency and major programs for each of the categories in which R&amp;D is present. Data were compiled for the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;04/24/2018&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2018/nsf18308/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf18308&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2018.04.24&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding, by Budget Function: Fiscal Years 2016–18&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2018&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reports&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:5152,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 18-304&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report summarizes trends in U.S. doctoral education by using data from the 2016 Survey of Earned Doctorates, an annual census of research doctorate recipients from U.S. universities. Important trends in this population are highlighted in this report—including the representation of women, minorities, and foreign nationals; fields that are attracting graduate students; time to complete doctoral degree; and employment opportunities after graduation. A total of 72 data tables accompany the report.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;03/29/2018&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2018/nsf18304/report/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf18304&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2018.03.29&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities: 2016 Report&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2018&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reference&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:5149,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This publication provides definitions of research and development from several U.S. and international sources. It serves as a reference for researchers and other users of data on R&amp;D expenditures. The definitions, drawn from their original sources and unedited, are organized into four sections.  The first presents statistical definitions of R&amp;D from the Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development (OECD) Frascati Manual 2015. The next three sections are organized by sectors of the U.S. economy that perform or fund R&amp;D—businesses (II), the federal government and state governments (III), and academic and nonprofit organizations (IV).&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;03/22/2018&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/randdef/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf00000&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2018.03.22&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Definitions of Research and Development: An Annotated Compilation of Official Sources&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2018&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:5112,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 18-302&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The 65 detailed statistical tables in this report present 2014 data on domestic and global research and development expenditures and the R&amp;D workforce, intellectual property, technology transfer, and innovation activities for companies operating in the United States. These data were collected by the Business R&amp;D Innovation Survey (BRDIS), conducted annually by the U.S. Census Bureau for the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation. BRDIS findings are used by government agencies, corporations, research organizations, and academics to assess trends in the performance and funding of R&amp;D.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;03/12/2018&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2018/nsf18302/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf18302&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2018.03.12&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;,&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business Research and Development and Innovation: 2014&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2018&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Characteristics of Scientists and Engineers with U.S. Doctorates&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Characteristics of Scientists and Engineers with U.S. Doctorates&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:5148,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Tables from the 2015 Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR) present data on the demographic characteristics, labor force status, occupations, employment, and median annual salaries of individuals with a U.S. research doctoral doctorate in a science, engineering, or health (SEH) field. The SDR is sponsored by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics (NCSES) within the National Science Foundation (NSF) and by the National Institutes of Health. NCSES significantly expanded the sample size for the 2015 SDR to produce estimates of employment characteristics by more than 200 fields of study. In addition, the SDR now represents all nationally and internationally residing U.S.-trained doctorate holders.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;03/12/2018&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://ncsesdata.nsf.gov/doctoratework/2015/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf00000&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2018.03.12&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients, 2015&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2018&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;R&amp;D Expenditures at Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;R&amp;D Expenditures at Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:5161,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 18-301&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The nation&#39;s 42 federally funded research and development centers (FFRDCs) spent $19.2 billion on R&amp;D in FY 2016, an annual increase of just over 4% in current dollars. In constant 2009 dollars, the total spent on R&amp;D within FFRDCs rose 3.0% from FY 2015 to FY 2016. Data are from the FY 2016 FFRDC Research and Development Survey, conducted by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;02/21/2018&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2018/nsf18301/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf18301&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2018.02.21&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers Continue Increase to $19.2 Billion in R&amp;D Spending in FY 2016&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2018&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;R&amp;D Expenditures at Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;R&amp;D Expenditures at Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:5158,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;These tables provide data on R&amp;D expenditures for federally funded research and development centers (FFRDCs). Expenditures data are displayed for individually named FFRDCs, by source of funding, and type of R&amp;D. Data are from the FY 2016 FFRDC Research and Development Survey, conducted by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;02/21/2018&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://ncsesdata.nsf.gov/datatables/ffrdcrd/2016/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf00000&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2018.02.21&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Expenditures: Fiscal Year 2016&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2018&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in S&amp;E&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in S&amp;E&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:5164,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;These tables provide detailed data on graduate students and postdoctoral appointees in science, engineering, and selected health fields. Trend data are provided on graduate student enrollment, postdoctoral appointments, and other doctorate-holding nonfaculty researcher (NFR) appointments, as well as counts of graduate students, postdoctoral appointees, and NFRs by characteristics, such as sex, race and ethnicity, citizenship, field, and primary source and mechanism of support. Data are from the Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering: Fall 2016, sponsored by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation and by the National Institutes of Health.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;02/21/2018&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://ncsesdata.nsf.gov/gradpostdoc/2016/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf00000&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2018.02.21&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering: Fall 2016&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2018&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in S&amp;E&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in S&amp;E&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:5170,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 18-307&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The number of full-time graduate students enrolled in science and engineering (S&amp;E) programs rose 0.8% in 2016 after rising by 2.8% in 2015. The 2016 increase was due to a 2.1% increase in the full-time enrollment of foreign students with temporary visas. In 2016, full-time S&amp;E foreign graduate student enrollment grew to 210,260 and represented 45% of all full-time S&amp;E graduate students. In contrast, full-time S&amp;E graduate enrollment of U.S. citizens and permanent residents declined for the fifth year in a row. Data are from the 2016 Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;02/08/2018&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2018/nsf18307/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf18307&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2018.02.08&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Full-Time Graduate Enrollment in Science and Engineering Continues to Grow in 2016 Due to Increased Enrollment by Foreign Students on Temporary Visas&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2018&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reports&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering Indicators&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering Indicators&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:5186,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSB 2018-1&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;&lt;i&gt;Science and Engineering Indicators 2018&lt;/i&gt; is now available. Published by the National Science Board, this biennial report provides a broad base of high-quality quantitative data about the U.S. and international science and engineering enterprise. Accompanying the report is a digest drawing attention to important trends and data points, as well as the State Indicators data tool that enables interactive exploration of U.S. state-specific indicators in science and engineering. The complete content of this digital report, including supporting detailed data tables, is available for download and sharing.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;01/15/2018&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2018/nsb20181/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsb20181&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2018.01.15&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering Indicators 2018&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2018&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:5167,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 18-306&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;New data indicate that research and development performed in the United States totaled $495.1 billion in 2015. The estimated total for 2016, based on performer-reported expectations, is $510.0 billion. These numbers compare to U.S. R&amp;D totals of $454.0 billion in 2013 and $475.4 billion in 2014. In 2008—just before the onset of the main economic effects of the national and international financial crisis and the Great Recession—the U.S. total was $404.8 billion. Data are from the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics, National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;12/14/2017&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2018/nsf18306/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf18306&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2017.12.14&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;U.S. R&amp;D Increased by $20 Billion in 2015, to $495 Billion; Estimates for 2016 Indicate a Rise to $510 Billion&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2017&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;State Government Research and Development&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;State Government Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:5163,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 18-305&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;State government agency expenditures for research and development totaled $2.3 billion in FY 2016, an increase of 3.1% from FY 2015. Five state governments (California, New York, Texas, Florida, and Ohio) accounted for 64% of all state government R&amp;D in FY 2016. This InfoBrief presents summary statistics from the FY 2016 Survey of State Government Research and Development, sponsored by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics, National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;12/13/2017&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2018/nsf18305/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf18305&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2017.12.13&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of State Government Research and Development&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of State Government Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;State Government R&amp;D Expenditures Increase 3.1% in FY 2016&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2017&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;State Government Research and Development&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;State Government Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:5127,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The 21 tables in this report present FY 2016 and time series data for the 50 states and the District of Columbia on total R&amp;D expenditures, intramural and extramural performers, source of funds, and R&amp;D by governmental function by state and department or agency. These data were collected by the Survey of State Government Research and Development, conducted by the U.S. Census Bureau for the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;12/13/2017&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://ncsesdata.nsf.gov/sgrd/2016/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf00000&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2017.12.13&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of State Government Research and Development&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of State Government Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of State Government Research and Development: FY 2016&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2017&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Interactive&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering State Profiles&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering State Profiles&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:5175,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Updated data for Science and Engineering State Profiles are now available. This product provides state-level rankings and totals of science and engineering data for the 50 states, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico. The data tool allows users to compare state profiles for up to seven states or download the entire data set. Science and Engineering State Profiles is produced by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics of the National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;12/07/2017&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/states/download/state-profiles-2017-10.xlsx&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf00000&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2017.12.07&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering State Profiles: Fall 2017 update&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2017&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:5151,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 18-304&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This release provides detailed tabular data from the 2016 Survey of Earned Doctorates, an annual census of all individuals receiving a research doctorate from U.S. universities during the 2015–16 academic year. Data tables show important trends in the U.S. doctorate population—including the representation of women, minorities, and foreign nationals; emergence of new fields of study; doctoral degree completion time; and employment plans after graduation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;12/06/2017&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2018/nsf18304/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf18304&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2017.12.06&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities: 2016&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2017&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD)&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD)&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:5157,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The tables present data for the FY 2016 Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey, from the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics (NCSES) within the National Science Foundation. Data are provided on R&amp;D expenditures at higher education institutions across all academic disciplines. Tables include R&amp;D expenditures by institution, R&amp;D field, geographic area, source of funds, type of work (basic research, applied research, and experimental development), cost categories (salaries, software, equipment, and indirect costs), and trends over time. Additionally for FY 2016, the fields of research and development were revised to better reflect the R&amp;D currently conducted at universities and colleges, and to ensure comparability of HERD survey fields across NCSES surveys and with the Department of Education&#39;s Classification of Instructional Programs (CIP) codes.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;11/30/2017&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://ncsesdata.nsf.gov/herd/2016/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf00000&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2017.11.30&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Research and Development: Fiscal Year 2016&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2017&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD)&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD)&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:5166,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 18-303&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Federal funding of higher education R&amp;D increased in both current and constant dollars for the first time in 5 years. Between FY 2015 and FY 2016, funding increased 2.5% in current dollars to $38.8 billion (a 1.4% increase when adjusted for inflation). The share of higher education R&amp;D supported by the federal government continues to decline, however. Universities report that since 2011 federally funded expenditures have dropped from 62.5% to 54.0% of total R&amp;D expenditures. These data are from the 2016 Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics (NCSES) within the National Science Foundation (NSF).&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;11/30/2017&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2018/nsf18303/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf18303&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2017.11.30&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Universities Report Increased Federal R&amp;D Funding after 4-year Decline; R&amp;D Fields\nRevised for FY 2016&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2017&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:5150,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 18-300&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Science and engineering (S&amp;E) research has traditionally been presented in peer-reviewed S&amp;E journals, books, and conference proceedings, and this publication output can be used as an indicator of scientific activity. New data show that S&amp;E publication output has continued to grow, reaching 2.3 million globally in 2014. This reflects a worldwide average annual growth rate of 6% between 2004 and 2014. The United States and China were the two largest producers of scientific publications in 2014 (19% and 17% of the world total). Data were tabulated by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;10/31/2017&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2018/nsf18300/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf18300&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2017.10.31&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering Publication Output Trends: 2014 Shows Rise of Developing Country Output while Developed Countries Dominate Highly Cited Publications&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2017&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:5125,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 17-321&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Fifteen percent of an estimated 1.3 million for-profit companies introduced one or more product or process innovations in 2012–14 (9% product innovations, and 12% process innovations). Innovation rates are similar to those from 2009–11 when 14% of companies introduced one or more product or process innovations (9% product innovations, and 10% process innovations). Data are from the 2014 Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS), from the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;08/28/2017&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2017/nsf17321/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf17321&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2017.08.28&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;,&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Rates of Innovation among U.S. Businesses Stay Steady: Data from the 2014 Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2017&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal S&amp;E Support to Universities, Colleges, and NPOs&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal S&amp;E Support to Universities, Colleges, and NPOs&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:5082,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Tables present FY 2015 data on federal obligations to academic and nonprofit institutions for science and engineering research and development. Data include type of activity and trends, as well as ranking by state, agency, and individual institution. Data are from the Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;08/24/2017&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://ncsesdata.nsf.gov/fedsupport/2015/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf00000&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2017.08.24&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Support for Science and Engineering to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions: Fiscal Year 2015&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2017&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:5145,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 17-320&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Businesses spent $356 billion on research and development performance in the United States in 2015, up 4.4% from the $341 billion spent in 2014. Funding from the companies&#39; own sources was $297 billion in 2015, a 5.0% increase from 2014. Funding from other sources was $59 billion in 2015 and $58 billion in 2014. Data for this InfoBrief are from the Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey, developed and cosponsored by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation and the U.S. Census Bureau.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;08/22/2017&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2017/nsf17320/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf17320&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2017.08.22&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;,&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business R&amp;D Performed in the United States Reached $356 Billion in 2015&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2017&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal S&amp;E Support to Universities, Colleges, and NPOs&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal S&amp;E Support to Universities, Colleges, and NPOs&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:5124,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 17-318&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Federal agencies obligated $30.5 billion to 1,016 academic institutions for science and engineering (S&amp;E) activities in FY 2015, a decrease from the previous year by 2% in current dollars and 3% after adjusting for inflation. Within the six categories of federal academic S&amp;E obligations—research and development; R&amp;D plant; facilities and equipment for instruction in S&amp;E; fellowships, traineeships, and training grants; general support for S&amp;E; and other S&amp;E activities—only fellowships, traineeships, and training grants showed an increase ($8 million, or 1%). These data are from the Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions from the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;07/07/2017&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2017/nsf17318/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf17318&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2017.07.07&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Science and Engineering Obligations to Universities and Colleges Declined 2% in FY 2015&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2017&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reports&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:5092,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 17-306&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report summarizes trends in U.S. doctoral education by using data from the Survey of Earned Doctorates, an annual census of research doctorate recipients from U.S. universities. Important trends in this population are highlighted in this report including the representation of women, minorities, and foreign nationals; emergence of new fields of study; time to complete doctoral degree; and employment opportunities after graduation. A total of 72 data tables accompany the report.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;06/28/2017&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2017/nsf17306/report/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf17306&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2017.06.28&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities: 2015 Report&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2017&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Characteristics of Scientists and Engineers with U.S. Doctorates&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Characteristics of Scientists and Engineers with U.S. Doctorates&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:5137,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 17-319&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics (NCSES) within the National Science Foundation (NSF) significantly expanded the sample size for the 2015 Survey of Doctorate Recipients to produce estimates of employment characteristics by more than 200 fields of study. In addition, the SDR now represents all nationally and internationally residing U.S.-trained doctorate holders. In 2015, an estimated 1,047,900 individuals worldwide held U.S. research doctoral degrees in science, engineering, and health (SEH) fields including 127,800 doctorate holders living abroad. The proportion of U.S.-residing SEH doctorate holders performing R&amp;D as a primary activity varies widely across the fields of study.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;06/28/2017&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2017/nsf17319/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf17319&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2017.06.28&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;The 2015 Survey of Doctorate Recipients Expands Its Population Coverage and Reporting on Field of Study&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2017&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Interactive&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering State Profiles&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering State Profiles&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:5144,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Updated data for Science and Engineering State Profiles are now available. This product provides state-level rankings and totals of science and engineering data for the 50 states, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico. The data tool allows users to compare state profiles for up to seven states or download the entire data set. Science and Engineering State Profiles is produced by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics of the National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;05/22/2017&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/states/download/state-profiles-2017-05.xlsx&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf00000&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2017.05.22&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering State Profiles: Spring 2017 update&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2017&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:5090,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 17-317&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;U.S. companies performed $73 billion in research and development outside the United States in 2013, or 18% of their worldwide total R&amp;D performance. These same companies performed $323 billion of R&amp;D in the United States. These estimates include the R&amp;D of U.S.-owned companies and U.S. subsidiaries of foreign-owned companies, but they exclude the foreign R&amp;D of companies not owned or controlled by the surveyed U.S.-located companies. Data are from the Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey, conducted by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;04/11/2017&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2017/nsf17317/&quo