million to 214.7 million. The rate of increase was substantially below the average 2-year growth rate (4.9%), as measured from FY 1988 to FY 2015. Data are from the biennial Survey of Science and Engineering Research Facilities, conducted by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;02/28/2017&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2017/nsf17315/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf17315&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2017.02.28&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Science and Engineering Research Facilities&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Science and Engineering Research Facilities&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Research Space at Academic Institutions Increased 1.4% between FY 2013 and FY 2015: Smallest Growth in Three Decades&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2017&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Facilities for S&amp;E Research&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Facilities for S&amp;E Research&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:5097,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report provides information on the amount and condition of science and engineering research space existing at eligible U.S. colleges and universities. Data tables include information on research space, new construction, deferred construction, and repair and renovation projects, all by field, state, and type of academic institution. Data in this report are from the FY 2015 Survey of Science and Engineering Research Facilities, conducted by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;02/28/2017&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://ncsesdata.nsf.gov/datatables/facilities/2015/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf00000&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2017.02.28&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Science and Engineering Research Facilities&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Science and Engineering Research Facilities&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering Research Facilities: Fiscal Year 2015&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2017&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in S&amp;E&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in S&amp;E&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:5084,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 17-309&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The total number of postdocs in science, engineering, and health in U.S. academic institutions held steady at nearly 64,000 in 2015. While the majority of the postdocs still worked in biological sciences and in clinical medicine, the share of postdocs in these two fields has declined over the past five years. From 2010 to 2015, biological sciences&#39; share of postdocs fell from 34.2% to 30.2% while clinical medicine dropped from 26.0% to 24.8%. Data are from the 2015 Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering, conducted by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;02/09/2017&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2017/nsf17309/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf17309&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2017.02.09&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Field Composition of Postdocs Shifts as Numbers Decline in Biological Sciences and in Clinical Medicine&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2017&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in S&amp;E&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in S&amp;E&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:5085,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;These tables provide detailed data on graduate students and postdoctoral appointees in science, engineering, and selected health fields. Trend data are provided on graduate student enrollment and postdoctoral appointments, as well as counts of graduate students and postdoctoral appointees by sex, race and ethnicity, citizenship, field, and primary source and mechanism of support. Data are from the Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering: Fall 2015, conducted by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;02/09/2017&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://ncsesdata.nsf.gov/datatables/gradpostdoc/2015/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf00000&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2017.02.09&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in S&amp;E: Fall 2015&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2017&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reports&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Women, Minorities, and Persons with Disabilities in STEM&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Women, Minorities, and Persons with Disabilities in STEM&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4556,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 17-310&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;&lt;em&gt;Women, Minorities, and Persons with Disabilities in Science and Engineering&lt;/em&gt; is a congressionally mandated biennial report that provides a broad base of quantitative information about the participation of these three groups in science and engineering education and employment. The Digest highlights key issues and trends through graphics and text. New to the Digest this cycle are data from a pilot study on early career doctorate holders. Detailed data tables available online provide information on higher education enrollment, degrees earned, institution types, and financial support, as well as on employment status, sector of employment, occupations, and salaries. Surveys conducted by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation provided a large portion of the data used in this report.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;01/30/2017&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2017/nsf17310/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf17310&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2017.01.30&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Surveys&quot;,&quot;National Survey of College Graduates (NSCG)&quot;,&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Survey&quot;,&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;,&quot;National Survey of Recent College Graduates (NSRCG)&quot;,&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Surveys&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Survey of College Graduates (NSCG)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Survey&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Survey of Recent College Graduates (NSRCG)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Women, Minorities, and Persons with Disabilities in Science and Engineering: 2017&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2017&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;SESTAT: Characteristics of Scientists and Engineers in the United States&quot;,&quot;Characteristics of College Graduates&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;SESTAT: Characteristics of Scientists and Engineers in the United States&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Characteristics of College Graduates&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:5115,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 17-312&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Overall, in February 2015, employed college graduates in the United States with a certification or license earned more than those without a credential beyond their highest college degree. Of the college-educated population, 39% held an active certification or license. Certifications and licenses among the college-educated population were most prevalent at the professional degree and master&#39;s degree levels. Data from the National Survey of College Graduates (NSCG), sponsored by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;01/26/2017&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2017/nsf17312/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf17312&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2017.01.26&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;National Survey of College Graduates (NSCG)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Survey of College Graduates (NSCG)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Prevalence of Certifications and Licenses among the College-Educated Population in the United States&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2017&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Early Career Doctorates&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Early Career Doctorates&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:5109,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 17-313&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;In the U.S. workforce, early career doctorates—persons receiving their first doctorate within the past 10 years—are a diverse group that includes both U.S. and non-U.S. citizens trained either in the United States or abroad. The Early Career Doctorates Survey (ECDS) gathers in-depth information about such individuals who are employed at U.S. master&#39;s- and doctorate-granting academic institutions, federally funded research and development centers, or the National Institutes of Health Intramural Research Program. Data are from the 2015 ECDS pilot study, sponsored by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;01/25/2017&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2017/nsf17313/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf17313&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2017.01.25&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Early Career Doctorates Survey (ECDS)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Early Career Doctorates Survey (ECDS)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Profile of Early Career Doctorates: 2015&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2017&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;State Government Research and Development&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;State Government Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:5079,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 17-308&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The 27 tables in this report present FY 2014 and FY 2015 data for the 50 states, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico on total R&amp;D expenditures, intramural and extramural performers, source of funds, and type of R&amp;D by state and department or agency. These data were collected by the Survey of State Government Research and Development, conducted by the U.S. Census Bureau for the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;01/24/2017&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2017/nsf17308/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf17308&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2017.01.24&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of State Government Research and Development&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of State Government Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of State Government Research and Development: FYs 2014 and 2015&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2017&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;State Government Research and Development&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;State Government Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:5080,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 17-307&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;State government agency expenditures for research and development totaled $2.2 billion in FY 2015, an increase of 16.9% from FY 2014. Five state governments (California, New York, Florida, Texas, and Ohio) accounted for 61% of all state government R&amp;D in FY 2015, an increase from 59% in FY 2014. This InfoBrief presents summary statistics from the FY 2014 and FY 2015 Survey of State Government R&amp;D, sponsored by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics within the National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;12/20/2016&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2017/nsf17307/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf17307&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2016.12.20&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of State Government Research and Development&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of State Government Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;State Government R&amp;D Expenditures Total More than $2.2 Billion in FY 2015&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2016&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding, by Budget Function&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding, by Budget Function&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4574,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 17-305&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report contains information on the budget authority of U.S. federal agencies to fund research and development and R&amp;D plant in FYs 2015–17. FY 2015 data are agencies&#39; actual budget authority. FY 2016 data (preliminary) are estimates. FY 2017 data are the funding levels proposed by the president&#39;s U.S. government budget for that year. (Final legislation on federal spending in FY 2017 had not been enacted by the president and Congress by the time of report release.) Two tables provide year-to-year time series data on actual federal budget authority for R&amp;D by broad function back to FY 1955.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;12/13/2016&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2017/nsf17305/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf17305&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2016.12.13&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding, by Budget Function: Fiscal Years 2015–17&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2016&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4563,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 17-306&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This release provides detailed tabular statistics from the 2015 Survey of Earned Doctorates, an annual census of doctorate recipients from U.S. universities. Tables show important trends in this population—including the representation of women, minorities, and foreign nationals; emergence of new fields of study; time to complete doctoral study; and employment opportunities after graduation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;12/06/2016&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2017/nsf17306/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf17306&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2016.12.06&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities: 2015&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2016&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding, by Budget Function&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding, by Budget Function&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:5087,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 17-304&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;New data indicate that federal budget authority for research and development and R&amp;D plant together totaled an estimated $149.0 billion (current dollars, preliminary data) in FY 2016, an increase of $10.5 billion (7.5%) over the FY 2015 level. This follows a $2.4 billion increase (1.8%) in FY 2015 and a $3.7 billion increase (2.8%) in FY 2014. In FY 2017, the president&#39;s proposed budget for the federal government calls for $153.9 billion in funding for R&amp;D and R&amp;D plant, a $4.9 billion increase (3.3%) from the previous year. Data are from the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics, National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;11/30/2016&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2017/nsf17304/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf17304&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2016.11.30&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Budget Authority for R&amp;D Continued to Move Upward in FYs 2015 and 2016, with a Further Increase Proposed for FY 2017&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2016&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD)&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD)&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:5095,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;These tables present data on research and development expenditures at higher education institutions across all academic disciplines. Tables include R&amp;D expenditures by institution, R&amp;D field, geographic area, source of funds, type of work (basic research, applied research, and development), cost categories (salaries, software, equipment, and indirect costs), and trends over time. Data are from the FY 2015 Higher Education Research and Development Survey, by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics, National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;11/17/2016&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://ncsesdata.nsf.gov/herd/2015/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf00000&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2016.11.17&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Research and Development: Fiscal Year 2015&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2016&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD)&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD)&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:5091,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 17-303&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Federal funding of higher education R&amp;D declined in both current and constant dollars for the fourth straight year. When adjusted for inflation, federal funding for academic R&amp;D declined 1.7% between FY 2014 and FY 2015 and has fallen almost 13% since its peak in FY 2011. This decrease continues the longest multiyear decline in federal funding for academic R&amp;D since data collection began in FY 1972. Data are from the Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics, National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;11/17/2016&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2017/nsf17303/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf17303&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2016.11.17&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Universities Report Fourth Straight Year of Declining Federal R&amp;D Funding in FY 2015&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2016&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Interactive&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering State Profiles&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering State Profiles&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:5083,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Updated data for Science and Engineering State Profiles are now available. This product provides state-level rankings and totals of science and engineering data for the 50 states, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico. The data tool allows users to compare state profiles for up to seven states or download entire data set. Science and Engineering State Profiles is produced by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics of the National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;11/07/2016&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/states/download/state-profiles-2016-08.xlsx&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf00000&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2016.11.07&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering State Profiles: 2016 update&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2016&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4548,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 17-302&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Companies active in research and development (those that paid for or performed R&amp;D) employed 1.5 million R&amp;D workers in the United States in 2013, according to the Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS). R&amp;D employees are defined in BRDIS as all employees who work on R&amp;D or who provide direct support to R&amp;D. Although these R&amp;D workers account for just over 1% of total business employment in the United States, they play a vital role in creating the new ideas and technologies that keep companies competitive, create new markets, and spur economic growth.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;10/31/2016&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2017/nsf17302/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf17302&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2016.10.31&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;,&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;A Snapshot of Business R&amp;D Employment in the United States&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2016&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Postdoctorates at Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Postdoctorates at Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4571,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 17-301&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;In 2015, 24 of 42 federally funded research and development centers (FFRDCs) in the United States employed a total of 2,696 postdocs. Although this number is lower than the peak of 3,011 reported in 2010, it shows a rebound after two consecutive survey years of decline. FFRDCs serve to meet the research and analytic needs of federal agencies, and their employment of postdocs helps to train the country&#39;s next generation of scientists and engineers. Data are from the 2015 Survey of Postdocs at FFRDCs.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;10/20/2016&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2017/nsf17301/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf17301&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2016.10.20&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Postdocs at Federally Funded Research and Development Centers&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Postdocs at Federally Funded Research and Development Centers&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Number of Postdocs at Federally Funded Research and Development Centers Rebounds After Several Years of Decline&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2016&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Postdoctorates at Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Postdoctorates at Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4572,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 17-300&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The set of detailed statistical tables presented in this report contain aggregate counts of postdocs at federally funded research and development centers (FFRDCs), by citizenship, ethnicity, race, sex, source of financial support, and field of research. Data are from the Survey of Postdocs at Federally Funded Research and Development Centers, which is the primary source of information on postdocs employed in FFRDCs in the United States.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;10/20/2016&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2017/nsf17300/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf17300&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2016.10.20&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Postdocs at Federally Funded Research and Development Centers&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Postdocs at Federally Funded Research and Development Centers&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Postdocs at Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers: Fall 2015&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2016&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4565,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 16-317&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Five states—California, Massachusetts, Michigan, Texas, and Washington—accounted for half of the research and development paid for and performed by companies in the United States in 2013, according to estimates from the 2013 Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS) from the National Science Foundation&#39;s National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics. Companies performed $265 billion of R&amp;D paid for by their own company expenses in the United States in 2013, of which $255 billion could be attributed to one of the 50 states or the District of Columbia. New data are also available on the top regional areas for self-funded R&amp;D performance. Top three areas are San Jose-San Francisco-Oakland, Los Angeles-Long Beach, and Seattle-Tacoma-Olympia.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/30/2016&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2016/nsf16317/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf16317&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2016.09.30&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;,&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Five States Account for Half of U.S. Business R&amp;D in 2013; New Data for Metropolitan Areas Available&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2016&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reports&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in S&amp;E&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in S&amp;E&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:5093,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 16-314&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This special report examines the impact of changes in the frame of institutions for the Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS) based on evaluation research conducted during the past several survey cycles. The 2011 Eligibility Screening Survey identified 165 newly eligible, previously unsurveyed institutions with at least one master&#39;s- or doctorate-granting program in science, engineering, or health. A total of 140 of these institutions were found to be eligible at the end of the 2013 GSS cycle. The analysis found that adding these new institutions had limited impact on overall counts and trends in the GSS. However, compared with previously eligible institutions, newly eligible institutions have larger proportions of computer science and psychology units and fewer in engineering and health. They also have larger proportions of graduate students that are self-supported, enrolled part time, and are women, minorities, and U.S. citizens or permanent residents.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/29/2016&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2016/nsf16314/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf16314&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2016.09.29&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Assessing the Impact of Frame Changes on Trend Data from the Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2016&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4568,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 16-316&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;New data indicate that research and experimental development (R&amp;D) performed in the United States totaled $477.7 billion in 2014. The estimated total for 2015, based on performer-reported projections, is $499.3 billion. These numbers compare to U.S. R&amp;D totals of $436.2 billion in 2012 and $456.6 billion in 2013. In 2008—just before the onset of the main economic effects of the national and international financial crisis and the Great Recession—the U.S. total was $407.0 billion. Data are from the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics, National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/15/2016&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2016/nsf16316/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf16316&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2016.09.15&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;U.S. R&amp;D Increased by More Than $20 Billion in Both 2013 and 2014, with Similar Increase Estimated for 2015&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2016&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4562,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 16-315&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Businesses spent $341 billion on R&amp;D performed in the United States in 2014, up 5.6% from the $323 billion spent in 2013. Funding from the companies&#39; own sources was $283 billion in 2014, a 6.7% increase from the $265 billion spent in 2013. Funding from other sources was $58 billion in both years. Data are from the Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey, which was developed and is cosponsored by the National Science Foundation and the Census Bureau.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;08/25/2016&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2016/nsf16315/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf16315&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2016.08.25&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;,&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) 