&quot;:&quot;https://ncses.nsf.gov/resource/master-gov-lists-ffrdc/prior-lists#card3454&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf00000&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2016.06.23&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Master Government List of Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers: FY 2016&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2016&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Working Paper&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:4333,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NCSES 16-200&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The two decades spanning 1988–2011 evidenced considerable growth in funding for academic research and development in science and engineering (S&amp;E), and smaller growth in the number of academic researchers and in the number of academic scientific publications. After adjustment for inflation, expenditures for academic R&amp;D in S&amp;E almost tripled during this period, rising from $17.3 billion to $49.7 billion. Throughout most of this period, funding for academic R&amp;D in S&amp;E grew steadily, with rapid increases from the late 1990s until around 2005 and slower growth since then. There was also steady but slower growth in the number of U.S.-trained academic researchers and in the number of annual academic sector publications.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;06/16/2016&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2016/ncses16200/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;ncses16200&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2016.06.16&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Trends in the Relationship between U.S. Academic Scientific Publication Output and Funding and Personnel Inputs: 1988–2011&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2016&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reports&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:4532,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 15-311&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Women, Minorities, and Persons with Disabilities in Science and Engineering (S&amp;E) provides the latest information about the participation of these groups in S&amp;E higher education and employment. This data update provides new statistics on U.S. demographics; freshman intentions to major in S&amp;E fields; enrollment status and primary source of support for S&amp;E graduate students; and S&amp;E doctorate recipients, by field, sex, race or ethnicity, disability status, primary source of support, baccalaureate origin, and postgraduate plans.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;06/13/2016&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/wmpd/archives/wmpd-2015.zip&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf15311&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2016.06.13&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Women, Minorities, and Persons with Disabilities in Science and Engineering: Data Update&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2016&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal S&amp;E Support to Universities, Colleges, and NPOs&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal S&amp;E Support to Universities, Colleges, and NPOs&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4553,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Tables present FY 2014 data on federal obligations to academic and nonprofit institutions for science and engineering research and development. Data include type of activity and trends, as well as ranking by state, agency, and individual institution. Data are from the Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;06/02/2016&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://ncsesdata.nsf.gov/fedsupport/2014/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf00000&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2016.06.02&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Support for Science and Engineering to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions: Fiscal Year 2014&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2016&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Interactive&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering State Profiles&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering State Profiles&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4549,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Updated data for Science and Engineering State Profiles are now available. This product provides state-level rankings and totals of science and engineering data for the 50 states, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico. The data tool allows users to compare state profiles for up to seven states or download entire data set. Science and Engineering State Profiles is produced by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics of the National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;06/02/2016&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/states/download/state-profiles-2015-12.xlsx&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf00000&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2016.06.02&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering State Profiles: 2016&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2016&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Other&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;International Investment and R&amp;D Data Link&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;International Investment and R&amp;D Data Link&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:5165,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This article presents new data on multinational enterprises (MNEs) located in the United States that engage in both research and development (R&amp;D) and international trade in innovation-related services. The data show that these MNEs are more R&amp;D-intensive in terms of employment than MNEs that perform R&amp;D but do not engage in innovation-related services trade. The data also show differences in trade balances in different types of innovation-related services between R&amp;D-performing U.S. parent companies of U.S. MNEs and R&amp;D-performing U.S. affiliates of foreign MNEs.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;05/23/2016&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://apps.bea.gov/scb/pdf/2016/05%20May/0516_innovation_related_services_trade_by_multinational_enterprises.pdf&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf00000&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2016.05.23&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;,&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Innovation-Related Services Trade by Multinational Enterprises (hosted at bea.gov)&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2016&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal S&amp;E Support to Universities, Colleges, and NPOs&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal S&amp;E Support to Universities, Colleges, and NPOs&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4531,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 16-312&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;In FY 2014, federal agencies obligated $30.8 billion to 996 academic institutions for science and engineering (S&amp;E) activities, a 6% increase from the $29.1 billion obligated to 995 academic institutions in FY 2013. This is the first increase in S&amp;E funding to academic institutions since FY 2009. Statistics are from the Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions from the National Science Foundation&#39;s National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;05/19/2016&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2016/nsf16312/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf16312&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2016.05.19&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Science and Engineering Obligations to Universities and Colleges Increase by 6% in FY 2014&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2016&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal Funds for R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Funds for R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4530,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 16-311&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Current-dollar federal obligations for research increased 6% to $62.9 billion from FY 2013 to FY 2014. Research is estimated to remain relatively flat at $63.4 billion in FY 2015 and is projected to increase by $2.7 billion (4%) to $66.2 billion in FY 2016. Total federal R&amp;D obligations increased 4% to $130.3 billion in FY 2014, are estimated to decrease 1% in FY 2015, and are projected to increase 6% in FY 2016. Data are from the Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development: FYs 2014–16, National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;04/20/2016&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2016/nsf16311/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf16311&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2016.04.20&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Funding for Research Increases by 6% in FY 2014; Total Federal R&amp;D Up 4%&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2016&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal Funds for R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Funds for R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4539,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This set of detailed tables present data on the research and development obligations and outlays of federal agencies. Obligations are provided by character of work (basic research, applied research, development, and R&amp;D plant), agency, field of science or engineering (for research only), geographic area, and performer. These data, collected by the Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development, are the primary source of information about federal funding for R&amp;D.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;04/20/2016&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://ncsesdata.nsf.gov/fedfunds/2014/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf00000&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2016.04.20&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Funds for Research and Development: Fiscal Years 2014–16&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2016&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4522,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 16-309&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Information and communications technologies (ICTs) have grown to be pervasive throughout the economy, and companies that develop these technologies account for a large share of business R&amp;D expenditures in the United States. Of the $323 billion of R&amp;D performed by companies in the United States in 2013, ICT industries accounted for 41% ($133 billion). For perspective, R&amp;D expenditures of ICT industries is 2.5 times larger than the pharmaceutical manufacturing industry, the single largest industry in terms of U.S. R&amp;D expenditures. Data are from the Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;04/13/2016&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2016/nsf16309/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf16309&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2016.04.13&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;,&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Information and Communications Technology Industries Account for $133 Billion of Business R&amp;D Performance in the United States in 2013&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2016&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reports&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4513,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 16-300&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report summarizes trends in U.S. doctoral education by using data from the Survey of Earned Doctorates, an annual census of research doctorate recipients from U.S. universities. Important trends in this population are highlighted in this report-including the representation of women, minorities, and foreign nationals; emergence of new fields of study; time to complete doctoral degree; and employment opportunities after graduation. A total of 70 data tables accompany the report.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;03/31/2016&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2016/nsf16300/digest/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf16300&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2016.03.31&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quo