Students and Postdoctorates in S&amp;E&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in S&amp;E&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4521,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 16-310&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;In 2014, a total of 601,883 graduate students were enrolled in S&amp;E master&#39;s or doctorate programs. Graduate enrollment within previously eligible extant GSS institutions rose by 3.0% (16,861 students) from 2013, while the institutional frame update led to a net increase of 14,722 S&amp;E graduate students. Findings are from the 2014 Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;03/29/2016&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2016/nsf16310/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf16310&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2016.03.29&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Influx of Foreign Graduate Students and Inclusion of Newly Eligible Institutions Lead to a Significant Increase in U.S. Graduate Enrollment in Science and Engineering&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2016&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4512,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 16-308&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Fourteen percent of U.S. companies introduced one or more product or process innovations in 2009–11. Much higher incidence rates are observed, however, in narrowly defined industries within both manufacturing and nonmanufacturing, such as chemicals (49%), computer and electronic products (54%), aerospace (42%), publishing (40%), data processing (41%), computer systems design (44%), and scientific R&amp;D services (43%). Data are from innovation questions included in the 2011 Business Research and Development and Innovation Survey. Companies surveyed represent an estimated 1.2 million for-profit companies, publicly or privately held, that have five or more employees and were active in the United States in 2011.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;03/08/2016&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2016/nsf16308/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf16308&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2016.03.08&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;,&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Update on U.S. Business Innovation: Findings from 2011 Survey&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2016&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;R&amp;D Expenditures at Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;R&amp;D Expenditures at Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4525,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 16-307&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The nation&#39;s 41 federally funded research and development centers (FFRDCs) spent $17.7 billion on R&amp;D in FY 2014. This was a 0.3% increase over the FY 2013 total. Eighteen of the FFRDCs reported declines from FY 2013. Findings were from the 2014 FFRDC Research and Development Survey, conducted by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics at the National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;02/29/2016&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2016/nsf16307/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf16307&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2016.02.29&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers Report Little Growth in R&amp;D Spending in FY 2014&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2016&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;R&amp;D Expenditures at Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;R&amp;D Expenditures at Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4510,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;These tables provide data on R&amp;D expenditures for federally funded research and development centers (FFRDCs) from the FY 2014 FFRDC Research and Development Survey. Expenditures data are displayed for individually named FFRDCs, by source of funding, type of administrator, and character of work.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;02/29/2016&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://ncsesdata.nsf.gov/ffrdcrd/2014/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf00000&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2016.02.29&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Survey: Fiscal Year 2014&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2016&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;International Investment and R&amp;D Data Link&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;International Investment and R&amp;D Data Link&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4506,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 16-306&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report summarizes newly developed data from a project linking R&amp;D and international investment data sets from the National Science Foundation&#39;s National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics, U.S. Census Bureau, and Bureau of Economic Analysis. The combined data sets provide information on the R&amp;D activities of U.S. multinational companies (MNCs) and of foreign MNCs with U.S. activities, information that was previously unavailable. Linked data include details at the industry level and selected company size for sales, employment and R&amp;D employment, R&amp;D performance, character of work, and source of funds. Selected data are also available by geographic detail.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;02/04/2016&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2016/nsf16306/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf16306&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2016.02.04&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;International Investment and R&amp;D Data Link: 2008–10&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2016&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;International Investment and R&amp;D Data Link&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;International Investment and R&amp;D Data Link&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4507,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 16-305&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The majority of U.S. business research and development is performed by multinational companies (MNCs). This report details findings of an interagency R&amp;D Data Link Project for 2008–10. The Project covers activities by MNCs, either owned by U.S. companies or located in the United States. Data presented in this report were obtained by linking company-specific information from surveys conducted by the National Science Foundation&#39;s National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics, the U.S. Census Bureau, and the Bureau of Economic Analysis.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;02/04/2016&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2016/nsf16305/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf16305&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2016.02.04&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;R&amp;D Performance of U.S.-Located Multinational Companies: Results from Multiagency Survey Linking Project&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2016&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reports&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering Indicators&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering Indicators&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4534,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSB-2016-1&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;&lt;em&gt;Science and Engineering Indicators 2016&lt;/em&gt; is now available. Published by the National Science Board, this biennial report provides a broad base of high-quality quantitative data about the U.S. and international science and engineering enterprise. Accompanying the report is the &lt;em&gt;Science and Engineering Indicators Digest 2016&lt;/em&gt;, as well as the State Indicators data tool that enables interactive exploration of U.S. state-specific indicators in science and engineering. The complete content of this digital report is downloadable in PDF, with source data for tables and figures also available in a spreadsheet format. Figures are additionally available for download in a presentation-style format.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;01/15/2016&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2016/nsb20161/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsb20161&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2016.01.15&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering Indicators 2016&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2016&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4504,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 16-300&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report summarizes trends in U.S. doctoral education by using data from the Survey of Earned Doctorates, an annual census of research doctorate recipients from U.S. universities. Important trends in this population are highlighted in this report—including the representation of women, minorities, and foreign nationals; emergence of new fields of study; time to complete doctoral degree; and employment opportunities after graduation. A total of 70 data tables accompany the report.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;12/03/2015&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2016/nsf16300/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf16300&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2015.12.03&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities: 2014&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2015&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding, by Budget Function&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding, by Budget Function&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4515,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 16-304&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;New data from the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics indicate that federal budget authority for research and development and R&amp;D plant together totaled an estimated $137.2 billion (current dollars, preliminary data) in FY 2015, an increase of $1.0 billion over the FY 2014 level. This follows a $3.7 billion increase in FY 2014 over the FY 2013 level. Nevertheless, this pair of increases only modestly offsets the successive declines experienced in FY 2011 (down $4.6 billion), FY 2012 (down $0.6 billion), and FY 2013 (down $11.3 billion).&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;11/23/2015&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2016/nsf16304/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf16304&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2015.11.23&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Budget Authority for R&amp;D in FYs 2014 and 2015 Turns Modestly Upward, but Extent of Increase in FY 2016 Uncertain&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2015&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding, by Budget Function&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding, by Budget Function&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4514,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 16-303&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report contains information on the budget authority of U.S. federal agencies to fund research and development and R&amp;D plant in FYs 2014–16. FY 2014 data are agencies&#39; actual budget authority. FY 2015 data (preliminary) are estimates. FY 2016 data are the funding levels proposed by the President&#39;s U.S. government budget for that year. Two tables provide year-to-year time series data on actual federal budget authority for R&amp;D by broad function back to FY 1955.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;11/23/2015&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2016/nsf16303/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf16303&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2015.11.23&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding, by Budget Function:  Fiscal Years 2014–16&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2015&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD)&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD)&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4509,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;These tables present data on research and development expenditures at higher education institutions across all academic disciplines. Tables include R&amp;D expenditures by institution, R&amp;D field, geographic area, source of funds, character of work (basic research, applied research, and development), cost categories (salaries, software, equipment, and indirect costs), and trends over time. Data are from the National Science Foundation&#39;s FY 2014 Higher Education Research and Development Survey.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;11/17/2015&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://ncsesdata.nsf.gov/herd/2014/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf00000&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2015.11.17&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Research and Development Survey, Fiscal Year 2014&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2015&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD)&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD)&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4516,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 16-302&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Federally funded R&amp;D at universities declined for the third straight year, to $37.9 billion in FY 2014 (current dollars). When adjusted for inflation, federal funding for higher education R&amp;D declined by 5.1% between FY 2013 and FY 2014 and has fallen over 11% since its peak in FY 2011. This is the longest multiyear decline in federal funding for academic R&amp;D since the beginning of the annually collected data series in FY 1972. Data are from the Higher Education Research and Development Survey, developed by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics, National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;11/17/2015&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2016/nsf16302/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf16302&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2015.11.17&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Universities Report Continuing Decline in Federal R&amp;D Funding in FY 2014&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2015&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4459,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 16-301&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report presents detailed tabular statistics from the 2012 cycle of the Business Research and Development and Innovation Survey. Tables detail findings by size of company, sources of R&amp;D funds, character of R&amp;D, and industry classified by the North American Industrial Classification System (NAICS) codes.  Also included are tables covering R&amp;D by business activity and state, R&amp;D performed outside the United States, and R&amp;D paid for by foreign sources; intellectual property, including patenting; and R&amp;D capital expenditures.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;10/29/2015&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2016/nsf16301/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf16301&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2015.10.29&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;,&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business Research and Development and Innovation: 2012&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2015&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;SESTAT: Characteristics of Scientists and Engineers in the United States&quot;,&quot;Characteristics of College Graduates&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;SESTAT: Characteristics of Scientists and Engineers in the United States&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Characteristics of College Graduates&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4508,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Tables from the 2013 Scientists and Engineers Statistical Data System (SESTAT) present data on the demographic characteristics, labor force status, occupations, employment, and median annual salaries of individuals with a bachelor&#39;s or higher-level degree in a science, engineering (S&amp;E) or S&amp;E-related field or working in an S&amp;E or S&amp;E-related occupation. Two demographic surveys sponsored by the National Science Foundation collect the data that are integrated into SESTAT.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/30/2015&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://ncsesdata.nsf.gov/us-workforce/2013/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf00000&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2015.09.30&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;National Survey of College Graduates (NSCG)&quot;,&quot;National Survey of Recent College Graduates (NSRCG)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Survey of College Graduates (NSCG)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Survey of Recent College Graduates (NSRCG)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Scientists and Engineers Statistical Data System (SESTAT), 2013&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2015&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Working Paper&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:4386,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NCSES 15-200&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Clean energy supply and pollution control technologies have become a policy focus in the United States and other countries, with research and development, commercial investment, and production increasing in these technologies. Researchers and policymakers are interested in metrics to better assess these rapidly emerging technologies. A working paper released by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics discusses a method to identify patents in clean energy supply and pollution control technologies. This method uses complex patent filters—a combination of patent classification codes and search terms—to identify patents in these technology areas, an approach that is likely far more accurate than patent filters that rely solely on patent classification codes. These filters have identified four broad technology areas that are relevant to clean energy supply and pollution control—alternative energy, pollution mitigation, energy storage, and smart grid—which are further divided into 28 specific technologies. These filters were initially developed to create indicators of inventive activity focused on clean energy supply and pollution control technologies for publication in &lt;cite&gt;Science and Engineering Indicators 2012&lt;/cite&gt;.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/24/2015&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2015/ncses15200/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;ncses15200&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2015.09.24&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Identifying Clean Energy Supply and Pollution Control Patents&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2015&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;SESTAT: Characteristics of Scientists and Engineers in the United States&quot;,&quot;Characteristics of College Graduates&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;SESTAT: Characteristics of Scientists and Engineers in the United States&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Characteristics of College Graduates&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4496,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 15-328&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;From 2003 to 2013, the number of scientists and engineers residing in the United States grew from 21.6 million to 29.0 million. Immigration has played an important factor in this growth. In 2013, 18% (5.2 million) of the scientists and engineers residing in the United States were immigrants, whereas in 2003, 16% (3.4 million) were immigrants. Immigrant scientists and engineers were more likely to earn post-baccalaureate degrees than were U.S. citizens who were born in the United States, Puerto Rico, or another U.S. territory or born abroad of U.S. citizen parents.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/10/2015&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2015/nsf15328/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf15328&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2015.09.10&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;National Survey of College Graduates (NSCG)&quot;,&quot;National Survey of Recent College Graduates (NSRCG)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Survey of College Graduates (NSCG)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Survey of Recent College Graduates (NSRCG)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Immigrants&#39; Growing Presence in the U.S. Science and Engineering Workforce: Education and Employment Characteristics in 2013&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2015&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4505,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 15-330&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Research and development performed in the United States totaled $456.1 billion in 2013. This is compared to $435.3 billion in 2012 (revised downward from an earlier estimate) and $427.8 billion in 2011. In 2008—just before the onset of the main economic effects of the national and international financial crisis and the Great Recession—U.S. R&amp;D totaled $407.0 billion. Data are from the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics, National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/08/2015&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2015/nsf15330/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf15330&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2015.09.08&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;U.S. R&amp;D Increased in 2013, Well Ahead of the Pace of Gross Domestic Product&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2015&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Working Paper&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:4209,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NCSES 15-201&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This working paper released by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics examines the consistency of doctorate-holding nonfaculty researcher (NFR) reporting in the Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS) since 2010, analyzes key reporting patterns, and attempts to validate the GSS data to better understand the overall quality of the NFR data. The accurate reporting of NFRs is of critical interest to the National Science Foundation and other stakeholders because it is the first step in understanding the use and impact of this increasingly significant part of the academic science and engineering workforce.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;08/31/2015&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2015/ncses15201/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;ncses15201&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2015.08.31&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Examining the Reporting of Nonfaculty Doctorate Researchers in the Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2015&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4503,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 15-329&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Companies spent $323 billion on research and development performed in the United States during 2013, 6.7% more than the $302 billion spent during 2012. Funding from the companies&#39; own sources was $247 billion during 2012 and $265 billion during 2013, a 7.1% increase. Funding from other sources was $55 billion during 2012 and $58 billion during 2013. Data for this InfoBrief are from the Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS), which was developed and is cosponsored by the National Science Foundation and the U.S. Census Bureau.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;08/20/2015&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2015/nsf15329/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf15329&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2015.08.20&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;,&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business R&amp;D Performance in the United States Increases Over 6% to $323 Billion in 2013&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2015&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Interactive&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering State Profiles&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering State Profiles&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4501,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Updated data for Science and Engineering State Profiles are now available. This product provides state-level rankings and totals of science and engineering data for the 50 states, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico. The data tool allows users to compare state profiles for up to 10 states. Science and Engineering State Profiles is produced by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics of the National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;07/13/2015&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/states/download/state-profiles-2015-05.xlsx&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf00000&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2015.07.13&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering State Profiles: 2015&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2015&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reference&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Master Government List of Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Master Government List of Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4502,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The Master Government list of Federally Funded Research and Development Centers (FFRDCs), maintained by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics of the National Science Foundation, has been updated for 2015. FFRDCs are listed by sponsoring agency, administrator type, location, and type of activity.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;07/10/2015&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://ncses.nsf.gov/resource/master-gov-lists-ffrdc/prior-lists#card3454&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf00000&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2015.07.10&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Master Government List of Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers: FY 2015&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2015&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal S&amp;E Support to Universities, Colleges, and NPOs&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal S&amp;E Support to Universities, Colleges, and NPOs&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4486,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 15-327&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The detailed statistical tables in this report present FY 2013 data on federal obligations to academic and nonprofit institutions for science and engineering research and development. Data include type of activity and trends, as well as ranking by state, agency, and individual institution. Data are from the Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;07/09/2015&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2015/nsf15327/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf15327&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2015.07.09&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions: FY 2013&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2015&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal S&amp;E Support to Universities, Colleges, and NPOs&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal S&amp;E Support to Universities, Colleges, and NPOs&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4489,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 15-325&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;In FY 2013, federal agencies obligated $29 billion to 995 academic institutions for science and engineering (S&amp;E) activities. This represents a 6% decrease in current dollars from the $31 billion obligated to 1,073 academic institutions in FY 2012. This is the third year in a row of declining S&amp;E funding to academic institutions. Data are from the Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions from the National Science Foundation, National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;06/30/2015&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2015/nsf15325/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf15325&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2015.06.30&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Science and Engineering Obligations to Universities and Colleges Drop by 6% in FY 2013&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2015&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;S&amp;E Degrees&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;S&amp;E Degrees&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4413,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 15-326&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This set of detailed statistical tables provide information on the number and types of bachelor&#39;s, master&#39;s and doctoral degrees awarded by U.S. institutions in science and engineering fields. It also provides information on the numbers of male and female degree recipients. The report includes select data from 1966–2012 that is gathered from two annual federal surveys: the Integrated Postsecondary Education Data System Completions Survey and the Survey of Earned Doctorates.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;06/30/2015&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2015/nsf15326/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf15326&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2015.06.30&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;,&quot;Integrated Postsecondary Education Data System Completions Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Integrated Postsecondary Education Data System Completions Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering Degrees:  1966–2012&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2015&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal Funds for R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Funds for R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4478,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 15-324&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report provides detailed tabular data on the research and development obligations and outlays of federal agencies. Obligations are provided by character of work (basic research, applied research, development, and R&amp;D plant), agency, field of science or engineering (for research only), geographic area, and performer. These data, collected by the Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development, are the primary source of information about federal funding for R&amp;D.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;06/29/2015&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2015/nsf15324/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf15324&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2015.06.29&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Funds for Research and Development: Fiscal Years 2013–15&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2015&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;State Government Research and Development&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;State Government Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4464,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 15-323&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The 25 tables in this report present FY 2012 and FY 2013 data for the 50 states, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico on total R&amp;D expenditures, intramural and extramural performers, basic research activities, source of funds, and type of R&amp;D by state and department or agency. These data were collected by the Survey of State Government Research and Development, conducted for the National Science Foundation by the U.S. Census Bureau.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;06/15/2015&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2015/nsf15323/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf15323&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2015.06.15&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of State Government Research and Development&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of State Government Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of State Government Research and Development: FYs 2012 and 2013&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2015&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Facilities for S&amp;E Research&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Facilities for S&amp;E Research&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4439,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 15-320&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The data in these detailed statistical tables are collected biennially through the National Science Foundation&#39;s congressionally mandated Survey of Science and Engineering Research Facilities. These tables provide data on the amount and condition of science and engineering research space existing at eligible U.S. colleges and universities. Data presented are from the survey conducted for FY 2013.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;06/04/2015&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2015/nsf15320/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf15320&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2015.06.04&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Science and Engineering Research Facilities&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Science and Engineering Research Facilities&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering Research Facilities: Fiscal Year 2013&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2015&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;S&amp;E Degrees, By Race/Ethnicity of Recipients&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;S&amp;E Degrees, By Race/Ethnicity of Recipients&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4431,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 15-321&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Detailed statistical tables provide trend data on the number of bachelor&#39;s, master&#39;s, doctorate, and first-professional degrees conferred in the United States, by race and ethnicity of degree recipients. The tables also show earned degrees, by gender, citizenship, broad science and engineering field, and health field. Data are based on two annual federal surveys: National Center for Education Statistics&#39;s Integrated Postsecondary Education Data System Completions Survey and the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics&#39;s Survey of Earned Doctorates.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;05/21/2015&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2015/nsf15321/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf15321&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2015.05.21&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering Degrees, by Race/Ethnicity of Recipients: 2002–12&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2015&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal Funds for R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Funds for R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4483,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 15-322&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Funds obligated by federal agencies for research and development and R&amp;D plant (facilities and fixed equipment) dropped from $141 billion to $127 billion, or 9%, in current dollars from FY 2012 to FY 2013. Federal obligations for R&amp;D and R&amp;D plant are estimated to rise 3% ($4 billion in current dollars) in FY 2014 and are projected to rise 2% in FY 2015 by $3 billion in current dollars. Data are from the National Science Foundation&#39;s Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development: FYs 2013–15.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;05/14/2015&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2015/nsf15322/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf15322&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2015.05.14&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Research and Development and R&amp;D Plant Funding Drop by 9% in FY 2013&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2015&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reports&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4456,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 15-304&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report summarizes trends in U.S. doctoral education by using data from the Survey of Earned Doctorates, an annual census of research doctorate recipients from U.S. universities. The report highlights important trends in this population—including the representation of women, minorities, and foreign nationals; emergence of new fields of study; time to complete doctoral degree; and employment opportunities after graduation. Accompanying online resources include interactive graphics and 70 detailed data tables, available as PDF and Excel files.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;04/29/2015&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/sed/2013/digest/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf15304&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2015.04.29&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities: 2013 Digest&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2015&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;R&amp;D Expenditures at Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;R&amp;D Expenditures at Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4480,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;These tables provide data on R&amp;D expenditures for federally funded research and development centers (FFRDCs) from the FY 2013 FFRDC Research and Development Survey. Expenditures data are displayed for individually named FFRDCs, by source of funding, type of administrator, and character of work.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;04/16/2015&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://ncsesdata.nsf.gov/ffrdc/2013/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf00000&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2015.04.16&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Survey: Fiscal Year 2013&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2015&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;R&amp;D Expenditures at Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;R&amp;D Expenditures at Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4479,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 15-319&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The nation&#39;s 40 federally funded R&amp;D centers (FFRDCs) spent $16.9 billion on research and development in FY 2013. The majority of the FFRDCs (24 centers) reported declines from FY 2012, and 17 reported two straight years of declines between FY 2011 and FY 2013. FFRDCs are privately operated R&amp;D organizations that are exclusively or substantially financed by the federal government.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;04/16/2015&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2015/nsf15319/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf15319&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2015.04.16&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Majority of Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers Report Declines in R&amp;D Spending in FY 2013&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2015&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in S&amp;E&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in S&amp;E&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4466,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 15-318&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The number of full-time graduate students enrolled in science and engineering (S&amp;E) programs rose by 2.4% in 2013 after remaining relatively flat for the past 2 years. This increase was largely due to a 7.9% increase in full-time enrollment of foreign students on temporary visas. In 2013, full-time S&amp;E foreign graduate enrollment reached an all-time high of 168,297 students, and they now represent 39.6% of all full-time S&amp;E graduate students, up from 35.9% in 2008.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;04/14/2015&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2015/nsf15318/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf15318&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2015.04.14&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Full-Time Graduate Enrollment in Science and Engineering Rose in 2013, Fueled by a Large Increase in Foreign Graduate Enrollment&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2015&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in S&amp;E&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in S&amp;E&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4453,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;These tables provide detailed data on graduate students and postdoctoral appointees  in science, engineering, and selected health fields. Data are from the Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering: Fall 2013 and provide trends in graduate student enrollment and postdoctoral appointments, as well as counts of graduate students and postdoctoral appointees by sex, race and ethnicity, citizenship, field, and primary source and mechanism of support.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;04/14/2015&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://ncsesdata.nsf.gov/gradpostdoc/2013/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf00000&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2015.04.14&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering: Fall 2013&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2015&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;SESTAT: Characteristics of Scientists and Engineers in the United States&quot;,&quot;Characteristics of College Graduates&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;SESTAT: Characteristics of Scientists and Engineers in the United States&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Characteristics of College Graduates&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4452,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 15-317&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The number of college graduates in the United States nearly doubled between 1993 and 2013, from 29 million to 55 million. The number of college graduates with degrees in science and engineering (S&amp;E) fields grew faster than the number of college graduates with degrees in non-S&amp;E fields. Additionally, in 2013, about 1 in 10 college graduates were employed in an S&amp;E occupation, and this proportion has remained largely unchanged since 1993. Data are from the National Survey of College Graduates.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;04/06/2015&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2015/nsf15317/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf15317&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2015.04.06&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;National Survey of College Graduates (NSCG)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Survey of College Graduates (NSCG)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Characteristics of the College-Educated Population and the Science and Engineering Workforce in the United States&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2015&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:4406,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 15-315&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Expenditures for research and development are now capitalized—incorporated as investment—in the U.S. gross domestic product (GDP) and other national income and product accounts (NIPAs) produced and published by the Bureau of Economic Analysis (BEA). R&amp;D investment is presented in the NIPAs in a new asset category called &#39;intellectual property products,&#39; or intangible assets, along with software and entertainment, literary, and artistic originals. New InfoBrief from the National Center for Scientific and Engineering Statistics details how these changes affect the GDP and the R&amp;D-to-GDP ratio.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;03/30/2015&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2015/nsf15315/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf15315&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2015.03.30&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;R&amp;D Recognized as Investment in U.S. GDP Statistics: GDP Increase Slightly Lowers R&amp;D-to-GDP Ratio&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2015&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Facilities for S&amp;E Research&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Facilities for S&amp;E Research&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4473,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 15-316&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Research-performing colleges and universities increased their science and engineering research space 4.7% from FY 2011 to FY 2013. Total research space increased 9.6 million net assignable square feet (NASF) over this period, from 202.2 million to 211.8 million. The biological and biomedical sciences constituted the largest share (27.0%) of all research space in FY 2013. This field, along with the agricultural and natural resources sciences, accounted for two-thirds of the 9.6 million NASF growth.  Data are from the biennial Survey of Science and Engineering Research Facilities.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;03/25/2015&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2015/nsf15316/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf15316&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2015.03.25&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Science and Engineering Research Facilities&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Science and Engineering Research Facilities&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Research Space at Academic Institutions Increased by 4.7% between FY 2011 and FY 2013&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2015&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Interactive&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering State Profiles&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering State Profiles&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4469,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Updated data for Science and Engineering State Profiles are now available. This product provides state-level rankings and totals of science and engineering data for the 50 states, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico. The data tool allows users to compare state profiles for up to 10 states. Science and Engineering State Profiles is produced by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics of the National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;03/04/2015&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/states/download/state-profiles-2014-11.xlsx&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf00000&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2015.03.04&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering State Profiles: 2014&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2015&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Postdoctorates at Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Postdoctorates at Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4451,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 15-312&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The set of detailed statistical tables presented in this report contain aggregate counts of postdocs at federally funded research and development centers (FFRDCs), by citizenship, ethnicity, race, sex, source of financial support, and field of research. Data are from the Survey of Postdocs at Federally Funded Research and Development Centers, which is the primary source of information on postdocs employed in FFRDCs in the United States.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;02/18/2015&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2015/nsf15312/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf15312&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2015.02.18&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Postdocs at Federally Funded Research and Development Centers&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Postdocs at Federally Funded Research and Development Centers&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Postdocs at Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers: Fall 2013&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2015&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;SESTAT: Characteristics of Scientists and Engineers in the United States&quot;,&quot;Characteristics of College Graduates&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;SESTAT: Characteristics of Scientists and Engineers in the United States&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Characteristics of College Graduates&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4435,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Tables from the 2010 Scientists and Engineers Statistical Data System (SESTAT) present data on the demographic characteristics, labor force status, occupations, employment, and median annual salaries of individuals with a bachelor&#39;s or higher-level degree in a science, engineering (S&amp;E) or S&amp;E-related field or working in an S&amp;E or S&amp;E-related occupation. The data are collected by three demographic surveys sponsored by the National Science Foundation and are integrated in SESTAT.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;02/18/2015&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://ncsesdata.nsf.gov/us-workforce/2010/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf00000&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2015.02.18&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;National Survey of College Graduates (NSCG)&quot;,&quot;National Survey of Recent College Graduates (NSRCG)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Survey of College Graduates (NSCG)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Survey of Recent College Graduates (NSRCG)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Scientists and Engineers Statistical Data System (SESTAT), 2010&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2015&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD)&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD)&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4475,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 15-314&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;University spending on research and development totaled $67.2 billion in FY 2013. When adjusted for inflation, higher education R&amp;D increased by less than half a percent in FY 2013. Among federal agencies, the Department of Health and Human Services, including the National Institutes of Health, continues to provide the majority of the R&amp;D funding (54%, or $21.2 billion). The National Science Foundation and the Department of Defense were the next largest funders, with $5.4 billion and $5.0 billion, respectively. Data are from the Higher Education Research and Development Survey.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;02/04/2015&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2015/nsf15314/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf15314&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2015.02.04&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D Expenditures Resume Slow Growth in FY 2013&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2015&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD)&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD)&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4474,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;These tables present data on research and development expenditures at higher education institutions across all academic disciplines. Tables include R&amp;D expenditures by institution, R&amp;D field, geographic area, source of funds, character of work (basic research, applied research, and development), and cost categories (salaries, software, equipment, and indirect costs). Many tables show trends over time. Data are from the National Science Foundation&#39;s FY 2013 Higher Education Research and Development Survey.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;02/04/2015&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://ncsesdata.nsf.gov/herd/2013/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf00000&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2015.02.04&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Research and Development Survey, Fiscal Year 2013&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2015&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reports&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Women, Minorities, and Persons with Disabilities in STEM&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Women, Minorities, and Persons with Disabilities in STEM&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4427,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 15-311&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Women, Minorities, and Persons with Disabilities in Science and Engineering (S&amp;E) provides a broad base of quantitative information about the participation of these groups in S&amp;E education and employment. A Digest highlights key issues and trends through graphics and text. Detailed statistical tables provide data on higher education enrollments, degrees, institutions, and financial support and on employment status, occupations, sectors, and salaries. Links to other NSF and non-NSF sources of data are provided.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;01/30/2015&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/wmpd/archives/wmpd-2015.zip&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf15311&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2015.01.30&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Surveys&quot;,&quot;National Survey of College Graduates (NSCG)&quot;,&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Survey&quot;,&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;,&quot;National Survey of Recent College Graduates (NSRCG)&quot;,&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Surveys&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Survey of College Graduates (NSCG)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Survey&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Survey of Recent College Graduates (NSRCG)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Women, Minorities, and Persons with Disabilities in Science and Engineering: 2015&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2015&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;State Government Research and Development&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;State Government Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4463,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 15-313&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;State government agency expenditures for research and development totaled $1.785 billion in FY 2013, virtually unchanged (down 0.6%) from the $1.795 billion reported in FY 2012. Five state governments (California, Ohio, Texas, New York, and Florida) accounted for 57% of all state government R&amp;D in both FY 2012 and FY 2013. Data are from the FY 2012 and FY 2013 Survey of State Government R&amp;D, sponsored by the National Science Foundation, National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;01/28/2015&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2015/nsf15313/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf15313&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2015.01.28&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of State Government Research and Development&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of State Government Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;State Government R&amp;D Expenditures Remain Unchanged in FY 2013 at $1.8 Billion&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2015&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4404,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 15-307&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report presents detailed tabular statistics from the 2011 cycle of the Business Research and Development and Innovation Survey. Tables detail findings by size of company, sources of R&amp;D funds, character of R&amp;D, and industry classified by the North American Industrial Classification System (NAICS) codes.  Also included are tables covering R&amp;D by business activity and state, R&amp;D performed outside the United States, and R&amp;D paid for by foreign sources; intellectual property, including patenting; innovation activity; and R&amp;D capital expenditures.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;12/30/2014&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2015/nsf15307/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf15307&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2014.12.30&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;,&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business Research and Development and Innovation: 2011&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2014&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Postdoctorates at Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Postdoctorates at Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4423,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 15-310&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;As of fall 2013, a total of 21 federally funded research and development centers (FFRDCs) in the United States had postdoc programs. These FFRDCs employed 2,613 postdocs, a 6.4% decrease from fall 2012. This finding corresponds with a slight decline in total R&amp;D spending reported by FFRDCs in FY 2012. Most FFRDCs with a postdoc program were funded by the Department of Energy (16 of 21 FFRDCs).&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;12/16/2014&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2015/nsf15310/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf15310&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2014.12.16&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Postdocs at Federally Funded Research and Development Centers&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Postdocs at Federally Funded Research and Development Centers&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Employment of Postdoctoral Researchers at Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers Declined in 2013&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2014&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal S&amp;E Support to Universities, Colleges, and NPOs&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal S&amp;E Support to Universities, Colleges, and NPOs&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4430,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 15-309&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;In FY 2012, federal agencies obligated $30.8 billion for science and engineering (S&amp;E) to 1,073 academic institutions. This represents a 2% decrease in current dollars from the $31.4 billion obligated to 1,134 academic institutions in FY 2011. Levels of support to minority-serving academic institutions in FY 2012 varied according to institution type, with historically black colleges and universities (HBCUs) seeing the largest drop (-10%). Federal S&amp;E support to nonprofit institutions declined 7% from the FY 2011 level.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;12/15/2014&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2015/nsf15309/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf15309&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2014.12.15&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal FY 2012 S&amp;E Obligations to Academic and Nonprofit Institutions: Focus on Minority-Serving Institutions&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2014&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal S&amp;E Support to Universities, Colleges, and NPOs&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal S&amp;E Support to Universities, Colleges, and NPOs&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4421,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 15-305&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The detailed statistical tables in this report present FY 2012 data on federal obligations to academic and nonprofit institutions for science and engineering research and development. Data include type of activity and trends, as well as ranking by state, agency, and individual institution. Data are from the Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;12/15/2014&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2015/nsf15305/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf15305&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2014.12.15&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions: FY 2012&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2014&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Characteristics of College Graduates&quot;,&quot;Characteristics of Scientists and Engineers with U.S. Doctorates&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Characteristics of College Graduates&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Characteristics of Scientists and Engineers with U.S. Doctorates&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4415,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 15-302&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Among U.S. doctoral graduates from academic years 2001–09 in the fields of science, engineering, and health, 89% reported their intention to live in the United States, a measure referred to as the expected stay rate. The actual stay rate in 2010 tracks the expected stay rate closely for U.S. citizen graduates, but noticeable differences are observed for doctoral graduates who were temporary visa holders at the time of graduation. For this group, many who initially reported an intention to stay eventually left the United States.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;12/04/2014&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2015/nsf15302/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf15302&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2014.12.04&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;National Survey of College Graduates (NSCG)&quot;,&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Survey of College Graduates (NSCG)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Employment Decisions of U.S. and Foreign Doctoral Graduates: A Comparative Study&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2014&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4425,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 15-304&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This release provides detailed tabular statistics from the 2013 Survey of Earned Doctorates, an annual census of doctorate recipients from U.S. universities. Tables show important trends in this population including the representation of women, minorities, and foreign nationals; emergence of new fields of study; time to complete doctoral study; and employment opportunities after graduation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;12/03/2014&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/sed/2013/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf15304&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2014.12.03&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities: 2013&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2014&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding, by Budget Function&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding, by Budget Function&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4458,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 15-308&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;New data indicate that federal budget authority for research and development and R&amp;D plant together totaled an estimated $135.7 billion (current dollars, preliminary data) in FY 2014, an increase of $3.2 billion (2.4%) over the FY 2013 level. Nonetheless, this increase only partially offset the successive declines experienced in FY 2011 (down $4.6 billion), FY 2012 (down $0.6 billion), and FY 2013 (down $11.3 billion). These funding cuts have fallen much more heavily on National defense than the nondefense budget functions.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;11/17/2014&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2015/nsf15308/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf15308&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2014.11.17&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Budget Authority for R&amp;D in FY 2014 Rises Slightly Above the FY 2013 Level; Extent of Increase in FY 2015 Uncertain&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2014&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding, by Budget Function&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding, by Budget Function&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4457,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 15-306&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report contains information on the budget authority of U.S. federal agencies to fund research and development and R&amp;D plant in FYs 2013–15. FY 2013 data are agencies&#39; actual budget authority. FY 2014 data (preliminary) are estimates. FY 2015 data are the funding levels proposed by the President&#39;s U.S. government budget for that year. Two tables provide year-to-year time series data on actual federal budget authority for R&amp;D by broad function back to FY 1955.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;11/17/2014&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2015/nsf15306/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf15306&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2014.11.17&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding, by Budget Function: Fiscal Years 2013–15&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2014&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reports&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:4428,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 13-304&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;&lt;em&gt;Women, Minorities, and Persons with Disabilities in Science and Engineering&lt;/em&gt; (S&amp;E) provides the latest information about the participation of these groups in S&amp;E higher education and employment. Updates in this release cover undergraduate and graduate enrollment, federal employment, occupations after graduation, median annual salaries, and disability status of scientists and engineers. For doctorate recipients, updates are also available on top baccalaureate institutions for women and persons from racial and ethnic minority groups, primary source of financial support, and postdoctorate status.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;10/31/2014&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/wmpd/archives/wmpd-2013.zip&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf13304&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2014.10.31&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Surveys&quot;,&quot;National Survey of College Graduates (NSCG)&quot;,&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Survey&quot;,&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;,&quot;National Survey of Recent College Graduates (NSRCG)&quot;,&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Surveys&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Survey of College Graduates (NSCG)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Survey&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Survey of Recent College Graduates (NSRCG)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Women, Minorities, and Persons with Disabilities in Science and Engineering: Data Update&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2014&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal S&amp;E Support to Universities, Colleges, and NPOs&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal S&amp;E Support to Universities, Colleges, and NPOs&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4412,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 15-301&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The detailed statistical tables presented in this report are based on data from two cycles of the Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions: FYs 2010 and 2011.  Data included in this report are federal obligations to academic and nonprofit institutions by type of activity, year, and state, agency, and individual institution rankings. FY 2010 data breaks down each category of support into ARRA (American Recovery and Reinvestment Act of 2009) and non-ARRA components.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;10/30/2014&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2015/nsf15301/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf15301&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2014.10.30&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions: FYs 2010 and 2011&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2014&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4420,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 15-303&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Companies spent $302 billion on research and development performed in the United States during 2012, 2.8% more than the $294 billion spent during 2011. Funding from the companies&#39; own sources was $239 billion during 2011 and $247 billion during 2012, a 3.6% increase; funding from other sources was $55 billion in both years. Data for this InfoBrief are from the Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey, which was developed and cosponsored by the National Science Foundation and the U.S. Census Bureau.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;10/28/2014&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2015/nsf15303/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf15303&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2014.10.28&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;,&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business R&amp;D Performance in the United States Tops $300 Billion in 2012&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2014&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:4402,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 15-300&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;U.S. knowledge-intensive (KI) services industries—business, finance, and information—produced 22% of U.S. gross domestic product (GDP) in 2012 and pay high wages to their 18 million employees. KI services and the smaller high-technology (HT) manufacturing industries—aerospace, communications, computers, pharmaceuticals, scientific instruments, and semiconductors—make up commercial knowledge and technology-intensive (KTI) industries. KTI industries, which have a particularly strong link to science and technology, play a big role in the U.S. economy and pay substantially higher wages than the private-sector average.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;10/14/2014&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/2015/nsf15300/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf15300&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2014.10.14&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;U.S. Knowledge-Intensive Services Industries Employ 18 Million and Pay High Wages&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2014&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal Funds for R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Funds for R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4418,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 14-316&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report provides detailed tabular data on the research and development obligations and outlays of federal agencies. Obligations are provided by character of work (basic research, applied research, development, and R&amp;D plant), agency, field of science or engineering (for research only), geographic area, and performer. These data, collected by the Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development, are the primary source of information about federal funding for R&amp;D.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/30/2014&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf14316/start.cfm&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf14316&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2014.09.30&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Funds for Research and Development FYs 2012–14&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2014&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Characteristics of Scientists and Engineers with U.S. Doctorates&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Characteristics of Scientists and Engineers with U.S. Doctorates&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4437,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Tables from the 2013 Survey of Doctorate Recipients present data on the demographic and employment characteristics of individuals who have received a research doctorate in a science, engineering, or health field from a U.S. academic institution. Data include the number and median salaries of doctoral scientists and engineers by field of doctorate and occupation, by demographic characteristics, and by employment-related characteristics.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/11/2014&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://ncsesdata.nsf.gov/doctoratework/2013/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf00000&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2014.09.11&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients, 2013&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2014&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Characteristics of Scientists and Engineers with U.S. Doctorates&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Characteristics of Scientists and Engineers with U.S. Doctorates&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4417,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 14-317&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;In 2013, an estimated 837,900 individuals in the United States held research doctoral degrees in science, engineering, and health (SEH) fields. Of these individuals, approximately 735,900 were in the labor force. The unemployment rate for SEH doctorate recipients in the labor force was 2.1% in February 2013, down from 2.4% in October 2010. Moreover, the 2013 unemployment rate of the SEH doctoral labor force was one-third of the February 2013 unemployment rate for the general population aged 25 years or older (6.3%).&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/11/2014&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf14317/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf14317&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2014.09.11&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Unemployment among Doctoral Scientists and Engineers Remained Below the National Average in 2013&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2014&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal Funds for R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Funds for R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4422,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 14-318&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Federal funding for basic research performed at universities and colleges decreased 0.3% between FY 2011 and FY 2012. Nonetheless, universities and colleges received 50.8% of the $31.0 billion total in federal obligations for basic research and accounted for 11.4% of total research and development obligations in FY 2012. Basic research at universities and colleges is estimated to increase to 11.8% of total R&amp;D obligations in FY 2013 and is projected to grow to 12.5% in FY 2014. Data are from the Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/04/2014&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf14318/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf14318&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2014.09.04&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Funding for Basic Research at Universities and Colleges Essentially Unchanged in FY 2012&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2014&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4414,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 14-315&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Companies performed $239 billion of R&amp;D in the United States in 2011 that were paid for by their own company expenses, of which $233 billion was distributed across the 50 states and the District of Columbia. San Jose-San Francisco-Oakland, Boston-Worcester-Providence, and Los Angeles-Long Beach were among the most commonly reported combined statistical areas for companies to have their primary R&amp;D location. Data are from the Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;08/26/2014&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf14315/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf14315&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2014.08.26&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;,&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;2011 Data Show U.S. Business R&amp;D Highly Concentrated by State and Metropolitan Location&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2014&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in S&amp;E&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in S&amp;E&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4436,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;These tables provide detailed data on graduate students, postdoctoral appointees, and doctorate-level nonfaculty researchers in science, engineering, and selected health fields. Data are from the Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering: Fall 2012 and provide trends in graduate student enrollment and postdoc appointments, as well as counts of graduate students and postdocs by sex, race and ethnicity, citizenship, field, and primary source and mechanism of support.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;07/30/2014&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://ncsesdata.nsf.gov/gradpostdoc/2012/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf00000&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2014.07.30&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering, Fall 2012&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2014&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal Funds for R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Funds for R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4408,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 14-312&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report provides detailed tabular data on the research and development obligations and outlays of federal agencies. Obligations are provided by character of work (basic research, applied research, development, and R&amp;D plant), agency, field of science or engineering (for research only), geographic area, and performer. These data, collected by the Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development, are the primary source of information about federal funding for R&amp;D.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;07/28/2014&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf14312/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf14312&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2014.07.28&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Funds for Research and Development: Fiscal Years 2011–13&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2014&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4407,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 14-314&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;In 2011, U.S.-located companies spent $29.6 billion for extramural research and development performed by domestic and overseas organizations. This amount includes contract or otherwise purchased R&amp;D ($24.0 billion) and payments to R&amp;D collaborators ($5.6 billion). Most of these extramural R&amp;D expenditures involve domestic providers and partners ($25.3 billion). For domestic extramural R&amp;D in 2011, two industry groups accounted for the majority of expenditures: pharmaceutical manufacturing (51%), and transportation equipment manufacturing (11%). Data are from the Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;07/22/2014&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf14314/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf14314&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2014.07.22&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;,&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Extramural R&amp;D Funding by U.S.-Located Businesses Nears $30 Billion in 2011&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2014&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Characteristics of Recent College Graduates&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Characteristics of Recent College Graduates&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4434,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Tables from the 2010 National Survey of Recent College Graduates present data on the demographic and employment characteristics of individuals who received a bachelor&#39;s or master&#39;s degree in a science, engineering, or health field from a U.S. academic institution during the two preceding academic years. Data include the number and median salaries of graduates by field of study and occupation, by demographic characteristics, and by employment-related characteristics. This survey was discontinued after the 2010 survey cycle.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;06/23/2014&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://ncsesdata.nsf.gov/recentgrads/2010/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf00000&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2014.06.23&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;National Survey of Recent College Graduates (NSRCG)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Survey of Recent College Graduates (NSRCG)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Survey of Recent College Graduates, 2010&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2014&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reports&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:4416,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 13-304A&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This data update provides new statistics for selected data tables in the report &lt;em&gt;Women, Minorities, and Persons with Disabilities in Science and Engineering.&lt;/em&gt; The report provides statistical information about the participation of women, minorities, and persons with disabilities in science and engineering (S&amp;E) education and employment. New data include U.S. demographics; freshman intentions to major in S&amp;E fields; undergraduate enrollment in engineering; disability status, by degree level; and degrees awarded, by degree level.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;05/30/2014&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/wmpd/archives/wmpd-2013.zip&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf13304a&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2014.05.30&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Women, Minorities, and Persons with Disabilities in Science and Engineering: Data Update&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2014&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reference&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Master Government List of Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Master Government List of Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4388,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The Master Government list of Federally Funded Research and Development Centers (FFRDCs), maintained by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics of the National Science Foundation, has been updated for 2014. FFRDCs are listed by sponsoring agency, administrator type, location, and type of activity.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;05/28/2014&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://ncses.nsf.gov/resource/master-gov-lists-ffrdc/prior-lists#card3454&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf00000&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2014.05.28&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Master Government List of Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers: FY 2014&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2014&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Interactive&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering State Profiles&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering State Profiles&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4383,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The fall 2013 and spring 2014 data updates for Science and Engineering State Profiles are now available. This product provides state-level rankings and totals of science and engineering data for the 50 states, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico. The data tool allows users to compare state profiles for up to 10 states. Science and Engineering State Profiles is produced by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics of the National Science Foundation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;05/27/2014&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/states/download/state_profiles_2014.xls&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf00000&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2014.05.27&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering State Profiles&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2014&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in S&amp;E&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in S&amp;E&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4411,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 14-313&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;From 2011 to 2012, the number of U.S. citizens and permanent residents enrolled in science and engineering (S&amp;E) graduate programs declined 1.7% to 385,343 students. Although offset by a 4.3% rise in foreign S&amp;E graduate students—to 176,075—growth in total S&amp;E graduate student enrollment stalled for the second year. A total of 43,841 S&amp;E postdocs were reported in 2012, a 0.6% decrease from 2011. Postdocs reported dropped among men (-0.5%) and women (-0.9%).&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;05/22/2014&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf14313/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf14313&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2014.05.22&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Foreign Graduate Enrollment in Science and Engineering Continues to Rise While Overall Graduate Enrollment Remains Flat&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2014&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;State Government Research and Development&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;State Government Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4405,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 14-311&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The 19 tables in this report present FY 2010 and FY 2011 data for the 50 states, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico on total R&amp;D expenditures, internal and external performers, basic research activities, source of funds, and type of R&amp;D (now including energy) by state and department or agency. These data were collected by the Survey of State Government Research and Development, conducted for the National Science Foundation by the U.S. Census Bureau.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;05/15/2014&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf14311/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf14311&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2014.05.15&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of State Government Research and Development&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of State Government Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of State Government Research and Development: FYs 2010 and 2011&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2014&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Characteristics of Scientists and Engineers with U.S. Doctorates&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Characteristics of Scientists and Engineers with U.S. Doctorates&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4433,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Tables from the 2010 Survey of Doctorate Recipients present data on the demographic and employment characteristics of individuals who have received a research doctorate in a science, engineering, or health field from a U.S. academic institution. Data include the number and median salaries of doctoral scientists and engineers by field of doctorate and occupation, by demographic characteristics, and by employment-related characteristics.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;05/06/2014&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://ncsesdata.nsf.gov/doctoratework/2010/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf00000&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2014.05.06&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients, 2010&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2014&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Characteristics of Scientists and Engineers with U.S. Doctorates&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Characteristics of Scientists and Engineers with U.S. Doctorates&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4284,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 14-310&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Unemployment among individuals in the United States with research doctorates in science, engineering, and health (SEH) fields was 2.4% in October 2010, up from 1.7% in October 2008. The average national unemployment rate in 2010 for individuals aged 25 or older was 8.2%. Unemployment was lowest among individuals with doctorates in mathematics and statistics (1.5%) and psychology (1.7%). Among SEH doctorate holders, 76% worked full time and 10% worked part time.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;04/17/2014&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf14310/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf14310&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2014.04.17&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Unemployment among Doctoral Scientists and Engineers Increased but Remained Below the National Average&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2014&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal S&amp;E Support to Universities, Colleges, and NPOs&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal S&amp;E Support to Universities, Colleges, and NPOs&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4361,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 14-309&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;In FY 2011, federal agencies obligated $31.4 billion to 1,134 academic institutions for science and engineering activities, a drop of 11.0% from FY 2010 amounts ($35.3 billion to 1,219 academic institutions). This decrease reflects the last of the American Recovery and Reinvestment Act of 2009 stimulus funds being obligated in FY 2010 ($5.1 billion, or 14.5%). Data are from the National Science Foundation&#39;s National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;03/25/2014&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf14309/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf14309&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2014.03.25&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Science and Engineering Obligations to Universities and Colleges Drop by 11% in FY 2011&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2014&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD)&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD)&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4432,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;These tables present data on research and development expenditures at higher education institutions across all academic disciplines. Tables include R&amp;D expenditures by institution, R&amp;D field, geographic area, source of funds, character of work (basic research, applied research, and development), and cost categories (salaries, software, equipment, and indirect costs). Many tables show trends over time. Data are from the National Science Foundation&#39;s FY 2012 Higher Education Research and Development Survey.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;03/12/2014&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://ncsesdata.nsf.gov/herd/2012/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf00000&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2014.03.12&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Research and Development Survey, Fiscal Year 2012&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2014&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Interactive&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:4377,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSB 14-01&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;&lt;em&gt;Science and Engineering Indicators 2014&lt;/em&gt; in HTML format is now available.  This release includes the &lt;a href=&#39;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/seind14/index.cfm/state-data&#39;&gt;State Data Tool&lt;/a&gt;, which provides a wealth of information on science and technology infrastructure by state and allows in-depth exploration of these data.  The &lt;a href=&#39;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/seind14/index.cfm/etc/tables.htm&#39;&gt;tables&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href=&#39;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/seind14/index.cfm/etc/figures.htm&#39;&gt;figures&lt;/a&gt; provided in &lt;em&gt;Science and Engineering Indicators 2014&lt;/em&gt; are also now available for download in Excel and image formats.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;03/05/2014&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/seind14/index.cfm/state-data&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsb1401&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2014.03.05&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering Indicators 2014 State Data Tool&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2014&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reports&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering Indicators&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering Indicators&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4355,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSB 14-01&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;&lt;a href=&#39;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/seind14/&#39;&gt;&lt;em&gt;Science and Engineering Indicators 2014&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/a&gt; is now available online. Published by the National Science Board, this biennial report provides a broad base of quantitative information on U.S. and international science and engineering. Appendix tables are available in PDF and spreadsheet formats, and presentation graphics are available in PowerPoint slide and image formats. Accompanying SEI 2014 is the interactive &lt;a href=&#39;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/digest/&#39;&gt;&lt;em&gt;Science and Engineering Indicators Digest 2014&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/a&gt;.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;02/06/2014&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/seind14/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsb1401&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2014.02.06&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering Indicators: 2014&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2014&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;R&amp;D Expenditures at Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;R&amp;D Expenditures at Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4321,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 14-302&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The detailed statistical tables presented in this report contain data on R&amp;D expenditures for federally funded research and development centers (FFRDCs) from the FY 2012 FFRDC Research and Development Survey. Expenditures data are displayed for individually named FFRDCs, by source of funding, type of administrator, and character of work.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;01/29/2014&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf14302&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf14302&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2014.01.29&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Survey: Fiscal Year 2012&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2014&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;R&amp;D Expenditures at Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;R&amp;D Expenditures at Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4322,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 14-308&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The nation&#39;s 39 federally funded research and development centers (FFRDCs) spent $17.4 billion on research and development in FY 2012. This was a slight decline from the $17.8 billion spent in FY 2011. Funding from federal agencies accounted for 97.5% ($17.0 billion) of the FFRDC&#39;s total expenditures in FY 2012, $352 million of which was funded by the American Recovery and Reinvestment Act of 2009.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;01/27/2014&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf14308/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf14308&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2014.01.27&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers Report Declines in R&amp;D Spending in FY 2012&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2014&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reports&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4311,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 14-305&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report summarizes trends in U.S. doctoral education by using data from the Survey of Earned Doctorates, an annual census of research doctorate recipients from U.S. universities. The report calls attention to important trends in this population—including the representation of women, minorities, and foreign nationals; emergence of new fields of study; time to complete doctoral degree; and employment opportunities after graduation. Accompanying online resources include interactive graphics and 70 detailed data tables, available as PDF and Excel files.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;01/16/2014&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/sed/digest/2012/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf14305&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2014.01.16&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities: 2012 Digest&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2014&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding, by Budget Function&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding, by Budget Function&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4335,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 14-306&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;New data indicate that federal budget authority for research and development and R&amp;D plant together in FY 2012 totaled $143.7 billion (current dollars). The corresponding figure for FY 2013 was an estimated $133.5 billion, a decline of 7.1% over the FY 2012 level, chiefly because of the across-the-board spending cuts (sequestration) mandated by the Budget Control Act of 2011. For FY 2014, the budget authority proposed by the President would rise to $144.4 billion—restoring a majority of the reductions since FY 2010&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;01/14/2014&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf14306/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf14306&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2014.01.14&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Budget Authority for R&amp;D Declines in FYs 2012 and 2013;  Increase Proposed for 2014&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2014&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding, by Budget Function&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding, by Budget Function&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4336,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 14-301&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report contains information on the budget authority of U.S. federal agencies to fund research and development and R&amp;D plant in FYs 2012–14. FY 2012 data are actual budget authority received by agencies. FY 2013 data (preliminary) are estimates. FY 2014 data are the funding levels proposed by the President&#39;s U.S. government budget for that year. Two tables provide year-to-year time series data on actual federal budget authority for R&amp;D by broad function back to FY 1955.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;01/14/2014&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf14301/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf14301&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2014.01.14&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding by Budget Function:  Fiscal Years 2012–14&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2014&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4326,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 14-304&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report contains detailed statistical tables that present data on current and historical patterns of U.S. R&amp;D expenditures by performing sector, source of funds, character of work, and state. The report also contains information on R&amp;D expenditures at U.S. federally funded research and development centers and international R&amp;D data pertaining to major industrialized nations. Data are drawn from four National Science Foundation surveys.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;12/30/2013&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf14304/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf14304&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2013.12.30&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources: 2011–12 Data Update&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2013&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4325,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 14-307&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;New survey data puts U.S. R&amp;D expenditures at $428.2 billion in 2011, an increase of $20.5 billion over the 2010 level. The preliminary estimate of the 2012 U.S. total for R&amp;D is $452.6 billion. After adjustment for inflation, R&amp;D grew at a faster rate than the gross domestic product in 2011 (2.8% versus 1.8%). Preliminary data for 2012 indicate similar findings (3.8% versus 2.2%).&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;12/30/2013&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf14307/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf14307&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2013.12.30&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;U.S. R&amp;D Resumes Growth in 2011 and 2012, Ahead of the Pace of the Gross Domestic Product&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2013&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4310,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 14-305&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This release provides detailed tabular statistics from the 2012 Survey of Earned Doctorates, an annual census of doctorate recipients from U.S. universities. Tables examine important trends in this population—including the representation of women, minorities, and foreign nationals; emergence of new fields of study; time to complete doctoral study; and employment opportunities after graduation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;12/05/2013&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/sed/2012/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf14305&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2013.12.05&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities:  2012 Data Tables&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2013&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD)&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD)&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4320,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 14-303&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;University spending on research and development in all fields totaled $65.8 billion in FY 2012. When adjusted for inflation, higher education R&amp;D declined by 1.1% in FY 2012. This represents the first constant-dollar decline since FY 1974 and ends a period of modest growth during FYs 2009–11, when R&amp;D expenditures increased an average of 5% each year. Data for this InfoBrief are from the National Science Foundation&#39;s Higher Education Research and Development Survey.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;11/25/2013&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf14303/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf14303&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2013.11.25&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education R&amp;D Expenditures Remain Flat in FY 2012&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2013&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reports&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:4309,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 13-304AB&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Women, Minorities, and Persons with Disabilities in Science and Engineering (S&amp;E) provides the latest information about the participation of women, minorities, and persons with disabilities in S&amp;E higher education and employment. Updates in this release cover the employment status, employment sector, median annual salaries, and disability status of scientists and engineers. For doctorate recipients, updates on their academic institutions, primary source of financial support, and postdoctorate status are also available.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;11/14/2013&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/wmpd/archives/wmpd-2013.zip&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf13304ab&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2013.11.14&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Surveys&quot;,&quot;National Survey of College Graduates (NSCG)&quot;,&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Survey&quot;,&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;,&quot;National Survey of Recent College Graduates (NSRCG)&quot;,&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Surveys&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Survey of College Graduates (NSCG)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Survey&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Survey of Recent College Graduates (NSRCG)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Women, Minorities, and Persons with Disabilities in Science and Engineering: Data Update&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2013&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;State Government Research and Development&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;State Government Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4316,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 14-300&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;State government agency expenditures for research and development totaled $1.404 billion in FY 2011, an 11.3% increase over the $1.261 billion reported in FY 2010. Expenditures for R&amp;D facilities (construction projects, major building renovations, and land and building acquisitions intended primarily for R&amp;D use) totaled $109 million in FY 2011, a 1.7% increase over the $107 million reported in FY 2010. Data are from the FY 2010 and FY 2011 Survey of State Government Research and Development.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;11/04/2013&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf14300/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf14300&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2013.11.04&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of State Government Research and Development&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of State Government Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;State Government R&amp;D Expenditures Increase 11.3% from FY 2010 to FY 2011&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2013&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal Funds for R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Funds for R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4315,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 13-336&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Current-dollar federal obligations for research decreased 9.0% between FY 2010 and FY 2011 ($63.7 billion to $58.0 billion). This decline resulted from the last of the 2009 American Recovery and Reinvestment Act stimulus funds being obligated in FY 2010. Research obligations are estimated to remain relatively flat at $58.1 billion in FY 2012 and are projected to increase by $1.9 billion (3.3%) to $60.0 billion in FY 2013. Data are from the Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/24/2013&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf13336/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf13336&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2013.09.24&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Funding for Research Drops by 9% in FY 2011&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2013&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:4148,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 13-334&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The total number of postdoctoral appointees reported in the 2010 Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS) grew to 63,415 in 2010, an increase of 10% over the 2009 total and 25% over the 2007 total. These 1- and 3-year growth rates are the highest in the history of the GSS and likely reflect improved reporting, as well as the continued expansion of postdoc employment in academia.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/23/2013&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf13334/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf13334&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2013.09.23&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Counts of Postdoctoral Appointees in Science, Engineering, and Health Rise with Reporting Improvements&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2013&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4312,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 13-335&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Companies spent $294 billion on research and development performed in the United States during 2011, compared with $279 billion during 2010. Funding from the companies own sources was $222 billion during 2010 and $239 billion during 2011; funding from other sources was $57 billion in 2010 and $55 billion in 2011. Data are from the Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey, which is cosponsored by the National Science Foundation and Census Bureau.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/19/2013&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf13335/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf13335&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2013.09.19&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;,&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business R&amp;D Performance in the United States Increased in 2011&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2013&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:4286,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 13-333&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Universities reported that 4.9%, or $3.2 billion, of their $65 billion total in research and development expenditures in FY 2011 was funded by businesses. Although payments to colleges and universities represent less than 1% of company R&amp;D spending, 29% of large- R&amp;D companies reported making payments or initiating new R&amp;D collaborations with academia in 2010. Data were from the Higher Education Research and Development Survey and the Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/19/2013&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf13333/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf13333&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2013.09.19&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;,&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Two NSF Surveys on R&amp;D Document Varied Relationships between Businesses and Academia&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2013&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4160,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 13-332&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report presents detailed tabular statistics from the 2008–10 cycles of the Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey. Tables detail findings by size of company, sources of R&amp;D funds, character of R&amp;D, and industry using the North American Industrial Classification System (NAICS) codes.  Also included are tables covering R&amp;D by business activity and state, performed outside the United States, and paid for by foreign sources; intellectual property including patenting; innovation activity; and R&amp;D capital expenditures.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/18/2013&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf13332/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf13332&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2013.09.18&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;,&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business Research and Development and Innovation: 2008–10&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2013&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in S&amp;E&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in S&amp;E&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4290,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 13-331&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report provides detailed data on graduate students, postdoctoral appointees, and doctorate-level nonfaculty researchers in science, engineering, and selected health fields. Data are from the Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering: Fall 2011 and provide trends in graduate student enrollment and postdoc appointments, as well as counts of graduate students and postdocs by sex, race and ethnicity, citizenship, field, and primary source and mechanism of support.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/04/2013&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf13331/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf13331&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2013.09.04&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering: Fall 2011&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2013&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Interactive&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering State Profiles&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering State Profiles&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4318,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;WEB 13-301&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Updated data are now available for &lt;em&gt;Science and Engineering State Profiles&lt;/em&gt;, a Web-based data tool developed by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics, National Science Foundation. The tool allows users to generate science and engineering profiles that summarize state-specific data on personnel and finances. The State Profiles data tool can display a single state&#39;s profile or a profile containing up to 10 states. Prior-year data for selected characteristics are also available.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;08/29/2013&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/states/download/state_profiles_2013.xls&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;web13301&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2013.08.29&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering State Profiles: 2013&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2013&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:4269,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 13-330&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Science and engineering (S&amp;E) employment in the United States is geographically concentrated in a small number of states and several major metropolitan areas within those states, according to data from the U.S. Census Bureau&#39;s 2011 American Community Survey. The three most populous states California, Texas, and New York together accounted for more than one-fourth of all U.S. S&amp;E employment. Several major metropolitan areas in those states together accounted for approximately 1 in 10 S&amp;E workers nationwide.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;08/19/2013&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf13330/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf13330&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2013.08.19&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Regional Concentrations of Scientists and Engineers in the United States&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2013&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Facilities for S&amp;E Research&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Facilities for S&amp;E Research&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4285,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 13-329&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Cyberinfrastructure resources at academic research institutions have increased substantially since 2005, according to data from the National Science Foundation&#39;s FY 2011 Survey of Science and Engineering Research Facilities. In FY 2011, 59% of academic institutions reported bandwidth of at least 1 gigabit per second (Gbps), compared with 21% of such institutions in FY 2005. The percentage of academic institutions with network connections of 10 Gbps or greater increased from 2% to 25% during this period.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;07/16/2013&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf13329/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf13329&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2013.07.16&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Science and Engineering Research Facilities&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Science and Engineering Research Facilities&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Computing and Networking Capacity Increases at Academic Research Institutions&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2013&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD)&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD)&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4240,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 13-325&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report presents detailed tabulated data on research and development expenditures at higher education institutions across all academic disciplines. Tables include R&amp;D expenditures by institution, R&amp;D field, geographic area, and source of funds. Many tables show trends over time. Data are from the FY 2011 Higher Education Research and Development Survey.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;07/11/2013&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf13325/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf13325&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2013.07.11&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Research and Development: Fiscal Year 2011&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2013&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal Funds for R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Funds for R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4243,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 13-326&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report provides detailed tabular data on the research and development obligations and outlays of federal agencies. Obligations are provided by character of work (basic research, applied research, development, and R&amp;D plant), agency, field of science or engineering (for research only), geographic area, and performer. These data, collected by the Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development, are the primary source of information about federal funding for R&amp;D.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;07/08/2013&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf13326/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf13326&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2013.07.08&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Funds for Research and Development: Fiscal Years 2010–12&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2013&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4155,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 13-322&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The data presented in Science and Engineering Doctorate Awards: 2009–10 show trends in the numbers of individuals who receive doctorates in science and engineering fields. Detailed information is provided by field of doctorate, doctoral institution, and characteristics of the doctorate recipient. The data also show trends in the postgraduation plans of doctorate recipients. Data are from the Survey of Earned Doctorates, a census of individuals receiving research doctoral degrees from accredited U.S. universities and colleges.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;07/02/2013&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf13322/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf13322&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2013.07.02&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering Doctorate Awards: 2009–10&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2013&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in S&amp;E&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in S&amp;E&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4273,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 13-328&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Over the past decade, enrollment of full-time graduate students in science and engineering (S&amp;E) grew approximately 25% between 2002 and 2011 (from approximately 325,500 to 411,200 students). Enrollment of part-time S&amp;E graduate students increased approximately 15% during this period. From 2010 to 2011, growth in part-time graduate enrollment (1.6%) outpaced that of full-time enrollment (0.5%) for the first time since 2005. Data are from the fall 2011 Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;06/20/2013&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf13328/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf13328&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2013.06.20&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Part-time Graduate Enrollment in Science and Engineering in 2011 Grew at a Higher Rate than Full-time Enrollment&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2013&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;SESTAT: Characteristics of Scientists and Engineers in the United States&quot;,&quot;Characteristics of College Graduates&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;SESTAT: Characteristics of Scientists and Engineers in the United States&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Characteristics of College Graduates&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4213,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 13-320&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report presents data from the 2008 Scientists and Engineers Statistical Data System (SESTAT), a comprehensive and integrated system of information about employment, education, and demographic characteristics of scientists and engineers in the United States. The detailed statistical tables presented here provide information on the number and median salaries of scientists and engineers by field of highest degree and occupation, as well as information on demographic and employment-related characteristics.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;06/19/2013&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf13320/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf13320&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2013.06.19&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Characteristics of Scientists and Engineers in the United States: 2008&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2013&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;S&amp;E Degrees&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;S&amp;E Degrees&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4266,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 13-327&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This set of detailed statistical tables provide information on the number and types of bachelor&#39;s, master&#39;s and doctoral degrees awarded by U.S. institutions in science and engineering fields. It also provides information on the characteristics of the degree recipients. The report includes select data from 1966–2006 that is gathered from two annual federal surveys: the Integrated Postsecondary Education Data System Completions Survey and the Survey of Earned Doctorates.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;06/17/2013&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf13327/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf13327&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2013.06.17&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Integrated Postsecondary Education Data System Completions Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Integrated Postsecondary Education Data System Completions Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering Degrees: 1966–2010&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2013&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4238,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 13-324&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Companies spent $279 billion on research and development performed in the United States during 2010, an amount that was essentially unchanged from the $282 billion spent during 2009. Funding from the companies own sources was $225 billion during 2009 and $222 billion during 2010; funding from other sources was $57 billion in both years. Data are from the Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey, which was developed and cosponsored by the National Science Foundation and the Census Bureau.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;06/04/2013&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf13324/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf13324&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2013.06.04&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;,&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business R&amp;D Performance Remained Virtually Unchanged in 2010&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2013&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reference&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Master Government List of Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Master Government List of Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4298,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The Master Government List of Federally Funded Research and Development Centers (FFRDCs) has been updated to reflect changes through May 2013. This list, maintained by the National Science Foundation, includes sponsoring agencies, administrator types, R&amp;D activity types, and locations.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;05/31/2013&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://ncses.nsf.gov/resource/master-gov-lists-ffrdc/prior-lists#card3454&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf00000&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2013.05.31&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Master Government List of Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers: FY 2013&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2013&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Postdoctorates at Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Postdoctorates at Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4154,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 13-321&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The nation&#39;s 39 federally funded research and development centers (FFRDCs) assist government agencies with scientific research and analysis, and many also provide training opportunities for the country&#39;s aspiring researchers and scientists through postdoctoral appointments. According to the National Science Foundation&#39;s 2010 Survey of Postdocs at FFRDCs, 3,011 postdocs were employed at 22 FFRDCs. This InfoBrief provides a first look at the demographic characteristics of these postdocs, their sources of financial support, and their fields of research.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;04/24/2013&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf13321/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf13321&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2013.04.24&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Postdocs at Federally Funded Research and Development Centers&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Postdocs at Federally Funded Research and Development Centers&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers Employed Over 3,000 Postdoctoral Researchers in 2010&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2013&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Postdoctorates at Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Postdoctorates at Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4288,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 13-319&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This set of detailed statistical tables presented in this report contain aggregate counts of postdocs at federally funded research and development centers (FFRDCs), according to citizenship, ethnicity, race, sex, source of financial support, and field of research. Data are from the Survey of Postdocs at Federally Funded Research and Development Centers, which is the primary source of information on postdocs employed in FFRDCs in the United States.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;04/24/2013&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf13319/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf13319&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2013.04.24&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Postdocs at Federally Funded Research and Development Centers&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Postdocs at Federally Funded Research and Development Centers&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Postdocs at Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers: Fall 2010&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2013&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in S&amp;E&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in S&amp;E&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4147,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 13-314&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Data presented in these detailed statistical tables are from the Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering: Fall 2010. The tables present information on trends in enrollment of science and engineering graduate students through 2010. Data are presented on trends in number of graduate students by sex, race/ethnicity, citizenship, field, and primary source and mechanism of support.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;04/23/2013&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf13314/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf13314&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2013.04.23&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering: Fall 2010&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2013&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4297,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 13-323&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Foreign institutions and U.S. research universities play large roles in the baccalaureate education of U.S.-trained science and engineering (S&amp;E) doctorate recipients. In 2011, 35% of individuals earning S&amp;E doctorates from U.S. universities held bachelor&#39;s degrees from foreign institutions, and 29% earned bachelor&#39;s degrees from U.S. doctorate-granting institutions with very high research activity. Among U.S. S&amp;E doctorate recipients, the proportion with a foreign bachelor&#39;s degree increased 4 percentage points from 2002 to 2011, whereas the proportion with a bachelor&#39;s degree from a U.S. institution declined slightly.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;04/22/2013&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf13323/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf13323&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2013.04.22&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;,&quot;Integrated Postsecondary Education Data System Completions Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Integrated Postsecondary Education Data System Completions Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Baccalaureate Origins of U.S.-trained S&amp;E Doctorate Recipients&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2013&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4268,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 13-318&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report contains detailed statistical tables that present data on current and historical patterns of U.S. R&amp;D expenditures by performing sector, source of funds, character of work, and state. The report also contains information on R&amp;D expenditures at U.S. federally funded research and development centers and international R&amp;D data pertaining to major industrialized nations. Data are drawn from four National Science Foundation surveys.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;04/15/2013&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf13318/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf13318&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2013.04.15&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Industrial Research and Development&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Industrial Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources: 2010–11 Data Update&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2013&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal Funds for R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Funds for R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4270,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 13-317&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Current-dollar federal obligations for research and development and R&amp;D plant (facilities and fixed equipment) increased 1.5% to $147.0 billion in FY 2010. This growth was driven by a $3.0 billion (81.3%) increase in R&amp;D plant obligations in FY 2010: $1.1 billion from the American Recovery and Reinvestment Act of 2009 (ARRA) and $1.9 billion from non-ARRA funds. ARRA funding accounted for 6.2% of the $147.0 billion FY 2010 total. Data are from the Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development: FYs 2010–12.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;03/26/2013&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf13317/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf13317&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2013.03.26&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Research and Development and R&amp;D Plant Obligations Show Modest Growth in FY 2010&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2013&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;R&amp;D Expenditures at Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;R&amp;D Expenditures at Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4249,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 13-316&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Federally funded research and development centers (FFRDCs) are privately operated research and development organizations that are exclusively or substantially financed by the federal government. In FY 2011, the nation&#39;s 40 FFRDCs spent $17.8 billion on R&amp;D, most of which was provided by federal funds ($17.4 billion, or 98%). Just over $850 million of the total was supplied by funds from the American Recovery and Reinvestment Act of 2009. Data for this report are from NSF&#39;s FY 2011 FFRDC R&amp;D Survey.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;03/25/2013&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf13316/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf13316&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2013.03.25&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers Spend $17.8 Billion on R&amp;D in FY 2011&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2013&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;R&amp;D Expenditures at Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;R&amp;D Expenditures at Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4247,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 13-315&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The detailed statistical tables presented in this report contain data on R&amp;D expenditures for federally funded research and development centers (FFRDCs) from the FY 2011 FFRDC Research and Development Survey. Expenditures data are displayed for individually named FFRDCs, by source of funding, type of administration, and character of work.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;03/25/2013&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf13315/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf13315&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2013.03.25&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Expenditures: Fiscal Year 2011&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2013&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reports&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Women, Minorities, and Persons with Disabilities in STEM&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Women, Minorities, and Persons with Disabilities in STEM&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4229,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 13-304&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Women, Minorities, and Persons with Disabilities in Science and Engineering (S&amp;E) provides a broad base of quantitative information about the participation of these groups in S&amp;E education and employment. A Digest highlights key issues and trends through graphics and text, and detailed statistical tables provide data on higher education enrollments, degrees, institutions, and financial support and on employment status, occupations, sectors, and salaries. Links to other NSF and non-NSF sources of data are provided.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;02/28/2013&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/wmpd/archives/wmpd-2013.zip&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf13304&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2013.02.28&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Higher Education Surveys&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Surveys&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Women, Minorities, and Persons with Disabilities in Science and Engineering: 2013&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2013&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4216,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 13-307&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;One out of five U.S. companies with research and development applied for a patent from the U.S. Patent and Trademark Office in 2008, according to the Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS). These companies applied for at least 136,751 U.S. patents and were issued at least 65,879 patents in 2008. This InfoBrief presents summary findings from the 2008 BRDIS pilot survey on the patenting activity of U.S. businesses with R&amp;D.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;02/13/2013&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf13307/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf13307&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2013.02.13&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;,&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;One in Five U.S. Businesses with R&amp;D Applied for a U.S. Patent in 2008&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2013&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Facilities for S&amp;E Research&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Facilities for S&amp;E Research&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4239,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 13-309&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The data in these detailed statistical tables are collected biennially through the National Science Foundation&#39;s congressionally mandated Survey of Science and Engineering Research Facilities. These tables provide data on the amount and condition of science and engineering research space existing at the eligible U.S. colleges and universities. The tables also provide data on the characteristics of networking and computing capacity. Data presented are from the survey conducted for FY 2011.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;02/05/2013&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf13309/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf13309&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2013.02.05&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Science and Engineering Research Facilities&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Science and Engineering Research Facilities&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering Research Facilities: Fiscal Year 2011&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2013&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Facilities for S&amp;E Research&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Facilities for S&amp;E Research&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4252,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 13-310&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Science and engineering research space at the nation&#39;s research-performing colleges and universities increased 3.5% from FY 2009 to FY 2011, growing to 202.9 million net assignable square feet (NASF). The largest share of research space was held by the biological and biomedical sciences (26.8% of total research space), and research space in this field grew by 8.0% during this period, accounting for the bulk of overall growth. Data are from the National Science Foundation&#39;s Survey of Science and Engineering Research Facilities.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;01/30/2013&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf13310/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf13310&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2013.01.30&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Science and Engineering Research Facilities&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Science and Engineering Research Facilities&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Research Space at Academic Institutions Increased 3.5% Between FY 2009 and FY 2011: Biomedical Fields Account for Most Growth&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2013&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding, by Budget Function&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding, by Budget Function&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4242,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 13-308&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report contains information on the budget authority of U.S. federal agencies to fund research and development and R&amp;D plant in FYs 2011–13. FY 2011 data are actual budget authority received by agencies. FY 2012 data (preliminary) are agency estimates of their final appropriations. FY 2013 data are the funding levels proposed by the President&#39;s U.S. government budget for that year. Several tables provide aggregate data on actual federal budget authority for R&amp;D by broad function for FY 2010 and earlier.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;01/17/2013&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf13308/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf13308&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2013.01.17&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding, by Budget Function: Fiscal Years 2011–13&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2013&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding, by Budget Function&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding, by Budget Function&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4237,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 13-312&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Federal budget authority for research and development and R&amp;D plant together totaled $140.6 billion (current dollars) in FY 2012. This is somewhat lower than the total of $144.4 billion in FY 2011 and even further below the $149.0 billion in FY 2010. Most of the drop in funding in these years resulted from reduced budget authority for defense-related R&amp;D. For FY 2013, the total budget authority for R&amp;D activity proposed by the President last year would rise somewhat to $142.2 billion.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;01/16/2013&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf13312/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf13312&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2013.01.16&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Budget Authority for R&amp;D Declines in FYs 2011 and 2012; Modest Increase Proposed for FY 2013&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2013&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4267,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 13-313&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Research and development performed in the United States totaled $406.7 billion (current dollars) in 2010, $2.9 billion above the 2009 level of $403.8 billion. The preliminary estimate for U.S. total R&amp;D in 2011 is $414.0 billion, a further increase of $7.3 billion. This growth in U.S. R&amp;D expenditures in 2010 and 2011 follows a $1.8 billion decline in 2009. R&amp;D performance resumed growth in 2010–11; however, there are indications the expansion is weak.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;01/10/2013&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf13313/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf13313&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2013.01.10&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;U.S. R&amp;D Spending Resumes Growth in 2010 and 2011 but Still Lags Behind the Pace of Expansion of the National Economy&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2013&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;SESTAT: Characteristics of Scientists and Engineers in the United States&quot;,&quot;Characteristics of College Graduates&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;SESTAT: Characteristics of Scientists and Engineers in the United States&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Characteristics of College Graduates&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4271,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 13-311&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Nearly 22 million persons classified as scientists and engineers were employed in the United States as of October 2010: about 5.4 million in science and engineering (S&amp;E) occupations, 7 million in S&amp;E-related occupations, and 9.5 million in occupations other than S&amp;E. These estimates are from the National Science Foundation&#39;s Scientists and Engineers Statistical Data System (SESTAT), and they are presented along with additional information detailing the employment and educational characteristics of scientists and engineers.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;01/07/2013&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf13311/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf13311&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2013.01.07&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;National Survey of College Graduates (NSCG)&quot;,&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;,&quot;National Survey of Recent College Graduates (NSRCG)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Survey of College Graduates (NSCG)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Survey of Recent College Graduates (NSRCG)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Employment and Educational Characteristics of Scientists and Engineers&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2013&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Characteristics of Scientists and Engineers with U.S. Doctorates&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Characteristics of Scientists and Engineers with U.S. Doctorates&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4214,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 13-302&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report contains detailed statistical tables that present data from the 2008 Survey of Doctorate Recipients, a biennial panel survey that collects data on the demographic and general employment characteristics of individuals who have received a research doctorate in a science, engineering, or health field from a U.S. academic institution. The published tables provide information on the number and median salaries of doctoral scientists and engineers by field of doctorate and occupation, by demographic characteristics, and by employment-related characteristics.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;12/20/2012&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf13302/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf13302&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2012.12.20&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Characteristics of Doctoral Scientists and Engineers in the United States: 2008&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2012&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal S&amp;E Support to Universities, Colleges, and NPOs&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal S&amp;E Support to Universities, Colleges, and NPOs&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4199,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 13-303&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The 52 detailed statistical tables presented in this report are based on data from the Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions: FY 2009. Data included in this report are federal academic and nonprofit obligations by type of activity, by year, by geographic division and state, by agency, and by individual institution rankings. Each category of support is broken down into ARRA (American Recovery and Reinvestment Act of 2009) and non-ARRA components.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;12/18/2012&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf13303/start.cfm&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf13303&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2012.12.18&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions: FY 2009&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2012&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4219,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 13-301&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report summarizes trends in U.S. doctoral education by using data from the Survey of Earned Doctorates, an annual census of doctorate recipients from U.S. universities. The report calls attention to important trends in this population—including the representation of women, minorities, and foreign nationals; emergence of new fields of study; time to complete doctoral study; and employment opportunities after graduation. Accompanying online resources include interactive graphics and 70 detailed data tables, available as PDF and Excel files.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;12/05/2012&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/sed/2011/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf13301&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2012.12.05&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities: 2011&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2012&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4198,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 13-306&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;In 2008, an estimated 92% of companies devoted all of their research and development efforts to one line of business. The remaining 8% diversified their R&amp;D spending across multiple lines of businesses, and these companies were the ones that tended to heavily invest in R&amp;D. Companies reporting more than one line of business accounted for $107 billion (33%) of the $328 billion worldwide R&amp;D expense for U.S. businesses. Data are from the Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;11/28/2012&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf13306/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf13306&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2012.11.28&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;,&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;New Data on Line of Business Improve Understanding of U.S. Industry R&amp;D Statistics&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2012&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Academic R&amp;D Expenditures&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD)&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Academic R&amp;D Expenditures&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD)&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4244,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 13-305&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;University spending on research and development in all fields continued to increase between FY 2010 and FY 2011, rising 6.3% to $65.1 billion, according to FY 2011 data from the National Science Foundation&#39;s Higher Education Research and Development Survey. Once again, funding from the American Recovery and Reinvestment Act of 2009 (ARRA) was responsible for much of the increase, with ARRA-funded expenditures totaling $4.2 billion in FY 2011.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;11/26/2012&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf13305/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf13305&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2012.11.26&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Universities Report Highest-Ever R&amp;D Spending of $65 Billion in FY 2011&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2012&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Characteristics of Scientists and Engineers with U.S. Doctorates&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Characteristics of Scientists and Engineers with U.S. Doctorates&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4176,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 13-300&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The proportion of foreign nationals who earned a research doctorate in science, engineering, or health in the United States has grown from about 17% during the 1960s to nearly 40% by 2010. Most foreign recipients who received a U.S. doctorate recently (academic years 2001–07) planned to stay in the United States after receiving their degree. Data in this InfoBrief are from the 2008 Survey of Doctorate Recipients and the 2010 Survey of Earned Doctorates.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;10/17/2012&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf13300/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf13300&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2012.10.17&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;International Mobility and Employment Characteristics among Recent Recipients of U.S. Doctorates&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2012&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reports&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:4259,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 11-309CD&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Women, Minorities, and Persons with Disabilities in Science and Engineering (S&amp;E) provides the latest information about the participation of women, minorities, and persons with disabilities in S&amp;E higher education and employment. Updates in this release include information on degrees awarded (associate&#39;s, bachelor&#39;s, master&#39;s, and doctorate) and undergraduate and graduate enrollment.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;10/11/2012&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/wmpd/archives/wmpd_2011.zip&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf11309cd&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2012.10.11&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Women, Minorities, and Persons with Disabilities: Data Updates&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2012&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Interactive&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:4232,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;WEB 12-302&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics of the National Science Foundation has released a new Web-based data tool for Science and Engineering State Profiles. The tool contains the latest data available and allows users to generate science and engineering profiles that summarize state-specific data on personnel and finances. The State Profiles data tool can display a single state&#39;s profile or a profile containing up to 10 states.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;10/09/2012&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/states/download/state_profiles_2012.xls&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;web12302&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2012.10.09&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Data Update for Science and Engineering State Profiles&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2012&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;International Investment and R&amp;D Data Link&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;International Investment and R&amp;D Data Link&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3872,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 12-327&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report summarizes newly developed data from a project linking research and development and international investment data sets from the National Science Foundation, U.S. Census Bureau, and Bureau of Economic Analysis. The combined data sets provide information on the R&amp;D activities of U.S. multinational companies (MNCs) and of foreign MNCs with U.S. activities, information that was previously unavailable from the original surveys. The project also produced benefits for participating agencies through improvements in survey frame, sampling, and data quality.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/28/2012&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf12327/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf12327&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2012.09.28&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;International Investment and R&amp;D Data Link: 2004–07&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2012&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;International Investment and R&amp;D Data Link&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;International Investment and R&amp;D Data Link&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4180,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 12-332&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Newly developed information from the Research and Development Data Link Project—a joint project that matched survey information from the National Science Foundation, U.S. Census Bureau, and Bureau of Economic Analysis—provides insight on the U.S. R&amp;D activities of multinational companies (MNCs), such as the character of R&amp;D work performed by these companies (basic research, applied research, and development). Findings for matched companies showed that both parent companies of U.S. MNCs and U.S. affiliates of foreign MNCs devote about three-fourths of their R&amp;D expenditures to development activities.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/17/2012&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf12332/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf12332&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2012.09.17&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Project Linking Multi-Agency Surveys Produces New Findings on R&amp;D by Multinational Companies&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2012&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;State Government Research and Development&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;State Government Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4217,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 12-331&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report contains detailed tabular data presenting results from the National Science Foundation&#39;s FY 2009 Survey of State Government R&amp;D. Tables include data on total R&amp;D expenditures and amounts for internal and external performers, the original source of funds (federal or nonfederal), and the amount devoted to basic research activities. Data also include the amount of expenditures for the construction and acquisition of R&amp;D facilities. Also, for the first time, state agency expenditures are presented for individual departments and agencies.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/10/2012&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf12331/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf12331&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2012.09.10&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of State Government Research and Development&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of State Government Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;State Government Research and Development: Fiscal Year 2009&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2012&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD)&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD)&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4211,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 12-330&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report presents detailed tabulated data on research and development expenditures of higher education institutions across all academic disciplines. Tables include academic expenditures by type of institution, field, geographic area, source of funds, and expenditures for research equipment. Many tables show trends over time. Data are from the 2010 Higher Education Research and Development Survey, a redesign of the previous annual collection, the Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/06/2012&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf12330/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf12330&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2012.09.06&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Research and Development: Fiscal Year 2010&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2012&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:4166,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 12-326&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Businesses perform a large share of their research and development in a small number of geographic areas, two of the largest being the San Jose-San Francisco-Oakland combined statistical area (CSA) and the New York-Newark-Bridgeport CSA. In these two areas alone, companies performed at least $29.3 billion of R&amp;D. Data were from companies that responded to the 2008 Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/04/2012&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf12326/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf12326&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2012.09.04&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;,&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Businesses Concentrate Their R&amp;D in a Small Number of Geographic Areas in the United States&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2012&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Characteristics of Recent College Graduates&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Characteristics of Recent College Graduates&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4169,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 12-328&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report presents detailed tabulated data, with associated technical notes, on persons who earned bachelor&#39;s or master&#39;s degrees in science, engineering, or health at U.S. postsecondary institutions. Tables cover graduates in academic years 2006 and 2007 and present data on demographics, education (such as, degrees, GPA, sources of support, and debt level), and employment (such as, sector, primary work activity, and salary). Data are collected directly from individuals through National Science Foundation&#39;s National Survey of Recent College Graduates&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;08/30/2012&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf12328/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf12328&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2012.08.30&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;National Survey of Recent College Graduates (NSRCG)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Survey of Recent College Graduates (NSRCG)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Characteristics of Recent Science and Engineering Graduates: 2008&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2012&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;SESTAT: Characteristics of Scientists and Engineers in the United States&quot;,&quot;Characteristics of College Graduates&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;SESTAT: Characteristics of Scientists and Engineers in the United States&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Characteristics of College Graduates&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4081,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 12-323&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;International collaboration is a key aspect of the globalization of science and engineering. In 2006, one in six U.S. scientists and engineers reported working with individuals in other countries. International collaboration was more likely to occur among persons working in the for-profit sector, men, and those with higher levels of educational attainment. Individuals who earned postsecondary degrees both in the United States and abroad reported the highest levels of international collaboration.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;08/29/2012&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf12323/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf12323&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2012.08.29&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;International Collaborations of Scientists and Engineers in the United States&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2012&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:4171,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 12-329&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;In 2008 businesses in the United States spent $290.7 billion on research and development they performed, 40% of which was applied to health and medical areas ($76.1 billion) and defense areas ($41.5 billion). Over 86% of the health or medical R&amp;D performed by businesses was paid for by the reporting companies themselves. However, most of the defense R&amp;D performed by companies was paid for by the federal government. Data are from the Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;08/22/2012&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf12329/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf12329&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2012.08.22&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;,&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Health and Defense Applications Account for 40% of Business R&amp;D in the United States&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2012&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Working Paper&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Characteristics of College Graduates&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Characteristics of College Graduates&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3998,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NCSES 12-201&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The National Science Foundation collects information on the science and engineering workforce through its National Survey of College Graduates (NSCG). The decennial Census long form, which provided the sampling frame for this survey, was eliminated in 2010, necessitating an alternative NSCG sampling frame. This paper proposes using data from the American Community Survey (ACS) to replace the decennial long-form sampling frame, presents quality enhancements possible when using the ACS, and discusses potential analytic uses that are possible with the ACS.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;08/17/2012&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/ncses12201/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;ncses12201&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2012.08.17&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;National Survey of College Graduates (NSCG)&quot;,&quot;National Survey of Recent College Graduates (NSRCG)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Survey of College Graduates (NSCG)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Survey of Recent College Graduates (NSRCG)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Using the American Community Survey as the Sampling Frame for the National Survey of College Graduates&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2012&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:4143,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 12-325&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Research collaboration involving multiple institutions is a growing trend, as can be seen by increases in the amount of total expenditures for research and development that universities pass through to others and receive from others (pass-through funds). Funds passed through universities to others grew from $1.4 billion in FY 2000 to $3.8 billion in FY 2009, and pass-through funds received by universities from others grew from $1.8 billion to $4.1 billion.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;08/02/2012&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf12325/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf12325&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2012.08.02&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Collaboration in Academic R&amp;D: A Decade of Growth in Pass-Through Funding&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2012&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding, by Budget Function&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding, by Budget Function&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4186,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 12-322&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report contains information on the budget authority of U.S. federal agencies to fund research and development and R&amp;D plant components of their programs in FY 2010–12. FY 2010 data are actual budget authority received by agencies. FY 2011 data (preliminary) are agency estimates of final appropriations. FY 2012 data are proposed funding levels. Several concluding tables provide aggregate data on actual federal budget authority for R&amp;D by broad function for FY 2009 and earlier years.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;08/01/2012&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf12322/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf12322&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2012.08.01&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding by Budget Function:  Fiscal Years 2010–12&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2012&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;State Government Research and Development&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;State Government Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4218,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 12-324&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;State agency expenditures for research and development totaled $1.2 billion in FY 2009, a 7% increase over the FY 2007 total of $1.1 billion, according to a new InfoBrief that reported data from the FY 2009 Survey of State Government R&amp;D Expenditures. The InfoBrief details nationwide and state-by-state totals of R&amp;D activities performed and funded by state government agencies. This is the first time survey data are available by individual state agency.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;07/19/2012&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf12324/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf12324&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2012.07.19&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of State Government Research and Development&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of State Government Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;State Research and Development Expenditures Total $1.2 Billion in FY 2009&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2012&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reports&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:4234,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 11-309AB&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Women, Minorities, and Persons with Disabilities in Science and Engineering (S&amp;E) provides the latest information about the participation of women, minorities, and persons with disabilities in S&amp;E higher education and employment. Updates in this release include information on employment, undergraduate engineering enrollment, first university degrees awarded in selected countries, doctoral degrees, characteristics of academic institutions, sources of support for doctorate recipients, and employment plans of S&amp;E doctorate recipients.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;07/16/2012&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/wmpd/archives/wmpd_2011.zip&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf11309ab&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2012.07.16&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Higher Education Surveys&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Surveys&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Women, Minorities, and Persons with Disabilities in S&amp;E: July 2012 table updates&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2012&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reference&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Master Government List of Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Master Government List of Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4221,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The Master Government List of Federally Funded Research and Development Centers (FFRDCs) has been updated to reflect changes through July 2012. This list, maintained by the National Science Foundation, includes sponsoring agencies, administrator types, R&amp;D activity types, and locations.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;07/13/2012&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://ncses.nsf.gov/resource/master-gov-lists-ffrdc/prior-lists#card3454&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf00000&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2012.07.13&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Master Government List of Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers: FY 2012&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2012&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal Funds for R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Funds for R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4177,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 12-318&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report provides detailed tabular data on the research and development obligations and outlays of federal agencies. Obligations are provided by character of work (basic research, applied research, development, and R&amp;D plant), agency, field of science or engineering (for research only), geographic area, and performer. These data, collected by the Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development, are the primary source of information about federal funding for R&amp;D.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;07/02/2012&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf12318/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf12318&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2012.07.02&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Funds for Research and Development: Fiscal Years 2009–11&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2012&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reports&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:4153,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 12-305&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report presents a statistical summary of the U.S. doctoral education system by using data from the Survey of Earned Doctorates, an annual census of doctorate recipients from U.S. universities. The report calls attention to important trends in demographic characteristics, educational history, sources of financial support, and postgraduation plans of doctorate recipients. The accompanying online resources include interactive graphics and 70 detailed data tables.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;06/29/2012&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/sed/2010/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf12305&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2012.06.29&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities: 2010 (Digest)&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2012&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4185,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 12-321&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report contains detailed statistical tables that present data on current and historical patterns of U.S. R&amp;D expenditures by source of funds, performing sector, character of work, and state. The report also contains information on R&amp;D expenditures at U.S. federally funded research and development centers and international R&amp;D data pertaining to major industrialized nations. Data are drawn from four National Science Foundation surveys.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;06/28/2012&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf12321/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf12321&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2012.06.28&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources: 2009 Data Update&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2012&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal S&amp;E Support to Universities, Colleges, and NPOs&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal S&amp;E Support to Universities, Colleges, and NPOs&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4204,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 12-320&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Funding provided by the American Recovery and Reinvestment Act of 2009 (ARRA) increased current-dollar federal obligations to academic institutions for science and engineering activities by $5.9 billion in FY 2009. ARRA funding accounted for 16.2% of the $36.2 billion obligated to 1,447 academic institutions. This total represents a 27.2% increase in current dollars over FY 2008. Data are from the National Science Foundation&#39;s Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;06/15/2012&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf12320/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf12320&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2012.06.15&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;ARRA Provided $5.9 Billion in Federal Science and Engineering Obligations to Universities and Colleges in FY 2009&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2012&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reports&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:4231,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 13-301&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report will continue the series of Summary Reports that have been produced by SED survey contractors since 1958. It will retain the structure of the 2010 report:  a printed digest format and an online version of the printed report that includes interactive data features.  A comprehensive set of data tables, published (online) as a separate publication,  accompanies the digest.  &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt; As in the 2010 edition, the 2011 digest will include 5 sections, each addressing a central question about the population of 2011 U.S. doctorate recipients, with observations supported by one or more color figures displaying SED data.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;06/07/2012&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/sed/2011/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf13301&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2012.06.07&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities: 2011 (Digest)&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2012&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;R&amp;D Expenditures at Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;R&amp;D Expenditures at Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4197,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 12-319&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The detailed statistical tables presented in this report contain data on R&amp;D expenditures for federally funded research and development centers (FFRDCs) from the FY 2010 FFRDC Research and Development Survey. Expenditures data are displayed for individually named FFRDCs, by source of funding, type of administration, and character of work.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;06/05/2012&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf12319/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf12319&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2012.06.05&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Expenditures: Fiscal Year 2010&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2012&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in S&amp;E&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in S&amp;E&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4202,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 12-317&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;About 632,700 graduate students were enrolled in science, engineering, and health programs in the United States as of fall 2010, up 30% from about 493,300 students in 2000, according to the National Science Foundation&#39;s Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering. First-time, full-time graduate student enrollment in science and engineering programs grew 50% over this period, from about 78,400 students in 2000 to about 118,500 students in 2010.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;05/31/2012&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf12317/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf12317&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2012.05.31&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Graduate Enrollment in Science and Engineering Grew Substantially in the Past Decade but Slowed in 2010&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2012&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal S&amp;E Support to Universities, Colleges, and NPOs&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal S&amp;E Support to Universities, Colleges, and NPOs&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4124,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 12-316&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The 36 detailed statistical tables presented in this report are based on data from the Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions: Fiscal Year 2008.  Data included in this report are federal academic and nonprofit obligations by type of activity (six types of activity for academic, two for nonprofits), by year, by geographic division and state, by agency, and by individual institution rankings.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;05/21/2012&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf12316/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf12316&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2012.05.21&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions: Fiscal Year 2008&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2012&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal S&amp;E Support to Universities, Colleges, and NPOs&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal S&amp;E Support to Universities, Colleges, and NPOs&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4110,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 12-312&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report replaces Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions: FY 2005 (NSF 07-333), released October 2007. After the close of the FY 2007 survey cycle, Department of Defense discovered a programming error that was made during the FY 2005–07 survey cycles and caused each advanced technology development dollar to be reported twice. These detailed statistical tables contain corrected data. Users are advised to use the data reported here, rather than the previously released statistics.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;05/08/2012&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf12312/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf12312&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2012.05.08&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions: FY 2005&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2012&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal Funds for R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Funds for R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4178,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 12-314&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Funding provided by the American Recovery and Reinvestment Act of 2009 (ARRA) increased current-dollar federal obligations for research and development and R&amp;D plant (facilities and fixed equipment) by $10.1 billion in FY 2009 and an estimated $9.1 billion in FY 2010. ARRA funding accounted for 7.4% of the $137 billion total obligated in FY 2009 by federal agencies for R&amp;D and R&amp;D plant and for 6.5% of the estimated $140 billion total in FY 2010.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;04/30/2012&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf12314/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf12314&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2012.04.30&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;ARRA Increased Federal Research and Development and R&amp;D Plant Obligations by $19.2 Billion for FY 2009–10&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2012&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Working Paper&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:4149,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NCSES 12-200&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;A working paper released by the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics identifies trends in the reporting of interdisciplinary dissertation research among doctoral graduates in the United States from 2001 to 2008. Analyses were based on responses to a question in the Survey of Earned Doctorates that asked for respondents&#39; field or fields of dissertation research. From 2001 to 2008, 28.4% of doctoral graduates reported two or more fields of dissertation research, which, in this report, indicates that their research was interdisciplinary. Rates of interdisciplinary dissertations were relatively stable across the 8 years in this analysis, ranging from 27.7% to 30.0%. However, the rate at which doctoral graduates reported interdisciplinary research varied across disciplines. A majority of respondents who reported two fields of research included two fields that are closely related to each other.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;04/27/2012&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/ncses12200/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;ncses12200&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2012.04.27&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Trends in Interdisciplinary Dissertation Research: An Analysis of the Survey of Earned Doctorates&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2012&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal Funds for R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Funds for R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4121,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 12-308&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The annual report Federal Funds for Research and Development: FY 2008–10 consists of 127 tables derived from the Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development: FY 2008–10 and from earlier surveys in this series. These data provide data users with the most timely data possible and are not readily available from any other sources.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;04/18/2012&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf12308&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf12308&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2012.04.18&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Funds for Research and Development: Fiscal Years 2008–10&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2012&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;R&amp;D Expenditures at Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;R&amp;D Expenditures at Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4172,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 12-315&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Research and development expenditures at the nation&#39;s 39 federally funded R&amp;D centers (FFRDCs) rose 10.6% from FY 2009 to FY 2010 ($15.2 billion to $16.8 billion). Over $1 billion of the FY 2010 total was supplied by funds from the American Recovery and Reinvestment Act of 2009. This 10.6% increase is the largest one-year increase since 2002, when expenditures increased 14.5%. Data are from the National Science Foundation&#39;s FFRDC Research and Development Survey.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;03/30/2012&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf12315/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf12315&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2012.03.30&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;ARRA Funding Raises R&amp;D Expenditures within Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers 11% to $16.8 Billion in FY 2010&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2012&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;SESTAT: Characteristics of Scientists and Engineers in the United States&quot;,&quot;Characteristics of College Graduates&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;SESTAT: Characteristics of Scientists and Engineers in the United States&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Characteristics of College Graduates&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4106,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 12-311&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;In 2008, compared with the overall science and engineering (S&amp;E) workforce and compared with the U.S. population as a whole, women were underrepresented in the industrial S&amp;E workforce. Whites and underrepresented minority groups were similarly represented in the industrial and the overall S&amp;E workforce, whereas Asians&#39; representation in the S&amp;E industrial workforce was slightly higher than in the overall S&amp;E workforce.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;03/29/2012&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf12311/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf12311&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2012.03.29&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Diversity in Science and Engineering Employment in Industry&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2012&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD)&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD)&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4196,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 12-313&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;University spending on research and development increased 6.9% between FY 2009 and FY 2010 to $61.2 billion, according to FY 2010 data from the National Science Foundation Higher Education Research and Development Survey. This increase was due in large part to the $2.7 billion in reported expenditures funded by the American Recovery and Reinvestment Act of 2009 (ARRA). The federal government funded 61% of the $61.2 billion total.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;03/28/2012&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf12313/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf12313&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2012.03.28&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;With Help from ARRA, Universities Report $61 Billion in FY 2010 Total R&amp;D; New Details from Redesigned Survey&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2012&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4162,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 12-310&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Spending on research and development conducted in the United States in 2009 totaled $400.5 billion (current dollars), somewhat below the 2008 level of $403.0 billion, although still well above the $377.0 billion spent in 2007. Although the level of total R&amp;D dropped in 2009 by 0.6%, the depth of the decline was much less than the 2.5% decline in gross domestic product.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;03/14/2012&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf12310/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf12310&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2012.03.14&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;U.S. R&amp;D Spending Suffered a Rare Decline in 2009 but Outpaced the Overall Economy&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2012&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4159,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 12-309&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Companies spent $282 billion on research and development performed in the United States during 2009 and $291 billion during 2008, according to estimates from the Business Research and Development and Innovation Survey. Funding from the companies&#39; own sources declined during this period ($233 billion to $225 billion); funding from other sources remained about the same during the 2 years, ($58 billion to $57 billion).&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;03/07/2012&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf12309/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf12309&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2012.03.07&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;,&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business R&amp;D Performed in the United States Cost $291 Billion in 2008 and $282 Billion in 2009&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2012&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4158,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 12-307&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Results from the Business Research and Development and Innovation Survey showed that in 2008 more U.S. businesses reported that trademarks (15%), trade secrets (14%), and copyrights (12%) were important forms of intellectual property (IP) protection, and fewer reported that design patents (6%), utility patents (5%), and mask works (copyright protection for semiconductor products) (3%) were important. However, the level of reliance on each of these forms of IP protection varied considerably across industry sectors.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;02/17/2012&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf12307/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf12307&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2012.02.17&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;,&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business Use of Intellectual Property Protection Documented in NSF Survey&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2012&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal S&amp;E Support to Universities, Colleges, and NPOs&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal S&amp;E Support to Universities, Colleges, and NPOs&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4157,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 12-306&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;In FY 2008 federal agencies obligated $28.4 billion to 1,316 academic institutions for science and engineering activities. Although this total represents a 4.0% increase in current dollars over FY 2004 levels, it represents a 7.5% decrease in inflation-adjusted 2005 dollars. These data are from the National Science Foundation Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;02/02/2012&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf12306/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf12306&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2012.02.02&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Obligations for Science and Engineering to Universities and Colleges Show Little Growth&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2012&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal S&amp;E Support to Universities, Colleges, and NPOs&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal S&amp;E Support to Universities, Colleges, and NPOs&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4094,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 12-302&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report replaces &lt;em&gt;Federal Science 