; (NSF 09-315) released in September 2009. After the close of the FY 2007 survey cycle, the Department of Defense (DOD) discovered a programming error that was made during the FY 2005–07 survey cycles and caused each advanced technology development dollar to be reported twice, as advanced technology development and also as major systems development. These detailed statistical tables contain corrected DOD data for FY 2005–07 and the most up-to-date data for all agencies. Users are advised to use the data reported here, rather than the previously released statistics. &quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;01/24/2012&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf12301/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf12301&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2012.01.24&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions: FY 2007&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2012&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reports&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering Indicators&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering Indicators&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4184,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSB 12-01&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Full online access to &lt;em&gt;Science and Engineering Indicators 2012 &lt;/em&gt;is now available. This biennial volume provides a broad base of quantitative information on the U.S. and international science and engineering enterprise. Supporting appendix tables are available in spreadsheet format. Accompanying &lt;em&gt;SEI 2012&lt;/em&gt; are the interactive &lt;em&gt;Science and Engineering Indicators Digest&lt;/em&gt; and the redesigned State Data Tool, which provides a wealth of information on science and technology infrastructure by state and allows in-depth exploration of these data.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;01/18/2012&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/seind12/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsb1201&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2012.01.18&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering Indicators 2012&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2012&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reports&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:4139,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 11-309-1&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;&lt;em&gt;Women, Minorities, and Persons with Disabilities in Science and Engineering&lt;/em&gt; (S&amp;E) provides a broad base of quantitative information about the participation of women, minorities, and persons with disabilities in S&amp;E higher education and employment. Data in this report are updated on a rolling basis. Updates in this release cover graduate enrollment, degrees awarded (associate&#39;s, bachelor&#39;s, master&#39;s, and doctoral degrees), characteristics of doctorate recipients&#39; academic institutions, primary source of financial support for doctoral recipients, and postdoctorate status.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;01/11/2012&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/wmpd/archives/wmpd_2011.zip&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf113091&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2012.01.11&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Higher Education Surveys&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Surveys&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Women, Minorities, and Persons with Disabilities in Science and Engineering: Data Update&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2012&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Characteristics of Scientists and Engineers with U.S. Doctorates&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Characteristics of Scientists and Engineers with U.S. Doctorates&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4138,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 12-304&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Agencies and researchers have tabulated data on race and ethnicity in various ways over the years. This InfoBrief describes two common methods for tabulating race and ethnicity (minimum and maximum number reporting each race) and uses these methods to present the racial and ethnic diversity of the science, engineering, and health doctoral population. In 2008, 98.8% of the estimated 752,000 members of this population reported being a single race, and 1.2% reported being two or more races.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;01/09/2012&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf12304/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf12304&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2012.01.09&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Racial and Ethnic Diversity among U.S.-Educated Science, Engineering, and Health Doctorate Recipients: Methods of Reporting Diversity&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2012&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in S&amp;E&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in S&amp;E&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4118,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 12-300&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Data presented in these detailed statistical tables are from the Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering: Fall 2009. The tables present information on trends in enrollment of science and engineering graduate students through 2009. Data are presented on trends in number of graduate students by sex, race/ethnicity, citizenship, field, and primary source and mechanism of support.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;12/27/2011&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf12300/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf12300&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2011.12.27&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering: Fall 2009&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2011&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4164,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 12-305&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The data tables for &lt;em&gt;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities: 2010&lt;/em&gt; show trends in the numbers of individuals who receive research doctoral degrees. Detailed information is provided by field of doctorate, doctoral institution, and characteristics of the doctorate recipient. The data also show trends in the postgraduation plans of doctorate recipients. Data are from the Survey of Earned Doctorates, a census of individuals receiving research doctoral degrees from accredited U.S. universities and colleges.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;12/06/2011&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/sed/2010/SED_2010.zip&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf12305&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2011.12.06&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Doctorate Recipients from United States Universities: 2010 Data Tables&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2011&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4161,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 12-303&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;U.S. academic institutions awarded fewer research doctorates in 2010 than in 2009, the first decline in doctorates awarded since 2002. Doctoral awards were down in five of the eight major science fields of study, in engineering, and in non-science and engineering fields. Numbers of doctorates awarded declined for men and women and for temporary visa holders. Among U.S. citizens and permanent residents, minorities continue to outpace whites in growth in science and engineering doctorates earned.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;11/22/2011&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf12303/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf12303&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2011.11.22&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Numbers of Doctorates Awarded in the United States Declined in 2010&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2011&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Women, Minorities, and Persons with Disabilities in STEM&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Women, Minorities, and Persons with Disabilities in STEM&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4115,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 11-320&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Although science, engineering, and health (SEH) doctorates awarded by U.S. institutions to minorities have been increasing in number and as a percentage over the past two decades, minority doctorate numbers and minority faculty numbers remain low. In 2008 underrepresented minorities earned about 9% of all SEH doctorates. Among SEH doctorate holders employed as faculty in universities and colleges, 13% were Asian, 4% were black, and 4% were Hispanic.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;10/11/2011&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf11320/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf11320&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2011.10.11&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Academic Institutions of Minority Faculty with Science, Engineering, and Health Doctorates&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2011&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Facilities for S&amp;E Research&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Facilities for S&amp;E Research&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4069,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 1