provide data on the characteristics of networking and computing capacity. The data presented are from the survey conducted in fiscal year 2007.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/23/2011&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf11322/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf11322&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2011.09.23&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Science and Engineering Research Facilities&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Science and Engineering Research Facilities&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering Research Facilities: Fiscal Year 2007&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2011&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;SESTAT: Characteristics of Scientists and Engineers in the United States&quot;,&quot;Characteristics of College Graduates&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;SESTAT: Characteristics of Scientists and Engineers in the United States&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Characteristics of College Graduates&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4008,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 11-318&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report presents data from the 2006 Scientists and Engineers Statistical Data System (SESTAT), a comprehensive and integrated system of information about employment, education, and demographic characteristics of scientists and engineers in the United States. The detailed statistical tables presented here provide information on the number and median salaries of scientists and engineers by field of highest degree and occupation, as well as information on demographic and employment-related characteristics.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/14/2011&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf11318/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf11318&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2011.09.14&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Characteristics of Scientists and Engineers in the United States: 2006&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2011&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:4073,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 11-307&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;U.S. exports of advanced technology products (ATP) fell 9% during the 2009 recession to $245 billion, less than half the decline of non-ATP exports. The U.S. decline was steepest in its ATP exports to Asia compared with its ATP exports to the European Union and the NAFTA trade zone. U.S. ATP exports recovered in 2010, with U.S. exports to Asia growing faster than its ATP exports to other regions, but lagged the growth in non-ATP exports.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/01/2011&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf11307/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf11307&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2011.09.01&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;U.S. Exports of Advanced Technology Products Declined Less Than Other U.S. Exports in 2009&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2011&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4031,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 11-321&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The data presented in Science and Engineering Doctorate Awards: 2007–08 show trends in the numbers of individuals who receive doctorates in science and engineering fields. Detailed information is provided by field of doctorate, doctoral institution, and characteristics of the doctorate recipient. The data also show trends in the postgraduation plans of doctorate recipients. Data are from the Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED), a census of individuals receiving research doctoral degrees from accredited U.S. universities and colleges.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;08/22/2011&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf11321/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf11321&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2011.08.22&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering Doctorate Awards: 2007–08&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2011&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal Funds for R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Funds for R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4129,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 11-324&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Funds obligated by federal agencies for research are projected to rise by 8.8% between fiscal year (FY) 2008 and FY 2009 in inflation-adjusted constant dollars, according to preliminary data from the National Science Foundation. This represents the first constant dollar increase in these obligations since FY 2004 and is due to the funds provided by the American Reinvestment and Recovery Act. However, research obligations for FY 2009 were estimated to be 2.3% less than they were for FY 2004.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;07/26/2011&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf11324/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf11324&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2011.07.26&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Funding of Basic and Applied Research Increases in FY 2009&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2011&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in S&amp;E&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in S&amp;E&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4101,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 11-319&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;From 1989 to 2009 the number of American minority graduate students in science and engineering (S&amp;E) more than doubled, growing from approximately 37,700 to 92,700. This gain almost tripled the number of Hispanics and American Indians/Alaska Natives and more than doubled the number of blacks and Asians/Pacific Islanders in graduate S&amp;E programs. Although enrollment grew substantially for persons from racial and ethnic minority groups, the number of non-Hispanic whites increased by less than 10%.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;07/20/2011&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf11319/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf11319&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2011.07.20&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Two Decades of Increasing Diversity More than Doubled the Number of Minority Graduate Students in Science and Engineering&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2011&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Academic R&amp;D Expenditures&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Academic R&amp;D Expenditures&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4065,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 11-313&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;These detailed statistical tables present data on expenditures on research and development among institutions of higher education. Tables include academic expenditures by type of institution, field, geographic area, source of funds (federal, state and local, industry, and institutional), and expenditures for research equipment. Many tables show trends over time. This report includes data from the Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges: Fiscal Year 2009.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;07/13/2011&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf11313/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf11313&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2011.07.13&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Academic Research and Development Expenditures: Fiscal Year 2009&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2011&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;SESTAT: Characteristics of Scientists and Engineers in the United States&quot;,&quot;Characteristics of College Graduates&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;SESTAT: Characteristics of Scientists and Engineers in the United States&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Characteristics of College Graduates&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4010,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 11-315&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report presents data from the 2003 Scientists and Engineers Statistical Data System (SESTAT), a comprehensive and integrated system of information about employment, education, and demographic characteristics of scientists and engineers in the United States. The detailed statistical tables presented here provide information on the number and median salaries of scientists and engineers by field of highest degree and occupation, as well as information on demographic and employment-related characteristics.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;07/13/2011&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf11315/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf11315&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2011.07.13&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Characteristics of Scientists and Engineers in the United States: 2003 (SESTAT)&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2011&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in S&amp;E&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in S&amp;E&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4072,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 11-311&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Data presented in these detailed statistical tables are from the Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering: Fall 2008. The tables present information on trends in enrollment of science and engineering graduate students through 2008. Data are presented on trends in number of graduate students by sex, race/ethnicity, citizenship, field, and primary source and mechanism of support.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;07/13/2011&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf11311/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf11311&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2011.07.13&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering: Fall 2008&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2011&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Characteristics of Recent College Graduates&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Characteristics of Recent College Graduates&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4013,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 11-317&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Over academic years 2001 to 2007 the percentage of science, engineering, and health graduates who had attended community college at some point in their studies remained fairly steady: around 50% for bachelor&#39;s degree recipients and just under 45% for master&#39;s degree recipients. Community college attendance was driven largely by a desire to earn credits toward a bachelor&#39;s degree, followed by financial reasons and then by a desire to gain further skills or knowledge in an academic or occupational field.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;06/27/2011&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf11317/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf11317&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2011.06.27&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;National Survey of Recent College Graduates (NSRCG)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Survey of Recent College Graduates (NSRCG)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Community Colleges: Playing an Important Role in the Education of Science, Engineering, and Health Graduates&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2011&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3956,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 11-301&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report presents all of the statistics produced from the 2006 and 2007 cycles of the Survey of Industrial Research and Development, by size of company, the sources of R&amp;D funds, character of R&amp;D, and industry using the North American Industrial Classification System (NAICS).  Also included are tables covering historical trends in R&amp;D; R&amp;D as a percentage of sales, employment, and cost of R&amp;D scientists and engineers; and R&amp;D statistics by state.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;06/24/2011&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf11301/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf11301&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2011.06.24&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;,&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Research and Development in Industry:  2006–07&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2011&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reference&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Master Government List of Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Master Government List of Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4128,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The Master Government List of Federally Funded Research and Development Centers (FFRDCs) has been updated to reflect changes through June 2011. This list, maintained by the National Science Foundation, includes sponsoring agencies, administrator types, R&amp;D activity types, and locations.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;06/22/2011&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://ncses.nsf.gov/resource/master-gov-lists-ffrdc/prior-lists#card3454&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf00000&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2011.06.22&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Master Government List of Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers: FY 2011&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2011&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;S&amp;E Degrees&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;S&amp;E Degrees&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4062,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 11-316&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report provides information on the number and types of bachelor&#39;s, master&#39;s, and doctoral degrees awarded by U.S. institutions in science and engineering fields. It also provides information on the sex of the degree recipients. The report includes select data from 1966-2008.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;06/13/2011&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf11316/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf11316&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2011.06.13&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Integrated Postsecondary Education Data System Completions Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Integrated Postsecondary Education Data System Completions Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;S&amp;E Degrees: 1966–2008&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2011&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Facilities for S&amp;E Research&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Facilities for S&amp;E Research&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4098,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 11-312&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;According to the National Science Foundation&#39;s biennial Survey of Science and Engineering Research Facilities, the amount of science and engineering (S&amp;E) research space at research-performing colleges and universities increased 4% between FY 2007 and FY 2009, from 188 million to 196 million net assignable square feet. This increase is almost three times the amount of growth found between FY 2005 and FY 2007 (1.5%) and follows two consecutive survey cycles with slowing growth.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;05/20/2011&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf11312/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf11312&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2011.05.20&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Science and Engineering Research Facilities&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Science and Engineering Research Facilities&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Research Space at Academic Institutions Increased 4% Between FY 2007 and\nFY 2009&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2011&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;R&amp;D Expenditures at Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;R&amp;D Expenditures at Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4067,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 11-314&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;These detailed statistical tables present data on R&amp;D expenditures for federally funded research and development centers (FFRDCs) from the FY 2009 Survey of R&amp;D Expenditures at FFRDCs. Expenditures data are displayed by FFRDC, source of funding, type of administration, and character of work.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;05/12/2011&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf11314/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf11314&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2011.05.12&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Expenditures: Fiscal Year 2009&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2011&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;R&amp;D Expenditures at Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;R&amp;D Expenditures at Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4096,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 11-310&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Research and development expenditures at the nation&#39;s 39 federally funded R&amp;D centers (FFRDCs) increased 3.5% between FY 2008 and FY 2009 ($14.7 billion to $15.2 billion), according to FY 2009 data from the National Science Foundation&#39;s Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at FFRDCs. The annual increase in FFRDC R&amp;D expenditures has outpaced the general rate of inflation in the economy for the past 3 years. Federal government funding accounted for 97.1% of the FY 2009 FFRDC expenditures total.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;03/02/2011&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf11310/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf11310&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2011.03.02&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;R&amp;D Expenditures at Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers Reach $15.2 Billion in FY 2009&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2011&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reports&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Women, Minorities, and Persons with Disabilities in STEM&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Women, Minorities, and Persons with Disabilities in STEM&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4093,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 11-309&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Women, Minorities, and Persons with Disabilities in Science and Engineering (S&amp;E) provides a broad base of quantitative information about the participation of women, minorities, and persons with disabilities in S&amp;E education and employment. A Digest highlights key issues and trends through graphics and text, and detailed statistical tables provide data on higher education enrollments, degrees, institutions, and financial support and on employment status, occupations, sectors, and salaries. Links to other NSF and non-NSF sources of data are provided.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;02/28/2011&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/wmpd/archives/wmpd_2011.zip&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf11309&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2011.02.28&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Higher Education Surveys&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Surveys&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Women, Minorities, and Persons with Disabilities in Science and Engineering: 2011&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2011&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;News&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:4125,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The Division of Science Resources Statistics is now the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics (NCSES). Mandated by the America COMPETES Reauthorization Act of 2010, the name change conveys the central role NCSES plays as a federal statistical agency and signals its expanded responsibilities. NCSES will retain its reporting line to the National Science Foundation&#39;s Directorate for Social, Behavioral, and Economic Sciences. The name change was announced in a press release on February 15, 2011.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;02/15/2011&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/news/news_summ.jsp?cntn_id=118648&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf00000&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2011.02.15&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;NSF&#39;s Division of Science Resources Statistics Is Now the National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2011&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Characteristics of Scientists and Engineers with U.S. Doctorates&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Characteristics of Scientists and Engineers with U.S. Doctorates&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4108,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 11-308&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;In October 2008, after the start of the recent economic recession, the increase in unemployment evident in the U.S. labor force was less apparent among science, engineering, or health (SEH) doctoral recipients. The unemployment rate was at 1.7% for the doctoral SEH labor force compared with 6.6% for the total U.S. labor force. Unemployment rates were not significantly different for individuals who held their doctorate for 2 years or less and those who held their degree longer.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;01/27/2011&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf11308/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf11308&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2011.01.27&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Unemployment Among Doctoral Scientists and Engineers Remained Below the National Average in 2008&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2011&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal Funds for R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Funds for R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4084,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 11-304&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;FY 2000–07 R&amp;D obligations are revised for Air Force, Department of Defense, and federal government totals. Air Force mistakenly excluded funds for operational systems development from R&amp;D totals reported to the FY 2000–02 through the FY 2007–09 Survey of Federal Funds for R&amp;D. Federal development was revised up by 9% for FY 2000 and 23% for FY 2007.Total federal R&amp;D funding was revised up 4% for FY 2000 and 12% for FY 2007.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;01/03/2011&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf11304/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf11304&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2011.01.03&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Department of the Air Force Revises Development Data for FY 2000–07&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2011&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:4080,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 11-303&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Between 2000 and 2009, the federal science and engineering workforce became more diverse, with increasing percentages of women (21% to 27%) and minorities (18% to 22%). In 2009, minority women accounted for 8% of the federal scientists and engineers, and persons with disabilities accounted for 5%. Occupational distribution differed by sex, race/ethnicity, and disability status. Differences in diversity within specific federal agencies were related to differences in diversity of occupation.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;01/03/2011&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf11303/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf11303&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2011.01.03&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Diversity in the Federal Science and Engineering Workforce&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2011&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Characteristics of Scientists and Engineers with U.S. Doctorates&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Characteristics of Scientists and Engineers with U.S. Doctorates&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3792,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 11-302&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Between 1993 and 2003 the age distribution of scientists and engineers with doctoral degrees working in postsecondary educational institutions shifted: in 2003 a higher percentage of persons age 56 and older were working in these institutions, with an offsetting reduction in those aged 36–55. Among those aged 56–70, retirement rates generally increased; among those aged 71–75, retirement rates decreased, with the largest drop occurring between 1993 and 1995 (87.5% to 83.2%).&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;12/23/2010&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf11302/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf11302&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2010.12.23&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;The End of Mandatory Retirement for Doctoral Scientists and Engineers in Postsecondary Institutions: Retirement Patterns 10 Years Later&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2010&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reports&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4086,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 11-306&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;A statistical summary of the U.S. doctoral education system is presented using data from the Survey of Earned Doctorates, an annual census of doctorate recipients from U.S. universities. The report calls attention to important trends in demographic characteristics, educational history, sources of financial support, and postgraduation plans of doctorate recipients. The accompanying online resources include interactive graphics and 66 detailed data tables, available both in portable document format (PDF) and as Microsoft Excel files.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;12/01/2010&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/sed/2009/SED_2009.zip&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf11306&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2010.12.01&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities: 2009&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2010&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4087,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 11-305&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Postgraduation plans of new doctorate recipients are examined for 2004–09. In this period, 2009 had the largest 1-year increase in proportion of postdoc positions taken. The proportion of those with employment prospects was just under the 2008 figure and about equaled 2007. Doctorates in science and engineering (S&amp;E) fields in 2009 were up 1.9% owing to growth in numbers of female S&amp;E doctorate recipients. Doctorates earned by temporary visa holders were down 3.5% from 2008.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;11/22/2010&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf11305/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf11305&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2010.11.22&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Numbers of Doctorates Awarded Continue to Grow in 2009; Indicators of Employment Outcomes Mixed&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2010&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;News&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:4099,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;SRS 11-203&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The SRS Buzz is a quarterly newsletter from the National Science Foundation&#39;s Division of Science Resources Statistics (SRS).  The Buzz contains highlights from SRS&#39;s most recently released data and publications.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;11/22/2010&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/newsletter/srs11203/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;srs11203&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2010.11.22&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;SRS Buzz July-September 2010&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2010&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Working Paper&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:1879,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;SRS 11-201&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This is the third report from a study by the National Science Foundation investigating the flattening of U.S. scientific article production in the 1990s. This report examines quantifiable relationships among publications, resource inputs, and institutional characteristics in the U.S. top 200 R&amp;D performing academic institutions. Variations among universities in academic R&amp;D expenditures, number of S&amp;E postdoctoral appointees, and, to a lesser extent, number of S&amp;E doctorate recipients largely account for differences in these universities&#39; publication outputs.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;11/19/2010&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/srs11201/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;srs11201&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2010.11.19&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Surveys&quot;,&quot;Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges&quot;,&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Surveys&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;U.S. Academic Scientific Publishing&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2010&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Working Paper&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:3952,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;SRS 11-200&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Declining response rates to government surveys reduce effective sample size and may cause nonresponse bias. Responding to this trend, the National Science Foundation studied effects of offering incentives for response to its Scientists and Engineers Statistical Data System (SESTAT) surveys. Results show that monetary incentives improve response rates and data quality, whereas nonmonetary incentives did neither. Prepaid monetary incentives may be more cost effective than postpaid incentives; timing of monetary offers may not affect their impact.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;11/03/2010&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/srs11200/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;srs11200&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2010.11.03&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;National Survey of College Graduates (NSCG)&quot;,&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;,&quot;National Survey of Recent College Graduates (NSRCG)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Survey of College Graduates (NSCG)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Survey of Recent College Graduates (NSRCG)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Incentive Experiments: NSF Experiences&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2010&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reports&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:4064,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 09-305DFG&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Women, Minorities, and Persons with Disabilities in Science and Engineering (S&amp;E) provides a broad base of quantitative information about the participation of women, minorities, and persons with disabilities in S&amp;E education and employment. Data in this report are updated on a rolling basis. Updates in this release cover &lt;a href=&#39;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/wmpd/enroll.cfm&#39;&gt;graduate enrollment&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href=&#39;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/wmpd/degrees.cfm&#39;&gt;doctoral degrees&lt;/a&gt;, and &lt;a href=&#39;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/wmpd/postdoc.cfm&#39;&gt;postdoctorate status&lt;/a&gt;.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;10/26/2010&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/wmpd/archives/wmpd_2009.zip&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf09305dfg&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2010.10.26&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Higher Education Surveys&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Surveys&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Women, Minorities, and Persons with Disabilities in S&amp;E: Graduate Enrollment, Doctoral Degrees, and Postdoctorate Status Data Update&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2010&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding, by Budget Function&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding, by Budget Function&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4083,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 10-323&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Data are reported on the budget authority of U.S. federal agencies to fund R&amp;D and R&amp;D plant components of their programs in FY 2009–11. FY 2011 data are proposed funding levels. FY 2010 data (preliminary) are agency estimates of final appropriations. FY 2009 data are actual budget authority received by agencies. Figures do not include estimates for R&amp;D funds appropriated through the American Recovery and Reinvestment Act (ARRA). ARRA funds are presented separately in the data tables.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;10/13/2010&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf10323/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf10323&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2010.10.13&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding by Budget Function: Fiscal Years 2009–11&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2010&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4078,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 11-300&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;New Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS) data provide a first look at the incidence of innovation by businesses operating in the United States. About 22% of manufacturing companies reported one or more product innovations in 2006–08; about 22% introduced process innovations. Nonmanufacturing companies—the majority represented in the survey—reported innovation incidence of 8% for products and 8% for processes. Companies performing and/or funding R&amp;D reported a far higher incidence of innovation than did companies without R&amp;D activity.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;10/05/2010&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf11300/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf11300&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2010.10.05&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;,&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;NSF Releases New Statistics on Business Innovation&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2010&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Facilities for S&amp;E Research&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Facilities for S&amp;E Research&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4058,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 10-328&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The number of institutions with bandwidth of at least 100 megabits increased by over 10 percentage points from FY 2005, and at least 70% had high performance networks connections, such as Internet2 or National LambdaRail. In addition, 20% made high-performance computing resources available to their campus community, and a subset shared these resources with other organizations outside their own. Cyberinfrastructure resources are substantially greater at doctorate-granting than at nondoctorate-granting institutions.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/27/2010&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf10328/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf10328&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2010.09.27&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Science and Engineering Research Facilities&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Science and Engineering Research Facilities&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Institutions Increase Networking Capacity, Gap Between Doctorate- and Nondoctorate-Granting Institutions Widens&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2010&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Academic R&amp;D Expenditures&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Academic R&amp;D Expenditures&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4066,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 10-329&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;University spending on R&amp;D in science and engineering reached $54.9 billion in FY 2009, with almost 60% funded by the federal government ($32.6 billion). The institutions themselves contributed the second largest amount of funding for their R&amp;D projects ($11.2 billion). Medical and biological sciences continue to account for over half of all R&amp;D expenditures with $18.2 billion and $10.2 billion, respectively. Data from a redesigned Higher Education R&amp;D Survey will be available by the fall of 2011.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/27/2010&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf10329/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf10329&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2010.09.27&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Universities Report $55 Billion in Science and Engineering R&amp;D Spending for FY 2009; Redesigned Survey to Launch in 2010&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2010&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding, by Budget Function&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding, by Budget Function&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4082,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 10-327&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Federal agencies report a proposed total budget authority of $143 billion for federally funded R&amp;D in FY 2011, down 0.3% in current dollars from FY 2010 preliminary appropriations of $143.9 billion and down an expected 2.3% in constant dollars. Proposed nondefense R&amp;D is $61.4 billion, up 6.3%, and defense R&amp;D is $82.0 billion, down 4.8% from FY 2010. The defense share of the FY 2011 proposed R&amp;D budget is 57.2%, down from 59.8% in FY 2010.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/09/2010&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf10327/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf10327&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2010.09.09&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Proposed Federal R&amp;D Funding for FY 2011 Dips to $143 Billion, with Cuts in National Defense R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2010&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:false,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Interactive&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Academic Institution Profiles&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Academic Institution Profiles&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4089,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;SRS 10-402&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The FY 2007 edition of the annual Academic Institutional Profiles presents institutional rankings and other institution-specific data from four surveys: Survey of R&amp;D Expenditures at Universities and Colleges; Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions; Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering; and Survey of Earned Doctorates.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;08/27/2010&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://ncsesdata.nsf.gov/profiles/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;srs10402&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2010.08.27&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Academic Institutional Profiles: 2007&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2010&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;R&amp;D Expenditures at Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;R&amp;D Expenditures at Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4052,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 10-325&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;New survey statistics indicate that R&amp;D expenditures by all federally funded research and developments centers (FFRDCs) totaled $14.7 billion in FY 2008. This represented about 35% of all federal R&amp;D performance that year and 4% of all U.S. R&amp;D. This total has generally increased in recent years&#39; averaging 3.9% annually over FY 2003-08. Funding by the federal government accounted for about 97% of the FY 2008 expenditure total.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;08/11/2010&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf10325/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf10325&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2010.08.11&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;FFRDC Research and Development Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;NSF Releases Statistics on R&amp;D Expenditures in FY 2008 by Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2010&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Characteristics of Recent College Graduates&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Characteristics of Recent College Graduates&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3890,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 10-318&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report presents detailed tabulated data, with associated technical notes, on persons who earned bachelor&#39;s or master&#39;s degrees in science, engineering, and health at U.S. postsecondary institutions. Tables cover graduates in academic years 2003-05 and present data on demographics (sex, race/ethnicity, citizenship), education (degrees, GPA, sources of support, debt level), and employment (sector, primary work activity, salary). Data are collected directly from individuals through the NSF National Survey of Recent College Graduates.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;08/10/2010&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf10318/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf10318&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2010.08.10&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;National Survey of Recent College Graduates (NSRCG)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Survey of Recent College Graduates (NSRCG)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Characteristics of Recent Science and Engineering Graduates: 2006&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2010&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;News&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:4104,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;SRS 10-403&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The SRS Buzz is a quarterly newsletter from the National Science Foundation&#39;s Division of Science Resources Statistics (SRS).  The Buzz contains highlights from SRS&#39;s most recently released data and publications.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;07/22/2010&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/newsletter/srs10403/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;srs10403&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2010.07.22&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;SRS Buzz April-June 2010&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2010&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:4045,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 10-324&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Enrollment of foreign science and engineering (S&amp;E) students at U.S. academic institutions grew from 2008 to 2009, with increases in students from China, India, the Middle East, and Africa but declines in students from Europe, Central and South America, and Canada. Numbers of first-time foreign S&amp;E students, particularly those from India, declined. Persistent weakness in the U.S. and global economies may affect numbers of new foreign students enrolling in U.S. institutions over the next several years.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;07/08/2010&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf10324/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf10324&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2010.07.08&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Foreign Science and Engineering Students in the United States&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2010&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4060,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 10-326&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;U.S. companies that performed or funded R&amp;D domestically or in their overseas locations employed 27.1 million workers worldwide in 2008, including 1.9 million R&amp;D employees, according to preliminary estimates of the new Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS). Domestic R&amp;D employment was estimated at 1.5 million, 77% of worldwide R&amp;D employment. R&amp;D employment intensity and company-performed R&amp;D per R&amp;D employee varied among domestic and foreign activities and by industry. Final data are expected in early 2011.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;07/08/2010&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf10326/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf10326&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2010.07.08&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;,&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;New Employment Statistics from the 2008 Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2010&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:3983,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 10-321&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Companies in the United States (U.S.-owned and U.S. affiliates) with R&amp;D activity reported worldwide sales of $11 trillion in 2008 and worldwide R&amp;D expenses of $330 billion. Company-performed R&amp;D accounted for $292 billion of worldwide R&amp;D expense, mostly ($234 billion) conducted in companies&#39; own U.S. facilities. Worldwide R&amp;D performance expenditures totaled $346 billion, $54 billion paid for by others. Data are the first from the Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey, developed by NSF and the U.S. Census Bureau.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;06/30/2010&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf10321/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf10321&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2010.06.30&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Public Financing of Research and Development, by State: 2006&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2010&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3750,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 10-319&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report presents all of the statistics produced from the 2005 cycle of the Survey of Industrial Research and Development by size of company, the sources of R&amp;D funds, character of R&amp;D, and industry using the North American Industrial Classification System (NAICS).  Also included are tables covering historical trends in R&amp;D; R&amp;D as a percentage of sales; employment and cost of R&amp;D scientists and engineers; and R&amp;D statistics by state.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;06/25/2010&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf10319/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf10319&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2010.06.25&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;,&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Research and Development in Industry: 2005&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2010&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reports&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:4050,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 09-305CE&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This release includes updates to &lt;a href=&#39;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/wmpd/degrees.cfm&#39;&gt;Undergraduate and Master&#39;s Degrees&lt;/a&gt; data.&lt;br /&gt; &lt;/br&gt;&lt;br /&gt; Women, Minorities, and Persons with Disabilities in Science and Engineering provides a broad base of quantitative information about participation of women, minorities, and persons with disabilities in science and engineering education and employment. The report contains data tables organized by topic (e.g., undergraduate enrollment, graduate degrees, employment) and also by group (e.g., Hispanics, minority women, persons with disabilities).&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;06/22/2010&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/wmpd/tables.cfm&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf09305ce&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2010.06.22&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Higher Education Surveys&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Surveys&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Women, Minorities, and Persons with Disabilities in S&amp;E - Undergraduate and Master&#39;s Degrees Data Update&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2010&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in S&amp;E&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in S&amp;E&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3973,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 10-307&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Data presented in these tables are from the Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering, Fall 2007. The tables present information on trends in enrollment of science and engineering graduate students through 2007. Data are presented on trends in number of graduate students by sex, race/ethnicity, citizenship, field, and primary source and mechanism of support.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;06/09/2010&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf10307/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf10307&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2010.06.09&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering: Fall 2007&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2010&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in S&amp;E&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in S&amp;E&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4002,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 10-320&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;A record 108,819 first-time, full-time science and engineering (S&amp;E) graduate students enrolled in fall 2008—20.6% of all S&amp;E graduate enrollments and a 1-year increase of 7.8%. First-time enrollment in engineering grew faster among U.S. citizens and permanent residents than among foreign students with temporary visas. Graduate enrollment in S&amp;E fields increased slightly more among men (2.9%) than among women (2.1%) from 2007 to 2008, reversing a long-term trend toward relatively greater enrollment gains by women.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;06/02/2010&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf10320/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf10320&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2010.06.02&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Jump in Fall 2008 Enrollments of First-Time, Full-Time S&amp;E Graduate Students&quot;,&