p;D Expenditures&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3944,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 10-311&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Detailed statistical tables present data on R&amp;D expenditures of higher education institutions and federally funded research and development centers (FFRDCs). Tables include academic expenditures by type of institution, field, geographic area, source of funds (Federal, state and local, industry, institutional), and expenditures for research equipment. Many tables show trends over time. Data from the Survey of R&amp;D Expenditures at Universities and Colleges: Fiscal Year 2008 are included in this report.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;04/06/2010&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf10311/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf10311&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2010.04.06&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Academic R&amp;D Expenditures: FY 2008&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2010&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:3776,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 10-315&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;From 2004 to 2008 employment in all occupations grew an average annual 1.3%. The comparable rate was 3.3 % for science and engineering (S&amp;E) occupations and 2.3% for STEM (science, technology, engineering, mathematics) occupations. In 2008 median annual earnings were $32,390 for all occupations, $72,940 for S&amp;E occupations, and $69,570 for STEM occupations. S&amp;E occupations represented 4.3% of the overall workforce and 7%–15% of the workforce in metropolitan areas with the highest S&amp;E employment.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;04/06/2010&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf10315/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf10315&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2010.04.06&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Occupational Employment Statistics Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Occupational Employment Statistics Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Employment in Science and Engineering Occupations reached 5.8 million in 2008&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2010&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reports&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:3981,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 09-305AB&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This release includes updates to &lt;a href=&#39;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/wmpd/usdemo.cfm&#39;&gt;U.S. Demographics&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href=&#39;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/wmpd/enroll.cfm&#39;&gt;Undergraduate Enrollment&lt;/a&gt; data.&lt;br /&gt; &lt;/br&gt;&lt;br /&gt; Women, Minorities, and Persons with Disabilities in Science and Engineering provides a broad base of quantitative information about participation of women, minorities, and persons with disabilities in science and engineering education and employment. The report contains data tables organized by topic (e.g., undergraduate enrollment, graduate degrees, employment) and also by group (e.g., Hispanics, minority women, persons with disabilities).&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;04/06/2010&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/wmpd/tables.cfm&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf09305ab&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2010.04.06&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Women, Minorities, and Persons with Disabilities in S&amp;E - U.S. Demographics and Undergraduate Enrollment Data Update&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2010&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:3931,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 10-316&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Statistics on doctorate recipients reporting two or more dissertation research fields were used to indicate interdisciplinary research. From 2001 to 2008 reports of multiple research fields fluctuated between 24% and 30%, mostly in the same broad field as the primary research. Biological sciences was the most frequent secondary science and engineering (S&amp;E) field; non-S&amp;E secondary fields were most common in social science, psychology, and computer sciences. Massachusetts Institute of Technology had the largest share of multiple-field reports (44%).&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;03/29/2010&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf10316/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf10316&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2010.03.29&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Interdisciplinary Dissertation Research&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2010&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4000,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 10-314&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Detailed statistical tables from four NSF surveys present current and historical patterns of U.S. R&amp;D expenditures by source of funds, performing sector, character of work, monetary unit, and state. R&amp;D expenditures at U.S. federally funded research and development centers (FFRDCs), and international R&amp;D data pertaining to major industrialized nations are also provided.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;03/29/2010&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf10314/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf10314&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2010.03.29&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources: 2008 Data Update&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2010&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding, by Budget Function&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding, by Budget Function&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4012,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 10-313&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Federal agencies have reported a preliminary R&amp;D budget authority of $157 billion in FY 2009, up 12% over FY 2008. American Recovery and Reinvestment Act (ARRA) funds account for $14.7 billion of the total. With 98% of ARRA funds scheduled for nondefense functions, nondefense R&amp;D—$71.6 billion—is up 29.3% over FY 2008. Health is the largest category. Preliminary defense R&amp;D authority—$85.6 billion—is up 1% over FY 2008 and allocated mostly for development.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;03/23/2010&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf10313/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf10313&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2010.03.23&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Recovery Act Funding Boosts FY 2009 Federal R&amp;D to an Estimated $157 Billion, 12% over FY 2008 Total&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2010&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3967,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 10-304&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Over the period 2003-07, R&amp;D performance by small U.S. firms (fewer than 500 employees) grew slightly faster than performance by larger companies. Small firms captured 18.7% of total industrial R&amp;D performance in 2007 and spent 8.6% of sales revenues on R&amp;D, versus 3.4% for larger firms. Scientists and engineers accounted for 13.3% of small firms&#39; total employment, versus 6.1% for larger firms. Microfirms (5-24 employees) show even greater patterns of growth and emphasis on R&amp;D.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;03/19/2010&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf10304/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf10304&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2010.03.19&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Indicators of U.S. Small Business&#39;s Role in R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2010&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;R&amp;D Expenditures at Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers&quot;],&quot;dataCollectio