onDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:4030,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;SRS 09-321&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This interagency report provides data from the Survey of Earned Doctorates on research doctoral degrees granted by U.S. institutions in all fields of study in academic years 2006.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;02/05/2010&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/doctorates/pdf/sed2006.pdf&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;srs09321&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2010.02.05&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities: Summary Report 2006&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2010&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3999,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 10-312&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;New preliminary estimates by the National Science Foundation indicate that U.S. R&amp;D expenditures totaled $397.6 billion (current dollars) in 2008, up from $372.5 billion in 2007. This increase in overall national R&amp;D performance represented growth in 2008 of 6.7% over the 2007 level. In 2008, the business sector continued to be the largest performer of U.S. R&amp;D. With regard to R&amp;D funding, the business sector was again the predominant source in 2008, providing an estimated 67.4% of the total. U.S. expenditures on R&amp;D totaled 2.79 % of GDP in 2008. Overall R&amp;D spending by the United States continues to far exceed that of all other countries.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;01/29/2010&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf10312/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf10312&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2010.01.29&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;New NSF Estimates Indicate that U.S. R&amp;D Spending Continued to Grow in 2008&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2010&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reports&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3996,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 10-309&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This interagency report provides data from the Survey of Earned Doctorates on research doctoral degrees granted by U.S. institutions in all fields of study in academic years 2007 and 2008. The report provides an overview of trends in doctorate-granting-institutions and in doctorate recipients&#39; study fields, demographic characteristics, parental education, educational history, time to degree, financial support and indebtedness, and postgraduation plans for 2008. Detailed data tables are provided for 2007 and 2008.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;12/02/2009&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf10309/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf10309&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2009.12.02&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities: Summary Report 2007-08&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2009&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3997,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 10-308&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Doctorates awarded by U.S. universities rose for the sixth year, to 48,802 in 2008, but the 1.4% increase over 2007 was the smallest for the period. Increases in science and engineering (S&amp;E) doctorates continued to drive overall growth, and growth in S&amp;E doctorates continued to be higher for women than for men. Most S&amp;E fields showed gains in 2008; biological sciences topped numbers of awards. Doctorates awarded in non-S&amp;E fields dropped 2.1% from 2007; education showed the only increase.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;11/19/2009&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf10308/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf10308&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2009.11.19&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Numbers of U.S. Doctorates Awarded Rise for Sixth Year, but Growth Slower&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2009&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;State Government Research and Development&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;State Government Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3989,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 10-301&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;These tables present the results of the National Science Foundation FY 2007 Survey of State R&amp;D Expenditures. The results include data on total R&amp;D expenditures and amounts for internal and external performers, the original source of funds (federal or nonfederal), and the amount devoted to basic research activities. Data also include the amount of expenditures for the construction and acquisition of R&amp;D facilities.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;11/19/2009&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf10301/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf10301&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2009.11.19&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of State Government Research and Development&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of State Government Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;State Agency Research and Development Expenditures: Fiscal Year 2007&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2009&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;State Government Research and Development&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;State Government Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3990,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 10-306&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;State government agencies spent $1.3 billion on R&amp;D and R&amp;D facilities in fiscal 2007 (National Science Foundation, FY 2007 Survey of State R&amp;D Expenditures). New York, Ohio, Pennsylvania, Florida, California, New Jersey, Illinois, Indiana, Maryland, and North Carolina accounted for 62% of the $1.2 billion state R&amp;D total. Over 75% of total state R&amp;D originated from state and other nonfederal sources. Academic institutions performed the largest share (44%). Basic research was 22% of total R&amp;D.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&qu