R&amp;D performance in 2006.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;11/04/2009&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf10302/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf10302&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2009.11.04&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering State Profiles: 2006–08&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2009&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal Funds for R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Funds for R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3964,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 10-303&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The annual report, Federal Funds for Research and Development:  FY 2006-08, consists of 131 tables derived from the Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development, for FY 2006-08, and from earlier surveys in this series.  These data provide data users with the most timely data possible and are not readily available from any other sources.   These statistical data tables will only be available via the World Wide Web.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;10/15/2009&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf10303/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf10303&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2009.10.15&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Funds for Research and Development: Fiscal Years 2006–08&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2009&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal Funds for R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Funds for R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3984,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 09-320&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Federal obligations for R&amp;D and R&amp;D plant totaled $116.5 billion in FY 2008, a 0.5% increase in current dollars and 1.9% decrease in constant dollars over FY 2007. Over the period FY 2004–2008 federal R&amp;D and P&amp;D plant obligations dropped $1.5 billion in constant dollars—an average annual decrease of 0.4%. Research obligations declined over FY 2004–08 at an average annual rate of 2.1%. Among agencies, research obligations have shown an average annual rise only at the Department of Defense.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;10/02/2009&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf09320/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf09320&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2009.10.02&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal R&amp;D Support Shows Little Change in FY 2008&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2009&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Academic R&amp;D Expenditures&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Academic R&amp;D Expenditures&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3943,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 09-318&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Federal funding of academic science and engineering R&amp;D rose 2.5% in current dollars in FY 2008 but for the third straight year was nearly flat in constant dollars. Although still the largest source, the federal share of academic R&amp;D funding fell to 60% in FY 2008.The Department of Health and Human Services funded 56% of the federal total. Public institutions spent $35.3 billion on R&amp;D, private institutions $16.6 billion. The Johns Hopkins University remained the largest R&amp;D-performing institution overall.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;10/01/2009&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf09318/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf09318&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2009.10.01&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Government is Largest Source of University R&amp;D Funding in S&amp;E; Share Drops in FY 2008&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2009&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:3968,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 09-315&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;&lt;b&gt;Erratum:&lt;/b&gt; All-agencies totals for U. Central FL and U. South FL in table 14 have been revised. Table 14 was replaced with a corrected version October 2009.&lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt; Data presented in this report are from the Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions: Fiscal Year 2007, and contains comprehensive detailed statistical tables.  Data included in this report are Federal academic and nonprofit obligations by type of activity (six types of activity for academic, two for nonprofits), by year, by geographic division and state, by agency, and by individual institution rankings.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/18/2009&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf09315/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf09315&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2009.09.18&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions: FY 2007&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2009&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal S&amp;E Support to Universities, Colleges, and NPOs&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal S&amp;E Support to Universities, Colleges, and NPOs&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3971,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 09-319&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Federal agencies obligated $28.5 billion to 1,216 academic institutions for S&amp;E activities in FY 2007, down 0.4% from FY 2006 and the first current-dollar decrease in 11 years. The Department of Health and Human Services contributed 61% of the total. Decreases were larger for tribal institutions (13.2%), historically black colleges and universities (HBCUs) (8.6%), and high-Hispanic enrollment institutions (HHEs) (1.6%). R&amp;D comprised 89% of total obligations, but 79%, 58%, and 33% of obligations to HHEs, HBCUs, and tribal institutions, respectively.&lt;br /&gt;&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/08/2009&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf09319/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf09319&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2009.09.08&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal S&amp;E Obligations to Three Types of Minority-Serving Institutions Decline in FY 2007&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2009&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reference&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Master Government List of Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Master Government List of Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3949,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;SRS 09-402&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The Master Government List of Federally Funded Research and Development Centers (FFRDCs) has been updated to reflect changes through April 2009. This list, maintained by the National Science Foundation, includes sponsoring agencies, administrator types, R&amp;D activity types, and locations.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;08/13/2009&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://ncses.nsf.gov/resource/master-gov-lists-ffrdc/prior-lists#card3454&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;srs09402&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2009.08.13&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Master Government List of Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers: FY 2009&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2009&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Characteristics of Scientists and Engineers with U.S. Doctorates&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Characteristics of Scientists and Engineers with U.S. Doctorates&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3920,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 09-317&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Data tables reporting race/ethnicity and corresponding technical tables have been replaced with corrected versions in the 2006 edition of the report Characteristics of Doctoral Scientists and Engineers in the United States, NSF 09-317.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;08/04/2009&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf09317/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf09317&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2009.08.04&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Characteristics of Doctoral Scientists and Engineers in the United States: 2006&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2009&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3953,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 09-316&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Data from the Survey of Industrial Research and Development show business R&amp;D expenditures increased from $248 billion in 2006 to $269 billion in 2007. Manufacturers performed 70% of business R&amp;D in 2007. Ten states accounted for 69% of the total (California 24%). Small companies performed 19% of the total. Domestic net sales, R&amp;D-to-sales ratio, and domestic employment in R&amp;D-performing companies increased. The number of full-time equivalent scientists and engineers performing business R&amp;D remained 1.1 million.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;07/10/2009&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf09316/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf09316&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2009.07.10&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;,&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;U.S. Business R&amp;D Expenditures Increase in 2007; Small Companies Performed 19% of Nation&#39;s Business R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2009&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in S&amp;E&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in S&amp;E&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3940,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 09-314&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The 2007 Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (S&amp;E) showed an annual growth rate  of 3.3% for enrollments in S&amp;E graduate programs, the highest since 2002. Sciences added more students in absolute numbers, but the annual growth rate in engineering fields (5.9%) was substantially higher than in science fields (2.4%). The proportion of foreign student enrollments remained below its 2002 high, despite an annual growth rate of 4.6%, the highest since 2002.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;06/24/2009&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf09314/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf09314&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2009.06.24&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;S&amp;E Graduate Enrollments Accelerate in 2007; Enrollments of Foreign Students Reach New High&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2009&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal S&amp;E Support to Universities, Colleges, and NPOs&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal S&amp;E Support to Universities, Colleges, and NPOs&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3970,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 09-313&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Due to significant errors in data reported by the Department of Defense (DOD) and the National Institutes of Health (NIH), this report has been revised. Correcting for these errors reduced the total R&amp;D obligations for George Washington University by $83.4 million, revising its ranking from 51st to 92nd and moved $46.2 million in R&amp;D obligations from the University of Central Florida to the University of South Florida.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;03/27/2009&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf09313/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf09313&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2009.03.27&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal R&amp;D Obligations to Universities and Colleges Totaled $25 Billion in FY 2007&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2009&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal S&amp;E Support to Universities, Colleges, and NPOs&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal S&amp;E Support to Universities, Colleges, and NPOs&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3877,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 09-310&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Data presented in this report are from the Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions: Fiscal Year 2006, and contains comprehensive detailed statistical tables.  Data included in this report are Federal academic and nonprofit obligations by type of activity (six types of activity for academic, two for nonprofits), by year, by geographic division and state, by agency, and by individual institution rankings.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;03/27/2009&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf09310/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf09310&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2009.03.27&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions: FY 2006&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2009&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal Scientists and Engineers&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Scientists and Engineers&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3783,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 09-312&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Federal employment of scientists and engineers changed little from 2003 to 2005—up 1.5% (3,127 individuals). Only the Department of State experienced a double-digit increase (20%); nine agencies reported decreases. Federally employed scientists and engineers showed increases in all race/ethnicity categories, especially Hispanics (8.1%) and blacks (6.4%); women (5.7%); and age groups 45 years and older (3.0%–8.5%). The number of federal jobs in physical sciences, civil engineering, and industrial engineering dropped.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;03/13/2009&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf09312/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf09312&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2009.03.13&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Employment of Scientists and Engineers Remained Steady from 2003 to 2005&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2009&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3861,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 09-311&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Detailed statistical tables present data on doctorate awards. Some show 1997-2006 doctorates in S&amp;E and broad non-S&amp;E fields by citizenship, sex, race/ethnicity, and country origins of doctorate recipients. Others show 2006 data on states and institutions of doctorate awards and postgraduation plans of doctorate recipients. Data are collected directly from doctorate recipients through the annual Survey of Earned Doctorates. The survey instrument appears at the end of the report.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;03/13/2009&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf09311/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf09311&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2009.03.13&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering Doctorate Awards: 2006&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2009&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Academic R&amp;D Expenditures&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Academic R&amp;D Expenditures&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3871,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 09-303&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Detailed statistical tables present data on R&amp;D expenditures of higher education institutions and federally funded research and development centers (FFRDCs). Tables include academic expenditures by type of institution, field, geographic area, source of funds (Federal, state and local, industry, institutional), and expenditures for research equipment. Many tables show trends over time. Data from the Survey of R&amp;D Expenditures at Universities and Colleges: Fiscal Year 2007 are included in this report.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;03/11/2009&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf09303/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf09303&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2009.03.11&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Academic Research and Development Expenditures: Fiscal Year 2007&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2009&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reports&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Women, Minorities, and Persons with Disabilities in STEM&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Women, Minorities, and Persons with Disabilities in STEM&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3950,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 09-305&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report continues a series of Congressionally-mandated biennial reports, providing data on the participation of women, minorities, and persons with disabilities in science and engineering education and employment. The data and analyses presented here can be used to track progress, inform the development of policies to increase participation in science and engineering, and evaluate the effectiveness of such policies.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;02/13/2009&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/wmpd/archives/wmpd_2009.zip&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf09305&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2009.02.13&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Higher Education Surveys&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Surveys&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Women, Minorities, and Persons with Disabilities in Science and Engineering: 2009&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2009&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal Funds for R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Funds for R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3936,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 09-309&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Federal funds obligated for research (basic and applied), development, and R&amp;D plant declined from $116.7 billion in FY 2007 to $113.2 billion in FY 2008 — a 4.8% decrease in constant dollars. From FY 2001–05 total obligations rose 22.2%; since then they have fallen 7.3%. The Department of Health and Human Services accounts for 53% of total federal research support, followed by the Department of Energy (exceeding the Department of Defense for the first time).&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;02/04/2009&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf09309/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf09309&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2009.02.04&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;FY 2008 Data Show Downward Trend in Federal R&amp;D Funding&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2009&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3638,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 09-301&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report presents all of the statistics produced from the 2004 cycle of the Survey of Industrial Research and Development by size of company, the sources of R&amp;D funds, character of R&amp;D, and industry using the North American Industrial Classification System (NAICS).  Also included are tables covering historical trends in R&amp;D; R&amp;D as a percent of sales; employment and cost of R&amp;D scientists and engineers; and R&amp;D statistics by state.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;01/12/2009&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf09301/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf09301&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2009.01.12&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;,&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Research and Development in Industry:  2004&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2009&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:3930,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 09-308&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The ratios of first university degrees in natural sciences and engineering (NS&amp;E) to the college-age population have increased substantially in 23 countries/economies, including the United States. In 1975 only Japan had a higher NS&amp;E ratio than the United States. By 2005 nearly all examined countries/economies had surpassed the U.S. ratio. Changes are due primarily to increased degree completion rather than greater emphasis on NS&amp;E education; however, increased emphasis on NS&amp;E was an important factor in some locations.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;01/09/2009&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf09308/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf09308&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2009.01.09&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Integrated Postsecondary Education Data System Completions Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Integrated Postsecondary Education Data System Completions Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Reasons for International Changes in the Ratio of Natural Science and Engineering Degrees to the College-Age Population&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2009&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal Scientists and Engineers&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Scientists and Engineers&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3782,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 09-302&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Detailed tables present 2003-05 data on civilian, federally employed white-collar scientists and engineers who hold at least a bachelor&#39;s degree. Characteristics include agency of employment, occupation, primary work activity, salary, geographic region and state, sex, race/ethnicity, and age. Occupational data are grouped according to NSF&#39;s SESTAT occupational classification system. Data are from the Office of Personnel Management&#39;s (OPM) Central Personnel Data File. An appendix contains a crosswalk of SESTAT and OPM occupational codes.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;12/17/2008&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf09302&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf09302&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2008.12.17&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Scientists and Engineers: 2003–05&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2008&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3937,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 09-304&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The new Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey was developed by NSF and the U.S. Census Bureau with an eye toward providing policymakers the information needed to assess whether national R&amp;D efforts are keeping the U.S. globally competitive. The survey will collect some innovation data in preparation for increasing the scope of innovation-related items in the future. Several topical areas have been added, expanded, or modified from the previous Survey of Industrial Research and Development.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;12/04/2008&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf09304/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf09304&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2008.12.04&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;,&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;NSF Announces New U.S. Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2008&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3932,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 09-307&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;U.S. universities awarded 48,079 doctorates in 2007, a record number. It was the fifth straight annual rise, driven mostly by increases in science and engineering (S&amp;E) fields. The growth rate in S&amp;E doctorates was highest for women and non-U.S. citizens. Nearly all S&amp;E fields had record numbers of awards in 2007, with the highest number being in biological sciences. Among non-S&amp;E fields, education doctorates increased for the first time since 2003.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;11/21/2008&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf09307/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf09307&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2008.11.21&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;2007 Records Fifth Consecutive Annual Increase in U.S. Doctoral Awards&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2008&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal Funds for R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Funds for R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3898,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 09-300&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The annual report, Federal Funds for Research and Development:  FY 2005-07, consists of 131 tables derived from the Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development, for FY 2005-07, and from earlier surveys in this series.  These data provide data users with the most timely data possible and are not readily available from any other sources.   These statistical data tables will only be available via the World Wide Web.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;11/14/2008&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf09300/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf09300&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2008.11.14&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Funds for Research and Development:  Fiscal Years 2005–07&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2008&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3924,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 08-318&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Detailed statistical tables from four NSF surveys present current and historical patterns of U.S. R&amp;D expenditures by source of funds, performing sector, character of work, monetary unit, and state. R&amp;D expenditures at U.S. federally funded research and development centers (FFRDCs), and international R&amp;D data pertaining to major industrialized nations are also provided.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;11/14/2008&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf08318/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf08318&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2008.11.14&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources: 2007 Data Update&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2008&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal S&amp;E Support to Universities, Colleges, and NPOs&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal S&amp;E Support to Universities, Colleges, and NPOs&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3879,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 08-316&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Federal agencies obligated $28.7 billion to 1,226 academic institutions for S&amp;E activities in FY 2006, a 1% increase in current dollars but a 2.3% decrease in inflation-adjusted dollars over FY 2005. The Department of Health and Human Services continued to be the largest source of funding. About $1.1 billion was obligated to 194 minority-serving institutions; funding to historically black colleges and universities (HBCUs) decreased in current dollars for the first time since FY 1998. Nonprofit institutions received $6.7 billion, a new high.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;10/07/2008&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf08316/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf08316&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2008.10.07&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal S&amp;E Obligations to Academic Institutions Reach New Highs in FY 2006 but Fail to Keep Up with Inflation&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2008&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;S&amp;E Degrees&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;S&amp;E Degrees&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3785,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 08-321&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report provides information on the number and types of bachelor&#39;s, master&#39;s and doctoral degrees awarded by U.S. institutions in science and engineering fields. It also provides information on the characteristics of the degree recipients. The report includes select data from 1966-2006.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;10/02/2008&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf08321/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf08321&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2008.10.02&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Integrated Postsecondary Education Data System Completions Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Integrated Postsecondary Education Data System Completions Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;S&amp;E Degrees: 1966–2006&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2008&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding, by Budget Function&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding, by Budget Function&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3880,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 08-315&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This annual report contains information about federal funding of the research and development (R&amp;D) and the R&amp;D plant components of agency programs, as proposed by the administration for fiscal year (FY) 2009. R&amp;D data in this report are classified into the same federal budget function categories used in the Budget of the United States Government, Fiscal Year 2009, prepared by the Office of Management and Budget (OMB). Proposed FY 2009 funding levels are for budget authority, which is the basis for initial congressional action.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/25/2008&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf08315/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf08315&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2008.09.25&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding by Budget Function: 2007-09&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2008&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3927,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 08-317&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Projections from NSF R&amp;D surveys forecast a 5.8% increase in current dollar R&amp;D expenditures for 2007, bringing the total to $368.1 billion. Nearly all of the increase in R&amp;D expenditures was attributable to industry, which also performed 72.0% of the total R&amp;D. While the share of R&amp;D performed by universities and colleges has been increasing for several decades, the expansion of R&amp;D expenditures by industry is still the most striking long-term trend.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;08/27/2008&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf08317/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf08317&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2008.08.27&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;New Estimates of National Research and Development Expenditures Show 5.8% Growth in 2007&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2008&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:3928,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 08-319&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;One-third of blacks who received S&amp;E doctorates in 2006 held baccalaureates from historically black colleges and universities (HBCUs), down from the late 1970s (40%+) but up from the early 1990s (25%). When normalized by the total number of baccalaureates awarded to blacks, HBCUs as a group yielded about as many black S&amp;E doctorates in 1986-2006 as non-HBCU institutions. HBCUs constituted the top 8 (and 20 of the top 50) baccalaureate-origin institutions of black S&amp;E doctorates in 1997-2006.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;08/27/2008&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf08319/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf08319&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2008.08.27&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Role of HBCUs as Baccalaureate-Origin Institutions of Black S&amp;E Doctorate Recipients&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2008&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Academic R&amp;D Expenditures&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Academic R&amp;D Expenditures&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3869,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 08-320&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Federal funding for academic R&amp;D in science and engineering rose by 1.1% in FY 2007 but declined in constant dollars for the second year in a row. Funding from all nonfederal sources grew, with the largest growth in industry-funded expenditures (11.6%). R&amp;D expenditures are shown for the top 20 high-Hispanic-enrollment institutions and the top 20 historically black colleges and universities. Data are from the Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;08/21/2008&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf08320/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf08320&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2008.08.21&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Universities Report Continued Decline in Real Federal S&amp;E R&amp;D Funding in FY 2007&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2008&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering State Profiles&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering State Profiles&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3874,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 08-314&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report, Science and Engineering State Profiles: 2005-07, provides statistics on finances and personnel for the 50 States, Washington, D.C., and Puerto Rico with special emphasis on R&amp;D performance in 2005.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;08/15/2008&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf08314/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf08314&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2008.08.15&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering State Profiles: 2005-07&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2008&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding, by Budget Function&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding, by Budget Function&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3881,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 08-312&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;President Bush&#39;s proposed FY 2009 budget for federal R&amp;D is $143 billion, a 3.4% increase over FY 2008. Defense R&amp;D would increase by 3.8%, with the largest portion going to the Air Force. Nondefense R&amp;D would increase by 2.8%, with increases for space, general science, and energy and decreases for health, natural resources/environment, and agriculture. The majority of the defense portion of R&amp;D funding is allocated for development, whereas the nondefense portion is primarily for research.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;08/12/2008&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf08312/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf08312&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2008.08.12&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;President&#39;s FY 2009 Budget Requests 3.4% Increase in R&amp;D Funding&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2008&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3863,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 08-313&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Data from the Survey of Industrial Research and Development show companies increased spending on R&amp;D, from $226 billion in 2005 to $248 billion in 2006. Manufacturing industries accounted for 69% of the 2006 figure. Two-thirds of the industrial R&amp;D was performed in ten states and California alone accounted for 24%. The number of full-time equivalent scientists and engineers performing industrial R&amp;D and the R&amp;D-to-sales ratio remained the same as in 2005.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;08/12/2008&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf08313/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf08313&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2008.08.12&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;,&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;U.S. Business R&amp;D Expenditures Increase in 2006; Companies&#39; Own and Federal Contributions Rise&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2008&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:3886,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 08-308&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Women constituted 33% of academic science and engineering (S&amp;E) doctoral employment and 30% of full-time faculty in 2006, up from 9% and 7% in 1973. Women made gains in tenured or tenure-track positions and every faculty rank, but their share of full professorships remains lower than their share of S&amp;E doctorates. These gains reflect increases in the number of S&amp;E doctorates awarded to women relative to men. Data are from the Survey of Doctorate Recipients.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;07/18/2008&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf08308&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf08308&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2008.07.18&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Thirty-Three Years of Women in S&amp;E Faculty Positions&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2008&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:3914,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 08-311&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Baccalaureate colleges produce 13% of S&amp;E bachelor&#39;s degrees, but they are important contributors to the pool of S&amp;E doctorate recipients; only research universities yield more S&amp;E doctorates per hundred bachelor&#39;s degrees. Trends in absolute numbers of S&amp;E doctorates by type of baccalaureate institution (public/private, Carnegie group) and a list of the top 50 baccalaureate-origin institutions of 1997-2006 S&amp;E doctorate recipients are presented. Data are from the Survey of Earned Doctorates and the NCES Completions Survey.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;07/14/2008&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf08311/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf08311&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2008.07.14&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;,&quot;Integrated Postsecondary Education Data System Completions Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Integrated Postsecondary Education Data System Completions Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Baccalaureate Origins of S&amp;E Doctorate Recipients&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2008&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;State Government Research and Development&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;State Government Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3850,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 08-310&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;These tables present the results of the National Science Foundation FY 2006 Survey of State R&amp;D Expenditures. The results include data on total R&amp;D expenditures and amounts for internal and external performers, the original source of funds (federal or nonfederal), and the amount devoted to basic research activities. Data also include the amount of expenditures for the construction and acquisition of R&amp;D facilities.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;05/22/2008&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf08310/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf08310&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2008.05.22&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of State Government Research and Development&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of State Government Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;State Agency Research and Development Expenditures: Fiscal Year 2006&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2008&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reports&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:3926,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;SRS 08-07CE&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Women, Minorities, and Persons with Disabilities in Science and Engineering provides a broad base of quantitative information about participation of women, minorities, and persons with disabilities in science and engineering education and employment. The report contains data tables organized by topic (e.g., undergraduate enrollment, graduate degrees, employment) and also by group (e.g., Hispanics, minority women, persons with disabilities). This release includes updates to &lt;a href=&#39;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/wmpd/underdeg.htm&#39;&gt;Undergraduate Degrees&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href=&#39;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/wmpd/graddeg.htm&#39;&gt;Master&#39;s Degrees&lt;/a&gt; data.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;05/09/2008&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/wmpd/tables.cfm&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;srs0807ce&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2008.05.09&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Women, Minorities, and Persons with Disabilities in S&amp;E - Undergraduate Degrees and Master&#39;s Degrees Data Update&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2008&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;State Government Research and Development&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;State Government Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3919,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 08-309&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Results of a new Survey of State R&amp;D Expenditures show that 252 state agencies spent more than $1 billion on research and development in fiscal year 2006. Pennsylvania, California, New York, Michigan, Ohio, and Florida accounted for 49% of the total. Three-fourths of state R&amp;D expenditures originated from state and other nonfederal sources. About half (49%) of the R&amp;D was performed by academic institutions. Applied research accounted for 79% of the expenditures, and basic research 21%.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;05/08/2008&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf08309/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf08309&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2008.05.08&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of State Government Research and Development&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of State Government Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;New NSF Survey Finds Six States Account for Nearly Half of State Agencies&#39; R&amp;D Expenditures&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2008&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in S&amp;E&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in S&amp;E&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3883,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 08-306&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Data presented in these tables are from the Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering, Fall 2006. The tables present information on trends in enrollment of science and engineering graduate students through 2006. Data are presented on trends in number of graduate students by sex, race/ethnicity, citizenship, field and primary source and mechanism of support.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;04/10/2008&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf08306/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf08306&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2008.04.10&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering: Fall 2006&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2008&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Characteristics of Scientists and Engineers with U.S. Doctorates&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Characteristics of Scientists and Engineers with U.S. Doctorates&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3893,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 08-307&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;According to the 2006 Survey of Doctorate Recipients, 45% of recent (within the 5 years preceding the survey) science, engineering, and health doctorate recipients were on or had completed a postdoctoral appointment (postdoc), up from 41% in 1995. Median appointment length remained at approximately 2 years. Postdoc participants were increasingly likely to have both health and retirement benefits. Work activity, employment sector, and reasons for taking a postdoc are also reported.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;03/27/2008&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf08307/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf08307&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2008.03.27&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Postdoc Participation of Science, Engineering, and Health Doctorate Recipients&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2008&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;SESTAT: Characteristics of Scientists and Engineers in the United States&quot;,&quot;Characteristics of College Graduates&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;SESTAT: Characteristics of Scientists and Engineers in the United States&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Characteristics of College Graduates&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3892,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 08-305&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Between 2003 and 2006 the unemployment rate for scientists and engineers dropped from 3.2% to 2.5%, remaining lower than the unemployment rate for the entire U.S. labor force. In 2006, 45% of scientists and engineers were women and 95% were U.S. citizens. Business/industry employed the largest share (69%) of scientists and engineers in 2006; employed scientists and engineers with professional degrees earned the highest median salaries ($120,000). (Data from the Scientists and Engineers Statistical Data System.)&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;03/27/2008&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf08305/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf08305&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2008.03.27&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Unemployment Rate of U.S. Scientists and Engineers Drops to Record Low 2.5% in 2006&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2008&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Characteristics of Recent College Graduates&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Characteristics of Recent College Graduates&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3844,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 08-304&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The 2006 National Survey of Recent College Graduates provides data on 2003-05 bachelor&#39;s and master&#39;s graduates in science, engineering, and health fields. Women outnumbered men 56% to 44%. Overall, 85% of recent graduates were employed in April 2006, two-thirds of them in business/industry. Recent graduates in engineering and computer/information sciences were more likely than those in other fields to be employed full-time and to be working in business/industry; they also earned the highest salaries.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;03/10/2008&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf08304/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf08304&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2008.03.10&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;National Survey of Recent College Graduates (NSRCG)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Survey of Recent College Graduates (NSRCG)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;An Overview of Science, Engineering, and Health Graduates: 2006&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2008&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal Funds for R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Funds for R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3805,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 08-303&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Figures adjusted for inflation show a nearly 3% decline in federal obligations for R&amp;D and R&amp;D plant for FY 2007, the first such decline since FY 2000. Total research obligations have been dropping since FY 2004, with the largest decline attributable to the Department of Health and Human Services. Obligations are shown by basic research, applied research, development, R&amp;D plant, and federal agency. Data are from the Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development: FY 2005-07.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;02/13/2008&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf08303/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf08303&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2008.02.13&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding Down in FY 2007&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2008&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reports&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering Indicators&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering Indicators&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3903,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSB 08-01&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Indicators 2008 in HTML and the Appendix Tables in spreadsheet format are now available on the NSF web site. Science and Engineering Indicators 2008, published by the National Science Board, provides a broad base of quantitative information on the U.S. and international science and engineering enterprise.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;01/15/2008&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/seind08/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsb0801&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2008.01.15&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering Indicators 2008&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2008&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in S&amp;E&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in S&amp;E&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3873,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 08-302&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The 2006 Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (S&amp;E) shows continued growth in graduate student enrollment (to 486,287) and postdoctoral appointments (to 34,813) in the United States. After 2 years of decline, enrollment of foreign students (temporary visa holders) rose, largely due to 16% growth in first-time, full-time enrollment over the 2005 level. S&amp;E graduate enrollment increased among women and underrepresented minorities and increased in all major fields except agricultural and computer sciences.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;01/03/2008&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf08302/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf08302&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2008.01.03&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;First-Time, Full-Time Graduate Student Enrollment in Science and Engineering Increases in 2006, Especially Among Foreign Students&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2008&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:3855,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 08-301&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The 2006 Survey of Earned Doctorates shows that almost 30,000 of the 46,000 U.S. doctorates awarded were in science and engineering (S&amp;E) fields-- a 6.7% increase over 2005. New records were attained in biological, computer, and social sciences, mathematics, chemistry, and engineering. Non-U.S. citizens earned 45% of all S&amp;E doctorates and a majority in computer sciences, mathematics, physics, and every engineering field. Citizens of China, India, and Korea earned the most S&amp;E doctorates among foreign recipients.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;11/20/2007&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf08301/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf08301&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2007.11.20&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;U.S. Doctoral Awards in Science and Engineering Continue Upward Trend in 2006&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2007&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Academic R&amp;D Expenditures&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Academic R&amp;D Expenditures&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3797,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 08-300&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Detailed statistical tables present data on R&amp;D expenditures of higher education institutions and federally funded research and development centers (FFRDCs). Tables include academic expenditures by type of institution, field, geographic area, source of funds (Federal, state and local, industry, institutional), and expenditures for research equipment. Many tables show trends over time. Data from the Survey of R&amp;D Expenditures at Universities and Colleges: Fiscal Year 2006 are included in this report.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;11/16/2007&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf08300/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf08300&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2007.11.16&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Academic Research and Development Expenditures: Fiscal Year 2006&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2007&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reports&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:3889,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;SRS 08-07DFG&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Women, Minorities, and Persons with Disabilities in Science and Engineering provides a broad base of quantitative information about participation of women, minorities, and persons with disabilities in science and engineering education and employment. The report contains data tables organized by topic (e.g., undergraduate enrollment, graduate degrees, employment) and also by group (e.g., Hispanics, minority women, persons with disabilities). This release includes updates to &lt;a href=&#39;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/wmpd/gradenrl.htm&#39;&gt;Graduate Enrollment&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href=&#39;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/wmpd/graddeg.htm&#39;&gt;Doctoral Degrees&lt;/a&gt;, and &lt;a href=&#39;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/wmpd/postdoc.htm&#39;&gt;Postdoc Status&lt;/a&gt; data.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;11/16/2007&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/wmpd/tables.cfm&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;srs0807dfg&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2007.11.16&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Women, Minorities, and Persons with Disabilities in S&amp;E - Graduate Enrollment, Doctoral Degrees, and Postdoc Status Data Update&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2007&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal S&amp;E Support to Universities, Colleges, and NPOs&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal S&amp;E Support to Universities, Colleges, and NPOs&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3835,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 07-326&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This InfoBrief is revised. Data for high-Hispanic-enrollment institutions presented in table 5 and related text were revised to correct errors in data reporting. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt; Latest statistics from the Survey of Federal Science and Engineering (S&amp;E) Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions show federal obligations to academic institutions for S&amp;E activities reached a new high of $28.4 billion in FY 2005, a 3.8% increase over FY 2004. In FY2005, federal S&amp;E support went to 1,227 academic institutions, including 91 institutions with high-Hispanic-enrollment schools, 79 historically black colleges and universities, and 29 tribal colleges.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;11/14/2007&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf07326/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf07326&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2007.11.14&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;FY 2005 Federal S&amp;E Obligations Reach Over 2,400 Academic and Nonprofit Institutions; Data Presented on Minority-Serving Institutions&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2007&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal S&amp;E Support to Universities, Colleges, and NPOs&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal S&amp;E Support to Universities, Colleges, and NPOs&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3795,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 07-333&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Data presented in this report are from the Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions: Fiscal Year 2005, and contains comprehensive detailed statistical tables.  Data included in this report are Federal academic and nonprofit obligations by type of activity (six types of activity for academic, two for nonprofits), by year, by geographic division and state, by agency, and by individual institution rankings.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;10/15/2007&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf07333/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf07333&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2007.10.15&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions: FY 2005&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2007&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3752,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 07-335&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Data from the Survey of Industrial Research and Development show industrial R&amp;D spending in the United States increased to $226 billion in 2005, with manufacturing companies spending 70% of the total. Domestic net sales and domestic employment for R&amp;D-performing companies increased. The R&amp;D-to-sales ratio and the number of full-time equivalent scientists and engineers performing industrial R&amp;D showed no change. Two-thirds of industrial R&amp;D was performed in California, Michigan, Massachusetts, New Jersey, Texas, Washington, Illinois, New York, Pennsylvania, and Connecticut.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;10/01/2007&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf07335/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf07335&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2007.10.01&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;,&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Expenditures for U.S. Industrial R&amp;D Continue to Increase in 2005; R&amp;D Performance Geographically Concentrated&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2007&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Academic R&amp;D Expenditures&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Academic R&amp;D Expenditures&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3854,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 07-336&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Data from the Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges show federal funding of academic R&amp;D in science and engineering fields at $30 billion in FY FY 2006--a 2.9% increase over FY 2005 in current dollars but a 0.1% decrease in inflation-adjusted dollars. The federal government funded over 60% of academic R&amp;D; Health and Human Services accounted for 57% of the federal contribution. Funding from institutions, up 9.7% in FY 2006, showed the largest gain.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/26/2007&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf07336/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf07336&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2007.09.26&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Universities Report Stalled Growth in Federal R&amp;D Funding in FY 2006&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2007&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3829,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 07-331&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Detailed statistical tables from four NSF surveys present current and historical patterns of U.S. R&amp;D expenditures by source of funds, performing sector, character of work, monetary unit, and state. R&amp;D expenditures at U.S. federally funded research and development centers (FFRDCs), and international R&amp;D data pertaining to major industrialized nations are also provided.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/14/2007&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf07331/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf07331&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2007.09.14&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources: 2006 Data Update&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2007&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:3756,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 07-328&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Non-OECD countries&#39; share of S&amp;E article output increased from 13% in 1993 to 16% in 2003. Brazil, China, India, Russia, and Taiwan produced two-thirds of non-OECD output, with China most prominent. Physical sciences and mathematics dominated the portfolios of China, India, and Russia. Life sciences dominated Brazil&#39;s portfolio. Taiwan&#39;s articles were more evenly distributed among the fields. International coauthorship rose rapidly during the period. Non-OECD collaborations with U.S. and European Union authors were most common.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/11/2007&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf07328/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf07328&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2007.09.11&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Brazil, China, India, Russia, and Taiwan Lead S&amp;E Article Output of the Non-OECD Countries&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2007&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Facilities for S&amp;E Research&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Facilities for S&amp;E Research&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3804,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 07-334&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Data from NSF&#39;s FY 2005 Survey of Science and Engineering Research Facilities show that research-performing colleges and universities continued enhancing their networking and computational resources. Bandwidth to external networks (e.g., Internet1, Abilene, National LambdaRail) has increased along with internal network and desktop port speeds. About 63% of institutions obtained some bandwidth from a consortium, many from state and/or regional research and education networks. Wireless coverage and high-performance computing have also increased.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/11/2007&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf07334/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf07334&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2007.09.11&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Science and Engineering Research Facilities&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Science and Engineering Research Facilities&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Most Colleges and Universities Obtain Bandwidth Through State and Regional Consortia&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2007&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding, by Budget Function&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding, by Budget Function&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3798,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 07-332&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This annual report contains information about federal funding of the research and development (R&amp;D) and the R&amp;D plant components of agency programs, as proposed by the administration for fiscal year (FY) 2008. R&amp;D data in this report are classified into the same federal budget function categories used in the Budget of the United States Government, Fiscal Year 2008, prepared by the Office of Management and Budget (OMB). Proposed FY 2008 funding levels are for budget authority, which is the basis for initial congressional action.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;08/28/2007&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf07332/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf07332&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2007.08.28&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding by Budget Function: Fiscal Years 2006-08&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2007&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:3731,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 07-329&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;During much of the 1990s, U.S. trade in advanced technology products grew and produced consistent trade surpluses. Beginning in 2000, the trade balance for U.S. technology products began to erode, with the U.S. seeing annual trade deficits beginning in 2002. The recent U.S. trade deficit in advanced technology products is largely due to the imbalance of trade with Asia. Among the 11 major technology areas, only aerospace and electronics products generated trade surpluses in recent years.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;08/22/2007&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf07329/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf07329&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2007.08.22&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Annual Deficits Continue for U.S. Trade in Advanced Technology Products&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2007&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Working Paper&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Characteristics of College Graduates&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Characteristics of College Graduates&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:1713,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;SRS 07-205&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report provides the basis for understanding how Current Population Survey (CPS) data may be used to satisfy the need for information on the science and engineering (S&amp;E) workforce without a bachelor&#39;s or higher degree. It looks at the comparability of CPS data and the NSF Scientists and Engineers Statistical Data System (SESTAT) data. The report discusses data collection and processing procedures, coverage issues and estimates of the S&amp;E population, and other statistical issues that may affect comparisons of the two data sources.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;08/17/2007&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/srs07205/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;srs07205&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2007.08.17&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;National Survey of College Graduates (NSCG)&quot;,&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Survey of College Graduates (NSCG)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Comparison of the National Science Foundation&#39;s Scientists and Engineers Statistical Data System (SESTAT) with the Bureau of Labor Statistics&#39; Current Population Survey (CPS)&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2007&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reports&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:1874,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 07-319&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The emergence of Asia as a powerful S&amp;T region is producing changes in the international S&amp;T order and is, to some extent, beginning to displace some traditional powers. This report provides standard indicators of S&amp;T infrastructure and performance to highlight the growth of the Asian S&amp;T enterprise. Where possible, it provides comparable information for Asia, the EU, and the United States. The aim is to give a quick glance across a broad set of S&amp;T indicators covering education, S&amp;E workforce, R&amp;D expenditures, S&amp;E publications, patents, and high-technology industries.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;08/02/2007&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf07319/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf07319&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2007.08.02&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Asia&#39;s Rising Science and Technology Strength: Comparative Indicators for Asia, the European Union, and the United States&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2007&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding, by Budget Function&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding, by Budget Function&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3842,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 07-327&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The Bush administration proposed budget for FY 2008 includes an increase overall for federal R&amp;D (up 1.0%) and for the largest component, national defense (up 0.9%). The second largest component, health, is budgeted to drop (down 0.8%). The largest percentage increases are slated for nondefense areas: general science (up 7.9%) and space research and technology (up 6.4%). Data are from agency submissions to the Office of Management and Budget.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;08/01/2007&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf07327/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf07327&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2007.08.01&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;President&#39;s FY 2008 Budget Requests 1% Increase in R&amp;D Funding&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2007&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Facilities for S&amp;E Research&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Facilities for S&amp;E Research&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3765,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 07-325&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The data in these detailed statistical tables are collected biennially through the National Science Foundation&#39;s (NSF) congressionally mandated Survey of Science and Engineering Research Facilities. These tables provide data on the amount and condition of science and engineering (S&amp;E) research space existing at the eligible U.S. colleges, universities, and nonprofit biomedical research institutions. The data presented are from the survey conducted in fiscal year 2005.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;07/19/2007&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf07325/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf07325&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2007.07.19&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Science and Engineering Research Facilities&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Science and Engineering Research Facilities&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;S&amp;E Research Facilities: FY 2005&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2007&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reports&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:1878,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 07-320&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report is part of a study on changing trends in U.S. production of scientific articles since the late 1980s. The study was initiated to investigate an unexpected finding that the number of U.S. S&amp;E articles published in peer-reviewed journals leveled off in the early 1990s, while the support for R&amp;D continued to increase. Quantitative data on patterns and trends in article production and citations from 1988 to 2003 are presented.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;07/18/2007&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf07320/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf07320&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2007.07.18&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Changing U.S. Output of Scientific Articles: 1988-2003&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2007&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Working Paper&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:1876,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;SRS 07-204&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report summarizes the results of interviews with academic scientists and engineers and administrators about changes in publishing behavior. It discusses key themes concerning changes in the academic research and publishing environment, the nature of academic research, and its institutional setting and culture. The focus is on the 1990s, with earlier developments included if they have a lasting effect on the S&amp;E publishing system.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;07/18/2007&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/srs07204/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;srs07204&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2007.07.18&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;The Changing Research and Publication Environment in American Research Universities&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2007&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Characteristics of College Graduates&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Characteristics of College Graduates&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3716,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 07-324&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;NSF&#39;s workforce survey data show the contribution of immigration to the growth in the U.S. S&amp;E workforce over the past decade. In 2003, 16% of the 21.6 million scientists and engineers in the U.S. were immigrants. Their primary reasons for first coming to the United States vary by when they came, region of birth, S&amp;E occupation, and degree field. Overall, family-related reasons were cited most often (37%), followed by educational opportunities (30%), job or economic opportunities (21%), and scientific or professional infrastructure (5%).&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;06/28/2007&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf07324/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf07324&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2007.06.28&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Why Did They Come to the United States?  A Profile of Immigrant Scientists and Engineers&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2007&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Working Paper&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:1737,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;SRS 07-203&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This paper provides an overview of research issues related to the effects of the growing migration of highly skilled workers on the world economy and economic policy, with special reference to the international mobility of scientists and engineers. It points out U.S. data sets (collected by the National Science Foundation Division of Science Resources Statistics, other Government agencies, and private organizations) that, along with databases from the Organization for Economic Cooperation and Development and the European Union, may be used to provide at least partial answers to questions prompted by these issues. The paper lays out a theoretically informed road map for the better understanding of these dynamic and far-reaching developments.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;06/14/2007&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/srs07203&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;srs07203&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2007.06.14&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;,&quot;National Survey of College Graduates (NSCG)&quot;,&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Survey of College Graduates (NSCG)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Research Issues in the International Migration of Highly Skilled Workers: A Perspective with Data from the United States&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2007&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Working Paper&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Characteristics of College Graduates&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Characteristics of College Graduates&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:1715,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;SRS 07-202&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This working paper reviews the 1990s design of the surveys of the SESTAT (Science and Engineers Statistical Data System) database. The report considers alternative methods for addressing issues of defining and achieving good coverage of the target population, to meet user data 