taCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3757,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 07-305&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Detailed statistical tables present data on doctorate awards. Some show 1996-2005 doctorates in S&amp;E and broad non-S&amp;E fields by citizenship, sex, race/ethnicity, and country origins of doctorate recipients. Others show 2005 data on states and institutions of doctorate awards and postgraduation plans of doctorate recipients. Data are collected directly from doctorate recipients through the annual Survey of Earned Doctorates. The survey instrument appears at the end of the report.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;12/22/2006&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf07305/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf07305&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2006.12.22&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;S&amp;E Doctorate Awards: 2005&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2006&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding, by Budget Function&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding, by Budget Function&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3734,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 07-303&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This annual report contains information about federal funding of the research and development (R&amp;D) and the R&amp;D plant components of agency programs, as proposed by the administration for fiscal year (FY) 2007. R&amp;D data in this report are classified into the same federal budget function categories used in the Budget of the United States Government, Fiscal Year 2007, prepared by the Office of Management and Budget (OMB). Proposed FY 2007 funding levels are for budget authority, which is the basis for initial congressional action.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;12/21/2006&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf07303/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf07303&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2006.12.21&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Research and Development Funding by Budget Function: Fiscal Years 2005-07&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2006&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3637,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 07-304&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Data from the Survey of Industrial Research and Development show companies&#39; R&amp;D spending increased from $201 billion in 2003 to $208 billion in 2004, with manufacturing companies accounting for 71% of the 2004 amount. R&amp;D funding from companies&#39; own and other nonfederal sources and from federal sources increased in 2004. Domestic net sales, domestic employment, and the number of full-time equivalent scientists and engineers performing industrial R&amp;D all decreased in R&amp;D-performing companies in 2004.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;12/20/2006&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf07304/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf07304&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2006.12.20&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;,&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;U.S. Industrial R&amp;D Performers Report Increased Expenditures for 2004&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2006&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal Funds for R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Funds for R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3749,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 07-302&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Thirty federal agencies provided $109.7 billion for R&amp;D and R&amp;D plant in FY 2004. Research accounted for 48.6% of all obligations (more than half for life sciences) and development accounted for 47.7%. Federal R&amp;D and R&amp;D plant obligations grew at an average annual rate of 4.7% from FY 1994 to FY 2004. Reclassification of activities in FY 2000 resulted in large differences in average growth rates between the first (2.2%) and last (9.1%) halves of the decade.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;12/18/2006&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf07302/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf07302&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2006.12.18&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Agencies Supported R&amp;D Growth over the Period FY 1994-2004&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2006&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3754,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 07-301&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The number of science and engineering (S&amp;E) doctorates conferred by U.S. universities reached 27,974 in 2005, surpassing the previous record of 27,273 reached in 1998. Women, non-U.S. citizens, and U.S. citizen Asians and underrepresented minorities also earned record numbers of S&amp;E doctorates. Non-U.S. citizens accounted for virtually all of the growth in S&amp;E doctorates since 2001. Fields reaching new highs in 2005 were biological sciences, engineering, mathematics, and computer sciences.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;11/17/2006&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf07301/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf07301&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2006.11.17&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;S&amp;E Doctorates Hit All-time High in 2005&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2006&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Characteristics of Recent College Graduates&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Characteristics of Recent College Graduates&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3641,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 06-329&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Detailed statistical tables present data on persons who earned S&amp;E bachelor&#39;s or master&#39;s degrees at U.S. academic institutions in the 2000-2001 and 2001-2002 academic years. Tables cover demographics (sex, race/ethnicity, citizenship), education (field, GPA, sources of support, debt level), and employment (sector, primary work activity, salary). Data are collected directly from individuals through the National Survey of Recent College Graduates.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;11/06/2006&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf06329/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf06329&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2006.11.06&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;National Survey of Recent College Graduates (NSRCG)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Survey of Recent College Graduates (NSRCG)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Characteristics of Recent Science and Engineering Graduates: 2003&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2006&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal S&amp;E Support to Universities, Colleges, and NPOs&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal S&amp;E Support to Universities, Colleges, and NPOs&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3747,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 07-300&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Federal obligations to academic institutions for science and engineering (S&amp;E) activities attained a record $27.3 billion in FY 2004, a 2.5% increase over FY 2003. Support reached new highs for R&amp;D and fellowships/traineeships/training grants, but declined by nearly half for R&amp;D plant and by smaller amounts for facilities/equipment for S&amp;E instruction, general support projects, and other S&amp;E activities. Federal obligations to nonprofit institutions increased by over 6%, reaching a record $6.1 billion.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;11/01/2006&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf07300/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf07300&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2006.11.01&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Academic Science and Engineering Obligations Rose by 2.5% in FY 2004&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2006&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding, by Budget Function&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding, by Budget Function&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3730,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 06-330&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;President Bush&#39;s proposed budget for FY 2007 includes a modest increase overall for federal R&amp;D and R&amp;D plant programs (up 1.2%) and for the largest component, national defense (up 1.0%). The second largest component, health, is budgeted to decrease slightly (down 0.2%). The largest percentage increases are slated for nondefense areas: general science (up 11.0%) and space research and technology (up 10.2%). Data are from agency submissions to the Office of Management and Budget.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;10/06/2006&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf06330/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf06330&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2006.10.06&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;President&#39;s Proposed FY 2007 Budget Shows Modest Increase in R&amp;D Funding&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2006&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reports&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:1821,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 06-319&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report documents the history of U.S. doctoral education from its beginnings in 1861 through 1999. Detailed tables and figures provide historical trend data for 20th century periods. Data since 1958 are from the Survey of Earned Doctorates; earlier data are from public records and the Department of Education. The report covers doctorate recipients&#39; demographic characteristics; study fields and institutions for bachelor&#39;s, master&#39;s, and doctorate degrees; financial support; indebtedness; time from baccalaureate to doctorate; and postgraduation plans.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;10/06/2006&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf06319/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf06319&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2006.10.06&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;U.S. Doctorates in the 20th Century&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2006&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3707,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 06-327&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Detailed statistical tables from four NSF surveys present current and historical patterns of U.S. R&amp;D expenditures by source of funds, performing sector, character of work, monetary unit, and state. R&amp;D expenditures at U.S. federally funded research and development centers (FFRDCs), and international R&amp;D data pertaining to major industrialized nations are also provided.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/25/2006&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf06327/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf06327&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2006.09.25&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources: 2004 Data Update&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2006&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:3718,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 06-328&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;R&amp;D support and publication data suggest that the three-decades-long trend of increasingly strong ties between industry and U.S. universities may have ended. Between 2001 and 2004, industrial R&amp;D support to academe fell 5.1%, from $2.2 to $2.1 billion. Industry&#39;s share of total academic R&amp;D support declined from 7.4% in 1999 to 4.9% in 2004. Industry-university coauthorship of scientific articles declined after 2001, and industrial patents cited fewer U.S. S&amp;E articles.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/15/2006&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf06328/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf06328&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2006.09.15&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Where has the Money Gone?  Declining Industrial Support of Academic R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2006&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in S&amp;E&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in S&amp;E&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3688,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 06-325&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Data presented in these tables are from the Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering, Fall 2004. The tables present information on trends in enrollment of science and engineering graduate students through 2003. Data are presented on trends in number of graduate students by sex, race/ethnicity, citizenship, field and primary source and mechanism of support.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;08/31/2006&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf06325/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf06325&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2006.08.31&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering: Fall 2004&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2006&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:3653,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 06-326&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Data from the Bureau of Economic Analysis show international trade in research, development, and testing (RDT) services is contributing to the U.S. trade surplus in business services overall, driven primarily by exports of services by U.S. affiliates of foreign multinational corporations.  In contrast, the unaffiliated trade surplus in RDT services has been down since 2001.  Figures and tables present U.S. affiliated and unaffiliated imports, exports, and trade balances (in U.S. current dollars) for RDT over time.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;08/22/2006&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf06326/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf06326&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2006.08.22&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Trade in R&amp;D-Related Services: A New Indicator of Industrial Knowledge Flows&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2006&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Academic R&amp;D Expenditures&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Academic R&amp;D Expenditures&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3683,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 06-323&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Detailed statistical tables present data on R&amp;D expenditures of higher education institutions and federally funded research and development centers (FFRDCs). Tables include academic expenditures by type of institution, field, geographic area, source of funds (Federal, state and local, industry, institutional), and expenditures for research equipment. Many tables show trends over time. Data are collected through the annual Survey of R&amp;D Expenditures at Universities and Colleges.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;08/09/2006&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf06323/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf06323&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2006.08.09&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Academic Research and Development Expenditures: Fiscal Year 2004&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2006&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:1849,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 06-322&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report will be the latest in the annual report series that presents the complete set of tables produced from the SRS-sponsored Survey of Industrial Research and Development. The report contains 44 detailed statistical and 7 descriptive tables from the 2002 cycle of the survey. The detailed statistical tables track historical trends in industrial R&amp;D spending, sources of funds for R&amp;D performance, the character of industrial R&amp;D, domestic employment of R&amp;D-performing companies, and cost per R&amp;D scientist or engineer, by industry using the North American Industrial Classification System (NAICS) and by detailed company size categories. The descriptive tables contain information about the statistical sample, sampling errors, and response and imputation rates.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;08/09/2006&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf06322/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf06322&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2006.08.09&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;,&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Research and Development in Industry: 2002&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2006&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;SESTAT: Characteristics of Scientists and Engineers in the United States&quot;,&quot;Characteristics of College Graduates&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;SESTAT: Characteristics of Scientists and Engineers in the United States&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Characteristics of College Graduates&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:1753,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 06-324&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Data from three 2003 demographic and workforce surveys provide insight into the educational and employment patterns of individuals whose first university degree was an S&amp;E bachelor&#39;s. Ten years after the bachelor&#39;s, 49% of recipients had earned an additional degree, although often in a non-S&amp;E field. Of those with only an S&amp;E bachelor&#39;s degree, 72% reported that their job was related to their degree; 57% reported their job required at least a bachelor&#39;s degree level of S&amp;E knowledge.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;07/31/2006&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf06324/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf06324&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2006.07.31&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;What Do People Do After Earning an Science and Engineering Bachelor&#39;s Degree?&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2006&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in S&amp;E&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in S&amp;E&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3689,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 06-321&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The 2004 Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (S&amp;E) shows a continued decrease in first-time, full-time graduate enrollment of foreign students (7% since 2003, 20% since 2001). Total full-time foreign enrollment also declined. Overall enrollment grew less than 0.5% but increased among U.S. women, blacks, and Hispanics, and in most major fields. Fewer foreign postdocs resulted in a 2% decline in S&amp;E postdocs, the first substantial decline since 1978.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;07/10/2006&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf06321/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf06321&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2006.07.10&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;First-time S&amp;E Graduate Enrollment of Foreign Students Drops for the Third Straight Year&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2006&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:3652,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 06-318&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Despite percentage gains by minorities in S&amp;E doctorates awarded in the U.S., the number of minority doctoral S&amp;E faculty remained small in 2003, especially in leading research institutions. Data from the 2003 Survey of Doctorate Recipients highlight selected characteristics of doctoral S&amp;E faculty by race/ethnicity, including year of doctorate, occupational field, rank, tenure status, sex, citizenship, and Carnegie classification of both the doctorate institution and the employer.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;06/15/2006&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf06318/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf06318&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2006.06.15&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Academic Institutions of Minority Faculty with S&amp;E Doctorates&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2006&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal S&amp;E Support to Universities, Colleges, and NPOs&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal S&amp;E Support to Universities, Colleges, and NPOs&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3627,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 06-309&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Data presented in this report are from the Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions: Fiscal Year 2003, and contains comprehensive detailed statistical tables.  Data included in this report are Federal academic and nonprofit obligations by type of activity (six types of activity for academic, two for nonprofits), by year, by geographic division and state, by agency, and by individual institution rankings.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;06/13/2006&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf06309/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf06309&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2006.06.13&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and NPOs: Fiscal Year 2003&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2006&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding, by Budget Function&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding, by Budget Function&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3650,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 06-317&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This annual report contains information about Federal funding of the research and development (R&amp;D) and the R&amp;D plant components of agency programs, as proposed by the administration for fiscal year (FY) 2006. R&amp;D data in this report are classified into the same Federal budget function categories, used in the Budget of the United States Government, Fiscal Year 2006, prepared by the Office of Management and Budget (OMB). Proposed FY 2006 funding levels are for budget authority (defined below), which is the basis for initial congressional action. In future Budget Function annual reports, these data will be revised to reflect congressional appropriation and actual program-funding decisions.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;06/02/2006&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf06317/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf06317&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2006.06.02&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surv