 in median total elapsed time from baccalaureate to doctorate (10.1 years), registered time to doctorate (7.5 years), and age at doctorate (33.3 years). Medians were generally lower, compared with medians for other doctorate recipients, for recipients of S&amp;E doctorates, doctorate recipients who did not earn master&#39;s degrees, those who graduated from research-extensive universities, and those whose primary graduate support was from assistantships, fellowships, and grants.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;03/24/2006&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf06312/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf06312&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2006.03.24&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Time to Degree of U.S. Research Doctorate Recipients&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2006&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3639,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 06-308&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Detailed statistical tables present data on doctorate awards. Some show 1995-2004 doctorates in S&amp;E and broad non-S&amp;E fields by citizenship, sex, race/ethnicity, and country origins of doctorate recipients. Others show 2004 data on states and institutions of doctorate awards and postgraduation plans of doctorate recipients. Data are collected directly from doctorate recipients through the annual Survey of Earned Doctorates. The survey instrument appears at the end of the report.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;03/14/2006&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf06308/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf06308&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2006.03.14&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;S&amp;E Doctorate Awards: 2004&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2006&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in S&amp;E&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in S&amp;E&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3643,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 06-307&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Data presented in these tables are from the Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering, Fall 2003. The tables present information on trends in enrollment of science and engineering graduate students through 2003. Data are presented on trends in number of graduate students by sex, race/ethnicity, citizenship, field and primary source and mechanism of support.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;03/09/2006&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf06307/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf06307&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2006.03.09&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering: Fall 2003&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2006&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reports&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering Indicators&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering Indicators&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3677,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSB 06-01&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The Science and Engineering Indicators, a biennial report series published by the National Science Board, is designed to provide a broad base of quantitative information about U.S. science, engineering, and technology for use by public and private policymakers. Because of the spread of scientific and technological capabilities around the world, this report presents a significant amount of material about these international capabilities and analyzes the U.S. position in this broader context.&lt;br /&gt;&lt;br /&gt;Science and Engineering Indicators 2006 contains quantitative analyses of key aspects of the scope, quality, and vitality of the Nation&#39;s science and engineering enterprise. The report presents information on science, mathematics, and engineering education at all levels; the scientific and engineering workforce; U.S. and international R&amp;D performance and competitiveness in high technology; public attitudes and understanding of science and engineering; and various aspects of state science and technology infrastructure through a series of state-level indicators. An overview chapter presents the key themes emerging from these analyses.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;02/23/2006&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/seind06/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsb0601&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2006.02.23&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering Indicators - 2006&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2006&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3647,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 06-306&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Data from four National Science Foundation R&amp;D surveys show 2003 R&amp;D expenditures grew to $291.9 billion, after declining in 2002 for the first time since 1953. Growth is projected to continue in 2004. The business sector, which performed 70% of U.S. R&amp;D in 2004, is largely responsible for the decline and subsequent recovery. Projected 2004 R&amp;D expenditures by sector and character of work are presented, along with long-term trends by performer and funding sector.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;02/07/2006&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf06306/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf06306&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2006.02.07&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;US R&amp;D Continues to Rebound in 2004&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2006&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Characteristics of College Graduates&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Characteristics of College Graduates&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3646,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 06-304&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Data from the National Survey of College Graduates show a 40% increase in the number of college graduates between 1993 and 2003. In 2003, the largest proportion of employed college graduates worked in the business/industry sector (67%), followed by educational institutions (22%) and government (11%). Half of doctorate holders were employed in science and engineering occupations, compared with 14% of employed college graduates overall. Median salaries differ by employment sector, occupation group, and level of degree.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;12/20/2005&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf06304/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf06304&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2005.12.20&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;2003 College Graduates in the U.S. Workforce: A Profile&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2005&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:2486,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 06-305&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Reversing the decline between 2001 and 2002, companies spent $204 billion on R&amp;D in 2003, a 5.2% increase. R&amp;D performing companies’ domestic net sales rose 17% over 2002 while employment remained level. R&amp;D outsourcing, collaborative R&amp;D, and R&amp;D performed in selected technology areas are discussed. Tables compare 2002 and 2003 expenditures by funding source, performing sector, company size, sales, and employment. Data are from the Survey of Industrial Research and Development.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;12/19/2005&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf06305/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf06305&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2005.12.19&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;,&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Increase in US Industrial R&amp;D Expenditures Reported for 2003 Makes Up For Earlier Decline&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2005&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Characteristics of Scientists and Engineers with U.S. Doctorates&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Characteristics of Scientists and Engineers with U.S. Doctorates&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:1789,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 06-302&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Data from the 2003 Survey of Doctorate Recipients show that science and engineering doctorate recipients who reported the longest work weeks either held degrees in biological or agricultural sciences (50.5 hours) or worked in the education sector (50.6 hours). There was no clear correlation between work-week length and years since doctorate, but work-week length did differ by tenure status and by sex when there were nonadult children in the household.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;12/09/2005&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf06302/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf06302&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2005.12.09&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;All In a Week&#39;s Work: Workweeks of Doctoral Scientists and Engineers&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2005&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Characteristics of Recent College Graduates&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Characteristics of Recent College Graduates&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3648,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 06-303&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Recent bachelor&#39;s and master&#39;s engineering graduates and graduates with computer sciences bachelor&#39;s are more likely to be employed full time and to earn higher salaries than are graduates in other fields. Industry continues to be the largest employer of recent graduates in science, engineering, or health. Data are from the 2003 National Survey of Recent College Graduates for graduates who received bachelor&#39;s or master&#39;s degrees in science, engineering, or health fields between July 2000 and June 2002.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;12/09/2005&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf06303/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf06303&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2005.12.09&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;National Survey of Recent College Graduates (NSRCG)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Survey of Recent College Graduates (NSRCG)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Recent Engineering and Computer Science Graduates Continue to Earn the Highest Salaries&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2005&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3640,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 06-301&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;According to data from the Survey of Earned Doctorates, doctoral awards in science and engineering rose in 2004 to 26,275, the second successive annual increase, but with considerable variation by field. Awards remained below the 1998 all-time high of 27,278. Non-U.S. citizens received about one-third of the 2004 awards, about the same as in 1995. Among U.S. citizens, Asians and underrepresented minority groups received a larger share of awards in 2004 than in 1995.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;11/22/2005&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf06301/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf06301&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2005.11.22&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;2004 Doctorate Awards Increase in Science and Engineering Fields for the Second Year in a Row&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2005&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal Funds for R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Funds for R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3623,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 06-300&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Federal obligations for R&amp;D and R&amp;D plant in FY 2005 are expected to increase 3.5% over the FY 2004 level, with obligations for research accounting for nearly half the total amount. Obligations are shown by basic research, applied research, development, R&amp;D plant, federal agency, and field of science and engineering. Six federal agencies account for 94% of estimated FY 2005 research dollars. Data are from the Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development: FY 2003-2005.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;11/17/2005&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf06300/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf06300&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2005.11.17&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Support for R&amp;D and R&amp;D Plant Projected at $110 Billion in FY 2005&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2005&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding, by Budget Function&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding, by Budget Function&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3622,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 05-322&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;President Bush&#39;s FY 2006 budget request for federal R&amp;D shows only a slight increase (0.2%) over FY 2005. The defense share of the federal R&amp;D total remains level, reversing the recent trend. In the nondefense component, the budget for space research and technology is slated to increase by 3.2%; levels are down for most other nondefense functions. Tables show FY 2001–06 data for total federal R&amp;D and FY 2003–06 funding by category and agency.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;10/06/2005&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf05322/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf05322&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2005.10.06&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;President&#39;s FY 2006 Budget Requests Level R&amp;D Funding&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2005&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Academic R&amp;D Expenditures&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Academic R&amp;D Expenditures&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3620,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 05-320&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Detailed statistical tables present data on R&amp;D expenditures of higher education institutions and federally funded research and development centers (FFRDCs). Tables include academic expenditures by type of institution, field, geographic area, source of funds (Federal, state and local, industry, institutional), and expenditures for research equipment. Many tables show trends over time. Data are collected through the annual Survey of R&amp;D Expenditures at Universities and Colleges.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/07/2005&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf05320/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf05320&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2005.09.07&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Academic R&amp;D Expenditures: FY 2003&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2005&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal S&amp;E Support to Universities, Colleges, and NPOs&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal S&amp;E Support to Universities, Colleges, and NPOs&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3619,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 05-321&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Data from the Survey of Federal Science and Engineering (S&amp;E) Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions show that federal obligations for academic S&amp;E activities reached a record $26.7 billion in FY 2003, a 9 percent increase in current dollars over FY 2002. Federal support also attained record levels for R&amp;D plant; fellowships, traineeships, and training grants; and other S&amp;E activities. Tables show support by agency, top 20 university recipients, and top 10 nonprofit recipients.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/02/2005&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf05321/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf05321&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2005.09.02&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal S&amp;E Obligations to Academic and Nonprofit Institutions Reached Record Highs in FY 2003&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2005&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reports&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:1780,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 05-318&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Data from the 1999 and 2001 National Survey of Recent College Graduates are used to examine interstate migration of recipients of science and engineering bachelor&#39;s and master&#39;s degrees between four life events: birth, high school graduation, bachelor&#39;s/master&#39;s degree graduation, and postgraduate employment. Migration was associated with several demographic, educational, and occupational characteristics of the degree recipients, and tables present these data.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;08/29/2005&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf05318&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf05318&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2005.08.29&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;National Survey of Recent College Graduates (NSRCG)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Survey of Recent College Graduates (NSRCG)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Interstate Migration Patterns of Recent Recipients of Bachelor&#39;s and Master&#39;s Degrees in S&amp;E&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2005&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Scientists, Engineers, and Technicians in Industry&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Scientists, Engineers, and Technicians in Industry&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:1887,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 05-313&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report presents estimates of the total number of positions filled by scientists, engineers, and technicians employed in the U.S. economy, by industry and by occupational category. Estimates are also presented for mean wages (hourly and annual) of scientists, engineers, and technicians by industry and by occupational category.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;08/17/2005&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf05313/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf05313&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2005.08.17&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Occupational Employment Statistics Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Occupational Employment Statistics Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Scientists, Engineers, and Technicians in the US: 2001&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2005&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in S&amp;E&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in S&amp;E&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3599,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 05-317&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The 2003 Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (S&amp;E) shows S&amp;E graduate enrollment increasing 4 percent and academic postdoctoral appointments increasing 6 percent since 2002. Sixty percent of postdoctoral appointees had temporary visas. Enrollment grew in all demographic groups and in all major S&amp;E fields and subfields except computer sciences. For the second consecutive year, first-time, full-time enrollment of temporary-visa holders decreased (8 percent), mostly among men. Tables show 1993-2003 trends.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;08/10/2005&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf05317/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf05317&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2005.08.10&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Graduate Enrollment in S&amp;E Programs Up in 2003, but Declines for First-Time Foreign Students&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2005&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Facilities for S&amp;E Research&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Facilities for S&amp;E Research&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:3077,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 05-314&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;NSF&#39;s Survey of Science and Engineering Research Facilities shows research space in academic institutions increased 11 percent from FY 2001 to FY 2003, the largest increase since FY 1988. Doctorate-granting institutions accounted for most total research space and construction of new space; biological and medical sciences were the primary users. Also reported are the first survey results on computing and networking infrastructure in academic institutions, including number and type of connections and connection speeds.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;07/26/2005&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf05314/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf05314&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2005.07.26&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Science and Engineering Research Facilities&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Science and Engineering Research Facilities&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Universities Continue to Expand Their Research Space with the Largest Increase Since 1988; Data Reported for Networking Capacity&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2005&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:3074,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 05-316&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Data from the NSF Survey of Industrial R&amp;D and the IRS show that research and experimentation (R&amp;E) tax credit claims averaged 11% annual growth from 1990 to 2001. Direct federal funding for industrial R&amp;D declined over the same period, but R&amp;E tax credit claims were still less than 4% of total industry R&amp;D expenditures in 2001. R&amp;E tax credit claims are shown by industry classification and as a percentage of industry-funded R&amp;D expenditures over time.&quot;,&quot