lse,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Funds for R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:1866,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 04-332&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Detailed tables present data on federal obligations for research to universities and colleges by federal agency and detailed field. Data are from annual surveys of federal funds for R&amp;D and represent research funding levels of the six federal agencies required to report research funding to universities and colleges. Obligations for basic and applied research are shown separately. Data are actual for 1973 through 2001 and estimated for 2002 and 2003.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;08/11/2004&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf04332/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf04332&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2004.08.11&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Funds for R&amp;D: FYs 1973-2003; Federal Obligations for Research to Universities and Colleges by Agency and Field of S&amp;E&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2004&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Facilities for S&amp;E Research&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Facilities for S&amp;E Research&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:1006,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 02-307&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Detailed statistical tables present 2001 data for research-performing colleges and universities and biomedical research institutions in the U.S. Tables show the amount of S&amp;E research space and the adequacy of this space to meet program commitments by field, type of institution, and geographic region. Also presented are data on S&amp;E and non-S&amp;E instructional space at colleges and universities. Data are from the Survey of Scientific and Engineering Research Facilities. &quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;08/02/2004&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf02307/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf02307&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2004.08.02&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Science and Engineering Research Facilities&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Science and Engineering Research Facilities&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;S&amp;E Research Facilities: 2001&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2004&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Academic R&amp;D Expenditures&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Academic R&amp;D Expenditures&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:1857,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 04-330&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Data presented from the Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges, FY 2002.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;07/30/2004&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf04330/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf04330&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2004.07.30&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Academic R&amp;D Expenditures: FY 2002&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2004&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal Funds for R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Funds for R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:1860,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 04-331&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Federal obligations for R&amp;D and R&amp;D plant in FY 2004 are expected to increase 4 percent over the FY 2003 level. Obligations are shown by basic research, applied research, development, R&amp;D plant, and federal agency. Six federal agencies will account for 93 percent of research dollars in FY 2004. Data are from the Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development: FY 2002–2004.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;07/29/2004&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf04331/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf04331&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2004.07.29&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Obligations for R&amp;D and R&amp;D Plant Expected to Reach Over $105 Billion in FY 2004&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2004&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding, by Budget Function&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding, by Budget Function&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:1803,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 04-329&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This annual report contains information about Federal funding of the research and development (R&amp;D) and the R&amp;D plant components of agency programs, as proposed by the administration for fiscal year (FY) 2004.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;07/22/2004&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf04329/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf04329&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2004.07.22&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding by Budget Function: FYs 2002-04&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2004&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:1762,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 04-328&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Between 1995 and 2001 real annual salaries increased and the S&amp;E workforce grew. Among newer doctoral cohorts, salary increases were greatest for those employed in industry, especially for those holding doctorates in computer and mathematical sciences, engineering, and physical sciences. The greatest workforce increases also were in the industrial sector. Information on publication-related activities and patents are discussed. Data are from the Survey of Doctoral Scientists and Engineers, 1995 and 2001.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;07/06/2004&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf04328/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf04328&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2004.07.06&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Employment Sector, Salaries, Publishing, and Patenting Activities of S&amp;E Doctorate Holders&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2004&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:1870,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 04-327&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The 2002 Survey of Earned Doctorates shows few U.S.-born S&amp;E doctorate recipients (3 percent) went  abroad after receiving the doctorate compared with naturalized citizens and permanent residents (5 percent) and temporary visa holders (25 percent). Top destinations for U.S.-born recipients in recent years have been Canada, the United Kingdom, Germany, France, Japan, Switzerland, and Australia. A majority of those who went abroad had fellowships, assistantships, traineeships, or other study planned. Tables and figures display short- and long-term trends.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;06/30/2004&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf04327/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf04327&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2004.06.30&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Emigration of US-born S&amp;E doctorate recipients&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2004&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in S&amp;E&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in S&amp;E&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:1872,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 04-326&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The 2002 Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering shows S&amp;E graduate enrollment and academic postdoctoral appointments both grew 6 percent since 2001, reaching all-time highs. Enrollment rose for all major S&amp;E fields, full- and part-time students, men and women, and all citizenship and racial/ethnic groups. This first post-9/11 survey reports declines in full-time, first-time enrollment of temporary visa holders in most fields, especially for men. Tables show 1992–2002 trends.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;06/28/2004&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf04326/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf04326&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2004.06.28&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Graduate Enrollment in S&amp;E Fields Reaches New Peak; First-Time Enrollment of Foreign Students Declines&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2004&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Survey&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:1839,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;SRS 04-902&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Description of the methods used to redesign the survey &quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;06/21/2004&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/srvyfacilities/meth03/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;srs04902&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2004.06.21&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Redesign of Survey of S&amp;E Research Facilities&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2004&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reports&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Women, Minorities, and Persons with Disabilities in STEM&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Women, Minorities, and Persons with Disabilities in STEM&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:1818,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 04-317&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;NSF is pleased to announce that Women, Minorities, and Persons with Disabilities in Science and Engineering is moving to a new concept to provide the most current information available. Rather than a static report, the new format is a dynamic web-based information source with data updated as they become available. Women, Minorities, and Persons with Disabilities in Science and Engineering provides a broad base of quantitative information about participation of women, minorities, and persons with disabilities in science and engineering education and employment. This information source offers no endorsement or recommendations on policies or programs. The report contains data tables organized by topic (e.g., undergraduate enrollment, graduate degrees, employment) and also by group (e.g., Hispanics, minority women, persons with disabilities). Presentation slides, which are charts in powerpoint, graphic, and spreadsheet format, are provided for easy downloading. Further, links to additional data sources (e.g., National Center for Education Statistics, American Council on Education) and reports on these topics are provided. A CD and hard copies of the report are updated every two years. Data on the website are updated as they become available.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;06/21/2004&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/wmpd/archives/wmpd_2004.zip&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf04317&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2004.06.21&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Women, Minorities, and Persons with Disabilities in S&amp;E: 2004&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2004&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reports&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:1765,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 04-323&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Female scientists and engineers are less successful than their male counterparts in several aspects of their academic careers. Comparisons of men and women employed full time in academe by tenure-track placement, tenure achievement, and academic rank are presented. The study, using data from the Survey of Doctorate Recipients, controlled for factors other than sex that might be related to career outcomes and looked at men and women at two time points in their careers.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;06/14/2004&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf04323/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf04323&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2004.06.14&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Gender Differences in the Careers of Academic Scientists and Engineers&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2004&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal S&amp;E Support to Universities, Colleges, and NPOs&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal S&amp;E Support to Universities, Colleges, and NPOs&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:1852,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 04-324&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Returns from 18 agencies to the FY 2002 Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions show new highs of federal obligations for S&amp;E activities—$24.4 billion to academe (more than 8 percent over FY 2001); $5.3 billion to independent nonprofit institutions (42 percent over FY 2001). Tables present obligations for six activities, leading agency funders, and leading institutional recipients. Academic category and agency data compare FY 2001 and 2002.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;06/09/2004&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf04324/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf04324&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2004.06.09&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal S&amp;E Obligations to Academic and NPOs Reach Record Highs in FY 2002&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2004&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:1851,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 04-325&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;In FY 2001 federal agencies provided over $404 million in support of S&amp;E activities to historically black colleges and universities (HBCUs), $509 million to Hispanic-serving institutions, and $29 million to tribal institutions. Dollars to minority-serving institutions were allocated less for R&amp;D and more for S&amp;E capacity-building activities, compared with dollars to other institutions. Tables show total and R&amp;D obligations by agency and institution and show R&amp;D expenditures and trend data for HBCUs.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;06/09/2004&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf04325/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf04325&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2004.06.09&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges&quot;,&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;The Extent of Federal S&amp;E Funding to Minority-Serving Institutions&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2004&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:1848,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 04-320&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The 2002 Survey of Industrial Research and Development shows the largest single-year reduction in industrial R&amp;D expenditures since the survey’s inception—down $7.7 billion (current dollars) and $8.9 billion (constant dollars) from 2001. Both company and federal funding of expenditures declined. R&amp;D outsourcing, collaborative R&amp;D, and R&amp;D performed in biotechnology, materials synthesis/processing, software development, and nanotechnology are discussed. Tables compare 2001 and 2002 expenditures by funding source, performing sector, company size, sales, and employment.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;05/20/2004&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf04320/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf04320&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2004.05.20&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;,&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Largest Single-Year Decline in Industrial R&amp;D Expenditures Reported for 2002&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2004&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;S&amp;E Degrees, By Race/Ethnicity of Recipients&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;S&amp;E Degrees, By Race/Ethnicity of Recipients&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:1831,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 04-318&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report provides trend data on the number of bachelor&#39;s, master&#39;s, and doctorate degrees conferred in the United States from 1992 to 2001, by race/ethnicity of the recipients of the degrees. The report also shows earned degrees by gender, broad S&amp;E fields, and citizenship. Earlier data are available from the NSF/SRS website, either in the archived publication: Science and Engineering Degrees, by Race/Ethnicity: 1987-1994, or from the WebCASPAR database for the years 1978-2001. &quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;05/17/2004&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf04318/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf04318&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2004.05.17&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Integrated Postsecondary Education Data System Completions Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Integrated Postsecondary Education Data System Completions Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;S&amp;E Degrees, by Race/Ethnicity of Recipients: 1992 - 2001&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2004&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Academic R&amp;D Expenditures&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Academic R&amp;D Expenditures&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:1940,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 04-319&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;For the FY 2002 Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges, more institutions reported increases in R&amp;D expenditures than in any of the prior six years. Expenditures topped $36.3 billion in FY 2002, up 10.9 percent over FY 2001 and almost 50 percent over FY 1997. Trend data show numbers of institutions with increased R&amp;D expenditures and expenditures by funding sources, character of work, and leading institutions.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;05/11/2004&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf04319/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf04319&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2004.05.11&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;US Academic R&amp;D Continues to Grow as More Universities and Colleges Expand Their R&amp;D Activities&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2004&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:1817,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 04-316&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Data from the 2001 National Survey of Recent College Graduates show salary levels, employment status, and likelihood to hold employment in S&amp;E differ depending on level and field of degree among individuals who received S&amp;E bachelor’s or master’s degrees in 1999 and 2000. Sector of employment for these individuals and the percentage who were full-time students in 2001 are also presented.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;