54&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf04309&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2004.03.25&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Master Government List of Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers: FY 2004&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2004&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:1785,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 04-308&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Changes in the number of postdoctoral appointments (postdocs) among new doctorate recipients in S&amp;E and non-S&amp;E fields and differences by field, sex, citizenship, race/ethnicity, and source of support are examined using Survey of Earned Doctorates data. Postdocs continue to be most common in biological sciences and among men and non-U.S. citizens. Universities’ share of postdoc support has increased as the U.S. government’s share has decreased. Tables and figures show data for 1982 and 1993–2002.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;03/25/2004&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf04308/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf04308&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2004.03.25&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Plans for Postdoctoral Research Appointments Among Recent US Doctorate Recipients&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2004&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:1826,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 04-305&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Detailed tables present information for a sample of U.S. companies associated with IT-based innovation activities or with the use of IT-based innovation. The importance of IT for conducting business, reducing costs, and attracting investment capital is shown by company characteristics and innovation status. Also presented are factors influencing the decision to innovate and costs and benefits of IT-based innovation. Data are from the Information Technology Innovation Survey conducted in 2001–2002.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;03/16/2004&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf04305/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf04305&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2004.03.16&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;IT Innovation Survey: Fall 2001&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2004&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:1799,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 04-306&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Trends are reported in R&amp;D-related foreign direct investment (FDI) and alliance activity by U.S. multinational corporations in mainland China. U.S. affiliates more than doubled from 1994 to 2000, making China the 11th largest host of U.S. R&amp;D expenditures overseas in 2000 (it was 30th in 1994). Data displays include annual FDI in China (from U.S. Bureau of Economic Analysis) and numbers of U.S.-China alliances by various characteristics (from Thompson Financial Joint Ventures/Alliances database).&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;02/13/2004&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf04306/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf04306&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2004.02.13&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;U.S.-China R&amp;D Linkages: Foreign Direct Investment and Industrial Alliances in the 1990s&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2004&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:1841,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 04-307&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Based on NSF projections for 2003, there has been little change in total U.S. R&amp;D expenditures in real dollar terms since 2000. Between 1994 and 2000 R&amp;D performance outpaced the growth of the overall economy, but that trend reversed between 2001 and 2003. Projected 2003 data on R&amp;D expenditures are shown by source of funds, performing sector, and character of work (basic research, applied research, development). R&amp;D expenditure data are from NSF R&amp;D surveys.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;02/13/2004&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf04307/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf04307&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2004.02.13&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;US R&amp;D Projected to Have Grown Marginally in 2003&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2004&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reports&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal S&amp;E Support to Universities, Colleges, and NPOs&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal S&amp;E Support to Universities, Colleges, and NPOs&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:1745,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 04-304&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Federal S&amp;E support to academic institutions has decreased in categories other than R&amp;D during the past three decades, and the number of institutions receiving federal S&amp;E support has dropped. In contrast, R&amp;D support has increased threefold since FY 1971. Data are from the Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions and are presented by funding agency, Carnegie class, and kind of support.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;02/05/2004&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf04304/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf04304&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2004.02.05&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Changes in Federal Support for Academic S&amp;E and R&amp;D Activities Since the 1970s&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2004&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Characteristics of Recent College Graduates&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Characteristics of Recent College Graduates&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:1823,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 04-302&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Detailed Statistical Tables that present data on the educational and employment characteristics of graduates who recently received their science or engineering degree from a U.S. academic institution.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;01/01/2004&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf04302/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf04302&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2004.01.01&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;National Survey of Recent College Graduates (NSRCG)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Survey of Recent College Graduates (NSRCG)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Characteristics of Recent S&amp;E Graduates: 2001&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2004&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:855,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 03-313&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Detailed statistical tables from four NSF surveys present current and historical patterns of U.S. R&amp;D expenditures by source of funds, R&amp;D performer, character of work, monetary unit, and state. Some data are annual from 1953; state data cover odd years from 1987 to 1997, and 1998, 1999, and 2000. Other tables show number of personnel engaged in national R&amp;D, R&amp;D expenditures at federally funded research and development centers (FFRDCs), and R&amp;D expenditures in industrialized nations. &quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;01/01/2004&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf03313/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf03313&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2004.01.01&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources: 2002 Data Update&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2004&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Doctorate Recipients from U.S. Universities&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:1828,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 04-303&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Detailed statistical tables present data on doctorate awards. Some show 1993–2002 doctorates in S&amp;E and broad non-S&amp;E fields by citizenship, sex, race/ethnicity, and country origins of doctorate recipients. Others show 2002 data on states and institutions of doctorate awards and postgraduation plans of doctorate recipients. Data are collected directly from doctorate recipients through the annual Survey of Earned Doctorates. The survey instrument appears at the end of the report. &quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;10/28/2003&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf04303/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf04303&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2003.10.28&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;S&amp;E Doctorate Awards: 2002&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2003&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding, by Budget Function&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding, by Budget Function&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:1643,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 04-300&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;President Bush’s proposed budget for FY 2004 includes a 5.7 percent increase for Federal R&amp;D: 7.0 percent for defense and 4.1 percent for nondefense programs. Defense R&amp;D would grow to 56.7 percent of the total. Funding for the Department of Homeland Security (DHS)’s activities would rise about 50 percent, distributed among national defense, general science, transportation, and agriculture. Tables show FY 2000–FY 2004 data for total Federal R&amp;D and FY 2003–FY 2004 data for DHS’s activities. &quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;10/14/2003&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf04300/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf04300&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2003.10.14&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;President&#39;s Budget Includes Modest Increase for R&amp;D in FY 2004; R&amp;D Funding for Homeland Security Contributes to Defense, Science, Transportation, and Agriculture&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2003&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:1644,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 04-301&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Results of the 2001 Survey of Industrial Research and Development show industrial R&amp;D performance declined from $199.5 billion in 2000 to $198.5 billion in 2001. Company funding of R&amp;D rose slightly in 2001 to $181.6 billion, despite a decline in performers&#39; aggregate sales to $4.8 trillion. A table of summary statistics presents data on industrial R&amp;D performance for 2000 and 2001 by source of funds, sector and size of company, sales, and employment.  &quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;10/09/2003&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf04301/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf04301&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2003.10.09&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;,&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;US Industry Sustains R&amp;D Expenditures During 2001 Despite Decline in Performers&#39; Aggregate Sales&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2003&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reports&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Women, Minorities, and Persons with Disabilities in STEM&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Women, Minorities, and Persons with Disabilities in STEM&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:1756,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 03-312&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This biennial report discusses trends in the participation of women, minorities, and persons with disabilities in S&amp;E fields. It covers precollege coursetaking and completion rates; higher education enrollments, degrees, institutions, and financial support; employment status, occupations, sectors, and salaries; and such concerns as the “digital divide,” international differences in women’s participation, declines in male enrollment, changes in the Asian population, and changes in definitions of disability. Detailed data tables are included. &quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/25/2003&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf03312/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf03312&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2003.09.25&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Women, Minorities, and Persons with Disabilities in S&amp;E: 2002&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2003&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:1141,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 03-327&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Data from the Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges show a 9 percent increase in academic R&amp;D spending in S&amp;E fields from FY 2000 to FY 2001. Federally funded expenditures, up 10 percent, accounted for 59 percent of all expenditures. Expenditures from institutions&#39; own sources increased 10 percent, from state and local governments 5 percent, and from industry 4 percent.  Tables show recent trends in expenditures by source, institution, and type of research. &quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/03/2003&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf03327/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf03327&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2003.09.03&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Academic R&amp;D Spending Maintains Growth from All Sources in FY 2001&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2003&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal S&amp;E Support to Universities, Colleges, and NPOs&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal S&amp;E Support to Universities, Colleges, and NPOs&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:1795,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 03-326&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Detailed statistical tables present data on Federal S&amp;E obligations to universities, colleges, and nonprofit organizations by type of activity, geographic area, institution, and Federal agency. Data represent 19 Federal agencies that participated in the FY 2001 Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions. Federal obligations to historically black colleges and universities are shown. Although most data relate to FY 2001, trend data cover FYs 1994–2001 and FYs 1963–2001.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;08/21/2003&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf03326/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf03326&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2003.08.21&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal S&amp;E Support to Universities, Colleges, and NPOs: FY 2001&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2003&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal Funds for R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Funds for R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:1021,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 03-325&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;08/14/2003&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf03325/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf03325&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2003.08.14&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Funds for R&amp;D, Detailed Historical Tables, FYs 1951-2002&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2003&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reports&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:1140,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 03-322&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;A review of literature on women scientists and engineers in academe indicates that women are disadvantaged compared with men. They earn less, achieve senior ranks and tenure less frequently, and publish less often. The review considers human capital theory (skills and abilities affecting performance), occupational choice, perceptions of discrimination, personal characteristics such as age and family responsibilities, and characteristics of employer institutions. Studies include national and institutional samples. A bibliography contains 70 references.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;07/30/2003&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf03322/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf03322&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2003.07.30&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Gender Differences in the Careers of Academic Scientists and Engineers: A Literature Review&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2003&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering State Profiles&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering State Profiles&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:1824,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 03-324&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This is a tabular listing of 52 one-page science and engineering (S&amp;E) Profiles (including ones for D.C. and Puerto Rico) that summarize state-specific data on personnel and finances. Also included is a summary of all data variables&#39; totals of all 52 states. This will be an SRS web-only document.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;07/22/2003&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf03324/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf03324&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2003.07.22&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;S&amp;E State Profiles: 2000-01&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2003&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reports&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:1138,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 03-323&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This review of about 380 recent documents focuses on the impact of information technology on the quality and dissemination of scientific results. The authors focus especially on electronic journal publishing and the following issues: defining electronic journal publishing; stakeholder interests and concerns; desirable features of the new electronic artifact; pricing and business models; intellectual property rights; peer review; archiving; impact measurement; changes in researchers&#39; behavior; implications for underserved populations; and information security and user privacy.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;07/16/2003&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf03323/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf03323&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2003.07.16&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;The Implications of IT for Scientific Journal Publishing: A Literature Review&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2003&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Bu