er&quot;:&quot;NSF 03-317&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;2003 InfoBriefs&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;04/14/2003&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf03317/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf03317&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2003.04.14&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Academic S&amp;E Obligations Increased 13 Percent in FY 2001: Record Highs Reported in Five of Six Funding Categories&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2003&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Academic R&amp;D Expenditures&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Academic R&amp;D Expenditures&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:965,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 03-316&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Detailed statistical tables present data on R&amp;D expenditures of 609 higher education institutions and 36 federally funded research and development centers (FFRDCs) for FY 2001. Tables include academic expenditures by type of institution, field, geographic area, source of funds (Federal, state and local, industry, institutional), and expenditures for research equipment. Many tables show trends over time (FYs 1994–2001). The survey instrument appears at the end of the report. &quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;04/10/2003&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf03316/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf03316&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2003.04.10&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges&quot;,&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Higher Education Research and Development (HERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Academic R&amp;D Expenditures: FY 2001&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2003&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in S&amp;E&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in S&amp;E&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:738,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 03-315&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The fall 2001 Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering shows an increase in total S&amp;E graduate enrollment of 4 percent over the fall 2000 figure. Foreign students accounted for much of the increase. Enrollment rose in most science fields, led by computer sciences at 10 percent. Engineering enrollment rose 5 percent. Recent trends are examined by detailed S&amp;E field, enrollment status, sex, citizenship, and race/ethnicity of students.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;04/07/2003&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf03315/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf03315&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2003.04.07&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Graduate Enrollment Increases in S&amp;E Fields, Especially in Engineering and Computer Sciences&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2003&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:739,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 03-314&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This analysis of patenting of Internet-related business methods compares the inventive activities of the United States with more than 40 other countries. Throughout the 1995–99 period, the U.S. led in the overall number of patent applications filed for Internet-related business methods; the number patented in more than one country (international patents); the number of organizations filing applications; and the number of highly cited patents. Data were drawn from the Derwent World Patents Index.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;03/27/2003&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf03314/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf03314&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2003.03.27&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;International Patenting of Internet-Related Business Methods&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2003&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Characteristics of Scientists and Engineers with U.S. Doctorates&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Characteristics of Scientists and Engineers with U.S. Doctorates&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:929,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 03-310&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Detailed statistical tables present 2001 data on the number and median salaries of doctoral scientists and engineers by field of doctorate and occupation; demographic characteristics (gender, race/ethnicity, citizenship); and employment-related characteristics (sector, employer location, various labor-force rates). Data are collected directly from individuals through the biennial Survey of Doctorate Recipients. The survey instrument appears at the end of the report.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;03/12/2003&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf03310/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf03310&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2003.03.12&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Characteristics of Doctoral Scientists and Engineers in the US: 2001&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2003&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reference&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Master Government List of Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Master Government List of Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:1055,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 03-308&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Federally Funded Research and Development Centers&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;01/27/2003&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://ncses.nsf.gov/resource/master-gov-lists-ffrdc/prior-lists#card3454&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf03308&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2003.01.27&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Master Government List of Federally Funded R&amp;D Centers: FY 2003&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2003&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;National Patterns of R&amp;D Resources&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:740,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 03-307&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Preliminary data from four NSF surveys indicate that R&amp;D expenditures will continue to rise in 2002 to $291.7 billion, but at a slower rate than in recent years. The slowdown is most evident in industry-financed R&amp;D, coinciding with the economic downturn. Federal investment, the dominant factor in recent R&amp;D growth, is expected to increase, particularly in defense and health. Long-term trends in R&amp;D expenditures and recent data by performer and funding source are presented.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;01/09/2003&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf03307/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf03307&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2003.01.09&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Slowing R&amp;D Growth Expected in 2002&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2003&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reports&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:1098,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 03-305&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report contains a bibliography of 208 references related to the implications of information technology for postsecondary distance education. It also describes the evolution of distance education from correspondence courses to delivery via Internet technologies; highlights the implications for institutions, learners, courses, and faculty; and compares fall 1995 and academic year 1997/98 data from the National Center for Education Statistics on institutions offering distance education, course offerings, enrollments, and technologies used.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;01/09/2003&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf03305/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf03305&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2003.01.09&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;The Application and Implications of Information Technologies in Postsecondary Distance Education: An Initial Bibliography (January 2003)&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2003&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business and Industry R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:741,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 03-306&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Results of the 2000 Survey of Industrial Research and Development show industrial R&amp;D expenditures increased from $182.7 billion in 1999 to $199.5 billion in 2000. The R&amp;D-to-sales ratio, which measures industrial R&amp;D expenditures over domestic net sales, rose from 3.7 to 3.8 percent. A table of summary statistics presents data on industrial R&amp;D performance for 1999 and 2000 by source of funds, sector and size of company, sales, and employment. &quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;12/31/2002&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf03306/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf03306&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2002.12.31&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;,&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business R&amp;D and Innovation Survey (BRDIS)&quot;},{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Business Enterprise Research and Development (BERD) Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;US Industrial R&amp;D Expenditures and R&amp;D-to-Sales Ratio Reach Historical Highs in 2000&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2002&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:742,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 03-304&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This brief overview of literature on the implications of information technology for scientific journal publishing focuses on electronic journals. Current estimates indicate there are 3,200–4,000 e-journals in science, technology, and medicine. Discussed are variations among scientific disciplines in acceptance of e-journals; changing roles of stakeholders in scientific publishing; unique functions and attributes of e-journal articles; use of hyperlinks to supplement bibliometric techniques for studying impacts of individual works; pricing issues; and copyright concerns.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;12/19/2002&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf03304/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf03304&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2002.12.19&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Scholars Debate the Implications of IT for Scientific Journal Publishing&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2002&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering State Profiles&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering State Profiles&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:743,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 03-303&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;According to 2000 data from NSF&#39;s R&amp;D surveys, national R&amp;D expenditures were highly concentrated in a small number of states, with the top 20 accounting for 87 percent of the $247 billion total. Different lists of top 10 states emerge depending on the criteria used: total R&amp;D expenditures, R&amp;D as a percentage of gross state product, R&amp;D by type of performer, and most federally financed expenditures. &quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;12/05/2002&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf03303/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf03303&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2002.12.05&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Top R&amp;D-Performing States Display Diverse R&amp;D Patterns in 2000&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2002&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:744,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 03-301&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;A review of 36 controlled evaluation studies in elementary and secondary schools published since 1990 indicates that instructional technology improved student achievement during the past 4 decades. Most studies reported significant positive effects in mathematics and science learning, although effectiveness of different approaches varied. The review discusses integrated learning systems in mathematics, computer tutorials and simulations in science, and microcomputer-based laboratories. &quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;12/03/2002&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf03301/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf03301&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2002.12.03&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;School Mathematics and Science Programs Benefit From Instructional Technology&quot;,&quot;topics&