&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding, by Budget Function&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding, by Budget Function&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:1044,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 02-330&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Detailed statistical tables present data on Federal R&amp;D funding for 23 agencies. Most data represent budget authority (the basis for initial congressional action). FY 2002 data are preliminary estimates; FY 2003 data are figures proposed by the administration. Tables include data by broad budget function (national defense and such civilian functions as health and energy), specific activity, and funding agency. Historical tables present long-term trends in Federal R&amp;D funding and basic research.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/06/2002&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf02330/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf02330&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2002.09.06&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding by Budget Function: FYs 2001-03&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2002&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:745,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 02-333&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This analysis of patenting of human DNA sequences compares the inventive activities of the U.S. with more than 40 other countries. Throughout the 1980–99 period, the U.S. led in the overall number of patent applications for human DNA sequences; the number patented in more than one country (international patents); the number of organizations filing applications; and the number of highly cited patents. Data are from two databases, GENESEQ and the Derwent World Patents Index. &quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/06/2002&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf02333/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf02333&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2002.09.06&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;International Patenting of Human DNA Sequences&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2002&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:746,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 02-331&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;High-tech production and trade trends are examined in seven Latin American countries: Argentina, Brazil, Chile, Colombia, Costa Rica, Mexico, and Venezuela. High-tech manufacturing grew in these newly industrialized economies (NIEs), increasing from $27 billion in 1991 to $52 billion in 2000. Individual industries, and their economic significance to these Latin American countries, are discussed. Comparisons are made with other NIEs. Manufacturing data come from DRI-WEFA World Industry Monitor, World Industry Service database.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;09/06/2002&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf02331/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf02331&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2002.09.06&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Latin America: High-Tech Manufacturing on the Rise, but Outpaced by East Asia&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2002&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Characteristics of Scientists and Engineers with U.S. Doctorates&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Characteristics of Scientists and Engineers with U.S. Doctorates&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:931,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 02-328&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Detailed statistical tables present 1999 information on the number and median salaries of doctoral scientists and engineers by field of doctorate and occupation; demographic characteristics (gender, race/ethnicity, citizenship); and employment-related characteristics (sector, employer location, various labor-force rates). Data are collected directly from individuals through the biennial Survey of Doctorate Recipients. The survey instrument appears at the end of the report.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;08/29/2002&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf02328/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf02328&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2002.08.29&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Characteristics of Doctoral Scientists and Engineers in the US: 1999&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2002&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reports&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Characteristics of Scientists and Engineers with U.S. Doctorates&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Characteristics of Scientists and Engineers with U.S. Doctorates&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:932,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 02-324&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;This report compares employment characteristics of older (55+) doctoral scientists and engineers with their younger counterparts and with older scientists and engineers holding bachelor&#39;s or master&#39;s degrees. In addition to showing the age distribution of the S&amp;E doctoral labor force, data are presented on sector of employment, full-time vs. part-time status, productivity rates, buyout offers, and health insurance co