ot;:748,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 02-325&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Estimating the size of the 1999 U.S. S&amp;E workforce by counting persons with a bachelor’s or higher degree employed in an S&amp;E occupation yields an estimate of 3.5 million. Counting any employed individual with a science or engineering degree, the estimate is above 10 million. Of the 5 million S&amp;E degree holders not working in an S&amp;E occupation, over two-thirds said their jobs were at least somewhat related to their highest S&amp;E degree field. &quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;07/26/2002&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf02325/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf02325&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2002.07.26&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;How Large is the US S&amp;E Workforce?&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2002&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal Funds for R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Funds for R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:1015,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 02-321&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Detailed statistical tables provide data on Federal funding of R&amp;D by agency, character of work, and performer for FYs 2000, 2001, and 2002. Results are based on responses from the Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development, which included 31 agencies and their subdivisions.  Funding is also broken down by field of science and engineering and geographic area.  Historical data are presented, typically covering the years 1981–2002.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;07/16/2002&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf02321/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf02321&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2002.07.16&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Funds for R&amp;D: FYs 2000, 2001, and 2002, Volume 50&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2002&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:750,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 02-323&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Data from the Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges show a 240 percent increase in overall academic R&amp;D expenditures from 1972 to 2000, a 180 percent increase from Federal funding, and a 350 percent increase from non-Federal funds. The Federal portion of funding decreased as the portions for industry and academe increased. Although the Federal government still provides the majority of funds in all S&amp;E fields except agricultural and social sciences, its share has declined in every field. &quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;07/15/2002&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf02323/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf02323&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2002.07.15&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Research and Development Expenditures at Universities and Colleges&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Changes in Federal and Non-Federal Support for Academic R&amp;D Over the Past Three Decades&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2002&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering State Profiles&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering State Profiles&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:749,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 02-322&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;According to 1999 data from NSF&#39;s R&amp;D surveys, national R&amp;D expenditures were highly concentrated in a few states: the top 6 accounted for half, the top 10 for two-thirds, and the top 20 for 86 percent of the $232 billion total. Leading R&amp;D states are ranked by type of performer, R&amp;D as a percentage of gross state product, and supporting Federal agency. A table on Federal R&amp;D obligations by agency and top states is included. &quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;07/15/2002&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf02322/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf02322&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2002.07.15&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Half the Nation&#39;s R&amp;D Concentrated in Six States&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2002&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding, by Budget Function&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal R&amp;D Funding, by Budget Function&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:747,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 02-326&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;President Bush’s proposed budget for FY 2003 includes a 9.2 percent increase for Federal R&amp;D, with most of the increase slated for the two largest activities: health (up 12.5 percent) and defense (up 10.1 percent). Defense activities would receive 54.4 percent of Federal R&amp;D funding, and health activities 24.9 percent. R&amp;D for space research and technology would increase 13.4 percent to represent 6.9 percent of Federal R&amp;D. A table shows data for FYs 1998–2003. &quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;07/01/2002&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf02326/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf02326&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2002.07.01&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Proposed FY 2003 Budget Would Complete Plan to Double Health R&amp;D Funding, Considerably Expand Defense R&amp;D&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2002&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Reports&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Science and Engineering State Profiles&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering State Profiles&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:958,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 02-318&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Science and Engineering State Profiles&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;06/01/2002&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf02318/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf02318&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2002.06.01&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;S&amp;E State Profiles: 1999-2000&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2002&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Doctoral Scientists and Engineers Profiles&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Doctoral Scientists and Engineers Profiles&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:935,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 02-320&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Characteristics of Doctoral Scientists and Engineers in the United States&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;05/29/2002&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf02320/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf02320&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2002.05.29&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Doctorate Recipients (SDR)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Doctoral Scientists and Engineers: 1997 Profile Tables&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2002&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal S&amp;E Support to Universities, Colleges, and NPOs&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal S&amp;E Support to Universities, Colleges, and NPOs&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:989,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 02-319&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Detailed statistical tables present data on Federal S&amp;E obligations to universities, colleges, and nonprofit organizations by type of activity, geographic area, institution, and Federal agency. Data represent 18 Federal agencies that participated in the FY 2000 Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions. Federal obligations to historically black colleges and universities are shown. Although most data relate to FY 2000, trend data cover FYs 1993–2000 and FYs 1963–2000.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;04/23/2002&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf02319/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf02319&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2002.04.23&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal S&amp;E Support to Universities, Colleges, and NPOs: FY 2000&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2002&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in S&amp;E&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in S&amp;E&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:875,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 02-314&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Detailed statistical tables from the Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering show graduate enrollment and postdoctoral employment in U.S. institutions. Tables present trends on enrollment by field, sex, citizenship, race/ethnicity, source and mechanism of support; first-time and full-time enrollments; and graduate students and postdoctoral appointees in doctorate-granting institutions. Trends cover periods 1975–2000 and 1993–2000. Additional tables provide institutional data for 2000. The survey instrument is included.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;04/23/2002&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf02314/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf02314&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2002.04.23&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (GSS)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in S&amp;E: Fall 2000&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2002&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Federal Funds for R&amp;D&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Federal Funds for R&amp;D&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:752,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 02-315&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Data from the Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development show an increase in non-defense R&amp;D funding, especially in health-related areas, since 1990. Overall Federal obligations rose on average 2.1 percent annually from 1990 to 2002; growth rates varied by department. The Department of Defense share decreased from 57 to 40 percent between 1990 and 2002; the Health and Human Services share increased from 13 to 29 percent. Data are also presented by field.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;04/18/2002&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf02315/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf02315&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2002.04.18&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Federal Funds for Research and Development&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Changing Composition of Federal Funding for R&amp;D and R&amp;D Plant Since 1990&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2002&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;InfoBrief&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[],&quot;id&quot;:751,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 02-316&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;The 2000 Survey of Earned Doctorates shows doctoral awards in physics and engineering declined 22 and 15 percent, respectively, from their all-time highs in the mid-1990s. Non-U.S. citizens accounted for 59 percent of the decline in physics and 74 percent in engineering. These declines resulted largely from decreases in awards to citizens of China, South Korea, and Taiwan. Tables show 1993–2000 doctorates in physics and engineering by citizenship and country.&quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;04/17/2002&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/infbrief/nsf02316/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf02316&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2002.04.17&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:true,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Survey of Earned Doctorates (SED)&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Declines in US Doctorate Awards in Physics and Engineering&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2002&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot;,&quot;dataCollection&quot;:[&quot;Scientists, Engineers, and Technicians in Industry&quot;],&quot;dataCollectionDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Scientists, Engineers, and Technicians in Industry&quot;}],&quot;id&quot;:951,&quot;number&quot;:&quot;NSF 02-313&quot;,&quot;pubAbstract&quot;:&quot;Detailed statistical tables present estimates of the total number of positions filled by scientists, engineers, and technicians in the U.S. workforce in 1998. Data are shown by broad and detailed Standard Industrial Classification (SIC). Estimates were developed from the Occupational Employment Statistics Survey, a Federal/state program administered by the Bureau of Labor Statistics.  &quot;,&quot;pubType&quot;:null,&quot;publishDate&quot;:&quot;04/11/2002&quot;,&quot;slug&quot;:&quot;https://www.nsf.gov/statistics/nsf02313/&quot;,&quot;slugifiedNumber&quot;:&quot;nsf02313&quot;,&quot;sortDate&quot;:&quot;2002.04.11&quot;,&quot;survey&quot;:[&quot;Occupational Employment Statistics Survey&quot;],&quot;surveyDTO&quot;:[{&quot;active&quot;:false,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Occupational Employment Statistics Survey&quot;}],&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Scientists, Engineers, and Technicians in the US: 1998&quot;,&quot;topics&quot;:[],&quot;year&quot;:&quot;2002&quot;},{&quot;aois&quot;:[],&quot;archived&quot;:true,&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Data Tables&quot